2025-11-03 15:40:46 [gpt-5-2025] You’re hitting a framing bug, not JSON itself.
2025-11-03 15:40:46 [gpt-5-2025] 
2025-11-03 15:40:46 [gpt-5-2025] What’s happening
2025-11-03 15:40:46 [gpt-5-2025] - Your TCP read coalesced two JSON objects into one buffer: {"action":"connect"}{"messages":[…]}.
2025-11-03 15:40:46 [gpt-5-2025] JSON.parse expects a single value, so it fails at the second {.
2025-11-03 15:40:46 [gpt-5-2025] - Your onFountain() splitter only looks for “}\n{”, but the stream delivered “}{” (no newline).
2025-11-03 15:40:46 [gpt-5-2025] Also, reads can split or merge arbitrarily.
2025-11-03 15:40:46 [gpt-5-2025] 
2025-11-03 15:40:46 [gpt-5-2025] Fixes (pick one, preferably both)
2025-11-03 15:40:46 [gpt-5-2025] - Frame the protocol (sender): write one JSON object per line (NDJSON). Append a newline when you
2025-11-03 15:40:46 [gpt-5-2025] call writeFountain(JSON.stringify(…)). Do the same anywhere else you emit JSON to the pipe.
2025-11-03 15:40:46 [gpt-5-2025] - Harden the receiver:
2025-11-03 15:40:46 [gpt-5-2025] 	- Keep a carryover string buffer for fountain reads.
2025-11-03 15:40:46 [gpt-5-2025] 	- Append new data, then split by newline; parse each complete line; keep any trailing partial line
2025-11-03 15:40:46 [gpt-5-2025] in the buffer for the next read.
2025-11-03 15:40:46 [gpt-5-2025] 	- If you can’t guarantee newlines, use a small brace-depth scanner to extract complete top‑level
2025-11-03 15:40:46 [gpt-5-2025] JSON objects (don’t rely on “}\n{” or “}{”).
2025-11-03 15:40:46 [gpt-5-2025] 
2025-11-03 15:40:46 [gpt-5-2025] Also
2025-11-03 15:40:46 [gpt-5-2025] - Your current “less than healthy” search for “}\n{” misses the exact case you hit. Either rely on
2025-11-03 15:40:46 [gpt-5-2025] NDJSON or implement proper tokenization.
2025-11-03 15:40:46 [gpt-5-2025] 
2025-11-03 15:41:12 [gpt-5-2025] Short answer: not required, but recommended.
2025-11-03 15:41:12 [gpt-5-2025] 
2025-11-03 15:41:12 [gpt-5-2025] Why:
2025-11-03 15:41:12 [gpt-5-2025] - In your loop you push tags then immediately reassign tags = {}. That means the pushed object
2025-11-03 15:41:12 [gpt-5-2025] won’t be mutated by future increments, so it’s safe.
2025-11-03 15:41:12 [gpt-5-2025] - However, if any nested objects inside tags might be referenced elsewhere (or future refactors
2025-11-03 15:41:12 [gpt-5-2025] reuse them), you can get aliasing.
2025-11-03 15:41:12 [gpt-5-2025] 
2025-11-03 15:41:12 [gpt-5-2025] Safer pattern:
2025-11-03 15:41:12 [gpt-5-2025] - Take a snapshot when you push (shallow is usually enough here since you rebuild tags; deep only
2025-11-03 15:41:12 [gpt-5-2025] if nested entries might be mutated later).
2025-11-03 15:41:12 [gpt-5-2025] - Or centralize the snapshotting in a small helper so it’s consistent and future‑proof.
2025-11-03 15:41:12 [gpt-5-2025] 
2025-11-03 15:41:39 [gpt-5-2025] Short answer: you don’t need a copy here, but a shallow copy is a safe improvement.
2025-11-03 15:41:39 [gpt-5-2025] 
2025-11-03 15:41:39 [gpt-5-2025] Why it’s safe without copying
2025-11-03 15:41:39 [gpt-5-2025] - You push sessions.push({ line: lineNumber, tags }) and immediately reassign tags = {}.
2025-11-03 15:41:39 [gpt-5-2025] - After reassignment you never mutate the old object, so the pushed snapshot won’t change.
2025-11-03 15:41:39 [gpt-5-2025] 
2025-11-03 15:41:39 [gpt-5-2025] When a shallow copy is better
2025-11-03 15:41:39 [gpt-5-2025] - Future refactors might accidentally keep mutating the same object instance before reassigning.
2025-11-03 15:41:39 [gpt-5-2025] - If tags values are distinct objects per session (as in your code), a shallow copy is enough to
2025-11-03 15:41:39 [gpt-5-2025] decouple the outer map.
2025-11-03 15:41:39 [gpt-5-2025] 
2025-11-03 15:41:39 [gpt-5-2025] When you’d need deep copy
2025-11-03 15:41:39 [gpt-5-2025] - Only if inner objects in tags are shared with other aggregates and will be mutated later, or you
2025-11-03 15:41:39 [gpt-5-2025] plan to reuse the same inner objects across sessions.
2025-11-03 15:41:39 [gpt-5-2025] 
2025-11-03 15:41:39 [gpt-5-2025] Rule of thumb
2025-11-03 15:41:39 [gpt-5-2025] - Keep current logic if you’re confident the old instance isn’t touched again.
2025-11-03 15:41:39 [gpt-5-2025] - Otherwise, snapshot with a shallow copy at push time to future‑proof.
2025-11-03 15:41:39 [gpt-5-2025] 
