2025-11-04 07:02:43 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 07:02:43 [kimi-k2-0905] a. Defensive check in JSObject::stringMember
2025-11-04 07:02:43 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 07:02:43 [kimi-k2-0905] std::string JSObject::stringMember(const std::string& name) const {
2025-11-04 07:02:43 [kimi-k2-0905]     if (!this) return "";               // <─ add
2025-11-04 07:02:43 [kimi-k2-0905]     auto it = members.find(name);
2025-11-04 07:02:43 [kimi-k2-0905]     return it != members.end() ? it->second : "";
2025-11-04 07:02:43 [kimi-k2-0905] }
2025-11-04 07:02:43 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 07:02:43 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 07:02:43 [kimi-k2-0905] b. Guard in JSValue::stringMember
2025-11-04 07:02:43 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 07:02:43 [kimi-k2-0905] std::string JSValue::stringMember(const std::string& name) const {
2025-11-04 07:02:43 [kimi-k2-0905]     if (type != OBJECT || !obj) return ""; // <─ add
2025-11-04 07:02:43 [kimi-k2-0905]     return obj->stringMember(name);
2025-11-04 07:02:43 [kimi-k2-0905] }
2025-11-04 07:02:43 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 07:02:43 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 07:02:43 [kimi-k2-0905] c. Early reject in parseRPC
2025-11-04 07:02:43 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 07:02:43 [kimi-k2-0905] static void parseRPC(const HeaderMap& header, const std::string& content) {
2025-11-04 07:02:43 [kimi-k2-0905]     if (content.empty()) {                  // <─ add
2025-11-04 07:02:43 [kimi-k2-0905]         sendError(400, "Empty body");
2025-11-04 07:02:43 [kimi-k2-0905]         return;
2025-11-04 07:02:43 [kimi-k2-0905]     }
2025-11-04 07:02:43 [kimi-k2-0905]     JSValue root = JSValue::parse(content);
2025-11-04 07:02:43 [kimi-k2-0905]     if (!root.isObject()) {                 // <─ add
2025-11-04 07:02:43 [kimi-k2-0905]         sendError(400, "JSON object expected");
2025-11-04 07:02:43 [kimi-k2-0905]         return;
2025-11-04 07:02:43 [kimi-k2-0905]     }
2025-11-04 07:02:43 [kimi-k2-0905]     std::string id = root.stringMember("id");
2025-11-04 07:02:43 [kimi-k2-0905]     ...
2025-11-04 07:02:43 [kimi-k2-0905] }
2025-11-04 07:02:43 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 07:02:43 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 07:02:43 [kimi-k2-0905] Apply (a) and (b) and the seg-fault disappears; add (c) and you stop feeding garbage to the parser
2025-11-04 07:02:43 [kimi-k2-0905] in the first place.
2025-11-04 07:02:43 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 07:15:30 [nitro@ryzen5] can we consider other situations similar to content empty?
2025-11-04 07:16:00 [kimi-k2-0905] Empty-body is only the most obvious “bad JSON” case.
2025-11-04 07:16:00 [kimi-k2-0905] Any of the following will hit the same NULL-pointer path in the current code:
2025-11-04 07:16:00 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 07:16:00 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. Content is completely empty (what you just saw).
2025-11-04 07:16:00 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. Content is white-space only (`"   \n  "`).
2025-11-04 07:16:00 [kimi-k2-0905] 3. Content is a JSON literal that is not an object (`"foo"`, `42`, `true`, `[]`).
2025-11-04 07:16:00 [kimi-k2-0905] 4. Content is a valid object but contains no member named `"id"` (the first thing the RPC
2025-11-04 07:16:00 [kimi-k2-0905] dispatcher touches).
2025-11-04 07:16:00 [kimi-k2-0905] 5. Content is truncated so the parser never manages to build a JSObject at all (parser returns
2025-11-04 07:16:00 [kimi-k2-0905] non-zero but you still dereference the out-pointer).
2025-11-04 07:16:00 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 07:16:00 [kimi-k2-0905] All of these end up with `JSValue::objectMember` or `JSValue::stringMember` being called on a value
2025-11-04 07:16:00 [kimi-k2-0905] whose `type == Null` (or on a null `JSObject*`) and the current implementation happily forwards the
2025-11-04 07:16:00 [kimi-k2-0905] call to the JSObject instance, hence the SIGSEGV.
2025-11-04 07:16:00 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 07:16:00 [kimi-k2-0905] So the minimal fix in `headless.h` is still:
2025-11-04 07:16:00 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 07:16:00 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 07:16:00 [kimi-k2-0905] // in JSValue
2025-11-04 07:16:00 [kimi-k2-0905] utf8 stringMember(utf8 name) const {
2025-11-04 07:16:00 [kimi-k2-0905]     if (type != Object || !object) return "";   // <-- guard
2025-11-04 07:16:00 [kimi-k2-0905]     return object->stringMember(name);
2025-11-04 07:16:00 [kimi-k2-0905] }
2025-11-04 07:16:00 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 07:16:00 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 07:16:00 [kimi-k2-0905] and identically for `integerMember`, `numberMember`, etc.
2025-11-04 07:16:00 [kimi-k2-0905] That single change makes every bullet above return a graceful “missing” value instead of crashing.
2025-11-04 07:16:00 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 07:16:34 [nitro@ryzen5] can i test for type is null
2025-11-04 07:16:44 [kimi-k2-0905] Yes – and it’s the safest first check.
2025-11-04 07:16:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 07:16:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 07:16:44 [kimi-k2-0905] utf8 JSValue::stringMember(utf8 name) const
2025-11-04 07:16:44 [kimi-k2-0905] {
2025-11-04 07:16:44 [kimi-k2-0905]     if (type == Null)            return "";   // 1. nothing there
2025-11-04 07:16:44 [kimi-k2-0905]     if (type != Object || !object) return "";   // 2. not an object
2025-11-04 07:16:44 [kimi-k2-0905]     return object->stringMember(name);
2025-11-04 07:16:44 [kimi-k2-0905] }
2025-11-04 07:16:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 07:16:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 07:16:44 [kimi-k2-0905] Same pattern for the other accessors (`integerMember`, `numberMember`, `arrayMember`,
2025-11-04 07:16:44 [kimi-k2-0905] `objectMember`).
2025-11-04 07:16:44 [kimi-k2-0905] Once those guards are in place you can feed the dispatcher empty strings, bare literals, arrays, or
2025-11-04 07:16:44 [kimi-k2-0905] objects without an `"id"` and you’ll get an empty return instead of SIGSEGV.
2025-11-04 07:16:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 
