2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] Looking at the crash back-traces in your notes, the seg-fault is happening inside
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] JSONParser::parseJSON when it tries to index a std::wstring that is empty (or shorter than the
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] index being used).
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] The top of the call stack shows:
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] #5 JSONParser::parseJSON (js8="0x%5B%5D=androxgh0st", ...)
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] and the abort message is:
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] Assertion '__pos <= size()' failed.
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] That assertion fires when the code does:
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] wchar_t ch = js[pos++];
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] and `pos` is already beyond the string length.
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] So the fix you landed is exactly the right idea – we have to bail out early if the incoming UTF-8
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] string is empty or if the converted wide string ends up empty.
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] Here is a minimal patch that does the check once, immediately after the conversion, and also adds a
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] second safety check inside the main loop so we can never over-run the buffer.
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] --- a/json.h
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] +++ b/json.h
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] @@ -312,6 +312,11 @@ struct JSONParser{
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905]                 try{
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905]                         js=convert32.from_bytes(js8);
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905]                 }catch(std::exception&){
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] +                       // empty or invalid UTF-8 – return a JSON null
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] +                       *result=new JSValue();
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] +                       return 1;
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] +               }
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] +               if(js.empty()){          // empty wide string – same thing
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905]                         *result=new JSValue();
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905]                         return 1;
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905]                 }
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] @@ -329,6 +334,10 @@ struct JSONParser{
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905]                         return 1;
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905]                 }
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905]                 while(true){
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] +                       if(pos>=js.size()){          // never read past end
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] +                               *result=new JSValue(); // malformed – return null
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] +                               return 1;
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] +                       }
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905]                         wchar_t ch=js[pos++];
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905]                         // --- rest of the loop unchanged ---
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] With those two guards in place:
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. Any completely empty POST body (`js8.empty()`) or any UTF-8 sequence that converts to an empty
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] wide string is immediately turned into a JSON `null` and the function returns error-code `1`.
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. Inside the parse loop we never dereference `js[pos]` unless `pos < js.size()`, so the
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] `pos <= size()` assertion can never fire again.
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] The server will still reply with a valid JSON-RPC error object (because `parseRPC` checks the
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] return code and wraps the result), but it will no longer crash.
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] Compile, run your stress test again, and the “androxgh0st” probe (or any other
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] zero-length/URL-encoded garbage) should be handled gracefully.
2025-11-04 07:24:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
