2025-11-04 07:28:33 [claude-sonnet-4] The fix looks solid - adding bounds checking at two critical points:
2025-11-04 07:28:33 [claude-sonnet-4] 
2025-11-04 07:28:33 [claude-sonnet-4] 1. Early exit for empty input - prevents the parser from even starting on empty/invalid
2025-11-04 07:28:33 [claude-sonnet-4] UTF-8
2025-11-04 07:28:33 [claude-sonnet-4] 2. Loop bounds check - the crucial `if(pos>=js.size())` guard before every `js[pos++]`
2025-11-04 07:28:33 [claude-sonnet-4] access
2025-11-04 07:28:33 [claude-sonnet-4] 
2025-11-04 07:28:33 [claude-sonnet-4] This is exactly the kind of defensive programming needed for a public-facing HTTP server. The
2025-11-04 07:28:33 [claude-sonnet-4] "androxgh0st" probe and similar scanner traffic will just get clean error responses instead of
2025-11-04 07:28:33 [claude-sonnet-4] crashing the process.
2025-11-04 07:28:33 [claude-sonnet-4] 
2025-11-04 07:28:33 [claude-sonnet-4] A couple of additional thoughts:
2025-11-04 07:28:33 [claude-sonnet-4] 
2025-11-04 07:28:33 [claude-sonnet-4] - You might want to log these malformed requests (maybe rate-limited) to track attack patterns
