2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] This is a Cisco ASA/AnyConnect-style exploit probe (`+CSCOL+` is a known traversal vector).
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] Good news: your server didn't serve the file, but it did leak internal paths and
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] responded with a delay, which is still noisy.
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] Here are devops-day hardening steps you can apply right now without rewriting the
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] world:
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] ✅ 1. Kill Path Leakage
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] • Problem:*
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] `filepath : ../../web/+CSCOL+/Java.jar` is exposed in logs and to the client.
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] • Fix:*
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] Strip all internal paths from responses and logs.
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] In `http.h`, change:
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] std::cout << "\tfilepath : " << filepath << std::endl;
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] • To:*
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] // std::cout << "\tfilepath : " << filepath << std::endl; // REMOVED
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] And never include `filepath` in the HTTP response body or headers.
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] ✅ 2. Drop the Connection Instantly on Malicious Path
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] • Problem:*
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] You’re sleeping for 9–12 seconds and still sending a 404. This burns resources.
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] • Fix:*
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] Immediately close the socket on suspicious paths. No response, no sleep.
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] Add a early-exit blacklist in `onRequest()`:
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] const bool isMalicious = path.find("+CSCOL+") != std::string::npos ||
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905]                          path.find("..") != std::string::npos ||
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905]                          path.find("~") == 0 ||
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905]                          path.find(".ssh") != std::string::npos;
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] if (isMalicious) {
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905]     close(fd);        // hard drop
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905]     return false;     // no keep-alive
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] }
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] ✅ 3. Rate-Limit by IP, Not by Request
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] • Problem:*
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] You’re sleeping per request. An attacker can open 100 sockets and burn 100 threads.
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] • Fix:*
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] Use a per-IP fail counter and ban after N bad paths.
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] Quick in-memory map (thread-safe):
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] std::mutex banMutex;
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] std::map<utf8, int> banMap;
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] bool isBanned(const utf8 &client) {
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905]     std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(banMutex);
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905]     return banMap[client] > 5;  // 5 strikes
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] }
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] void recordBadPath(const utf8 &client) {
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905]     std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(banMutex);
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905]     banMap[client]++;
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] }
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] Use it:
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] if (isBanned(client)) {
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905]     close(fd);
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905]     return false;
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] }
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] if (isMalicious) {
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905]     recordBadPath(client);
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905]     close(fd);
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905]     return false;
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] }
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] ✅ 4. Don’t Log Client IPv6 in Full
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] • Problem:*
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] `a00:b73c::` is logged in full. If logs leak, you leak internal addressing.
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] • Fix:*
2025-11-04 10:11:21 [kimi-k2-0905] Mask the last 64 bits (host part):
