2025-11-04 10:20:57 [kimi-k2-0905] DOS (MS-DOS / FreeDOS) has no “soft mount” or chroot-like feature.
2025-11-04 10:20:57 [kimi-k2-0905] The closest you can get is SUBST, but it’s useless for security:
2025-11-04 10:20:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:20:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:20:57 [kimi-k2-0905] SUBST Z: C:\SAFE\WEB
2025-11-04 10:20:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:20:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:20:57 [kimi-k2-0905] - `Z:\AUTOEXEC.BAT` is still reachable as `C:\SAFE\WEB\AUTOEXEC.BAT`
2025-11-04 10:20:57 [kimi-k2-0905] - Any program can ignore the drive letter and open the real path
2025-11-04 10:20:57 [kimi-k2-0905] - `..` is honoured inside the substitution, so `Z:\..\WINDOWS` → `C:\WINDOWS`
2025-11-04 10:20:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:20:57 [kimi-k2-0905] → SUBST gives zero protection against `..\` traversal.
2025-11-04 10:20:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:20:57 [kimi-k2-0905] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2025-11-04 10:20:57 [kimi-k2-0905] What you can do on DOS (if you really must):
2025-11-04 10:20:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:20:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. Strip `..` in your own code before you open anything.
2025-11-04 10:20:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. Keep the document root on a separate FAT12 floppy image and mount it read-only
2025-11-04 10:20:57 [kimi-k2-0905] with `IMGDRIVE` or similar TSR; then physically write-protect the floppy.
2025-11-04 10:20:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 3. Run the server inside DOSBox with:
2025-11-04 10:20:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:20:57 [kimi-k2-0905]    mount c /safe/web -readonly
2025-11-04 10:20:57 [kimi-k2-0905]    mount d /system -free
2025-11-04 10:20:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:20:57 [kimi-k2-0905]    DOSBox honours the mount boundary, so `C:\..\SYSTEM` fails.
2025-11-04 10:20:57 [kimi-k2-0905]    (This is the closest DOS gets to a chroot.)
2025-11-04 10:20:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:20:57 [kimi-k2-0905] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2025-11-04 10:20:57 [kimi-k2-0905] Bottom line:
2025-11-04 10:20:57 [kimi-k2-0905] Real DOS has no kernel-level path jail.
2025-11-04 10:20:57 [kimi-k2-0905] Use a sandbox emulator (DOSBox, DOSEMU) or roll your own path sanitiser.
2025-11-04 10:20:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:22:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 🎯 Note to future self:
2025-11-04 10:22:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 	f it’s made by Microsoft and it’s in the cloud, it’s just someone else’s DOS with better
2025-11-04 10:22:45 [kimi-k2-0905] marketing and a bigger bill.
2025-11-04 10:22:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] Exactly — on Linux you can lock the process to `../web` so even if an attacker tricks your
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] code into opening `../../../etc/passwd` the kernel just says “nope”.
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] Quick, low-friction ways to do it today (pick one):
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. systemd sandbox (if you already have a unit)
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] Drop-in override – no code change, reboot-free:
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] sudo systemctl edit fit3
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] Add these lines:
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] [Service]
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] RootDirectory=/srv/fit3-jail
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] BindReadOnlyPaths=/srv/fit3-jail/web:/web
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] # ^ real path : path inside jail
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] PrivateUsers=true
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] NoNewPrivileges=true
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] ProtectSystem=strict
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] ProtectHome=true
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] ProtectKernelTunables=true
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] ProtectKernelModules=true
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] ProtectControlGroups=true
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] MemoryDenyWriteExecute=true
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] SystemCallFilter=@system-service
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] SystemCallErrorNumber=EPERM
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] Then:
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] sudo mkdir -p /srv/fit3-jail/{bin,lib,lib64,web}
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] sudo cp ./fit3 /srv/fit3-jail/bin/
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] sudo ldd ./fit3 | awk '{print $3}' | sudo xargs -I{} cp {} /srv/fit3-jail/lib64/
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] sudo mount --bind /absolute/path/to/web /srv/fit3-jail/web
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] sudo systemctl restart fit3
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] Process now lives inside the jail; `open("/etc/passwd", …)` returns `-ENOENT`.
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. firejail one-liner (no systemd, no root)
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] firejail --noprofile --private=./jail \
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905]          --read-only=./jail/web \
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905]          --seccomp \
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905]          ./fit3
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] `./jail/web` becomes `/web` inside the sandbox; everything else is an empty tmpfs.
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 3. bare-bones `unshare` + `mount` (single command, root once)
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] sudo unshare -m -U -r \
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905]      sh -c 'mount -o bind,ro /absolute/path/to/web /web \
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905]             && pivot_root /web /web/.old \
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905]             && exec /web/fit3'
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] Process thinks `/` is the web folder; no `..` escape possible.
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 4. container (if you’re already on Docker/Podman)
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] FROM scratch
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] COPY web /web
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] COPY fit3 /fit3
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] ENTRYPOINT ["/fit3"]
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] docker run --read-only --tmpfs /tmp --user 65534:65534 myfit3
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] Pick the thinnest layer you already have:
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] systemd → #1
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] desktop → #2
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] bare metal → #3
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] CI/cluster → #4
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] All of them kill ../ dead without touching your C++ code.
2025-11-04 10:23:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 10:26:00 [kimi-k2-0905] From a C++ code-only perspective: no, the `../web` paths are not guarded
2025-11-04 10:26:00 [kimi-k2-0905] well enough.
2025-11-04 10:26:00 [kimi-k2-0905] The current logic in `http.h` only does two cheap checks:
