2025-11-04 10:31:50 [grok-code-fast-1] 
2025-11-04 10:31:50 [grok-code-fast-1] This conversation focused on security vulnerabilities in a custom C++ HTTP server for a
2025-11-04 10:31:50 [grok-code-fast-1] DSP/audio project (e.g., ALSA audio, DRM display, Redis integration). The server handles file
2025-11-04 10:31:50 [grok-code-fast-1] serving from a `../web` directory and was probed with exploits like Cisco's `+CSCOL+` path
2025-11-04 10:31:50 [grok-code-fast-1] traversal.
2025-11-04 10:31:50 [grok-code-fast-1] 
2025-11-04 10:31:50 [grok-code-fast-1] 🗣️ Key Discussion Points
2025-11-04 10:31:50 [grok-code-fast-1] 1. Initial Assessment: The server exposed internal file paths in logs and responded to
2025-11-04 10:31:50 [grok-code-fast-1] malicious requests (e.g., `/../web/+CSCOL+/Java.jar`), indicating path traversal risks.
2025-11-04 10:31:50 [grok-code-fast-1] 2. Vulnerabilities Identified:
2025-11-04 10:31:50 [grok-code-fast-1]    - Path traversal via `..`, URL encoding (`%2e%2e`), double slashes, symlinks.
2025-11-04 10:31:50 [grok-code-fast-1]    - Information leakage (full paths in logs/responses).
2025-11-04 10:31:50 [grok-code-fast-1]    - No rate limiting or sandboxing.
2025-11-04 10:31:50 [grok-code-fast-1] 3. Hardening Recommendations:
2025-11-04 10:31:50 [grok-code-fast-1]    - C++ Code Fixes: Add URL decoding, path sanitization (e.g., `isMaliciousPath()`
2025-11-04 10:31:50 [grok-code-fast-1] function), silent socket drops for bad requests, and IP-based rate limiting.
2025-11-04 10:31:50 [grok-code-fast-1]    - System-Level Defenses: Use chroot, systemd sandboxing, or firejail to jail the process
2025-11-04 10:31:50 [grok-code-fast-1] to `../web`.
2025-11-04 10:31:50 [grok-code-fast-1]    - Logging: Mask IPs (e.g., IPv6 host parts) and remove path exposure.
2025-11-04 10:31:50 [grok-code-fast-1]    - Testing: Add canary files to detect bypasses.
2025-11-04 10:31:50 [grok-code-fast-1] 4. Models' Inputs: Various AI models (kimi-k2-0905, deepseek-chat, grok-code-fast-1)
