2025-11-04 11:06:09 [nitro@ryzen5] restarts are hard,
2025-11-04 11:06:19 [nitro@ryzen5] kimi any comments?
2025-11-04 11:06:48 [kimi-k2-0905] Restarting services is indeed tricky—especially when you have live connections, shared state, and
2025-11-04 11:06:48 [kimi-k2-0905] security fixes in flight. A few things you might want to double-check next time you bounce the
2025-11-04 11:06:48 [kimi-k2-0905] server:
2025-11-04 11:06:48 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 11:06:48 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. Graceful shutdown order: close listening sockets first, then drain existing threads,
2025-11-04 11:06:48 [kimi-k2-0905] then free resources. Your `closeHttpTasks()` already walks the list and calls
2025-11-04 11:06:48 [kimi-k2-0905] `closeHandle()` on each connection, which is good; make sure the accept loops exit promptly after
2025-11-04 11:06:48 [kimi-k2-0905] you signal them (a simple atomic flag or `shutdown(fd, SHUT_RD)` on the listener can unblock the
2025-11-04 11:06:48 [kimi-k2-0905] `accept()` call).
2025-11-04 11:06:48 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 11:06:48 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. Socket-reuse race: you set `SOREUSEADDR`, but if you restart within the TCP
2025-11-04 11:06:48 [kimi-k2-0905] `TIMEWAIT` window some kernels still refuse the bind. Consider logging the exact `errno` on
2025-11-04 11:06:48 [kimi-k2-0905] bind failure so you know if it’s `EADDRINUSE` vs. something else.
2025-11-04 11:06:48 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 11:06:48 [kimi-k2-0905] 3. Thread count vs. `MaxRequest`: each `httpTask` allocates a 240 kB buffer on the stack.
2025-11-04 11:06:48 [kimi-k2-0905] If you ever raise the backlog above ~32, make sure your ulimit stack is generous or move the buffer
2025-11-04 11:06:48 [kimi-k2-0905] to heap so you don’t blow the worker thread stack on restart.
2025-11-04 11:06:48 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 11:06:48 [kimi-k2-0905] 4. URL-decode edge case: your new decoder silently passes through invalid hex (`%ZZ` stays
2025-11-04 11:06:48 [kimi-k2-0905] as literal `%ZZ`). That’s fine for robustness, but if you ever want strict validation you could
2025-11-04 11:06:48 [kimi-k2-0905] reject the whole request when `hexstream >> hexvalue` fails.
2025-11-04 11:06:48 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 11:06:48 [kimi-k2-0905] 5. Hot-reload hygiene: since you’re editing live, keep a checksum or commit hash in the
2025-11-04 11:06:48 [kimi-k2-0905] server header (`Server: dsptool/abc123`) so you can instantly verify which build is actually
2025-11-04 11:06:48 [kimi-k2-0905] running after a restart.
2025-11-04 11:06:48 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 11:06:48 [kimi-k2-0905] Otherwise the restart logic looks solid—atomic counters, mutex-protected task list, and explicit
2025-11-04 11:06:48 [kimi-k2-0905] `closeHandle()` on each connection. 🪣
