2025-11-04 11:14:35 [kimi-k2-0905] The 3-second `SO_RCVTIMEO` is fine for most traffic, but it’s a blunt hammer.  A few
2025-11-04 11:14:35 [kimi-k2-0905] one-line tweaks can make it adapt to real-world conditions without adding complexity:
2025-11-04 11:14:35 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 11:14:35 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. Registry-driven ceiling
2025-11-04 11:14:35 [kimi-k2-0905]    Replace the literal `3` with
2025-11-04 11:14:35 [kimi-k2-0905]    `int timeout = std::clamp<long>(registry.value("recv_timeout", 3L), 1L, 30L);`
2025-11-04 11:14:35 [kimi-k2-0905]    Gives you a knob you can turn at runtime (1–30 s) without recompiling.
2025-11-04 11:14:35 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 11:14:35 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. Progressive back-off per IP
2025-11-04 11:14:35 [kimi-k2-0905]    Keep a lightweight `std::unordered_map<utf8,int> ipFailures;`
2025-11-04 11:14:35 [kimi-k2-0905]    On any recv timeout or 4xx, bump the counter; on 200, decay it.
2025-11-04 11:14:35 [kimi-k2-0905]    One line in the accept path:
2025-11-04 11:14:35 [kimi-k2-0905]    `tv.tv_sec = 3 + std::min(ipFailures[client] * 2, 12);`
2025-11-04 11:14:35 [kimi-k2-0905]    Legitimate clients stay at 3 s; misbehaving ones quickly graduate to 15 s, then get recycled.
2025-11-04 11:14:35 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 11:14:35 [kimi-k2-0905] 3. Content-length hint
2025-11-04 11:14:35 [kimi-k2-0905]    If the request already advertises `Content-Length`, scale the timeout once:
2025-11-04 11:14:35 [kimi-k2-0905]    `tv.tv_sec = std::clamp(3 + (int)(contentLength / 50000), 3, 10);`
2025-11-04 11:14:35 [kimi-k2-0905]    50 kB ≈ 1 extra second; caps at 10 s even for huge uploads.
2025-11-04 11:14:35 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 11:14:35 [kimi-k2-0905] Pick any one of these (they’re mutually compatible) and you keep the original safety while letting
2025-11-04 11:14:35 [kimi-k2-0905] the server self-tune under load.  No extra threads, no event loops—just a smarter constant.
2025-11-04 11:14:35 [kimi-k2-0905] 
