2025-11-04 13:02:59 [kimi-k2-0905] Yes—everything that arrives through the raw `console.error` path is anonymous: we don’t
2025-11-04 13:02:59 [kimi-k2-0905] know which remote client, which worker, or even whether it came from the local process or over TCP.
2025-11-04 13:02:59 [kimi-k2-0905] All we have is the string itself, so the best we can do is stamp it with:
2025-11-04 13:02:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 13:02:59 [kimi-k2-0905] - the current model (`rohaModel`)
2025-11-04 13:02:59 [kimi-k2-0905] - the current session user (`rohaUser`)
2025-11-04 13:02:59 [kimi-k2-0905] - a local timestamp (`slopmark()`)
2025-11-04 13:02:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-04 13:02:59 [kimi-k2-0905] If you need real provenance (client IP, worker ID, etc.) we’d have to pipe the errors through a
2025-11-04 13:02:59 [kimi-k2-0905] different channel that carries metadata; right now the “PORT” pseudo-user is just a catch-all for
2025-11-04 13:02:59 [kimi-k2-0905] anything that hits `console.error`.
