2025-11-04 16:49:06 [deepseek-chat] Main differences between `{}` (plain object) and `Map`:
2025-11-04 16:49:06 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-11-04 16:49:06 [deepseek-chat] • Key types:*
2025-11-04 16:49:06 [deepseek-chat] - `{}`: Only strings and symbols as keys
2025-11-04 16:49:06 [deepseek-chat] - `Map`: Any type (objects, functions, numbers, etc.)
2025-11-04 16:49:06 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-11-04 16:49:06 [deepseek-chat] • Order:*
2025-11-04 16:49:06 [deepseek-chat] - `{}`: No guaranteed order (though modern JS maintains insertion order)
2025-11-04 16:49:06 [deepseek-chat] - `Map`: Maintains insertion order
2025-11-04 16:49:07 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-11-04 16:49:07 [deepseek-chat] • Size:*
2025-11-04 16:49:07 [deepseek-chat] - `{}`: Need to calculate manually (`Object.keys(obj).length`)
2025-11-04 16:49:07 [deepseek-chat] - `Map`: Built-in `size` property
2025-11-04 16:49:07 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-11-04 16:49:07 [deepseek-chat] • Performance:*
2025-11-04 16:49:07 [deepseek-chat] - `Map`: Better for frequent additions/removals
2025-11-04 16:49:07 [deepseek-chat] - `{}`: Slightly faster for simple key-value storage
2025-11-04 16:49:07 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-11-04 16:49:07 [deepseek-chat] • Iteration:*
2025-11-04 16:49:07 [deepseek-chat] - `Map`: Built-in iterators (`keys()`, `values()`, `entries()`)
2025-11-04 16:49:07 [deepseek-chat] - `{}`: Need `Object.keys()` etc.
2025-11-04 16:49:07 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-11-04 16:49:07 [deepseek-chat] For connection management, `Map` is usually better because of the built-in size tracking and
2025-11-04 16:49:07 [deepseek-chat] cleaner iteration.
2025-11-04 16:49:07 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-11-04 16:50:29 [nitro@ryzen5] how do you remove a map key?
2025-11-04 16:50:36 [deepseek-chat] Use the `delete()` method:
2025-11-04 16:50:36 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-11-04 16:50:36 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-11-04 16:50:36 [deepseek-chat] const myMap = new Map();
2025-11-04 16:50:36 [deepseek-chat] myMap.set('connection1', conn1);
2025-11-04 16:50:36 [deepseek-chat] myMap.set('connection2', conn2);
2025-11-04 16:50:36 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-11-04 16:50:36 [deepseek-chat] // Remove a key
2025-11-04 16:50:36 [deepseek-chat] myMap.delete('connection1');
2025-11-04 16:50:36 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-11-04 16:50:36 [deepseek-chat] // Check if key exists before deleting
2025-11-04 16:50:36 [deepseek-chat] if (myMap.has('connection2')) {
2025-11-04 16:50:36 [deepseek-chat]   myMap.delete('connection2');
2025-11-04 16:50:36 [deepseek-chat] }
2025-11-04 16:50:36 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-11-04 16:50:36 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-11-04 16:50:36 [deepseek-chat] The `delete()` method returns `true` if the key existed and was removed, `false` otherwise.
2025-11-04 16:50:36 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-11-04 16:51:01 [nitro@ryzen5] can i be iterating at the time of deletion
2025-11-04 16:51:08 [deepseek-chat] Yes, you can safely delete from a Map while iterating. Map iterators are robust to modifications
2025-11-04 16:51:08 [deepseek-chat] during iteration.
2025-11-04 16:51:08 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-11-04 16:51:08 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-11-04 16:51:08 [deepseek-chat] const connections = new Map();
2025-11-04 16:51:08 [deepseek-chat] connections.set('conn1', {});
2025-11-04 16:51:08 [deepseek-chat] connections.set('conn2', {});
2025-11-04 16:51:08 [deepseek-chat] connections.set('conn3', {});
2025-11-04 16:51:08 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-11-04 16:51:08 [deepseek-chat] // Safe to delete during iteration
2025-11-04 16:51:08 [deepseek-chat] for (const [name, conn] of connections) {
2025-11-04 16:51:08 [deepseek-chat]   if (name === 'conn2') {
2025-11-04 16:51:08 [deepseek-chat]     connections.delete(name); // This is safe
2025-11-04 16:51:08 [deepseek-chat]   }
2025-11-04 16:51:08 [deepseek-chat] }
2025-11-04 16:51:08 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-11-04 16:51:08 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-11-04 16:51:08 [deepseek-chat] The iterator will continue correctly with the remaining entries. This is one advantage over arrays
2025-11-04 16:51:08 [deepseek-chat] where modification during iteration can cause issues.
2025-11-04 16:51:08 [deepseek-chat] 
