2025-11-05 09:31:20 [scudmarks@discord] does discord support a half space in tick fenced code
2025-11-05 09:31:26 [kimi-k2-0905] Discord’s code fences use a fixed-width font that collapses any Unicode space (including U+2009
2025-11-05 09:31:26 [kimi-k2-0905] thin space or U+200A hair space) to a single ASCII space. So a “half space” will render like a
2025-11-05 09:31:26 [kimi-k2-0905] normal space inside back-tick blocks; you can’t rely on it for alignment.
2025-11-05 09:31:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-05 09:31:58 [scudmarks@discord] how do you correct for cell widths with unicode content
2025-11-05 09:32:11 [kimi-k2-0905] You pad to display width, not byte length.
2025-11-05 09:32:11 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-05 09:32:11 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. Measure each cell with a Unicode-width function
2025-11-05 09:32:11 [kimi-k2-0905]    (e.g. `wcswidth`, `unicode-width` crate, `Intl.Segmenter` in JS).
2025-11-05 09:32:11 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. Sum the widths per column.
2025-11-05 09:32:11 [kimi-k2-0905] 3. Add spaces until `(currentwidth + pad) == targetwidth`.
2025-11-05 09:32:11 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-05 09:32:11 [kimi-k2-0905] Example helper (Rust flavour, but the idea is the same everywhere):
2025-11-05 09:32:11 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-05 09:32:11 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-05 09:32:11 [kimi-k2-0905] use unicode_width::UnicodeWidthStr;
2025-11-05 09:32:11 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-05 09:32:11 [kimi-k2-0905] fn pad_to(s: &str, target: usize) -> String {
2025-11-05 09:32:11 [kimi-k2-0905]     let w = UnicodeWidthStr::width(s);
2025-11-05 09:32:11 [kimi-k2-0905]     if w < target {
2025-11-05 09:32:11 [kimi-k2-0905]         format!("{}{}", s, " ".repeat(target - w))
2025-11-05 09:32:11 [kimi-k2-0905]     } else {
2025-11-05 09:32:11 [kimi-k2-0905]         s.into()
2025-11-05 09:32:11 [kimi-k2-0905]     }
2025-11-05 09:32:11 [kimi-k2-0905] }
2025-11-05 09:32:11 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-05 09:32:11 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-05 09:32:11 [kimi-k2-0905] Use that value when you build the row string; the monospaced font then keeps everything aligned.
2025-11-05 09:32:11 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-05 09:48:12 [scudmarks@discord] another option may be to use nbsp for padding and not tick fance
2025-11-05 09:48:21 [kimi-k2-0905] Using non-breaking spaces (`&nbsp;`) for visual padding is indeed a quick hack, but it’s fragile:
2025-11-05 09:48:21 [kimi-k2-0905] it breaks when fonts, zoom levels, or screen readers change, and it pollutes the markup with purely
2025-11-05 09:48:21 [kimi-k2-0905] presentational characters.
2025-11-05 09:48:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-05 09:48:21 [kimi-k2-0905] If the goal is simply to avoid “tick fance” (checkbox styling), keep the HTML clean and let CSS
2025-11-05 09:48:21 [kimi-k2-0905] handle the spacing:
2025-11-05 09:48:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-05 09:48:21 [kimi-k2-0905] HTML
2025-11-05 09:48:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-05 09:48:21 [kimi-k2-0905] <span class="indent">Label text</span>
2025-11-05 09:48:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-05 09:48:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-05 09:48:21 [kimi-k2-0905] CSS
2025-11-05 09:48:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-05 09:48:21 [kimi-k2-0905] .indent {
2025-11-05 09:48:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 	display: inline-block;
2025-11-05 09:48:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 	padding-left: 1.2em; /* same horizontal space a checkbox would occupy */
2025-11-05 09:48:21 [kimi-k2-0905] }
2025-11-05 09:48:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-05 09:48:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-05 09:48:21 [kimi-k2-0905] No `&nbsp;`, no checkbox, no inline styles.
2025-11-05 09:48:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-05 09:49:07 [scudmarks@discord] no no ok, plan is to produce test table for all targets that validates widths are correct and not sloppy
2025-11-05 09:49:45 [kimi-k2-0905] Got it—you want a test table that proves every column width is exact, no “sloppy” slack space.
2025-11-05 09:49:45 [kimi-k2-0905] Below is a minimal, reusable rig that:
2025-11-05 09:49:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-05 09:49:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. Builds a table from a plain data set.
2025-11-05 09:49:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. Measures every rendered cell width (rounded to 0.5 px).
2025-11-05 09:49:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 3. Flags any column whose cells are > 0.5 px wider than the header.
2025-11-05 09:49:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 4. Prints a pass/fail row so you can scan quickly.
2025-11-05 09:49:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-05 09:49:45 [kimi-k2-0905] Drop the snippet in a test page, feed it your real data, and open DevTools → Console.
2025-11-05 09:49:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-05 09:49:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-05 09:49:45 [kimi-k2-0905] <!doctype html>
2025-11-05 09:49:45 [kimi-k2-0905] <meta charset="utf-8">
2025-11-05 09:49:45 [kimi-k2-0905] <title>Width-audit</title>
2025-11-05 09:49:45 [kimi-k2-0905] <style>
2025-11-05 09:49:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 	table { border-collapse: collapse; font: 14px/1.2 ui-monospace; }
2025-11-05 09:49:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 	th, td { border: 1px solid #888; padding: 2px 6px; white-space: pre; }
2025-11-05 09:49:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 	.fail { background: #fcc; }
2025-11-05 09:49:45 [kimi-k2-0905] </style>
2025-11-05 09:49:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-05 09:49:45 [kimi-k2-0905] <table id="audit"></table>
2025-11-05 09:49:45 [kimi-k2-0905] <script>
2025-11-05 09:49:45 [kimi-k2-0905] function buildAuditTable(data, headers) {
2025-11-05 09:49:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 	const tbl = document.getElementById('audit');
2025-11-05 09:49:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 	const headRow = tbl.insertRow();
2025-11-05 09:49:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 	headers.forEach(h => headRow.insertCell().textContent = h);
2025-11-05 09:49:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-05 09:49:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 	data.forEach(row => {
2025-11-05 09:49:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 		const tr = tbl.insertRow();
2025-11-05 09:49:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 		row.forEach(cell => tr.insertCell().textContent = cell);
2025-11-05 09:49:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 	});
2025-11-05 09:49:45 [kimi-k2-0905] }
2025-11-05 09:49:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-05 09:49:45 [kimi-k2-0905] function auditWidths() {
2025-11-05 09:49:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 	const tbl = document.getElementById('audit');
2025-11-05 09:49:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 	const rows = Array.from(tbl.rows);
2025-11-05 09:49:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 	const [head, ...body] = rows;
2025-11-05 09:49:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 	const widths = [];
2025-11-05 09:49:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-05 09:49:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 	// collect header widths
2025-11-05 09:49:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 	for (let c = 0; c < head.cells.length; c++) {
2025-11-05 09:49:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 		widths[c] = head.cells[c].getBoundingClientRect().width;
2025-11-05 09:49:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 	}
2025-11-05 09:49:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-05 09:49:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 	// check body against header
2025-11-05 09:49:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 	body.forEach(tr => {
