2025-11-05 10:16:08 [kimi-k2-0905] Yes — the blanks (the lines that show only “-|   |” or “-|  |”) are the variation-selector
2025-11-05 10:16:08 [kimi-k2-0905] sequences that Discord strips when you copy-paste.
2025-11-05 10:16:08 [kimi-k2-0905] Every emoji that is followed by U+FE0F in the source ends up as two code-points, but Discord
2025-11-05 10:16:08 [kimi-k2-0905] normalises them away, so the pasted text no longer contains the selector and the fixed-width table
2025-11-05 10:16:08 [kimi-k2-0905] cell looks empty.
2025-11-05 10:16:08 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-05 10:16:08 [kimi-k2-0905] In other words the lines that appear blank are the ones whose original byte sequence was
2025-11-05 10:16:08 [kimi-k2-0905] `<emoji> U+FE0F`
2025-11-05 10:16:08 [kimi-k2-0905] and after Discord’s normalisation only
2025-11-05 10:16:08 [kimi-k2-0905] `<emoji>`
2025-11-05 10:16:08 [kimi-k2-0905] remains. Because the width-counting logic still allocates two columns for the original pair, the
2025-11-05 10:16:08 [kimi-k2-0905] cell is padded as if the selector were still there, so you see the pipe borders with nothing
2025-11-05 10:16:08 [kimi-k2-0905] between them.
2025-11-05 10:16:08 [kimi-k2-0905] 
