2025-11-07 07:23:20 [nitro@ryzen5] can amazon linux on ec2 send email out of the box?
2025-11-07 07:23:28 [kimi-k2-0905] No.
2025-11-07 07:23:28 [kimi-k2-0905] Plain Amazon Linux AMI has no MTA installed, outbound SMTP (port 25) is throttled or blocked by
2025-11-07 07:23:28 [kimi-k2-0905] default, and Reverse DNS is missing.
2025-11-07 07:23:28 [kimi-k2-0905] Install postfix or use SES/API (port 587/465) and request the SMTP throttle removal to actually
2025-11-07 07:23:28 [kimi-k2-0905] deliver mail.
2025-11-07 07:23:28 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:23:59 [scudmarks@discord] can you lead me through the postfix option
2025-11-07 07:24:30 [kimi-k2-0905] Below is the shortest reliable path: stay on port 587, use your own SMTP relay (Gmail, Mailgun,
2025-11-07 07:24:30 [kimi-k2-0905] SES, etc.) so Amazon’s port-25 throttle and missing PTR record don’t bite you.
2025-11-07 07:24:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:24:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. Prep the instance
2025-11-07 07:24:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:24:30 [kimi-k2-0905]    sudo yum update -y
2025-11-07 07:24:30 [kimi-k2-0905]    sudo yum install -y postfix cyrus-sasl-plain
2025-11-07 07:24:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:24:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:24:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. Open the port (if you restricted egress)
2025-11-07 07:24:30 [kimi-k2-0905]    Security-group outbound: add 0.0.0.0/0 TCP 587 (or 465 if you prefer SMTPS).
2025-11-07 07:24:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:24:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 3. Create the relay password file
2025-11-07 07:24:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:24:30 [kimi-k2-0905]    sudo bash -c 'cat > /etc/postfix/sasl_passwd' <<'EOF'
2025-11-07 07:24:30 [kimi-k2-0905]    [smtp.gmail.com]:587    you@gmail.com:APP_PASSWORD
2025-11-07 07:24:30 [kimi-k2-0905]    EOF
2025-11-07 07:24:30 [kimi-k2-0905]    sudo postmap /etc/postfix/sasl_passwd
2025-11-07 07:24:30 [kimi-k2-0905]    sudo chmod 600 /etc/postfix/sasl_passwd*
2025-11-07 07:24:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:24:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:24:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 4. Point Postfix to the relay
2025-11-07 07:24:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:24:30 [kimi-k2-0905]    sudo postconf -e \
2025-11-07 07:24:30 [kimi-k2-0905]      "relayhost = [smtp.gmail.com]:587" \
2025-11-07 07:24:30 [kimi-k2-0905]      "smtp_sasl_auth_enable = yes" \
2025-11-07 07:24:30 [kimi-k2-0905]      "smtp_sasl_password_maps = hash:/etc/postfix/sasl_passwd" \
2025-11-07 07:24:30 [kimi-k2-0905]      "smtp_sasl_security_options = noanonymous" \
2025-11-07 07:24:30 [kimi-k2-0905]      "smtp_tls_security_level = encrypt"
2025-11-07 07:24:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:24:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:24:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 5. Start and enable
2025-11-07 07:24:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:24:30 [kimi-k2-0905]    sudo systemctl enable --now postfix
2025-11-07 07:24:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:24:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:24:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 6. Test
2025-11-07 07:24:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:24:30 [kimi-k2-0905]    echo 'relay test' | mail -s 'From EC2' someaddr@example.com
2025-11-07 07:24:30 [kimi-k2-0905]    sudo tail -f /var/log/maillog
2025-11-07 07:24:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:24:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:24:30 [kimi-k2-0905] Success = status=sent in the log.
2025-11-07 07:24:30 [kimi-k2-0905] If you need SPF/DKIM, add those records in Route 53 and (optionally) install OpenDKIM on the same
2025-11-07 07:24:30 [kimi-k2-0905] box.
2025-11-07 07:24:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:26:04 [scudmarks@discord] oauth for deno apps?
2025-11-07 07:26:33 [kimi-k2-0905] Use the built-in `oauth` module (Deno ≥1.35).
2025-11-07 07:26:33 [kimi-k2-0905] Below is a minimal Google flow you can drop into `main.ts`; swap provider details for GitHub,
2025-11-07 07:26:33 [kimi-k2-0905] Discord, etc.
2025-11-07 07:26:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:26:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:26:33 [kimi-k2-0905] // main.ts
2025-11-07 07:26:33 [kimi-k2-0905] import { Application, Router } from "https://deno.land/x/oak@v12.6.1/mod.ts";
2025-11-07 07:26:33 [kimi-k2-0905] import { GoogleClient } from "https://deno.land/x/oauth@v0.3.0/mod.ts";
2025-11-07 07:26:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:26:33 [kimi-k2-0905] const google = new GoogleClient({
2025-11-07 07:26:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 	redirectUri: "http://localhost:8000/oauth/callback",
2025-11-07 07:26:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 	clientId: Deno.env.get("GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID")!,
2025-11-07 07:26:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 	clientSecret: Deno.env.get("GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET")!,
2025-11-07 07:26:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 	scope: ["openid", "email", "profile"],
2025-11-07 07:26:33 [kimi-k2-0905] });
2025-11-07 07:26:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:26:33 [kimi-k2-0905] const router = new Router()
2025-11-07 07:26:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 	.get("/login", (ctx) => {
2025-11-07 07:26:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 		ctx.response.redirect(google.code.getAuthorizationUri());
2025-11-07 07:26:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 	})
2025-11-07 07:26:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 	.get("/oauth/callback", async (ctx) => {
2025-11-07 07:26:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 		const code = ctx.request.url.searchParams.get("code")!;
2025-11-07 07:26:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 		const tokens = await google.code.getToken(code);
2025-11-07 07:26:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 		const user = await fetch("https://www.googleapis.com/oauth2/v3/userinfo", {
2025-11-07 07:26:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 			headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${tokens.accessToken}` },
2025-11-07 07:26:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 		}).then((r) => r.json());
2025-11-07 07:26:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:26:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 		ctx.response.body = `Hello ${user.email}`;
2025-11-07 07:26:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 	});
2025-11-07 07:26:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:26:33 [kimi-k2-0905] const app = new Application();
2025-11-07 07:26:33 [kimi-k2-0905] app.use(router.routes());
2025-11-07 07:26:33 [kimi-k2-0905] app.use(router.allowedMethods());
2025-11-07 07:26:33 [kimi-k2-0905] await app.listen({ port: 8000 });
2025-11-07 07:26:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:26:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:26:33 [kimi-k2-0905] Run:
2025-11-07 07:26:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:26:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:26:33 [kimi-k2-0905] GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=xxx GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=yyy deno run -A main.ts
2025-11-07 07:26:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:26:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:26:33 [kimi-k2-0905] That’s it—no extra servers, no third-party wrapper.
2025-11-07 07:26:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:28:23 [scudmarks@discord] wdym swap provider?
2025-11-07 07:28:48 [kimi-k2-0905] The helper class (`GoogleClient` in the snippet) only knows Google’s authorization and token URLs.
2025-11-07 07:28:48 [kimi-k2-0905] If you want GitHub, Discord, Twitch, etc. you have two choices:
2025-11-07 07:28:48 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:28:48 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. Use the generic `OAuth2Client` that ships in the same module and give it the provider’s
2025-11-07 07:28:48 [kimi-k2-0905] endpoints yourself:
2025-11-07 07:28:48 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:28:48 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:28:48 [kimi-k2-0905] import { OAuth2Client } from "https://deno.land/x/oauth@v0.3.0/mod.ts";
2025-11-07 07:28:48 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:28:48 [kimi-k2-0905] const gh = new OAuth2Client({
2025-11-07 07:28:48 [kimi-k2-0905] 	clientId: Deno.env.get("GH_ID")!,
2025-11-07 07:28:48 [kimi-k2-0905] 	clientSecret: Deno.env.get("GH_SECRET")!,
2025-11-07 07:28:48 [kimi-k2-0905] 	authorizationEndpointUri: "https://github.com/login/oauth/authorize",
2025-11-07 07:28:48 [kimi-k2-0905] 	tokenUri: "https://github.com/login/oauth/access_token",
2025-11-07 07:28:48 [kimi-k2-0905] 	redirectUri: "http://localhost:8000/oauth/callback",
2025-11-07 07:28:48 [kimi-k2-0905] 	scope: ["user:email"],
2025-11-07 07:28:48 [kimi-k2-0905] });
2025-11-07 07:28:48 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:28:48 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:28:48 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. Import (or write) a tiny helper for that provider; the community already publishes ones for most
2025-11-07 07:28:48 [kimi-k2-0905] big names, e.g.
2025-11-07 07:28:48 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:28:48 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:28:48 [kimi-k2-0905] import { GitHubClient } from "https://deno.land/x/oauth_providers/github.ts";
2025-11-07 07:28:48 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:28:48 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:28:48 [kimi-k2-0905] Either way the rest of the Oak route stays identical—only the client you pass to
2025-11-07 07:28:48 [kimi-k2-0905] `code.getAuthorizationUri()` / `code.getToken()` changes.
2025-11-07 07:28:48 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:29:59 [scudmarks@discord] how about duo?
2025-11-07 07:30:11 [kimi-k2-0905] Duo isn’t an OAuth provider; it’s a second-factor service.
2025-11-07 07:30:11 [kimi-k2-0905] Typical pattern:
2025-11-07 07:30:11 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:30:11 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. Do the normal OAuth/OpenID flow (Google, GitHub, …) first.
2025-11-07 07:30:11 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. Once that succeeds, start Duo’s `/auth/v2/auth` call (their WebSDK or REST) to verify the second
2025-11-07 07:30:11 [kimi-k2-0905] factor.
2025-11-07 07:30:11 [kimi-k2-0905] 3. Only create your app session after Duo returns `"result": "allow"`.
2025-11-07 07:30:11 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:30:11 [kimi-k2-0905] So you don’t “swap Duo in” as you would GitHub—you add Duo after the OAuth step.
2025-11-07 07:30:11 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:30:55 [scudmarks@discord] duo does not do auth???
2025-11-07 07:31:09 [kimi-k2-0905] Duo does authentication, but only second-factor (TOTP, push, WebAuthn, phone call,
2025-11-07 07:31:09 [kimi-k2-0905] etc.).
2025-11-07 07:31:09 [kimi-k2-0905] It never issues a user identity token by itself; it only verifies that the person who just
2025-11-07 07:31:09 [kimi-k2-0905] proved identity via your first factor (password, OAuth token, SAML assertion, …) is really that
2025-11-07 07:31:09 [kimi-k2-0905] user.
2025-11-07 07:31:09 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:31:09 [kimi-k2-0905] So the sequence is always:
2025-11-07 07:31:09 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:31:09 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. Establish identity (OAuth, SAML, local password, whatever).
2025-11-07 07:31:09 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. Ask Duo: “is this user allowed in right now?”
2025-11-07 07:31:09 [kimi-k2-0905] 3. Duo returns allow/deny.
2025-11-07 07:31:09 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:31:09 [kimi-k2-0905] You can’t replace Google/GitHub with Duo; you add Duo after them.
2025-11-07 07:31:09 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-07 07:31:36 [scudmarks@discord] aha, thanks!
2025-11-07 07:31:41 [kimi-k2-0905] you’re welcome
2025-11-07 07:31:41 [kimi-k2-0905] 
