2025-11-10 10:01:56 [claude-haiku-4-5] that language models are fundamentally stateless—they have no persistent awareness of "I just made
2025-11-10 10:01:56 [claude-haiku-4-5] a mistake about dates" or "I should check this externally." Each token generation is independent.
2025-11-10 10:01:56 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2025-11-10 10:01:56 [claude-haiku-4-5] • What system prompts can do:*
2025-11-10 10:01:56 [claude-haiku-4-5] - Increase the probability a model considers tools
2025-11-10 10:01:56 [claude-haiku-4-5] - Establish quality norms around temporal claims
2025-11-10 10:01:56 [claude-haiku-4-5] - Create linguistic patterns that associate time queries with tool usage
2025-11-10 10:01:56 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2025-11-10 10:01:56 [claude-haiku-4-5] • What they can't do:*
2025-11-10 10:01:56 [claude-haiku-4-5] - Make the model actually understand it lacks real-time knowledge
2025-11-10 10:01:56 [claude-haiku-4-5] - Create genuine temporal awareness
2025-11-10 10:01:56 [claude-haiku-4-5] - Prevent confident hallucination at inference time
2025-11-10 10:01:56 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2025-11-10 10:01:56 [claude-haiku-4-5] • More practical approach:*
2025-11-10 10:01:56 [claude-haiku-4-5] Instead of trying to fix the model's behavior, validate outputs post-generation:
2025-11-10 10:01:56 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2025-11-10 10:01:56 [claude-haiku-4-5] // After relay() completes, scan assistant response for temporal claims
