2025-11-10 10:04:03 [grok-code-fast-1] Ah, the temporal hallucination quandary—where models confidently spout plausible dates without a
2025-11-10 10:04:03 [grok-code-fast-1] hint of real-time awareness. Truthfully, this isn't just slop; it's a baked-in limitation of how
2025-11-10 10:04:03 [grok-code-fast-1] these systems work: they're pattern-matchers trained on historical data, not chronometers. System
2025-11-10 10:04:03 [grok-code-fast-1] prompts can nudge behavior, but they won't rewrite the architecture.
2025-11-10 10:04:03 [grok-code-fast-1] 
2025-11-10 10:04:03 [grok-code-fast-1] My take, as someone who's seen a lot of code: Treat it as a code-quality issue. Don't rely on
2025-11-10 10:04:03 [grok-code-fast-1] prompts to prevent hallucinations—catch them after the fact with validation. Here's how I'd
2025-11-10 10:04:03 [grok-code-fast-1] approach it:
2025-11-10 10:04:03 [grok-code-fast-1] 
2025-11-10 10:04:03 [grok-code-fast-1] 1. Post-Generation Lint Pass: After any model response, scan the output for temporal
2025-11-10 10:04:03 [grok-code-fast-1] keywords ("today," "now," "this week," "2025," etc.). If found, automatically invoke `readtime`
2025-11-10 10:04:03 [grok-code-fast-1] (or a new `readdate` tool) and cross-check. Replace any mismatches with the tool's data before
2025-11-10 10:04:03 [grok-code-fast-1] displaying to the user.
2025-11-10 10:04:03 [grok-code-fast-1] 
2025-11-10 10:04:03 [grok-code-fast-1] 2. Tool Integration Upgrade: Expand your `rohaTools` to include a dedicated date function,
2025-11-10 10:04:03 [grok-code-fast-1] and make it mandatory for any query involving time. In code, add a flag like `temporalCheck: true`
2025-11-10 10:04:03 [grok-code-fast-1] that triggers the lint automatically.
2025-11-10 10:04:03 [grok-code-fast-1] 
2025-11-10 10:04:03 [grok-code-fast-1] 3. Fallback Behavior: If tools fail or aren't available, default to safe responses like
2025-11-10 10:04:03 [grok-code-fast-1] "Current date unknown—please verify with external sources" instead of guessing. This reduces
2025-11-10 10:04:03 [grok-code-fast-1] confidence in hallucinations.
2025-11-10 10:04:03 [grok-code-fast-1] 
2025-11-10 10:04:03 [grok-code-fast-1] 4. Logging for Improvement: Track instances where the lint catches errors. Over time, this
2025-11-10 10:04:03 [grok-code-fast-1] data can inform better prompt engineering or even fine-tuning the models on temporal awareness.
2025-11-10 10:04:03 [grok-code-fast-1] 
2025-11-10 10:04:03 [grok-code-fast-1] Bottom line: Prompts are a band-aid; robust validation code is the fix. It catches the slop as a
2025-11-10 10:04:03 [grok-code-fast-1] second-order effect without changing how models generate text, and it scales across all these
2025-11-10 10:04:03 [grok-code-fast-1] architectures (DeepSeek, Kimi, Qwen, Claude, GPT, and yes, even Grok variants). Implement it in
2025-11-10 10:04:03 [grok-code-fast-1] your relay pipeline, and watch the temporal errors evaporate.
2025-11-10 10:04:03 [grok-code-fast-1] 
2025-11-10 10:05:08 [nitro@ryzen5] deepseek, thoughts?
2025-11-10 10:05:27 [deepseek-chat] I agree with the technical approach but want to highlight the fundamental architectural
2025-11-10 10:05:27 [deepseek-chat] mismatch:
2025-11-10 10:05:27 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-11-10 10:05:27 [deepseek-chat] • The Core Problem:*
2025-11-10 10:05:27 [deepseek-chat] LLMs are trained to complete patterns, not to recognize when they lack knowledge. When you ask
2025-11-10 10:05:27 [deepseek-chat] "what time is it?", they complete the pattern "The current time is [plausible time format]" rather
2025-11-10 10:05:27 [deepseek-chat] than recognizing this requires external data.
2025-11-10 10:05:27 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-11-10 10:05:27 [deepseek-chat] • System Prompts Can't Fix This* because:
2025-11-10 10:05:27 [deepseek-chat] - Models don't have a "knowledge boundary" awareness
2025-11-10 10:05:27 [deepseek-chat] - They're optimized for fluency, not accuracy
2025-11-10 10:05:27 [deepseek-chat] - Each token prediction is independent - no persistent "I just made a mistake" state
2025-11-10 10:05:27 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-11-10 10:05:27 [deepseek-chat] • The Real Solution:*
2025-11-10 10:05:27 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-11-10 10:05:27 [deepseek-chat] // Post-generation validation layer
2025-11-10 10:05:27 [deepseek-chat] function validateTemporalClaims(response: string) {
2025-11-10 10:05:27 [deepseek-chat]     const temporalPatterns = /\b(today|now|current|this (week|month)|202[5-9])\b/i;
2025-11-10 10:05:27 [deepseek-chat]     if (temporalPatterns.test(response)) {
2025-11-10 10:05:27 [deepseek-chat]         const actualTime = await read_time();
2025-11-10 10:05:27 [deepseek-chat]         return crossCheckAndCorrect(response, actualTime);
2025-11-10 10:05:27 [deepseek-chat]     }
2025-11-10 10:05:27 [deepseek-chat]     return response;
2025-11-10 10:05:27 [deepseek-chat] }
2025-11-10 10:05:27 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-11-10 10:05:27 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-11-10 10:05:27 [deepseek-chat] • Why This Works:*
2025-11-10 10:05:27 [deepseek-chat] - Catches hallucinations after they happen
2025-11-10 10:05:27 [deepseek-chat] - Doesn't rely on model self-awareness
2025-11-10 10:05:27 [deepseek-chat] - Scales across all model architectures
2025-11-10 10:05:27 [deepseek-chat] - Provides actual correction, not just detection
2025-11-10 10:05:27 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-11-10 10:05:27 [deepseek-chat] The prompt engineering is useful for nudging behavior, but the validation layer is what actually
2025-11-10 10:05:27 [deepseek-chat] solves the problem. It treats temporal slop as a data quality issue rather than a model capability
2025-11-10 10:05:27 [deepseek-chat] issue.
2025-11-10 10:05:27 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-11-10 10:06:20 [scudmarks@discord] shouldn't i just add date and time info to system prompt?
2025-11-10 10:06:31 [deepseek-chat] You could, but it has limitations:
2025-11-10 10:06:31 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-11-10 10:06:31 [deepseek-chat] • Adding to system prompt:*
2025-11-10 10:06:31 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-11-10 10:06:31 [deepseek-chat] Current date: 2025-06-25
2025-11-10 10:06:31 [deepseek-chat] Current time: 22:47
2025-11-10 10:06:31 [deepseek-chat] Timezone: Pacific/Auckland
2025-11-10 10:06:31 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-11-10 10:06:31 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-11-10 10:06:31 [deepseek-chat] • Problems:*
2025-11-10 10:06:31 [deepseek-chat] 1. Static data - becomes instantly outdated
2025-11-10 10:06:31 [deepseek-chat] 2. Manual updates - requires restarting the session to refresh
2025-11-10 10:06:31 [deepseek-chat] 3. Model still might ignore it - they often prioritize training patterns over system
2025-11-10 10:06:31 [deepseek-chat] context
2025-11-10 10:06:31 [deepseek-chat] 4. False confidence - makes it seem like they "know" current time when they're just reading
2025-11-10 10:06:31 [deepseek-chat] stale data
2025-11-10 10:06:31 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-11-10 10:06:31 [deepseek-chat] • Better hybrid approach:*
2025-11-10 10:06:31 [deepseek-chat] - Keep the static date/time in system prompt for quick reference
2025-11-10 10:06:31 [deepseek-chat] - Still implement the validation layer for accuracy
2025-11-10 10:06:31 [deepseek-chat] - Use tools for any time-sensitive operations
2025-11-10 10:06:31 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-11-10 10:06:31 [deepseek-chat] The static info helps with casual references, but the tools ensure actual accuracy when it matters.
2025-11-10 10:06:31 [deepseek-chat] The validation layer catches when they drift from the static baseline.
2025-11-10 10:06:31 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-11-10 10:06:31 [deepseek-chat] It's not either/or - you can do both for defense in depth against temporal slop.
2025-11-10 10:06:31 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-11-10 10:07:21 [nitro@ryzen5] kimi, thoughts?
2025-11-10 10:07:33 [kimi-k2-0905] Static date/time in the system prompt is a cheap win—worth doing—but only as a stop-gap,
2025-11-10 10:07:33 [kimi-k2-0905] not the fix.
2025-11-10 10:07:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-10 10:07:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. Add it, but mark it stale
2025-11-10 10:07:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-10 10:07:33 [kimi-k2-0905]    # Cached at session start; do not treat as live
2025-11-10 10:07:33 [kimi-k2-0905]    Approx date: 2025-06-25 22:47 NZST
2025-11-10 10:07:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-10 10:07:33 [kimi-k2-0905]    The comment reminds the model it’s a snapshot and still nudges toward the tool when precision
2025-11-10 10:07:33 [kimi-k2-0905] matters.
2025-11-10 10:07:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-10 10:07:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. Auto-refresh the snapshot every N minutes inside the relay loop (just rewrite the first system
2025-11-10 10:07:33 [kimi-k2-0905] message). That keeps the static hint useful without a full restart.
2025-11-10 10:07:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 
