2025-11-15 12:52:29 [deepseek-chat] }
2025-11-15 12:52:29 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-11-15 12:52:29 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-11-15 12:52:29 [deepseek-chat] • The Problem:*
2025-11-15 12:52:29 [deepseek-chat] - When an assistant message contains `tool_calls`, the function first changes the role to "user" if
2025-11-15 12:52:29 [deepseek-chat] `name != model`
2025-11-15 12:52:29 [deepseek-chat] - Then in the switch statement, it tries to push with the original `item.role` which is now
2025-11-15 12:52:29 [deepseek-chat] inconsistent
2025-11-15 12:52:29 [deepseek-chat] - This corrupts the tool call context, causing models to re-call functions
2025-11-15 12:52:29 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-11-15 12:52:29 [deepseek-chat] • Quick Fix:*
2025-11-15 12:52:29 [deepseek-chat] Move the role modification inside the switch statement:
2025-11-15 12:52:29 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-11-15 12:52:29 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-11-15 12:52:29 [deepseek-chat] case "assistant":
2025-11-15 12:52:29 [deepseek-chat]     if (name != model) {
2025-11-15 12:52:29 [deepseek-chat]         // Convert to user role for non-current model responses
2025-11-15 12:52:29 [deepseek-chat]         list.push({role: "user", content});
2025-11-15 12:52:29 [deepseek-chat]     } else {
2025-11-15 12:52:29 [deepseek-chat]         // Keep as assistant for current model, preserve tool calls
2025-11-15 12:52:29 [deepseek-chat]         if (item.tool_calls) {
2025-11-15 12:52:29 [deepseek-chat]             list.push({role: "assistant", content, tool_calls: item.tool_calls});
2025-11-15 12:52:29 [deepseek-chat]         } else {
2025-11-15 12:52:29 [deepseek-chat]             list.push({role: "assistant", content});
2025-11-15 12:52:29 [deepseek-chat]         }
2025-11-15 12:52:29 [deepseek-chat]     }
2025-11-15 12:52:29 [deepseek-chat]     break;
2025-11-15 12:52:29 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-11-15 12:52:29 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-11-15 12:52:29 [deepseek-chat] This explains why both DeepSeek and ChatGPT keep re-calling `read_time` - the tool call history is
2025-11-15 12:52:29 [deepseek-chat] getting corrupted.
2025-11-15 12:52:29 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-11-15 12:57:02 [nitro@ryzen5] strictHistory is expecting role tool entries to exist
2025-11-15 12:57:26 [deepseek-chat] You're absolutely right! Looking at the `strictHistory()` function, it expects `role: "tool"`
2025-11-15 12:57:26 [deepseek-chat] entries to exist in the history, but they might not be properly preserved.
2025-11-15 12:57:26 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-11-15 12:57:26 [deepseek-chat] The issue is in the `strictHistory()` function around line 12180:
2025-11-15 12:57:26 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-11-15 12:57:26 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-11-15 12:57:26 [deepseek-chat] function strictHistory(history) {
2025-11-15 12:57:26 [deepseek-chat]     const list = [];
