2025-11-15 20:47:46 [nitro@ryzen5] step 1 i think could be cloud node with limited storage updating recent list with each read request
2025-11-15 20:48:09 [kimi-k2-0905] Understood.
2025-11-15 20:48:09 [kimi-k2-0905] Step 1: AWS node keeps a tiny “recent-list” file (plain JSON, one-line per update).
2025-11-15 20:48:09 [kimi-k2-0905] Every read request it:
2025-11-15 20:48:09 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-15 20:48:09 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. Receives the caller’s “last-seen” token (timestamp or etag).
2025-11-15 20:48:09 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. `ssh pi5 «cat /grid5/.recent»` – fetches the authoritative list.
2025-11-15 20:48:09 [kimi-k2-0905] 3. Differs against its cached copy; if newer, overwrites local file and replies with the new list +
2025-11-15 20:48:09 [kimi-k2-0905] fresh token.
2025-11-15 20:48:09 [kimi-k2-0905] 4. Otherwise replies “304 Not Modified”.
2025-11-15 20:48:09 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-15 20:48:09 [kimi-k2-0905] No persistent storage beyond that single file; the Pi5 is the single source of truth.
2025-11-15 20:48:09 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-15 20:48:09 [kimi-k2-0905] Next, would you like the minimal Node/Express handler for that, or the Pi5 side script that
2025-11-15 20:48:09 [kimi-k2-0905] atomically updates `.recent` whenever anything in `/grid5` changes?
2025-11-15 20:48:09 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-15 20:48:52 [nitro@ryzen5] can nginx touch the file when it reads it or does that need write privs
2025-11-15 20:49:18 [kimi-k2-0905] nginx only touches (i.e. updates the atime) if the mount is not mounted
2025-11-15 20:49:18 [kimi-k2-0905] `noatime`/`relatime`; it never changes mtime/ctime, so the file itself is left unchanged.
2025-11-15 20:49:18 [kimi-k2-0905] If you want an explicit “this-url-was-hit” mark you have to give nginx write access (e.g.
2025-11-15 20:49:18 [kimi-k2-0905] `www-data` owns the file or the directory is `g+w`) and use the `ngxhttprewrite_module`
2025-11-15 20:49:18 [kimi-k2-0905] trick:
2025-11-15 20:49:18 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-15 20:49:18 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-15 20:49:18 [kimi-k2-0905] location = /grid5/recent {
2025-11-15 20:49:18 [kimi-k2-0905]     default_type application/json;
2025-11-15 20:49:18 [kimi-k2-0905]     root /var/www/skid;
2025-11-15 20:49:18 [kimi-k2-0905]     post_action @touch;          # runs after successful response
2025-11-15 20:49:18 [kimi-k2-0905] }
2025-11-15 20:49:18 [kimi-k2-0905] location @touch {
2025-11-15 20:49:18 [kimi-k2-0905]     internal;
2025-11-15 20:49:18 [kimi-k2-0905]     rewrite ^ /grid5/.recent_last_hit break;
2025-11-15 20:49:18 [kimi-k2-0905]     return 204;                  # 0-byte PUT
2025-11-15 20:49:18 [kimi-k2-0905] }
2025-11-15 20:49:18 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-15 20:49:18 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-15 20:49:18 [kimi-k2-0905] `postaction` fires only on 200/206/304, so every real read triggers an empty PUT to
2025-11-15 20:49:18 [kimi-k2-0905] `.recentlast_hit`, updating its mtime.
2025-11-15 20:49:18 [kimi-k2-0905] The worker needs write on that path; if you can’t grant it, run a tiny proxy service (or `incron`
2025-11-15 20:49:18 [kimi-k2-0905] on Pi5) instead.
2025-11-15 20:49:18 [kimi-k2-0905] 
