2025-11-17 10:06:33 [nitro@ryzen5] in linux can you list all histories or just local session
2025-11-17 10:06:55 [kimi-k2-0905] `history` only shows the current shell session.
2025-11-17 10:06:55 [kimi-k2-0905] To see every command that ever hit the disk you need something that was already logging; bash
2025-11-17 10:06:55 [kimi-k2-0905] itself doesn’t keep a global list.
2025-11-17 10:06:55 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 10:06:55 [kimi-k2-0905] Check what you actually have:
2025-11-17 10:06:55 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 10:06:55 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 10:06:55 [kimi-k2-0905] # 1. current session
2025-11-17 10:06:55 [kimi-k2-0905] history
2025-11-17 10:06:55 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 10:06:55 [kimi-k2-0905] # 2. older bash sessions (if HISTTIMEFORMAT was set earlier)
2025-11-17 10:06:55 [kimi-k2-0905] cat ~/.bash_history
2025-11-17 10:06:55 [kimi-k2-0905] 
