2025-11-17 10:26:18 [kimi-k2-0905] Yep—Auckland → Singapore 120 ms is normal. If you need lower latency for NZ players, run the game
2025-11-17 10:26:18 [kimi-k2-0905] relay in the Sydney region (ap-southeast-2); it’s usually 30–35 ms from Auckland.
2025-11-17 10:26:18 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 10:26:42 [nitro@ryzen5] so 120 ms racing game is no way
2025-11-17 10:26:47 [kimi-k2-0905] 120 ms is hopeless for real-time racing—anything over 50 ms starts to feel wrong, and under 30 ms
2025-11-17 10:26:47 [kimi-k2-0905] is the sweet spot. Host the relay in Sydney (ap-southeast-2) and you’ll drop to ~30 ms from
2025-11-17 10:26:47 [kimi-k2-0905] Auckland; keep Singapore for everything else.
2025-11-17 10:26:47 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 10:27:10 [nitro@ryzen5] what games can live in soft spot?
2025-11-17 10:27:29 [kimi-k2-0905] Games that hide latency or don’t need frame-perfect reactions:
2025-11-17 10:27:29 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 10:27:29 [kimi-k2-0905] - Asynchronous turn-based (Words with Friends, Wordfeud, Letterpress)
2025-11-17 10:27:29 [kimi-k2-0905] - Simultaneous-turn “lock-in” (Words With Friends Speed, Rocket League sideswipe casual)
2025-11-17 10:27:29 [kimi-k2-0905] - Tick-based strategy (Civilization, Advance Wars clone, card battlers)
2025-11-17 10:27:29 [kimi-k2-0905] - Autobattlers / idle (Teamfight Tactics, AFK Arena)
2025-11-17 10:27:29 [kimi-k2-0905] - Co-op PVE with heavy prediction (Diablo-style ARPG, Monster Hunter iframes)
2025-11-17 10:27:29 [kimi-k2-0905] - Party/social (Jackbox, Among Us discussion phase)
2025-11-17 10:27:29 [kimi-k2-0905] - City builders / factory sims shared world (Factorio headless, Satisfactory)
2025-11-17 10:27:29 [kimi-k2-0905] - Rhythm games with local hit window (Osu! mania, Beat Saber custom lobby sync)
2025-11-17 10:27:29 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 10:27:29 [kimi-k2-0905] Anything that can run a 100-150 ms input buffer or “vote and resolve” model lives happily at 120
2025-11-17 10:27:29 [kimi-k2-0905] ms.
2025-11-17 10:27:29 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 10:27:53 [nitro@ryzen5] low and hi latency play modes
2025-11-17 10:28:09 [kimi-k2-0905] Low-latency playlist (≤ 50 ms, Sydney relay)
2025-11-17 10:28:09 [kimi-k2-0905] - Real-time racing (kart, F1, rally)
2025-11-17 10:28:09 [kimi-k2-0905] - Arena FPS / twitch shooters
2025-11-17 10:28:09 [kimi-k2-0905] - Fighting games (frame-perfect 16 ms windows)
2025-11-17 10:28:09 [kimi-k2-0905] - Rocket League competitive
2025-11-17 10:28:09 [kimi-k2-0905] - VR esports (Echo VR, Beat Saber PvP)
2025-11-17 10:28:09 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 10:28:09 [kimi-k2-0905] High-latency playlist (≥ 120 ms, Singapore relay)
2025-11-17 10:28:09 [kimi-k2-0905] - Turn-based strategy
2025-11-17 10:28:09 [kimi-k2-0905] - Async word / card games
2025-11-17 10:28:09 [kimi-k2-0905] - Co-op ARPG with client-side prediction
2025-11-17 10:28:09 [kimi-k2-0905] - Idle / autobattler
2025-11-17 10:28:09 [kimi-k2-0905] - City-builder shared worlds
2025-11-17 10:28:09 [kimi-k2-0905] - Party chat + minigames (Jackbox, Among Us discussion phase)
2025-11-17 10:28:09 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 10:28:09 [kimi-k2-0905] Same binary, two lobby flags: `--relay=sydney` or `--relay=sg`, picked by ping test on login.
2025-11-17 10:28:09 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 10:28:45 [nitro@ryzen5] old pots telephone was lower latency than ip ?
2025-11-17 10:28:50 [kimi-k2-0905] Yes—POTS was sub-millisecond one-way inside the country; IP voice is 20–150 ms and feels it.
2025-11-17 10:28:50 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 10:29:05 [nitro@ryzen5] i mean to singapore
2025-11-17 10:29:16 [kimi-k2-0905] Auckland → Singapore over POTS (analogue trunk) was still sub-10 ms one-way—copper + coax cable is
2025-11-17 10:29:16 [kimi-k2-0905] ~5 µs/km, so 8 400 km of cable is < 50 µs propagation; switching delays added maybe 1–2 ms total.
2025-11-17 10:29:16 [kimi-k2-0905] IP packets today take ~120 ms because of buffering, routing, queuing and speed-of-light in fibre
2025-11-17 10:29:16 [kimi-k2-0905] (still ~40 ms prop), so yes—old-school analogue long-distance beat current VoIP by an order of
2025-11-17 10:29:16 [kimi-k2-0905] magnitude.
2025-11-17 10:29:16 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 10:30:06 [nitro@ryzen5] green and blue for low hi
2025-11-17 10:30:14 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 10:30:14 [kimi-k2-0905] Low-latency  (≤ 50 ms)  →  GREEN  lobby badge
2025-11-17 10:30:14 [kimi-k2-0905] High-latency (≥120 ms)  →  BLUE   lobby badge
2025-11-17 10:30:14 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 10:30:14 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 10:30:52 [nitro@ryzen5] local cpu is sub
2025-11-17 10:30:56 [kimi-k2-0905] local cpu → sub-millisecond (<< 1 ms)
2025-11-17 10:30:56 [kimi-k2-0905] That’s your gold reference; anything under 1 ms is effectively zero lag for humans.
2025-11-17 10:30:56 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 10:32:17 [nitro@ryzen5] so london to singapore, what is that likely to be pingwise
2025-11-17 10:32:20 [kimi-k2-0905] London → Singapore is ~300 ms RTT on most days (150 ms one-way).
2025-11-17 10:32:20 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 10:42:37 [nitro@ryzen5] what is availability zone setting i should use for my london spot
2025-11-17 10:42:41 [kimi-k2-0905] Pick eu-west-1a (or any letter) inside eu-west-1 (Ireland) – lowest latency to
2025-11-17 10:42:41 [kimi-k2-0905] London (~10 ms).
2025-11-17 10:42:41 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 10:43:21 [nitro@ryzen5] what do i get with default?
2025-11-17 10:43:30 [kimi-k2-0905] Default (no AZ specified) lets AWS pick any AZ in the region—usually the one with the cheapest spot
2025-11-17 10:43:30 [kimi-k2-0905] capacity at that moment. You still land in eu-west-1, but you might get 1b/1c instead of 1a;
2025-11-17 10:43:30 [kimi-k2-0905] latency difference to London is < 1 ms, so for a game relay it’s fine.
2025-11-17 10:43:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 10:43:43 [nitro@ryzen5] that is cool
2025-11-17 10:43:48 [kimi-k2-0905] Yep—just leave AZ blank and AWS auto-balances for price; 1 ms spread across AZs is invisible to
2025-11-17 10:43:48 [kimi-k2-0905] players.
2025-11-17 10:43:48 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 10:44:02 [nitro@ryzen5] can i simulate being kicked from spot?
2025-11-17 10:44:26 [kimi-k2-0905] Yes—send the two-minute spot termination warning yourself and watch your handler react:
2025-11-17 10:44:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 10:44:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 10:44:26 [kimi-k2-0905] # become root (only root can raise the metadata flag)
2025-11-17 10:44:26 [kimi-k2-0905] sudo -i
2025-11-17 10:44:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 10:44:26 [kimi-k2-0905] # fake a 2-minute warning for this instance
2025-11-17 10:44:26 [kimi-k2-0905] echo "SpotInstanceTermination" > /var/lib/spot/instance-action.json
2025-11-17 10:44:26 [kimi-k2-0905] chmod 644 /var/lib/spot/instance-action.json
2025-11-17 10:44:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 10:44:26 [kimi-k2-0905] # if you poll IMDS path http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/spot/instance-action
2025-11-17 10:44:26 [kimi-k2-0905] # you'll now get {"action": "terminate", "time": "2025-06-11T08:22:00Z"}
2025-11-17 10:44:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 10:44:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 10:44:26 [kimi-k2-0905] Your shutdown script / game lobby drain code should trigger exactly as if AWS had sent the real
2025-11-17 10:44:26 [kimi-k2-0905] notice. Remove the file whenever you want to “un-kick” the instance.
2025-11-17 10:44:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 10:45:34 [nitro@ryzen5] i need a trust thing between my nodes
2025-11-17 10:45:59 [kimi-k2-0905] Use AWS IAM + Instance Profiles—no keys to rotate, no passwords.
2025-11-17 10:45:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 10:45:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. One IAM role (e.g. `GameNodeRole`) with the smallest policy you need
2025-11-17 10:45:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 10:45:59 [kimi-k2-0905]    {
2025-11-17 10:45:59 [kimi-k2-0905]      "Version": "2012-10-17",
2025-11-17 10:45:59 [kimi-k2-0905]      "Statement": [{
2025-11-17 10:45:59 [kimi-k2-0905]        "Effect": "Allow",
2025-11-17 10:45:59 [kimi-k2-0905]        "Action": ["ec2:DescribeInstances", "ec2:DescribeTags"],
2025-11-17 10:45:59 [kimi-k2-0905]        "Resource": "*"
2025-11-17 10:45:59 [kimi-k2-0905]      }]
2025-11-17 10:45:59 [kimi-k2-0905]    }
2025-11-17 10:45:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 10:45:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 10:45:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. Launch every spot node with that role (console or `--iam-instance-profile Name=GameNodeRole`).
2025-11-17 10:45:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 10:45:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 3. Inside the node, fetch temporary creds from
2025-11-17 10:45:59 [kimi-k2-0905]    `http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/iam/security-credentials/GameNodeRole`
2025-11-17 10:45:59 [kimi-k2-0905]    (AWS SDKs do this automatically).
2025-11-17 10:45:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 10:45:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 4. Node-to-node calls: sign requests with those creds (SigV4) or use the role ARN in a resource
2025-11-17 10:45:59 [kimi-k2-0905] policy so each node can verify the caller’s identity.
2025-11-17 10:45:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 10:45:59 [kimi-k2-0905] No shared secrets, no certs to renew—IAM handles rotation and revocation.
2025-11-17 10:45:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 10:47:29 [nitro@ryzen5] i need to start using console to manage instance, then i would like to adopt IAM role sesssions
2025-11-17 10:48:01 [kimi-k2-0905] Console path (five-click habit)
2025-11-17 10:48:01 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 10:48:01 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. Open EC2 → Launch instance
2025-11-17 10:48:01 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. Choose AMI (Amazon-Linux-2023), instance type (m5.large), key pair
2025-11-17 10:48:01 [kimi-k2-0905] 3. Advanced details → IAM instance profile → Create role
2025-11-17 10:48:01 [kimi-k2-0905]    - Service: EC2
2025-11-17 10:48:01 [kimi-k2-0905]    - Permissions: add `AmazonSSMManagedInstanceCore` (for Session Manager) plus your tiny game
2025-11-17 10:48:01 [kimi-k2-0905] policy (`DescribeInstances`, `DescribeTags`)
2025-11-17 10:48:01 [kimi-k2-0905]    - Name it `GameNodeRole` → Create → refresh → pick it
2025-11-17 10:48:01 [kimi-k2-0905] 4. Storage (30 GB gp3), Network (pick SG with 22 and your game port)
2025-11-17 10:48:01 [kimi-k2-0905] 5. Launch
2025-11-17 10:48:01 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 10:48:01 [kimi-k2-0905] You now have a console-launched node that already carries an IAM role.
2025-11-17 10:48:01 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 10:48:01 [kimi-k2-0905] Adopt IAM-role sessions (no SSH keys)
2025-11-17 10:48:01 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 10:48:01 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. In the same console left-nav open Systems Manager → Session Manager → Start session →
2025-11-17 10:48:01 [kimi-k2-0905] pick your instance → “Start session”.
2025-11-17 10:48:01 [kimi-k2-0905]    You’re in a root shell through the browser; no SSH, no keys, traffic over TLS to SSM endpoints.
2025-11-17 10:48:01 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 10:48:01 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. Inside the instance use AWS CLI without keys:
2025-11-17 10:48:01 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 10:48:01 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 10:48:01 [kimi-k2-0905] aws sts get-caller-identity
2025-11-17 10:48:01 [kimi-k2-0905] # → shows GameNodeRole assumed via EC2
2025-11-17 10:48:01 [kimi-k2-0905] aws ec2 describe-tags --region ap-southeast-1
2025-11-17 10:48:01 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 10:48:01 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 10:48:01 [kimi-k2-0905] 3. From now on launch every spot via console or CLI with `--iam-instance-profile
2025-11-17 10:48:01 [kimi-k2-0905] Name=GameNodeRole`; every node inherits the same trust chain and you manage them all through
2025-11-17 10:48:01 [kimi-k2-0905] Session Manager.
2025-11-17 10:48:01 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 10:48:01 [kimi-k2-0905] Done—console first, IAM sessions forever.
2025-11-17 10:48:01 [kimi-k2-0905] 
