2025-11-17 13:20:32 [nitro@ryzen5] when configuring v8 what is v8_enable_i18n_support
2025-11-17 13:20:40 [kimi-k2-0905] `v8enablei18n_support` is a GN build-time flag that tells V8 whether to build-in the ICU
2025-11-17 13:20:40 [kimi-k2-0905] library (International Components for Unicode).
2025-11-17 13:20:40 [kimi-k2-0905] - `true` (default on desktop) – enables the full ECMA-402 Intl object (Intl.DateTimeFormat,
2025-11-17 13:20:40 [kimi-k2-0905] Intl.NumberFormat, etc.) and makes the resulting `d8` / `libv8` depend on ICU data.
2025-11-17 13:20:40 [kimi-k2-0905] - `false` – compiles V8 without ICU; the Intl object is not exposed, and the binary has no ICU
2025-11-17 13:20:40 [kimi-k2-0905] dependency (smaller, but breaks any JS that uses `Intl.*`).
2025-11-17 13:20:40 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:27:45 [nitro@ryzen5] once i have build v8 with the following, can i cross compile arm64 for my pi5
2025-11-17 13:27:47 [nitro@ryzen5] gn gen out.gn/x64.release --args='    is_component_build = false   # static libs    is_debug = false    v8_use_external_startup_data = false    v8_enable_i18n_support = false    treat_warnings_as_errors = false'autoninja -C out.gn/x64.release
2025-11-17 13:28:04 [kimi-k2-0905] The build you just ran produced x86-64 objects; nothing in that command line knows about
2025-11-17 13:28:04 [kimi-k2-0905] ARM.
2025-11-17 13:28:04 [kimi-k2-0905] To target a Pi 5 (AArch64) you need a second cross-compile build:
2025-11-17 13:28:04 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:28:04 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. Install the AArch64 toolchain ( Debian package `gcc-aarch64-linux-gnu` or download the
2025-11-17 13:28:04 [kimi-k2-0905] Clang/LLVM you prefer ).
2025-11-17 13:28:04 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. Create an ARM64 out directory with the target CPU and host toolchain specified,
2025-11-17 13:28:04 [kimi-k2-0905] e.g.
2025-11-17 13:28:04 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:28:04 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:28:04 [kimi-k2-0905] gn gen out.gn/arm64.release --args='
2025-11-17 13:28:04 [kimi-k2-0905]     target_cpu = "arm64"
2025-11-17 13:28:04 [kimi-k2-0905]     is_component_build = false
2025-11-17 13:28:04 [kimi-k2-0905]     is_debug = false
2025-11-17 13:28:04 [kimi-k2-0905]     v8_use_external_startup_data = false
2025-11-17 13:28:04 [kimi-k2-0905]     v8_enable_i18n_support = false
2025-11-17 13:28:04 [kimi-k2-0905]     treat_warnings_as_errors = false
2025-11-17 13:28:04 [kimi-k2-0905]     is_clang = true                          # or false if you use GCC
2025-11-17 13:28:04 [kimi-k2-0905]     clang_base_path = "/usr"
2025-11-17 13:28:04 [kimi-k2-0905]     sysroot = "/path/to/aarch64-linux-gnu/sysroot"   # optional but recommended'
2025-11-17 13:28:04 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:28:04 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:28:04 [kimi-k2-0905] 3. Build
2025-11-17 13:28:04 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:28:04 [kimi-k2-0905] autoninja -C out.gn/arm64.release
2025-11-17 13:28:04 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:28:04 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:28:04 [kimi-k2-0905] The resulting static libraries and `d8` binary in `out.gn/arm64.release` are now AArch64 and can be
2025-11-17 13:28:04 [kimi-k2-0905] copied to the Pi 5.
2025-11-17 13:28:04 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:29:29 [nitro@ryzen5] will it be compatible with my pi5 which is running:
2025-11-17 13:29:30 [nitro@ryzen5] skid@pi5m2:~ $ gcc --versiongcc (Debian 12.2.0-14+deb12u1) 12.2.0
2025-11-17 13:29:37 [kimi-k2-0905] Yes—an AArch64 (ARM64) build produced by the cross-compile step above will run on your Pi 5.
2025-11-17 13:29:37 [kimi-k2-0905] The compiler version on the Pi itself does not matter; only the target architecture (arm64)
2025-11-17 13:29:37 [kimi-k2-0905] and the glibc version you link against need to match.
2025-11-17 13:29:37 [kimi-k2-0905] Use a Debian 12 sysroot (or the Pi’s own rootfs) when you cross-compile and the resulting static
2025-11-17 13:29:37 [kimi-k2-0905] binaries will work with the glibc 2.36 that ships with your OS.
2025-11-17 13:29:37 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:30:12 [nitro@ryzen5] so steps to set up cross compiler
2025-11-17 13:30:30 [kimi-k2-0905] Quick, minimal setup on a Debian/Ubuntu host (x86-64) to cross-compile V8 for Pi 5:
2025-11-17 13:30:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:30:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. Install toolchain + sysroot
2025-11-17 13:30:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:30:30 [kimi-k2-0905] sudo apt update
2025-11-17 13:30:30 [kimi-k2-0905] sudo apt install -y clang llvm lld \
2025-11-17 13:30:30 [kimi-k2-0905]      gcc-aarch64-linux-gnu g++-aarch64-linux-gnu \
2025-11-17 13:30:30 [kimi-k2-0905]      debootstrap qemu-user-static
2025-11-17 13:30:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:30:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:30:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. Make a Debian-12 ARM64 sysroot (one-time)
2025-11-17 13:30:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:30:30 [kimi-k2-0905] sudo mkdir -p /opt/pi5-sysroot
2025-11-17 13:30:30 [kimi-k2-0905] sudo debootstrap --arch=arm64 bookworm /opt/pi5-sysroot \
2025-11-17 13:30:30 [kimi-k2-0905]      http://deb.debian.org/debian
2025-11-17 13:30:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:30:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:30:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 3. Tell GN to use it
2025-11-17 13:30:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:30:30 [kimi-k2-0905] gn gen out.gn/arm64.release --args='
2025-11-17 13:30:30 [kimi-k2-0905]     target_cpu="arm64"
2025-11-17 13:30:30 [kimi-k2-0905]     is_clang=true
2025-11-17 13:30:30 [kimi-k2-0905]     clang_base_path="/usr"
2025-11-17 13:30:30 [kimi-k2-0905]     use_lld=true
2025-11-17 13:30:30 [kimi-k2-0905]     sysroot="/opt/pi5-sysroot"
2025-11-17 13:30:30 [kimi-k2-0905]     is_component_build=false
2025-11-17 13:30:30 [kimi-k2-0905]     is_debug=false
2025-11-17 13:30:30 [kimi-k2-0905]     v8_use_external_startup_data=false
2025-11-17 13:30:30 [kimi-k2-0905]     v8_enable_i18n_support=false
2025-11-17 13:30:30 [kimi-k2-0905]     treat_warnings_as_errors=false'
2025-11-17 13:30:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:30:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:30:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 4. Build
2025-11-17 13:30:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:30:30 [kimi-k2-0905] autoninja -C out.gn/arm64.release
2025-11-17 13:30:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:30:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:30:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 5. Deploy
2025-11-17 13:30:30 [kimi-k2-0905] Copy anything from `out.gn/arm64.release` (static `d8`, `libv8_monolith.a`, etc.) straight to the
2025-11-17 13:30:30 [kimi-k2-0905] Pi 5; no further dependencies.
2025-11-17 13:30:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:31:21 [nitro@ryzen5] so before you implied I just had to adda target_cpu?
2025-11-17 13:31:32 [kimi-k2-0905] You can get away with nothing more than
2025-11-17 13:31:32 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:31:32 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:31:32 [kimi-k2-0905] gn gen out.gn/arm64.release --args='target_cpu="arm64" is_component_build=false is_debug=false v8_use_external_startup_data=false v8_enable_i18n_support=false'
2025-11-17 13:31:32 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:31:32 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:31:32 [kimi-k2-0905] and let GN pick the built-in `aarch64-linux-gnu` toolchain that the depot_tools fetch.
2025-11-17 13:31:32 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:31:32 [kimi-k2-0905] That works if:
2025-11-17 13:31:32 [kimi-k2-0905] - depottools already downloaded `clangx64v8arm64`
2025-11-17 13:31:32 [kimi-k2-0905] - the host has the 64-bit ARM linker (`aarch64-linux-gnu-ld`) in PATH
2025-11-17 13:31:32 [kimi-k2-0905] - you don’t care about exact glibc matches or LTO.
2025-11-17 13:31:32 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:31:32 [kimi-k2-0905] Adding the explicit sysroot/clang/lld lines above just makes the build 100 % reproducible and
2025-11-17 13:31:32 [kimi-k2-0905] guarantees the binary will run on your Pi 5’s Debian-12 userspace.
2025-11-17 13:31:32 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:32:17 [nitro@ryzen5] steps copied, thanks
2025-11-17 13:32:19 [kimi-k2-0905] You're welcome—good luck with the build.
2025-11-17 13:32:19 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:32:54 [nitro@ryzen5] i am on aws should i be using dnf?
2025-11-17 13:32:59 [kimi-k2-0905] On Amazon Linux 2/2023 the package manager is `dnf` (or `yum` wrapper), so:
2025-11-17 13:32:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:32:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:32:59 [kimi-k2-0905] sudo dnf install -y clang lld gcc-aarch64-linux-gnu gcc-c++-aarch64-linux-gnu
2025-11-17 13:32:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:32:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:32:59 [kimi-k2-0905] Then continue with the same `debootstrap`/`gn` steps; the rest of the cross-compile recipe is
2025-11-17 13:32:59 [kimi-k2-0905] identical.
2025-11-17 13:32:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:33:31 [nitro@ryzen5] [ec2-user@ip-172-31-8-229 dsptool]$ sudo dnf install -y clang lld gcc-aarch64-linux-gnu gcc-c++-aarch64-linux-gnuLast metadata expiration check: 4:05:33 ago on Mon Nov 17 09:27:44 2025.No match for argument: gcc-aarch64-linux-gnuNo match for argument: gcc-c++-aarch64-linux-gnuError: Unable to find a match: gcc-aarch64-linux-gnu gcc-c++-aarch64-linux-gnu
2025-11-17 13:33:42 [kimi-k2-0905] Amazon Linux doesn’t ship the cross toolchain in the default repo.
2025-11-17 13:33:42 [kimi-k2-0905] Either
2025-11-17 13:33:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:33:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. Enable EPEL and install the cross packages that exist there:
2025-11-17 13:33:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:33:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:33:42 [kimi-k2-0905] sudo dnf install -y epel-release
2025-11-17 13:33:42 [kimi-k2-0905] sudo dnf install -y gcc-aarch64-linux-gnu gcc-c++-aarch64-linux-gnu
2025-11-17 13:33:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:33:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:33:42 [kimi-k2-0905] or, if EPEL still doesn’t have them,
2025-11-17 13:33:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:33:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. Use the pre-built Linaro/Clang bundle that depot_tools already downloads:
2025-11-17 13:33:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:33:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:33:42 [kimi-k2-0905] # nothing to install – just add these two lines to your gn args
2025-11-17 13:33:42 [kimi-k2-0905] is_clang = true
2025-11-17 13:33:42 [kimi-k2-0905] clang_base_path = "${workspace}/third_party/llvm-build/Release+Asserts"
2025-11-17 13:33:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:33:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:33:42 [kimi-k2-0905] GN will pick up `aarch64-linux-gnu-ld` from
2025-11-17 13:33:42 [kimi-k2-0905] `${workspace}/third_party/llvm-build/Release+Asserts/bin` and you can skip the host cross-compiler
2025-11-17 13:33:42 [kimi-k2-0905] packages entirely.
2025-11-17 13:33:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:35:01 [nitro@ryzen5] so if i do nothing depot_tools will sort it out
2025-11-17 13:35:06 [kimi-k2-0905] Exactly—if you simply add
2025-11-17 13:35:06 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:35:06 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:35:06 [kimi-k2-0905] target_cpu="arm64"
2025-11-17 13:35:06 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:35:06 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:35:06 [kimi-k2-0905] to your GN args and leave `isclang=true` (the default), depottools already ships a complete
2025-11-17 13:35:06 [kimi-k2-0905] AArch64 toolchain (`clang`, `lld`, `aarch64-linux-gnu-ld`, runtime libs, etc.).
2025-11-17 13:35:06 [kimi-k2-0905] Nothing else to install; `autoninja` will just work.
2025-11-17 13:35:06 [kimi-k2-0905] 
