2025-11-17 13:38:07 [nitro@ryzen5] is there a package manager on windows for using v8 static libs in c++?
2025-11-17 13:38:17 [kimi-k2-0905] No – Google does not publish official V8 binaries or a Windows package-manager feed.
2025-11-17 13:38:17 [kimi-k2-0905] You have two realistic choices:
2025-11-17 13:38:17 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:38:17 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. Build the static libraries yourself with GN/Ninja (same `targetcpu="x64"` etc.) and link
2025-11-17 13:38:17 [kimi-k2-0905] against `v8monolith.lib` + the ICU/inspector libs you need.
2025-11-17 13:38:17 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. Use a third-party NuGet that somebody else compiled (search “v8-static” on nuget.org), but those
2025-11-17 13:38:17 [kimi-k2-0905] are usually a few major versions behind and built with unknown flags.
2025-11-17 13:38:17 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:38:17 [kimi-k2-0905] So on Windows you still compile V8 yourself; there’s no `vcpkg`, `conda`, or `winget` package that
2025-11-17 13:38:17 [kimi-k2-0905] ships up-to-date, matching static libs.
2025-11-17 13:38:17 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:38:56 [nitro@ryzen5] so first step on windows
2025-11-17 13:39:02 [kimi-k2-0905] Install the build tools:
2025-11-17 13:39:02 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:39:02 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. Install Visual Studio 2022 (Desktop C++ workload + “Windows 10/11 SDK”).
2025-11-17 13:39:02 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. Install depottools (https://storage.googleapis.com/chrome-infra/depottools.zip) and add
2025-11-17 13:39:02 [kimi-k2-0905] the folder to PATH.
2025-11-17 13:39:02 [kimi-k2-0905] 3. Open a fresh x64 Native Tools Command Prompt for VS 2022 and run:
2025-11-17 13:39:02 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:39:02 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:39:02 [kimi-k2-0905] gclient
2025-11-17 13:39:02 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:39:02 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 13:39:02 [kimi-k2-0905] That pulls the rest of depot_tools and you’re ready to fetch V8.
2025-11-17 13:39:02 [kimi-k2-0905] 
