2025-11-17 14:00:32 [nitro@ryzen5] what are options for hooking v8 to native sockets , i suppose follow node convention?
2025-11-17 14:01:01 [kimi-k2-0905] Three practical ways, in descending order of effort:
2025-11-17 14:01:01 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 14:01:01 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. Re-use Node’s implementation
2025-11-17 14:01:01 [kimi-k2-0905]    Clone the Node repo, lift `src/tcpwrap.cc`, `src/connectionwrap.cc`,
2025-11-17 14:01:01 [kimi-k2-0905] `src/streambase-inl.h`, and the `uvpoll_t` integration.
2025-11-17 14:01:01 [kimi-k2-0905]    Pro: battle-tested, keeps the same JS API (`net.Socket`, `net.Server`).
2025-11-17 14:01:01 [kimi-k2-0905]    Con: drags in libuv and a lot of Node scaffolding.
2025-11-17 14:01:01 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 14:01:01 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. Roll your own minimal binding with libuv (same reactor loop Node uses)
2025-11-17 14:01:01 [kimi-k2-0905]    - Add `uvtcpt` handles to your isolate’s embedder slot.
2025-11-17 14:01:01 [kimi-k2-0905]    - Expose `tcp.connect(port, host, cb)`, `tcp.onread`, `tcp.write(buf)` in a global `native_net`
2025-11-17 14:01:01 [kimi-k2-0905] object.
2025-11-17 14:01:01 [kimi-k2-0905]    - Call `uvrun(loop, UVRUN_NOWAIT)` once per turn of the micro-task queue.
2025-11-17 14:01:01 [kimi-k2-0905]    Keeps the event-driven style without pulling all of Node.
2025-11-17 14:01:01 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 14:01:01 [kimi-k2-0905] 3. Zero-deps, blocking-only wrapper (good for quick demos)
2025-11-17 14:01:01 [kimi-k2-0905]    - Write a simple `int socket_connect(const char* host, int port)` etc. in C.
2025-11-17 14:01:01 [kimi-k2-0905]    - Wrap with `FunctionTemplate`, return an integer fd to JS.
2025-11-17 14:01:01 [kimi-k2-0905]    - Expose `read(fd, buf, len)` / `write(fd, buf, len)` that do plain `::recv`/`::send`.
2025-11-17 14:01:01 [kimi-k2-0905]    No reactor, no threading—JS thread blocks; fine for CLI tools, useless for servers.
2025-11-17 14:01:01 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 14:01:01 [kimi-k2-0905] Pick the layer that matches how much of Node’s behaviour you want to re-implement; there is no
2025-11-17 14:01:01 [kimi-k2-0905] “official” V8 socket API—every embedder wires up its own I/O integration.
2025-11-17 14:01:01 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 14:02:16 [nitro@ryzen5] can you spec the node apis, so i can be compatible?
2025-11-17 14:02:42 [kimi-k2-0905] Minimum surface you need to re-create so existing Node net code “just runs”:
2025-11-17 14:02:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 14:02:42 [kimi-k2-0905] Global constructors
2025-11-17 14:02:42 [kimi-k2-0905] `net.Socket([options])`
2025-11-17 14:02:42 [kimi-k2-0905] `net.Server([options][, connectionListener])`
2025-11-17 14:02:42 [kimi-k2-0905] `net.createConnection(port[, host][, cb])`  alias `net.connect(...)`
2025-11-17 14:02:42 [kimi-k2-0905] `net.createServer([opts][, listener])`
2025-11-17 14:02:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 14:02:42 [kimi-k2-0905] Socket instance (extends <EventEmitter>)
2025-11-17 14:02:42 [kimi-k2-0905] Events: `'connect'`, `'data'`, `'end'`, `'close'`, `'error'`, `'timeout'`, `'drain'`
2025-11-17 14:02:42 [kimi-k2-0905] Properties: `readable`, `writable`, `destroyed`, `localAddress`, `localPort`, `remoteAddress`,
2025-11-17 14:02:42 [kimi-k2-0905] `remotePort`, `pending`
2025-11-17 14:02:42 [kimi-k2-0905] Methods:
2025-11-17 14:02:42 [kimi-k2-0905] - `write(chunk[, encoding][, cb]) → bool`
2025-11-17 14:02:42 [kimi-k2-0905] - `end([chunk][, encoding][, cb])`
2025-11-17 14:02:42 [kimi-k2-0905] - `destroy([error])`
2025-11-17 14:02:42 [kimi-k2-0905] - `pause()`, `resume()`
2025-11-17 14:02:42 [kimi-k2-0905] - `setEncoding(enc)`, `setTimeout(ms[, cb])`, `setNoDelay([bool])`, `setKeepAlive([bool][, ms])`
2025-11-17 14:02:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 14:02:42 [kimi-k2-0905] Server instance
2025-11-17 14:02:42 [kimi-k2-0905] Events: `'connection'`, `'listening'`, `'close'`, `'error'`
2025-11-17 14:02:42 [kimi-k2-0905] Methods:
2025-11-17 14:02:42 [kimi-k2-0905] - `listen(port[, host][, backlog][, cb])`
2025-11-17 14:02:42 [kimi-k2-0905] - `close([cb])`
2025-11-17 14:02:42 [kimi-k2-0905] - `address() → {address,family,port}`
2025-11-17 14:02:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 14:02:42 [kimi-k2-0905] Buffer integration
2025-11-17 14:02:42 [kimi-k2-0905] All I/O delivered as `Buffer` objects (Uint8Array with `node::Buffer` prototype).
2025-11-17 14:02:42 [kimi-k2-0905] Static helpers: `Buffer.from(string[, enc])`, `buf.toString([enc])`, `Buffer.alloc`,
2025-11-17 14:02:42 [kimi-k2-0905] `Buffer.concat`.
2025-11-17 14:02:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 14:02:42 [kimi-k2-0905] Utility constants
2025-11-17 14:02:42 [kimi-k2-0905] `net.isIP(input) → 0|4|6`
2025-11-17 14:02:42 [kimi-k2-0905] `net.isIPv4`, `net.isIPv6`
2025-11-17 14:02:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-17 14:02:42 [kimi-k2-0905] If your native layer exposes exactly the signatures above (same argument order, same default
2025-11-17 14:02:42 [kimi-k2-0905] values, same EventEmitter semantics, same Buffer contract) most npm packages that only do
2025-11-17 14:02:42 [kimi-k2-0905] `require('net')` will work unchanged.
2025-11-17 14:02:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 
