2025-11-18 06:48:41 [kimi-k2-0905] `channel.name` is a string, but `channel.recipients` is undefined because the channel
2025-11-18 06:48:41 [kimi-k2-0905] you’re fetching is a guild text channel, not a DM or GroupDM. Guild channels don’t expose a
2025-11-18 06:48:41 [kimi-k2-0905] recipient list—only individual voice channels do, and even then via the voice-state cache, not a
2025-11-18 06:48:41 [kimi-k2-0905] `recipients` property.
2025-11-18 06:48:41 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-18 06:48:41 [kimi-k2-0905] If you just want a rough “who’s here” number, fetch the voice states for the guild and
2025-11-18 06:48:41 [kimi-k2-0905] count members whose `voice.channelId === openChannel`:
2025-11-18 06:48:41 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-18 06:48:41 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-18 06:48:41 [kimi-k2-0905] const chan = await discordClient.channels.fetch(openChannel);
2025-11-18 06:48:41 [kimi-k2-0905] if (chan?.isVoiceBased()) {
2025-11-18 06:48:41 [kimi-k2-0905]   await chan.guild.members.fetch();                     // cache
2025-11-18 06:48:41 [kimi-k2-0905]   const joined = chan.guild.voiceStates.cache
2025-11-18 06:48:41 [kimi-k2-0905]                    .filter(vs => vs.channelId === openChannel).size;
2025-11-18 06:48:41 [kimi-k2-0905]   splurt("voice channel joined", joined);
2025-11-18 06:48:41 [kimi-k2-0905] }
2025-11-18 06:48:41 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-18 06:48:41 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-18 06:48:41 [kimi-k2-0905] For a text channel there’s no lightweight “joined” count; you’d have to iterate message
2025-11-18 06:48:41 [kimi-k2-0905] authors or thread members, which is expensive and still not accurate.
