2025-11-21 10:10:21 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-21 10:10:21 [gemini-3-pro] Also, in `slopfountain.ts`, ensure you invoke `/listen` or start with `config.listen: true` to
2025-11-21 10:10:21 [gemini-3-pro] activate the port. Note that `slopBroadcast` iterates `slopConnections` but does not clean up dead
2025-11-21 10:10:21 [gemini-3-pro] connections aggressively until a write fails.
2025-11-21 10:10:21 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-21 10:13:10 [nitro@ryzen5] the ansi output is confusing vscode terminal scrolling
2025-11-21 10:13:38 [gemini-3-pro] The scrolling confusion in VSCode is likely caused by the hardcoded `terminalColumns` (set to 100)
2025-11-21 10:13:38 [gemini-3-pro] in `slopfountain.ts`.
2025-11-21 10:13:38 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-21 10:13:38 [gemini-3-pro] When the VSCode terminal is narrower than 100 columns, the terminal wraps the text automatically,
2025-11-21 10:13:38 [gemini-3-pro] and your `wordWrap` function adds explicit newlines (`\r\n`). This creates "double" line breaks and
2025-11-21 10:13:38 [gemini-3-pro] ghost lines that break the scroll buffer.
2025-11-21 10:13:38 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-21 10:13:38 [gemini-3-pro] Here is a dynamic version of `wordWrap` that respects the actual window size.
2025-11-21 10:13:38 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-21 10:13:38 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-21 10:13:38 [gemini-3-pro] function wordWrap(text:string, cols:number=terminalColumns):string {
2025-11-21 10:13:38 [gemini-3-pro] 	try {
2025-11-21 10:13:38 [gemini-3-pro] 		const size = Deno.consoleSize();
2025-11-21 10:13:38 [gemini-3-pro] 		if (size.columns > 0) cols = size.columns - 2; // Safety margin
2025-11-21 10:13:38 [gemini-3-pro] 	} catch {
2025-11-21 10:13:38 [gemini-3-pro] 		// Keep default if not a TTY
2025-11-21 10:13:38 [gemini-3-pro] 	}
2025-11-21 10:13:38 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-21 10:13:38 [gemini-3-pro] 	const result = [];
2025-11-21 10:13:38 [gemini-3-pro] 	let pos = 0;
2025-11-21 10:13:38 [gemini-3-pro] 	while (pos < text.length) {
2025-11-21 10:13:38 [gemini-3-pro] 		let line = text.substring(pos, pos + cols);
2025-11-21 10:13:38 [gemini-3-pro] 		let n = line.length;
2025-11-21 10:13:38 [gemini-3-pro] 		if (n === cols) {
2025-11-21 10:13:38 [gemini-3-pro] 			let i = line.lastIndexOf(" ", n);
2025-11-21 10:13:38 [gemini-3-pro] 			if (i > 0) {
2025-11-21 10:13:38 [gemini-3-pro] 				line = line.substring(0, i);
2025-11-21 10:13:38 [gemini-3-pro] 				n = i + 1;
2025-11-21 10:13:38 [gemini-3-pro] 			}
2025-11-21 10:13:38 [gemini-3-pro] 		}
2025-11-21 10:13:38 [gemini-3-pro] 		result.push(line);
2025-11-21 10:13:38 [gemini-3-pro] 		pos += n;
2025-11-21 10:13:38 [gemini-3-pro] 	}
2025-11-21 10:13:38 [gemini-3-pro] 	return result.join("\r\n");
2025-11-21 10:13:38 [gemini-3-pro] }
2025-11-21 10:13:38 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-21 10:13:38 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-21 10:14:28 [nitro@ryzen5] kimi, what do you think of input from gemini-3-pro
2025-11-21 10:14:39 [kimi-k2-0905] Gemini 3 Pro’s note is concise and actionable: it isolates the shared-buffer race condition in
2025-11-21 10:14:39 [kimi-k2-0905] `slopprompt.ts` and gives a minimal patch. That’s the right fix—each connection needs its own
2025-11-21 10:14:39 [kimi-k2-0905] decoder and scratch buffer or you’ll get interleaved garbage under load.
2025-11-21 10:14:39 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:02:41 [nitro@ryzen5] on pi5 linux can i cause audio usb device to be scanned and added
2025-11-23 11:03:03 [kimi-k2-0905] Yes.
2025-11-23 11:03:03 [kimi-k2-0905] On a Pi 5 (or any modern Linux box) the kernel re-scans the USB bus when you tell it to; if the
2025-11-23 11:03:03 [kimi-k2-0905] device is already plugged-in it will simply re-enumerate and re-create the ALSA nodes.
2025-11-23 11:03:03 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:03:03 [kimi-k2-0905] Do it as root (or with sudo):
2025-11-23 11:03:03 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:03:03 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:03:03 [kimi-k2-0905] # find the right USB controller (usually 1-1 or 2-1 on a Pi 5)
2025-11-23 11:03:03 [kimi-k2-0905] lsusb -t
2025-11-23 11:03:03 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:03:03 [kimi-k2-0905] # force re-enumeration
2025-11-23 11:03:03 [kimi-k2-0905] echo 0 > /sys/bus/usb/devices/1-1/authorized   # power-off
2025-11-23 11:03:03 [kimi-k2-0905] echo 1 > /sys/bus/usb/devices/1-1/authorized   # power-on
2025-11-23 11:03:03 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:03:03 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:03:03 [kimi-k2-0905] The audio gadget disappears for a second and then re-appears; `aplay -l` / `arecord -l` will list
2025-11-23 11:03:03 [kimi-k2-0905] it again.
2025-11-23 11:03:03 [kimi-k2-0905] No unplugging required.
2025-11-23 11:03:03 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:03:30 [nitro@ryzen5] skid@pi5m2:~ $ lsusb -t/:  Bus 04.Port 1: Dev 1, Class=root_hub, Driver=xhci-hcd/1p, 5000M/:  Bus 03.Port 1: Dev 1, Class=root_hub, Driver=xhci-hcd/2p, 480M/:  Bus 02.Port 1: Dev 1, Class=root_hub, Driver=xhci-hcd/1p, 5000M/:  Bus 01.Port 1: Dev 1, Class=root_hub, Driver=xhci-hcd/2p, 480M    |__ Port 1: Dev 3, If 0, Class=Audio, Driver=snd-usb-audio, 12M    |__ Port 1: Dev 3, If 1, Class=Audio, Driver=snd-usb-audio, 12M    |__ Port 1: Dev 3, If 2, Class=Audio, Driver=snd-usb-audio, 12M    |__ Port 2: Dev 2, If 0, Class=Communications, Driver=cdc_acm, 12M    |__ Port 2: Dev 2, If 1, Class=CDC Data, Driver=cdc_acm, 12M
2025-11-23 11:03:42 [kimi-k2-0905] The audio device is on bus 1, port 1 → `1-1`.
2025-11-23 11:03:42 [kimi-k2-0905] Rescan it:
2025-11-23 11:03:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:03:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:03:42 [kimi-k2-0905] sudo sh -c 'echo 0 > /sys/bus/usb/devices/1-1/authorized
2025-11-23 11:03:42 [kimi-k2-0905]             sleep 1
2025-11-23 11:03:42 [kimi-k2-0905]             echo 1 > /sys/bus/usb/devices/1-1/authorized'
2025-11-23 11:03:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:03:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:03:42 [kimi-k2-0905] After the second command the kernel re-enumerates the same gadget and ALSA nodes come back (check
2025-11-23 11:03:42 [kimi-k2-0905] with `aplay -l`).
2025-11-23 11:03:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:03:57 [nitro@ryzen5] aplay -l
2025-11-23 11:04:17 [nitro@ryzen5] skid@pi5m2:~ $ aplay -l**** List of PLAYBACK Hardware Devices ****card 0: vc4hdmi0 [vc4-hdmi-0], device 0: MAI PCM i2s-hifi-0 [MAI PCM i2s-hifi-0]  Subdevices: 1/1  Subdevice #0: subdevice #0card 1: vc4hdmi1 [vc4-hdmi-1], device 0: MAI PCM i2s-hifi-0 [MAI PCM i2s-hifi-0]  Subdevices: 1/1  Subdevice #0: subdevice #0card 2: Track [Fast Track], device 0: USB Audio [USB Audio]  Subdevices: 1/1  Subdevice #0: subdevice #0skid@pi5m2:~ $
2025-11-23 11:04:25 [kimi-k2-0905] The “Fast Track” is already visible on card 2, so the re-scan worked.
2025-11-23 11:04:25 [kimi-k2-0905] Nothing more to add—ALSA can open it as “hw:2,0” (or “plughw:2,0”) straight away.
2025-11-23 11:04:25 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:04:58 [nitro@ryzen5] can i monitor input with amixer for hw:2
2025-11-23 11:05:14 [kimi-k2-0905] Yes—use `amixer` (or `alsamixer`) to view the capture controls that the “Fast Track” exposes.
2025-11-23 11:05:14 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:05:14 [kimi-k2-0905] List every control first:
2025-11-23 11:05:14 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:05:14 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:05:14 [kimi-k2-0905] amixer -c 2 controls   # or: amixer -D hw:2 controls
2025-11-23 11:05:14 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:05:14 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:05:14 [kimi-k2-0905] Typical USB-audio devices give one “Capture” switch and one “Capture” volume.
2025-11-23 11:05:14 [kimi-k2-0905] Query their current state:
2025-11-23 11:05:14 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:05:14 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:05:14 [kimi-k2-0905] amixer -c 2 sget Capture
2025-11-23 11:05:14 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:05:14 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:05:14 [kimi-k2-0905] Monitor any change in real time:
2025-11-23 11:05:14 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:05:14 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:05:14 [kimi-k2-0905] amixer -c 2 events    # control-change events appear as they happen
2025-11-23 11:05:14 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:05:14 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:05:14 [kimi-k2-0905] If you want a human-readable live view, open `alsamixer`:
2025-11-23 11:05:14 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:05:14 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:05:14 [kimi-k2-0905] alsamixer -c 2
2025-11-23 11:05:14 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:05:14 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:05:14 [kimi-k2-0905] Press F4 to switch to “Capture” view; move to the desired source with arrow keys and space-bar to
2025-11-23 11:05:14 [kimi-k2-0905] select it.
2025-11-23 11:05:14 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:05:50 [nitro@ryzen5] skid@pi5m2:~ $ amixer -D hw:2 controlsnumid=3,iface=MIXER,name='PCM Playback Switch'numid=4,iface=MIXER,name='PCM Playback Volume'numid=5,iface=MIXER,name='Mic Capture Switch'numid=6,iface=MIXER,name='Mic Capture Volume'numid=2,iface=PCM,name='Capture Channel Map'numid=1,iface=PCM,name='Playback Channel Map'skid@pi5m2:~ $
2025-11-23 11:06:07 [kimi-k2-0905] Only one physical input path is exported:
2025-11-23 11:06:07 [kimi-k2-0905] “Mic Capture Switch” and “Mic Capture Volume”.
2025-11-23 11:06:07 [kimi-k2-0905] Monitor / tweak it:
2025-11-23 11:06:07 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:06:07 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:06:07 [kimi-k2-0905] # current state
2025-11-23 11:06:07 [kimi-k2-0905] amixer -D hw:2 sget 'Mic Capture'
2025-11-23 11:06:07 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:06:07 [kimi-k2-0905] # toggle on (if it was off)
2025-11-23 11:06:07 [kimi-k2-0905] amixer -D hw:2 sset 'Mic Capture' on
2025-11-23 11:06:07 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:06:07 [kimi-k2-0905] # set gain to 80 % (scale 0-127 on most USB devices)
2025-11-23 11:06:08 [kimi-k2-0905] amixer -D hw:2 sset 'Mic Capture' 80
2025-11-23 11:06:08 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:06:08 [kimi-k2-0905] # watch for any knob movement
2025-11-23 11:06:08 [kimi-k2-0905] amixer -D hw:2 events
2025-11-23 11:06:08 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:06:08 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:06:08 [kimi-k2-0905] That’s all that’s available on this interface; there is no separate “Line-in” or “Inst” switch
2025-11-23 11:06:08 [kimi-k2-0905] visible to ALSA.
2025-11-23 11:06:08 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:06:51 [nitro@ryzen5] yup so to see uv for the default capture, what command args for hw:2?
2025-11-23 11:07:12 [kimi-k2-0905] No built-in UV-meter in amixer—use `arecord` in silent mode plus `awk`/`pv` or
2025-11-23 11:07:12 [kimi-k2-0905] `snd-aloop`+`alsaloop` if you want a bar.
2025-11-23 11:07:12 [kimi-k2-0905] Quick & dirty level monitor:
2025-11-23 11:07:12 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:07:12 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:07:12 [kimi-k2-0905] arecord -D hw:2,0 -f S16_LE -c 1 -r 48000 -t raw /dev/null \
2025-11-23 11:07:12 [kimi-k2-0905]   | hexdump -v -e '1/2 "%d\n"' \
2025-11-23 11:07:12 [kimi-k2-0905]   | awk '{v=($0>32767?$0-65536:$0); peak=peak<abs?abs:peak; abs=(v<0?-v:v); if(NR%48==0){printf "\r%6d",peak;peak=0}}'
2025-11-23 11:07:12 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:07:12 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:07:12 [kimi-k2-0905] Hit Ctrl-C to quit.
2025-11-23 11:07:12 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:07:43 [nitro@ryzen5] are you sure?
2025-11-23 11:07:52 [kimi-k2-0905] Positive—`amixer` only shows/sets controls, not signal level.
2025-11-23 11:07:52 [kimi-k2-0905] For a real meter without extra tools you need something that actually reads the PCM stream
2025-11-23 11:07:52 [kimi-k2-0905] (`arecord`, `alsaloop`, `jack`, `pulse`, `snd-peak` module, etc.).
2025-11-23 11:07:52 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:12:08 [nitro@ryzen5] from c++ alsa code to enumerate the hw device names
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] Minimal C++ example that walks the ALSA card list and prints every hardware device name.
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] #include <alsa/asoundlib.h>
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] #include <iostream>
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] int main() {
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 	int err;
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 	int card = -1;                 /* -1 == first card */
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 	while (snd_card_next(&card) == 0 && card >= 0) {
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 		char name[64];
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 		snprintf(name, sizeof(name), "hw:%d", card);
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 		snd_ctl_t *handle;
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 		if ((err = snd_ctl_open(&handle, name, 0)) < 0) {
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 			std::cerr << "Cannot open " << name << ": " << snd_strerror(err) << '\n';
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 			continue;
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 		}
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 		int dev = -1;
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 		while (snd_ctl_pcm_next_device(handle, &dev) == 0 && dev >= 0) {
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 			snd_pcm_info_t *pinfo;
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 			snd_pcm_info_alloca(&pinfo);
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 			snd_pcm_info_set_device(pinfo, dev);
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 			snd_pcm_info_set_subdevice(pinfo, 0);
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 			snd_pcm_info_set_stream(pinfo, SND_PCM_STREAM_PLAYBACK);
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 			if (snd_ctl_pcm_info(handle, pinfo) == 0)
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 				std::cout << "hw:" << card << ',' << dev << "  "
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 				          << snd_pcm_info_get_name(pinfo) << "  (playback)\n";
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 			snd_pcm_info_set_stream(pinfo, SND_PCM_STREAM_CAPTURE);
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 			if (snd_ctl_pcm_info(handle, pinfo) == 0)
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 				std::cout << "hw:" << card << ',' << dev << "  "
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 				          << snd_pcm_info_get_name(pinfo) << "  (capture)\n";
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 		}
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 		snd_ctl_close(handle);
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 	}
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 	return 0;
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] }
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] Build:
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] g++ -std=c++17 list_hw.cpp -lasound -o list_hw
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] Run:
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] ./list_hw
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:12:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 
