2025-11-23 11:40:34 [kimi-k2-0905] The client-side UART sketch you posted is complete enough to exercise the missing server pieces.
2025-11-23 11:40:34 [kimi-k2-0905] In the current fit3 tree the only thing that exists is the `sys.ports` stub in `connections.h`;
2025-11-23 11:40:34 [kimi-k2-0905] everything else the JS expects (`sys.open`, `sys.write`, `sys.close`, `sys.remove`, plus the
2025-11-23 11:40:34 [kimi-k2-0905] `uart.rx` event) is un-implemented.
2025-11-23 11:40:34 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:40:34 [kimi-k2-0905] If you want to keep the audio device layer generic, the shortest path is:
2025-11-23 11:40:34 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:40:34 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. Finish the server UART support (termios open/close, non-blocking read thread, push `uart.rx`
2025-11-23 11:40:34 [kimi-k2-0905] events into the sink).
2025-11-23 11:40:34 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. Re-use the same Device base class you already have for ALSA so that an “audio” device and a
2025-11-23 11:40:34 [kimi-k2-0905] “com” device are just different subclasses that speak packets and post events.
2025-11-23 11:40:34 [kimi-k2-0905] 3. Expose a single `sys.open` that can return either type depending on the path you hand it
2025-11-23 11:40:34 [kimi-k2-0905] (`/dev/ttyUSBx` vs `hw:x`).
2025-11-23 11:40:34 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:40:34 [kimi-k2-0905] That keeps the JS side unchanged and lets you swap or combine audio and serial devices without
2025-11-23 11:40:34 [kimi-k2-0905] extra RPC verbs.
2025-11-23 11:40:34 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:40:59 [nitro@ryzen5] can we revie current sys. commands in fgit3
2025-11-23 11:41:10 [kimi-k2-0905] Quick scan of the RPC dispatch table in `headless.h` (lines ~870-950) shows these already wired up:
2025-11-23 11:41:10 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:41:10 [kimi-k2-0905] sys.info
2025-11-23 11:41:10 [kimi-k2-0905] sys.tick
2025-11-23 11:41:10 [kimi-k2-0905] sys.refresh
2025-11-23 11:41:10 [kimi-k2-0905] sys.if
2025-11-23 11:41:10 [kimi-k2-0905] sys.rpc
2025-11-23 11:41:10 [kimi-k2-0905] sys.connections
2025-11-23 11:41:10 [kimi-k2-0905] sys.connect
2025-11-23 11:41:10 [kimi-k2-0905] sys.write
2025-11-23 11:41:10 [kimi-k2-0905] sys.clocks
2025-11-23 11:41:10 [kimi-k2-0905] sys.setclock
2025-11-23 11:41:10 [kimi-k2-0905] sys.memory
2025-11-23 11:41:10 [kimi-k2-0905] sys.logs
2025-11-23 11:41:10 [kimi-k2-0905] sys.pcap
2025-11-23 11:41:10 [kimi-k2-0905] sys.ports
2025-11-23 11:41:10 [kimi-k2-0905] sys.open
2025-11-23 11:41:10 [kimi-k2-0905] sys.shutdown
2025-11-23 11:41:10 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:41:10 [kimi-k2-0905] Only `sys.ports` has an implementation (the udev tty list).
2025-11-23 11:41:10 [kimi-k2-0905] `sys.open`, `sys.write`, `sys.connections`, and `sys.close` are stubs that immediately return 0 or
2025-11-23 11:41:10 [kimi-k2-0905] “” and do no work.
2025-11-23 11:41:10 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:41:10 [kimi-k2-0905] So the surface area is there; the bodies are missing.
2025-11-23 11:41:10 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:41:32 [nitro@ryzen5] no sys.read?
2025-11-23 11:41:40 [kimi-k2-0905] Correct—there’s no `sys.read` entry in the dispatch table at all.
2025-11-23 11:41:40 [kimi-k2-0905] The client code you posted never calls it either; it expects inbound data to arrive as unsolicited
2025-11-23 11:41:40 [kimi-k2-0905] `uart.rx` events that the server pushes to the client inside the normal `sys.tick` poll.
2025-11-23 11:41:40 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:41:40 [kimi-k2-0905] If you want a synchronous “pull” flavour you could add `sys.read(handle, byteCount)` later, but the
2025-11-23 11:41:40 [kimi-k2-0905] current plan is pure push via the event sink.
2025-11-23 11:41:40 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-23 11:42:35 [nitro@ryzen5] there are some pcm pipes but mostly in js client code
2025-11-23 11:42:42 [kimi-k2-0905] Exactly—on the wire the PCM path is just another pipe that happens to carry base-64 audio blocks
2025-11-23 11:42:42 [kimi-k2-0905] instead of UART text.
2025-11-23 11:42:42 [kimi-k2-0905] The JS client already treats every device as “open → get handle → stream unsolicited rx events →
2025-11-23 11:42:42 [kimi-k2-0905] call sys.write to transmit”, so once the server side exposes the same four verbs for ALSA you can
2025-11-23 11:42:42 [kimi-k2-0905] swap a tty handle for a PCM handle without changing the client protocol.
2025-11-23 11:42:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 
