2025-11-25 12:07:53 [nitro@ryzen5] what is no command ?
2025-11-25 12:08:00 [kimi-k2-0905] It’s just Deno telling you that the `test` task in `deno.json` (or `deno.jsonc`) doesn’t define a
2025-11-25 12:08:00 [kimi-k2-0905] command to run after printing the banner.
2025-11-25 12:08:00 [kimi-k2-0905] In other words, the task finished successfully and there’s nothing else for it to do.
2025-11-25 12:08:00 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-25 12:08:18 [nitro@ryzen5] can i add a return to shut it up?
2025-11-25 12:08:30 [kimi-k2-0905] Yes—give the task a dummy command (or make it run the script and exit).
2025-11-25 12:08:30 [kimi-k2-0905] In `deno.json` (or `deno.jsonc`) change the `test` task to something like:
2025-11-25 12:08:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-25 12:08:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-25 12:08:30 [kimi-k2-0905] "tasks": {
2025-11-25 12:08:30 [kimi-k2-0905]   "test": "deno run --allow-net sloppyredis.ts && echo"
2025-11-25 12:08:30 [kimi-k2-0905] }
2025-11-25 12:08:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-25 12:08:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-25 12:08:30 [kimi-k2-0905] or simply
2025-11-25 12:08:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-25 12:08:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-25 12:08:30 [kimi-k2-0905] "test": "deno run --allow-net sloppyredis.ts"
2025-11-25 12:08:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-25 12:08:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-25 12:08:30 [kimi-k2-0905] Either way suppresses the “no command” notice; the first adds an explicit no-op (`echo`), the
2025-11-25 12:08:30 [kimi-k2-0905] second relies on Deno exiting normally after the script finishes.
2025-11-25 12:08:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
