2025-11-27 11:09:06 [gemini-3-pro] Here is a review of the `species.json` file (version 1.3.2).
2025-11-27 11:09:06 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-27 11:09:06 [gemini-3-pro] The JSON syntax is valid and the structure is consistent. However, there are a few data integrity
2025-11-27 11:09:06 [gemini-3-pro] issues regarding code uniqueness and potential redundancies.
2025-11-27 11:09:06 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-27 11:09:06 [gemini-3-pro] 1. Duplicate Code Across Orders
2025-11-27 11:09:06 [gemini-3-pro] • `faur`: This code appears in both Hymenoptera and Dermaptera.
2025-11-27 11:09:06 [gemini-3-pro]        In Dermaptera, it correctly corresponds to Forficula auricularia* (European
2025-11-27 11:09:06 [gemini-3-pro] Earwig).
2025-11-27 11:09:06 [gemini-3-pro]        In Hymenoptera, it is likely a typo or collision (possibly for a Formica*
2025-11-27 11:09:06 [gemini-3-pro] species).
2025-11-27 11:09:06 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-27 11:09:06 [gemini-3-pro] 2. Potential Redundancies
2025-11-27 11:09:06 [gemini-3-pro] • Lepidoptera: You have both `danpl` and `dapl`. Both likely refer to the
2025-11-27 11:09:06 [gemini-3-pro] Monarch butterfly (Danaus plexippus). It is cleaner to keep just one (usually `danpl`
2025-11-27 11:09:06 [gemini-3-pro] matches the 3+3 or 4+4 naming convention better).
2025-11-27 11:09:06 [gemini-3-pro] • Hymenoptera:
2025-11-27 11:09:06 [gemini-3-pro]        `linvi`: This appears alongside `solinv` (Solenopsis invicta).
2025-11-27 11:09:06 [gemini-3-pro] `linvi` might be a typo for Linepithema* (Argentine ant, usually `lhum`) or a confused acronym
2025-11-27 11:09:06 [gemini-3-pro] for the fire ant.
2025-11-27 11:09:06 [gemini-3-pro]        `bomb` vs `boml`: Bombus* species. Ensure these represent distinct
2025-11-27 11:09:06 [gemini-3-pro] species groups and not just generic overlaps.
2025-11-27 11:09:06 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-27 11:09:06 [gemini-3-pro] 3. Taxonomy Note
2025-11-27 11:09:06 [gemini-3-pro] • Isoptera (Termites): Modern classification places termites within Blattodea
2025-11-27 11:09:06 [gemini-3-pro] (Cockroaches). Keeping them separate is fine for functional databases, but be aware of the
2025-11-27 11:09:06 [gemini-3-pro] phylogenetic overlap.
2025-11-27 11:09:06 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-27 11:09:06 [gemini-3-pro] 4. Code Format
2025-11-27 11:09:06 [gemini-3-pro] • Most codes follow a 4-letter format, but there is a mix of 5+ letters (`fortai`, `solinv`,
2025-11-27 11:09:06 [gemini-3-pro] `bloat`). Ensure your parsing logic handles variable lengths if strict 4-char width is expected by
2025-11-27 11:09:06 [gemini-3-pro] the consumer application.
2025-11-27 11:09:06 [gemini-3-pro] 
