2025-11-27 14:24:34 [gemini-3-pro] In the provided dataset, `tspi` appears in four different orders. Biologically, these likely refer
2025-11-27 14:24:34 [gemini-3-pro] to different species, such as:
2025-11-27 14:24:34 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-27 14:24:34 [gemini-3-pro] • Nematoda: Trichinella spiralis (Roundworm)
2025-11-27 14:24:34 [gemini-3-pro] • Platyhelminthes: Taenia pisiformis (Tapeworm)
2025-11-27 14:24:34 [gemini-3-pro] • Chilopoda / Diplopoda: Various species (e.g., Thereuonema, Trigoniulus).
2025-11-27 14:24:34 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-27 14:24:34 [gemini-3-pro] • Is it specific enough?*
2025-11-27 14:24:34 [gemini-3-pro] Since your JSON structure groups codes by Order, it acts as a namespace. It is
2025-11-27 14:24:34 [gemini-3-pro] acceptable for `tspi` to exist in all groups, provided your software looks up codes within
2025-11-27 14:24:34 [gemini-3-pro] a specific order (e.g., `Nematoda > tspi`).
2025-11-27 14:24:34 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-27 14:24:34 [gemini-3-pro] If you intend to use these codes as a flat, global index (where `tspi` must point to only one
2025-11-27 14:24:34 [gemini-3-pro] species across the entire database), then you must rename three of them to ensure global
2025-11-27 14:24:34 [gemini-3-pro] uniqueness.
2025-11-27 14:24:34 [gemini-3-pro] 
