2025-11-27 14:25:55 [gemini-3-pro] The short code `tspi` is universally recognized in bioinformatics (e.g., KEGG, UniProt) as
2025-11-27 14:25:55 [gemini-3-pro] Trichinella spiralis, which is a Nematode (Roundworm).
2025-11-27 14:25:55 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-27 14:25:55 [gemini-3-pro] In your dataset, it appears in four distinct groups:
2025-11-27 14:25:55 [gemini-3-pro] 1.  Nematoda: Correct (Trichinella spiralis).
2025-11-27 14:25:55 [gemini-3-pro] 2.  Platyhelminthes: Likely an error (often confused with tapeworms, but it is a
2025-11-27 14:25:55 [gemini-3-pro] roundworm).
2025-11-27 14:25:55 [gemini-3-pro] 3.  Chilopoda: Duplicate.
2025-11-27 14:25:55 [gemini-3-pro] 4.  Diplopoda: Duplicate.
2025-11-27 14:25:55 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-27 14:25:55 [gemini-3-pro] • Is it okay to be in all groups?*
2025-11-27 14:25:55 [gemini-3-pro] If your application relies on a "flat" lookup (e.g., `getSpeciesByCode("tspi")`), this is not
2025-11-27 14:25:55 [gemini-3-pro] okay; it will cause collisions. Even if your data is structured hierarchically, using the same
2025-11-27 14:25:55 [gemini-3-pro] short-code for four different organisms defeats the purpose of a unique identifier system.
2025-11-27 14:25:55 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-27 14:25:55 [gemini-3-pro] • Recommendation:*
2025-11-27 14:25:55 [gemini-3-pro] Keep `tspi` in Nematoda to adhere to existing standards. You should generate alternative
2025-11-27 14:25:55 [gemini-3-pro] codes (e.g., `thsp`, `tspi2`) or remove the entries for the other three groups.
2025-11-27 14:25:55 [gemini-3-pro] 
