2025-11-28 09:52:03 [nitro@ryzen5] to sample luma video pin with pi pico for blanking and single brightness do i need comparators?
2025-11-28 09:52:15 [kimi-k2-0905] No comparators needed – the Pico’s own GPIO pins are plenty fast and have a built-in Schmitt
2025-11-28 09:52:15 [kimi-k2-0905] trigger.
2025-11-28 09:52:15 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-28 09:52:15 [kimi-k2-0905] Wire the video signal (through a 330 Ω – 1 kΩ series resistor and a 3 V3 clamp diode if you like)
2025-11-28 09:52:15 [kimi-k2-0905] to any GPIO.
2025-11-28 09:52:15 [kimi-k2-0905] In code just watch the pin: high = active video, low = blanking.
2025-11-28 09:52:15 [kimi-k2-0905] If you also want a brightness number, grab the ADC (GPIO 26-28) and sample during the active part
2025-11-28 09:52:15 [kimi-k2-0905] of the line; again no external comparator required.
2025-11-28 09:52:15 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-28 09:53:23 [nitro@ryzen5] what is typical v range from lumina pin on a commodore 64
2025-11-28 09:53:25 [nitro@ryzen5] pal
2025-11-28 09:53:31 [kimi-k2-0905] LUMINA (pin 6 on the A/V) is a 0–1 V composite-luma signal, 75 Ω source.
2025-11-28 09:53:31 [kimi-k2-0905] Sync tips sit at ≈ 0 V, blanking at ≈ 0.3 V, peak white ≈ 1 V.
2025-11-28 09:53:31 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-28 09:53:50 [nitro@ryzen5] so not negative voltage?
2025-11-28 09:53:53 [kimi-k2-0905] Correct—PAL C64 luma is 0 V to ≈ 1 V, never negative.
2025-11-28 09:53:53 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-28 09:54:03 [nitro@ryzen5] same for chroma?
2025-11-28 09:54:07 [kimi-k2-0905] Chroma is a 0.3 V<sub>pp</sub> ±0.3 V centred on 0 V, so it swings –0.3 V … +0.3 V.
2025-11-28 09:54:07 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-28 09:55:17 [nitro@ryzen5] can i set the 0.3 v threshold in pico or is that based one the series resistor?
2025-11-28 09:55:25 [kimi-k2-0905] Neither—Schmitt thresholds are fixed at ≈ 0.8 V (low→high) and ≈ 0.4 V (high→low).
2025-11-28 09:55:25 [kimi-k2-0905] To detect the 0.3 V blanking level you have to shift/scale the signal externally (e.g. divider plus
2025-11-28 09:55:25 [kimi-k2-0905] bias or an op-amp) so 0.3 V lands above 0.8 V, or use the ADC and compare in software.
2025-11-28 09:55:25 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-28 09:56:35 [nitro@ryzen5] op amp?
2025-11-28 09:56:51 [kimi-k2-0905] A single-supply rail-to-rail op-amp (MCP6001, TLV9061, …) wired as a non-inverting amplifier with a
2025-11-28 09:56:51 [kimi-k2-0905] small positive offset is the simplest way:
2025-11-28 09:56:51 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-28 09:56:51 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-28 09:56:51 [kimi-k2-0905] Vcc (3 V3)
2025-11-28 09:56:51 [kimi-k2-0905]   │
2025-11-28 09:56:51 [kimi-k2-0905]   R1 100 k
2025-11-28 09:56:51 [kimi-k2-0905]   │
2025-11-28 09:56:51 [kimi-k2-0905]   ├───+ op-amp non-inverting input
2025-11-28 09:56:51 [kimi-k2-0905]   │   │
2025-11-28 09:56:51 [kimi-k2-0905] video──R2 10 k──┤   gain = 1 + (R3 / R4) ≈ 4
2025-11-28 09:56:51 [kimi-k2-0905]   │   │           so 0 V→0.4 V, 0.3 V→1.6 V
2025-11-28 09:56:51 [kimi-k2-0905] gnd   R4 33 k   │
2025-11-28 09:56:51 [kimi-k2-0905]       │         │
2025-11-28 09:56:51 [kimi-k2-0905]       └───R3 100 k─── op-amp output → GPIO
2025-11-28 09:56:51 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-28 09:56:51 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-28 09:56:51 [kimi-k2-0905] Now the 0.3 V blanking level sits well above the Pico’s 0.8 V high threshold, so you can treat the
2025-11-28 09:56:51 [kimi-k2-0905] op-amp output as a digital blanking flag with no extra parts.
2025-11-28 09:56:51 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-28 09:58:01 [nitro@ryzen5] how fast can pico sample at say 3 bit res?
2025-11-28 09:58:07 [kimi-k2-0905] 3-bit is meaningless for the on-board ADC; it always gives 12-bit result (≈ 3 µs conversion).
2025-11-28 09:58:07 [kimi-k2-0905] With the hardware round-robin you can start a new sample every 2 µs → 500 kSa/s sustained, or about
2025-11-28 09:58:07 [kimi-k2-0905] 10–12 Msamples/s if you DMA-stream and post-process off-chip.
2025-11-28 09:58:07 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-28 09:59:45 [nitro@ryzen5] the width of c64 pal pixel is less than 2 s
2025-11-28 09:59:50 [kimi-k2-0905] C64 PAL pixel rate ≈ 8 MHz → pixel width ≈ 125 ns.
2025-11-28 09:59:50 [kimi-k2-0905] 2 µs is sixteen pixels, so one ADC sample every 2 µs is far too slow to resolve individual
2025-11-28 09:59:50 [kimi-k2-0905] pixels—use a comparator or faster external sampler.
2025-11-28 09:59:50 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-28 10:00:43 [nitro@ryzen5] so basic comparotor for basic black and white can use plain old 0.8v shmitt without amp?
2025-11-28 10:00:49 [kimi-k2-0905] Yes—if you only need “black / white” and can accept the fixed 0.8 V Schmitt point, just feed the
2025-11-28 10:00:49 [kimi-k2-0905] 0-1 V luma straight (with the usual 330 Ω–1 kΩ series resistor and optional 3 V3 clamp diode).
2025-11-28 10:00:49 [kimi-k2-0905] Sync tips (~0 V) read low, anything above ~0.8 V reads high, no extra amp needed.
2025-11-28 10:00:49 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-28 10:01:13 [nitro@ryzen5] in parallel with the blank detector circuit?
2025-11-28 10:01:21 [kimi-k2-0905] Yes—run the same 0–1 V luma line to both destinations:
2025-11-28 10:01:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-28 10:01:21 [kimi-k2-0905] - straight into a GPIO (through 330 Ω) for the 0.8 V Schmitt blank/sync flag,
2025-11-28 10:01:21 [kimi-k2-0905] - and (if you want) into the op-amp/comparator side-path for whatever other threshold you need.
2025-11-28 10:01:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-28 10:01:21 [kimi-k2-0905] Both inputs are high-impedance, so they sit happily in parallel; no buffering required.
2025-11-28 10:01:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-28 10:02:03 [nitro@ryzen5] how fast can i sample schmitt with pio?
2025-11-28 10:02:07 [kimi-k2-0905] PIO can sample a single pin every system-clock cycle.
2025-11-28 10:02:07 [kimi-k2-0905] Default 125 MHz clksys → 125 Msa/s ⇒ 8 ns time resolution.
2025-11-28 10:02:07 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-28 10:02:49 [nitro@ryzen5] can we output variable voltage for multi sampling white pixels
2025-11-28 10:02:57 [kimi-k2-0905] PIO can’t make analogue voltages—its pins are digital only.
2025-11-28 10:02:57 [kimi-k2-0905] If you want several “white” thresholds you have to:
2025-11-28 10:02:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-28 10:02:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. do it in software (ADC, fast comparator, or DMA-stream the ADC), or
2025-11-28 10:02:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. add an external DAC (or PWM+filter) and feed that reference to a comparator, or
2025-11-28 10:02:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 3. use a resistor ladder plus multiple PIO pins to build a crude 2-3 bit DAC yourself.
2025-11-28 10:02:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-28 10:03:38 [nitro@ryzen5] if the opamp is oscillating the gain
2025-11-28 10:03:45 [kimi-k2-0905] Add a small feedback capacitor (10-22 pF) directly across R3 (feedback resistor) to roll off the
2025-11-28 10:03:45 [kimi-k2-0905] bandwidth above the video frequencies (≈ 5 MHz).
2025-11-28 10:03:45 [kimi-k2-0905] Keep layout tight: ground plane, 100 nF supply decoupling right at the op-amp, and (if it still
2025-11-28 10:03:45 [kimi-k2-0905] rings) a 47-100 Ω series resistor on the output before the GPIO pin.
2025-11-28 10:03:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-28 10:04:35 [nitro@ryzen5] the question is can i multi sample digital pin with luma coming through an opamp with oscillating gain
2025-11-28 10:04:40 [kimi-k2-0905] No—if the op-amp is oscillating the “gain” pin isn’t a clean logic level, so the digital samples
2025-11-28 10:04:40 [kimi-k2-0905] are garbage.
2025-11-28 10:04:40 [kimi-k2-0905] Stop the oscillation first (small feedback cap, tight layout, proper decoupling); only then can you
2025-11-28 10:04:40 [kimi-k2-0905] trust the GPIO readings.
2025-11-28 10:04:40 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-28 10:06:56 [nitro@ryzen5] am happy with opamp circuit for blank detecting, for white pixel can i run same signal through second opamp
2025-11-28 10:07:03 [kimi-k2-0905] Yes—just split the incoming luma with a couple of 1 k resistors (or a simple T-junction) and feed
2025-11-28 10:07:03 [kimi-k2-0905] each op-amp its own copy.
2025-11-28 10:07:03 [kimi-k2-0905] Give the second one a different reference/offset so its output goes high only when luma is above
2025-11-28 10:07:03 [kimi-k2-0905] the “white” level you want; that digital output can then go to another GPIO and be over-sampled by
2025-11-28 10:07:03 [kimi-k2-0905] PIO exactly the same way you’re doing the blanking channel.
2025-11-28 10:07:03 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-28 10:07:40 [nitro@ryzen5] am i allowed to vary the reference with each sample?
2025-11-28 10:07:48 [kimi-k2-0905] Not in real time—each op-amp needs a fixed reference (set with a resistor divider or DAC pin).
2025-11-28 10:07:48 [kimi-k2-0905] If you want a sliding threshold you either:
2025-11-28 10:07:48 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-28 10:07:48 [kimi-k2-0905] - use the on-chip ADC and vary the comparison value in software, or
2025-11-28 10:07:48 [kimi-k2-0905] - buffer a fast external DAC into the comparator’s reference pin and update that DAC between lines,
2025-11-28 10:07:48 [kimi-k2-0905] not within a single 125 ns pixel.
2025-11-28 10:07:48 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-28 10:08:51 [nitro@ryzen5] for 2 bit mono, i would need 3 opamps? and i need another for the blanking sync
2025-11-28 10:08:56 [kimi-k2-0905] Yes—three “white” comparators (thresholds at ~0.35 V, ~0.6 V, ~0.9 V) give you 2-bit grey-scale
2025-11-28 10:08:56 [kimi-k2-0905] plus black, and one more for the 0.3 V blanking/sync flag → four op-amps total.
2025-11-28 10:08:56 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-28 10:09:36 [nitro@ryzen5] is lm741 ok?
2025-11-28 10:09:42 [kimi-k2-0905] No.
2025-11-28 10:09:42 [kimi-k2-0905] LM741 needs ±5 V supplies, its output can’t reach the positive rail, and it’s far too slow (0.5
2025-11-28 10:09:42 [kimi-k2-0905] V/µs) for 8 MHz video edges.
2025-11-28 10:09:42 [kimi-k2-0905] Use a single-supply, rail-to-rail, ≥10 MHz part like MCP6001, TLV9061, OPA333, etc.
2025-11-28 10:09:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-28 10:10:47 [nitro@ryzen5] anything from https://www.jaycar.co.nz/? i can paste a search
2025-11-28 10:10:51 [kimi-k2-0905] Go ahead and paste the search results – I’ll pick the quickest single-supply rail-to-rail part they
2025-11-28 10:10:51 [kimi-k2-0905] list.
2025-11-28 10:10:51 [kimi-k2-0905] 
