2025-11-28 12:25:20 [nitro@ryzen5] a socket that holds a bunch of LM319
2025-11-28 12:25:28 [kimi-k2-0905] LM319 is a dual comparator in a DIP-14 package; there’s no multi-device socket made for it.
2025-11-28 12:25:28 [kimi-k2-0905] If you want one mechanical assembly, use a standard 14-pin DIP machine-tooled socket per device and
2025-11-28 12:25:28 [kimi-k2-0905] butt them together on 0.3" spacing, or buy a DIP-socket strip (Snap-off SIP sockets) and place two
2025-11-28 12:25:28 [kimi-k2-0905] side-by-side so the 14 pins line up.
2025-11-28 12:25:28 [kimi-k2-0905] No vendor makes a "gang" socket for multiple LM319s.
2025-11-28 12:25:28 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-28 12:27:21 [nitro@ryzen5] what are standard dip socket sizes?
2025-11-28 12:27:33 [kimi-k2-0905] Standard DIP socket spacing is 0.3" (7.62 mm) row-to-row; pin pitch is 0.1" (2.54 mm).
2025-11-28 12:27:33 [kimi-k2-0905] Common body widths:
2025-11-28 12:27:33 [kimi-k2-0905] - 0.3" rows: 8, 14, 16, 18, 20, 24, 28, 32, 40 pins
2025-11-28 12:27:33 [kimi-k2-0905] - 0.6" rows: 24, 28, 32, 40, 48, 52, 64 pins
2025-11-28 12:27:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-28 12:27:33 [kimi-k2-0905] LM319 is 14-pin DIP, so use a 14-pin 0.3" socket.
2025-11-28 12:27:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-28 12:28:27 [nitro@ryzen5] so 2 will fit a 28?
2025-11-28 12:28:36 [kimi-k2-0905] No — a 28-pin 0.3"-row DIP socket has one 0.1"-pitch row of 14 pins on each side.
2025-11-28 12:28:36 [kimi-k2-0905] Two LM319s side-by-side would need two separate 14-pin sockets; they can’t share a single
2025-11-28 12:28:36 [kimi-k2-0905] 28-pin socket because the pin rows would clash.
2025-11-28 12:28:36 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-28 12:28:55 [nitro@ryzen5] end on end silly
2025-11-28 12:29:00 [kimi-k2-0905] End-to-end works: two 14-pin sockets butted lengthwise on 0.1" pitch gives a 28-pin footprint,
2025-11-28 12:29:00 [kimi-k2-0905] electrically separate but mechanically one row.
2025-11-28 12:29:00 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-28 12:29:19 [nitro@ryzen5] name for dip sockets
2025-11-28 12:29:23 [kimi-k2-0905] Machine-tooled DIP socket (or “precision DIP socket”) is the usual trade name; vendors also list
2025-11-28 12:29:23 [kimi-k2-0905] them as “DIP socket strip” or “turned-pin DIP socket.”
2025-11-28 12:29:23 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-28 12:34:24 [nitro@ryzen5] new plan LM393 is dip8 so how many on a 40 pin socket?
2025-11-28 12:34:45 [kimi-k2-0905] LM393 is 8-pin DIP (0.3" row spacing).
2025-11-28 12:34:45 [kimi-k2-0905] A 40-pin DIP socket has two rows of 20 pins on 0.1" pitch.
2025-11-28 12:34:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 20 pins per row ÷ 8 pins per device → two full devices per row, using 16 pins and leaving 4
2025-11-28 12:34:45 [kimi-k2-0905] pins unused at each end.
2025-11-28 12:34:45 [kimi-k2-0905] So one 40-pin socket can accept 2 LM393s per row × 2 rows = 4 devices total.
2025-11-28 12:34:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
