2025-11-28 13:44:02 [gemini-3-pro] Is there a DIP8 version?
2025-11-28 13:44:02 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-28 13:44:02 [gemini-3-pro] • No, there is no direct DIP8 version of the LM319.*
2025-11-28 13:44:02 [gemini-3-pro] The LM319 requires 14 pins because it features separate Logic Ground and Analog Supply pins to
2025-11-28 13:44:02 [gemini-3-pro] interface with various logic families (TTL, RTL, DTL) while isolating the inputs. A standard 8-pin
2025-11-28 13:44:02 [gemini-3-pro] package does not have enough pins to support this architecture for two comparators.
2025-11-28 13:44:02 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-28 13:44:02 [gemini-3-pro] • Alternatives for DIP8:*
2025-11-28 13:44:02 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-28 13:44:02 [gemini-3-pro] 1.  LM311 (Single Comparator): If you can use two separate chips, the LM311 is the closest
2025-11-28 13:44:02 [gemini-3-pro] functional equivalent in a DIP8 package. It offers similar high-speed performance and flexibility
2025-11-28 13:44:02 [gemini-3-pro] (separate grounds/strobes) but contains only one comparator per chip.
2025-11-28 13:44:02 [gemini-3-pro] 2.  LT1720 (Dual Comparator): If you need a dual comparator in DIP8 that is fast, the
2025-11-28 13:44:02 [gemini-3-pro] Analog Devices (Linear Tech) LT1720 is a modern replacement. It is significantly faster (4.5ns vs
2025-11-28 13:44:02 [gemini-3-pro] 80ns) and fits the standard dual DIP8 footprint, though it lacks the specific high-voltage
2025-11-28 13:44:02 [gemini-3-pro] open-collector flexibility of the older LM319.
2025-11-28 13:44:02 [gemini-3-pro] 3.  LM393 (Dual Comparator): This is the standard industry dual comparator in DIP8, but it
2025-11-28 13:44:02 [gemini-3-pro] is much slower (1.3µs) than the LM319. It is not recommended if your circuit relies on the
2025-11-28 13:44:02 [gemini-3-pro] high speed of the LM319.
2025-11-28 13:44:02 [gemini-3-pro] 
