2025-11-28 13:45:48 [nitro@ryzen5]  LUMA 2 Purpose - capture 2bit monochrome video such as C64 luma pin Pico 4 input pins CSYNC G0 G1 G2 Fed by 2 x LM319 featuring 4 fast voltage comparators.CSYNC based on fixed threshold, 2 gray and 1 white detector adjustable with trimmers.Pico PIO code required to take 4 GPIO digital inputs and format output of 320 wide 240 high 50 hz 2 bit monochrome stream.The bandwidth for 320 x 240 x 50 / 2 = 0.96MB/s which can be streamed over USB1.1.A 5V circuit unless otherwise noted## BOM * 1 x RCA Jack: PCB mount (Luma input).* 1 x Resistor (75 $\Omega$): Termination resistor to Ground (essential to prevent signal reflection).* 2 x LM319: Dual High-Speed Comparators.* 4 x Resistors (2.2k $\Omega$ to 4.7k $\Omega$): Pull-up resistors connecting the LM319 outputs to the Pico's 3.3V rail.* 2 x Capacitors (0.1 $\mu$F): Decoupling capacitors for the LM319 power pins.* 3 x Trimmer Potentiometers (10k $\Omega$): Multi-turn precision trimmers recommended for fine-tuning G0, G1, and G2 thresholds.* 2 x Resistors (Fixed): Voltage divider to set the fixed CSYNC threshold (typically just above 0V/Blanking level).* 1 x Raspberry Pi Pico (RP2040)
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] LM319 Pinout (DIP14)
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] The LM319 is a dual high-speed comparator. Note that it separates the logic ground from the supply
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] ground.
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] ╭────────┬───────────────────────┬────────┬───────────────────────╮
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] │  Pin   │  Function             │  Pin   │  Function             │
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] ├────────┼───────────────────────┼────────┼───────────────────────┤
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] │  :---  │  :---                 │  :---  │  :---                 │
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] │  1     │  NC                   │  14    │  NC                   │
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] │  2     │  NC                   │  13    │  NC                   │
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] │  3     │  **GND** (Supply V-)  │  12    │  Input 2 (-)          │
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] │  4     │  Input 1 (-)          │  11    │  **VCC** (Supply V+)  │
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] │  5     │  Input 1 (+)          │  10    │  Input 2 (+)          │
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] │  6     │  **GND** (Logic 1)    │  9     │  Output 2             │
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] │  7     │  Output 1             │  8     │  **GND** (Logic 2)    │
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] ╰────────┴───────────────────────┴────────┴───────────────────────╯
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] DIP8 Alternative
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] • No direct equivalent exists in DIP8.*
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] The LM319 has an 80ns response time. Common DIP8 dual comparators (like the LM393) are
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] significantly slower (~1300ns) and will blur the video image.
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] • Option A: Stick with LM319 (DIP14).
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] • Option B: Use two LM311 chips (Single High-Speed Comparator, DIP8).
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] • Option C: Use a modern SOIC-8 dual high-speed comparator (e.g., TLV3502 or
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] MCP6562) on a DIP8 adapter PCB.
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] Raspberry Pi Pico PIO Code
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] This MicroPython code sets up a PIO state machine to sync with the video signal and capture the 4
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] GPIO pins.
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] • Note on Bandwidth:* The PIO captures raw 4-bit data (CSYNC + 3 Luma). To achieve the
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] 0.96MB/s target (2-bit packed), the RP2040 CPU must process the raw buffer using a Look-Up Table
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] (LUT) to convert the "thermometer code" from the comparators into 2-bit values before USB
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] transmission.
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] import rp2
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] from machine import Pin
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] # Constants
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] VIDEO_PIN_BASE = 0  # GPIO 0, 1, 2, 3 (CSYNC, G0, G1, G2)
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] PIXEL_CLOCK = 6150000  # ~6.15 MHz for 320px active line
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] SYNC_PIN_INDEX = 0  # Relative to base, assuming CSYNC is lowest bit
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] @rp2.asm_pio(autopush=True, push_thresh=32, fifo_join=rp2.PIO.JOIN_RX)
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] def capture_scanline():
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     # 1. Wait for HSYNC (Active Low)
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     # Assumes CSYNC pin is mapped to the 'jmp pin' or input pin 0
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     wait(0, pin, 0)
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     # 2. Wait for Back Porch (HSYNC goes High)
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     wait(1, pin, 0)
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     # 3. Delay to center capture (Back Porch adjustment)
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     # Adjust 'x' cycles to align left edge of screen
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     set(x, 20) [31]
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     label("porch_delay")
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     jmp(x_dec, "porch_delay")
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     # 4. Capture 320 pixels
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     # Loop 320 times.
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     # Note: X register is 32-bit, but limited immediate loading.
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     # We unroll or use a loop. Here we capture 320 pixels.
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     set(x, 19)             # Loop 20 times (16 pixels per loop = 320)
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     label("capture_loop")
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     # Capture 16 pixels (Unrolled for speed/timing precision)
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     in_(pins, 4)           # Sample 4 pins
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     in_(pins, 4)
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     in_(pins, 4)
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     in_(pins, 4)
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     in_(pins, 4)
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     in_(pins, 4)
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     in_(pins, 4)
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     in_(pins, 4)
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     in_(pins, 4)
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     in_(pins, 4)
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     in_(pins, 4)
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     in_(pins, 4)
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     in_(pins, 4)
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     in_(pins, 4)
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     in_(pins, 4)
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     in_(pins, 4)
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     jmp(x_dec, "capture_loop")
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     # End of line, wait for next sync in main loop or wrap automatically
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] def init_video_capture(sm_id, pin_base):
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     # Initialize GPIO
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     for i in range(4):
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]         p = Pin(pin_base + i, Pin.IN)
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     # Create State Machine
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     # input_base: First pin of the 4 inputs
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     # jmp_pin: The pin used for WAIT instructions (CSYNC)
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     sm = rp2.StateMachine(
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]         sm_id,
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]         capture_scanline,
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]         freq=PIXEL_CLOCK * 4, # Run SM faster to allow precise delays
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]         in_base=Pin(pin_base),
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]         jmp_pin=Pin(pin_base + SYNC_PIN_INDEX)
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     )
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     sm.active(1)
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     return sm
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] def capture_frame(sm):
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     # 320 pixels * 4 bits = 1280 bits = 40 words (32-bit) per line
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     # 240 lines * 40 words = 9600 words
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     # DMA is recommended here in production code
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     frame_buffer = []
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     # Simple blocking capture for demonstration
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     # In reality, use DMA to transfer to a bytearray
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     for line in range(240):
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]         for _ in range(40):
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]             frame_buffer.append(sm.get())
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro]     return frame_buffer
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-28 13:46:38 [gemini-3-pro] 
