2025-11-28 17:13:17 [claude-opus-4-5] The shared files show a sophisticated multi-model LLM research tool (Slop Fountain) and its Unix
2025-11-28 17:13:17 [claude-opus-4-5] socket server component (SloppyPipe).
2025-11-28 17:13:17 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-28 17:13:17 [claude-opus-4-5] • slopfountain.ts* - Main application (~2800 lines)
2025-11-28 17:13:17 [claude-opus-4-5] - Multi-provider support: OpenAI, Anthropic, Google Gemini, DeepSeek
2025-11-28 17:13:17 [claude-opus-4-5] - Tool calling with function declarations
2025-11-28 17:13:17 [claude-opus-4-5] - File sharing with caching (SHA-256 hashing)
2025-11-28 17:13:17 [claude-opus-4-5] - Session history management with squashing
2025-11-28 17:13:17 [claude-opus-4-5] - TTS integration (GPT voices, Gemini voices)
2025-11-28 17:13:17 [claude-opus-4-5] - ANSI/markdown rendering with table box drawing
2025-11-28 17:13:17 [claude-opus-4-5] - Configuration flags system
2025-11-28 17:13:17 [claude-opus-4-5] - Cost tracking per model/account
2025-11-28 17:13:17 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-28 17:13:17 [claude-opus-4-5] • sloppypipe.ts* - Socket relay (~120 lines)
2025-11-28 17:13:17 [claude-opus-4-5] - Unix domain socket server at `/tmp/sloppy.sock`
2025-11-28 17:13:17 [claude-opus-4-5] - Bridges external connections to Fountain
2025-11-28 17:13:17 [claude-opus-4-5] - Simple session management via `connections` record
2025-11-28 17:13:17 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-28 17:13:17 [claude-opus-4-5] Notable patterns:
2025-11-28 17:13:17 [claude-opus-4-5] - Uses `rid` property on connections (Deno 1.x API - deprecated in Deno 2)
2025-11-28 17:13:17 [claude-opus-4-5] - Named functions throughout as preferred
2025-11-28 17:13:17 [claude-opus-4-5] - Tab indentation consistent
2025-11-28 17:13:17 [claude-opus-4-5] - Heavy use of async/await with Promise.race for multiplexing
2025-11-28 17:13:17 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-28 17:13:17 [claude-opus-4-5] The `rid` usage in sloppypipe.ts may need updating for Deno 2.x compatibility since resource IDs
2025-11-28 17:13:17 [claude-opus-4-5] were removed. The connection object itself should be used as the key instead.
2025-11-28 17:13:17 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-28 17:31:25 [nitro@ryzen5] what does connection object type look like?
2025-11-28 17:31:34 [claude-opus-4-5] In Deno 2.x, for Unix domain sockets, `Deno.listen()` returns a `Deno.Listener` and connections are
2025-11-28 17:31:34 [claude-opus-4-5] `Deno.Conn` objects.
2025-11-28 17:31:34 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-28 17:31:34 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-28 17:31:34 [claude-opus-4-5] interface Conn {
2025-11-28 17:31:34 [claude-opus-4-5] 	readonly localAddr: Addr;
2025-11-28 17:31:34 [claude-opus-4-5] 	readonly remoteAddr: Addr;
2025-11-28 17:31:34 [claude-opus-4-5] 	readonly readable: ReadableStream<Uint8Array>;
2025-11-28 17:31:34 [claude-opus-4-5] 	readonly writable: WritableStream<Uint8Array>;
2025-11-28 17:31:34 [claude-opus-4-5] 	read(p: Uint8Array): Promise<number | null>;
2025-11-28 17:31:34 [claude-opus-4-5] 	write(p: Uint8Array): Promise<number>;
2025-11-28 17:31:34 [claude-opus-4-5] 	close(): void;
2025-11-28 17:31:34 [claude-opus-4-5] 	closeWrite(): Promise<void>;
2025-11-28 17:31:34 [claude-opus-4-5] 	ref(): void;
2025-11-28 17:31:34 [claude-opus-4-5] 	unref(): void;
2025-11-28 17:31:34 [claude-opus-4-5] }
2025-11-28 17:31:34 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-28 17:31:34 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-28 17:31:34 [claude-opus-4-5] For Unix sockets specifically, `localAddr` and `remoteAddr` are `Deno.UnixAddr`:
2025-11-28 17:31:34 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-28 17:31:34 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-28 17:31:34 [claude-opus-4-5] interface UnixAddr {
2025-11-28 17:31:34 [claude-opus-4-5] 	transport: "unix" | "unixpacket";
2025-11-28 17:31:34 [claude-opus-4-5] 	path: string;
2025-11-28 17:31:34 [claude-opus-4-5] }
2025-11-28 17:31:34 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-28 17:31:34 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-28 17:31:34 [claude-opus-4-5] The `rid` property was removed in Deno 2. For keying connections, you could use:
2025-11-28 17:31:34 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-28 17:31:34 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-28 17:31:34 [claude-opus-4-5] let nextId = 0;
2025-11-28 17:31:34 [claude-opus-4-5] const connections: Map<number, SocketSession> = new Map();
2025-11-28 17:31:34 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-28 17:31:34 [claude-opus-4-5] async function onConnection(connection: Deno.Conn) {
2025-11-28 17:31:34 [claude-opus-4-5] 	const id = nextId++;
2025-11-28 17:31:34 [claude-opus-4-5] 	const session = new SocketSession(connection, id);
2025-11-28 17:31:34 [claude-opus-4-5] 	connections.set(id, session);
2025-11-28 17:31:34 [claude-opus-4-5] 	// ...
2025-11-28 17:31:34 [claude-opus-4-5] }
2025-11-28 17:31:34 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-28 17:31:34 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-28 17:31:34 [claude-opus-4-5] Or use the connection object itself as a Map key:
2025-11-28 17:31:34 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-28 17:31:34 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-28 17:31:34 [claude-opus-4-5] const connections: Map<Deno.Conn, SocketSession> = new Map();
2025-11-28 17:31:34 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-28 17:31:34 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-28 17:34:03 [nitro@ryzen5] read and write return number of bytes trasnferred?
2025-11-28 17:34:28 [claude-opus-4-5] Yes, both return `Promise<number>` for bytes transferred, with one exception:
2025-11-28 17:34:28 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-28 17:34:28 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-28 17:34:28 [claude-opus-4-5] read(p: Uint8Array): Promise<number | null>
2025-11-28 17:34:28 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-28 17:34:28 [claude-opus-4-5] - Returns bytes read into buffer
2025-11-28 17:34:28 [claude-opus-4-5] - Returns `null` on EOF (connection closed)
2025-11-28 17:34:28 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-28 17:34:28 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-28 17:34:28 [claude-opus-4-5] write(p: Uint8Array): Promise<number>
2025-11-28 17:34:28 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-28 17:34:28 [claude-opus-4-5] - Returns bytes written
2025-11-28 17:34:28 [claude-opus-4-5] - May be less than `p.length` (partial write)
2025-11-28 17:34:28 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-28 17:34:28 [claude-opus-4-5] For reliable writes you need a loop:
2025-11-28 17:34:28 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-28 17:34:28 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-28 17:34:28 [claude-opus-4-5] async function writeAll(conn: Deno.Conn, data: Uint8Array): Promise<void> {
2025-11-28 17:34:28 [claude-opus-4-5] 	let written = 0;
2025-11-28 17:34:28 [claude-opus-4-5] 	while (written < data.length) {
2025-11-28 17:34:28 [claude-opus-4-5] 		written += await conn.write(data.subarray(written));
2025-11-28 17:34:28 [claude-opus-4-5] 	}
2025-11-28 17:34:28 [claude-opus-4-5] }
2025-11-28 17:34:28 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-28 17:34:28 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-28 17:34:28 [claude-opus-4-5] Or use the streams API which handles this:
2025-11-28 17:34:28 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-28 17:34:28 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-28 17:34:28 [claude-opus-4-5] const writer = conn.writable.getWriter();
2025-11-28 17:34:28 [claude-opus-4-5] await writer.write(data);
2025-11-28 17:34:28 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-28 17:34:28 [claude-opus-4-5] 
