2025-11-29 09:03:32 [nitro@ryzen5] hi claude
2025-11-29 09:03:35 [claude-opus-4-5] hi skid, how can I help you today?
2025-11-29 09:03:35 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:04:11 [nitro@ryzen5] the serial daisy chain with C64 has a name?
2025-11-29 09:05:00 [claude-opus-4-5] The Commodore 64 uses the IEC bus (also called the CBM bus or serial bus) for connecting
2025-11-29 09:05:00 [claude-opus-4-5] disk drives, printers and other peripherals in a daisy chain configuration.
2025-11-29 09:05:00 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:05:09 [nitro@ryzen5] IEC stands for?
2025-11-29 09:05:30 [claude-opus-4-5] • IEC stands for International Electrotechnical Commission*.
2025-11-29 09:05:30 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:05:30 [claude-opus-4-5] Though worth noting Commodore's implementation was a proprietary serial protocol - they just
2025-11-29 09:05:30 [claude-opus-4-5] borrowed the name from the standard DIN connector type they used.
2025-11-29 09:05:30 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:06:15 [nitro@ryzen5] does the bus have a IEC number dsignator?
2025-11-29 09:06:34 [claude-opus-4-5] Yes, the connector is based on the IEC 60130-9 standard (originally IEC 130-9) which
2025-11-29 09:06:34 [claude-opus-4-5] defines the DIN connector pinouts.
2025-11-29 09:06:34 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:06:34 [claude-opus-4-5] Commodore used a 6-pin DIN connector for their serial bus, though the protocol itself was
2025-11-29 09:06:34 [claude-opus-4-5] Commodore's own design.
2025-11-29 09:06:34 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:07:19 [nitro@ryzen5] oh so thats just a reference to the DIN?
2025-11-29 09:07:23 [claude-opus-4-5] Yes exactly - the "IEC" name just refers to the DIN connector standard, not the protocol itself.
2025-11-29 09:07:23 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:07:23 [claude-opus-4-5] The actual serial protocol Commodore used was their own proprietary design, sometimes called the
2025-11-29 09:07:23 [claude-opus-4-5] CBM serial bus or just Commodore serial bus to be more accurate.
2025-11-29 09:07:23 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:08:03 [nitro@ryzen5] commodore serial bus - is 5v?
2025-11-29 09:08:09 [claude-opus-4-5] Yes, the Commodore serial bus operates at 5V TTL levels.
2025-11-29 09:08:09 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:08:34 [nitro@ryzen5] to spy on it with rp2040 i need converters?
2025-11-29 09:08:40 [claude-opus-4-5] No, you should be fine directly - the RP2040 GPIO is 3.3V but is 5V tolerant for
2025-11-29 09:08:40 [claude-opus-4-5] input.
2025-11-29 09:08:40 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:08:40 [claude-opus-4-5] For just snooping/reading the bus lines, you can connect directly. If you need to drive the bus
2025-11-29 09:08:40 [claude-opus-4-5] (act as a device), you'd want level shifters or open-drain configuration since the CBM bus uses
2025-11-29 09:08:40 [claude-opus-4-5] active-low signalling with accent pullups.
2025-11-29 09:08:40 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:09:05 [nitro@ryzen5] do 1541 have two connectors?
2025-11-29 09:09:11 [claude-opus-4-5] Yes, the 1541 has two 6-pin DIN connectors - one for input, one for output - to allow daisy
2025-11-29 09:09:11 [claude-opus-4-5] chaining multiple devices on the bus.
2025-11-29 09:09:11 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:11:33 [nitro@ryzen5] i will wire up a header, if I want to drive the bus in future i have spare LM319 channels?
2025-11-29 09:11:45 [claude-opus-4-5] The LM319 is a comparator, not really suited for driving the bus.
2025-11-29 09:11:45 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:11:45 [claude-opus-4-5] For driving the CBM serial bus you'd want something like:
2025-11-29 09:11:45 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:11:45 [claude-opus-4-5] - 74LS06 or 74LS07 (open-collector inverters/buffers)
2025-11-29 09:11:45 [claude-opus-4-5] - 74LVC1G07 (single gate versions)
2025-11-29 09:11:45 [claude-opus-4-5] - Or just use the RP2040 GPIO in open-drain mode with external pullups
2025-11-29 09:11:45 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:11:45 [claude-opus-4-5] The bus uses open-collector/open-drain signalling so devices can pull lines low without conflict.
2025-11-29 09:11:45 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:12:50 [nitro@ryzen5] lets keep it simple, i can just pull up my outputs when i want to drive? gpio in ouput mode and what else
2025-11-29 09:12:55 [claude-opus-4-5] For open-drain style output on RP2040:
2025-11-29 09:12:55 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:12:55 [claude-opus-4-5] - Set GPIO as output
2025-11-29 09:12:55 [claude-opus-4-5] - Write 0 to pull the line low (active)
2025-11-29 09:12:55 [claude-opus-4-5] - Set GPIO as input to release (let external pullups bring it high)
2025-11-29 09:12:55 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:12:55 [claude-opus-4-5] So you toggle between output-low and input mode, rather than driving high directly. Keeps you safe
2025-11-29 09:12:55 [claude-opus-4-5] from bus contention.
2025-11-29 09:12:55 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:13:55 [nitro@ryzen5] and i can communicate just by pulling low when i want to communicate data?
2025-11-29 09:14:02 [claude-opus-4-5] Yes, the CBM serial bus uses three main lines:
2025-11-29 09:14:02 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:14:02 [claude-opus-4-5] - CLK (clock)
2025-11-29 09:14:02 [claude-opus-4-5] - DATA
2025-11-29 09:14:02 [claude-opus-4-5] - ATN (attention)
2025-11-29 09:14:02 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:14:02 [claude-opus-4-5] All active-low, open-drain. Devices communicate by pulling lines low and releasing them.
2025-11-29 09:14:02 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:14:02 [claude-opus-4-5] The protocol is a handshake-based bit-bang serial - the controller and device take turns driving
2025-11-29 09:14:02 [claude-opus-4-5] CLK and DATA to transfer bits. ATN is used by the host to signal commands vs data.
2025-11-29 09:14:02 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:14:02 [claude-opus-4-5] So yes, you pull low to assert, release to let it float high - that's the basis of all
2025-11-29 09:14:02 [claude-opus-4-5] communication on the bus.
2025-11-29 09:14:02 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:14:28 [nitro@ryzen5] can i read GPIO or do i use second pin?
2025-11-29 09:14:33 [claude-opus-4-5] You can read the same GPIO pin - the RP2040 lets you read the input state of a pin even when it's
2025-11-29 09:14:33 [claude-opus-4-5] configured as output.
2025-11-29 09:14:33 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:14:33 [claude-opus-4-5] So one pin per line is enough for bidirectional communication.
2025-11-29 09:14:33 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:15:55 [nitro@ryzen5] so to play on serial bus, i just have to pull to ground when writing 0's otherwise it is happy in write mode data 1 and will float high?
2025-11-29 09:16:00 [claude-opus-4-5] Not quite - you can't just write 1 to drive high, that would actively push 5V contention issues.
2025-11-29 09:16:00 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:16:00 [claude-opus-4-5] You need to:
2025-11-29 09:16:00 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:16:00 [claude-opus-4-5] - Write 0: output mode, pin low (pulling bus down)
2025-11-29 09:16:00 [claude-opus-4-5] - Release: switch to input mode (bus floats high via pullups)
2025-11-29 09:16:00 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:16:00 [claude-opus-4-5] So it's output-low or input, never output-high. That's the open-drain behaviour you need to
2025-11-29 09:16:00 [claude-opus-4-5] emulate.
2025-11-29 09:16:00 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:16:23 [nitro@ryzen5] wo, so gpio input mode is release?
2025-11-29 09:17:11 [nitro@ryzen5] this doesn't need any other components, just din sockets and rp2040?
2025-11-29 09:17:17 [claude-opus-4-5] Yes exactly - GPIO input mode = high impedance = release the line.
2025-11-29 09:17:17 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:17:17 [claude-opus-4-5] And correct, for basic snooping and even participating on the bus, you just need:
2025-11-29 09:17:17 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:17:17 [claude-opus-4-5] - DIN sockets
2025-11-29 09:17:17 [claude-opus-4-5] - RP2040
2025-11-29 09:17:17 [claude-opus-4-5] - Wires
2025-11-29 09:17:17 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:17:17 [claude-opus-4-5] The bus already has pullups on the C64 side. Keep your wires reasonably short and you're good to
2025-11-29 09:17:17 [claude-opus-4-5] go.
2025-11-29 09:17:17 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:19:51 [nitro@ryzen5] can pio modifiy the gpio direction bit?
2025-11-29 09:19:57 [claude-opus-4-5] Yes, PIO can control GPIO direction directly.
2025-11-29 09:19:57 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:19:57 [claude-opus-4-5] Each PIO instruction can set pins as inputs or outputs using:
2025-11-29 09:19:57 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:19:57 [claude-opus-4-5] - pindirs destination
2025-11-29 09:19:57 [claude-opus-4-5] - SET pindirs, value
2025-11-29 09:19:57 [claude-opus-4-5] - OUT pindirs, count
2025-11-29 09:19:57 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:19:57 [claude-opus-4-5] So you can do the open-drain toggle entirely within a PIO state machine - no CPU involvement needed
2025-11-29 09:19:57 [claude-opus-4-5] for the bit-banging.
2025-11-29 09:19:57 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:23:27 [nitro@ryzen5] spec for the luminance pin on c64? blanking black white voltages
2025-11-29 09:23:32 [claude-opus-4-5] The C64 luma output is roughly:
2025-11-29 09:23:32 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:23:32 [claude-opus-4-5] - Sync: ~0V
2025-11-29 09:23:32 [claude-opus-4-5] - Blanking/Black: ~0.3-0.4V
2025-11-29 09:23:32 [claude-opus-4-5] - White: ~1.0-1.1V
2025-11-29 09:23:32 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:23:32 [claude-opus-4-5] It's a composite-style signal with sync tips, into roughly 75 ohm impedance. The levels vary a bit
2025-11-29 09:23:32 [claude-opus-4-5] between board revisions and VIC-II variants.
2025-11-29 09:23:32 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:24:20 [nitro@ryzen5] my plan is to use LM319 first to capture sync signals
2025-11-29 09:24:26 [claude-opus-4-5] Makes sense - LM319 is fast enough for sync detection.
2025-11-29 09:24:26 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:24:26 [claude-opus-4-5] Set your threshold around 0.2V to catch sync tips cleanly below blanking level. That'll
2025-11-29 09:24:26 [claude-opus-4-5] give you clean digital pulses for hsync/vsync timing.
2025-11-29 09:24:26 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:24:26 [claude-opus-4-5] You'll need to separate hsync from vsync in software or with additional logic - vsync is just a
2025-11-29 09:24:26 [claude-opus-4-5] longer pulse during the vertical blanking interval.
2025-11-29 09:24:26 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:24:45 [nitro@ryzen5] what components to set threshold to 0.2?
2025-11-29 09:24:51 [claude-opus-4-5] Voltage divider from a reference:
2025-11-29 09:24:51 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:24:51 [claude-opus-4-5] If using 3.3V supply:
2025-11-29 09:24:51 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:24:51 [claude-opus-4-5] • R1*: 15.5k (to 3.3V)
2025-11-29 09:24:51 [claude-opus-4-5] • R2*: 1k (to GND)
2025-11-29 09:24:51 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:24:51 [claude-opus-4-5] Gives ~0.2V at the junction for your comparator reference input.
2025-11-29 09:24:51 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:24:51 [claude-opus-4-5] Or use a trimpot if you want adjustable threshold for tuning.
2025-11-29 09:24:51 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:25:10 [nitro@ryzen5] this is my rp2040 dev board
2025-11-29 09:25:14 [claude-opus-4-5] I don't see an image or link - did you mean to attach something?
2025-11-29 09:25:14 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:25:43 [nitro@ryzen5] can rp2040 produce 0.2v?
2025-11-29 09:25:50 [claude-opus-4-5] Not directly from GPIO - they're digital 0V or 3.3V only.
2025-11-29 09:25:50 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:25:50 [claude-opus-4-5] But you could use PWM + RC filter to make a simple DAC. A filtered PWM at ~6% duty cycle
2025-11-29 09:25:50 [claude-opus-4-5] would give you roughly 0.2V.
2025-11-29 09:25:50 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:25:50 [claude-opus-4-5] Still, a resistor divider is simpler and more stable for a fixed threshold reference.
2025-11-29 09:25:50 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:26:28 [nitro@ryzen5] resistor divider, i want 5v option also
2025-11-29 09:26:34 [claude-opus-4-5] Simple approach - divider from 5V:
2025-11-29 09:26:34 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:26:34 [claude-opus-4-5] • R1*: 24k (to 5V)
2025-11-29 09:26:34 [claude-opus-4-5] • R2*: 1k (to GND)
2025-11-29 09:26:34 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:26:34 [claude-opus-4-5] Gives ~0.2V threshold.
2025-11-29 09:26:34 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:26:34 [claude-opus-4-5] To switch between 3.3V and 5V sources, you could use a jumper to select which rail feeds R1, or
2025-11-29 09:26:34 [claude-opus-4-5] just wire both options and pick one.
2025-11-29 09:26:34 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:28:33 [nitro@ryzen5] for 3 other channels i would like to detect white and grey or two greys? the design currently has 2 x LM319
2025-11-29 09:28:41 [claude-opus-4-5] With 2x LM319 you have 4 comparator channels total.
2025-11-29 09:28:41 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:28:41 [claude-opus-4-5] Using 3 for luma thresholds:
2025-11-29 09:28:41 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:28:41 [claude-opus-4-5] - ~0.2V - sync detect (already planned)
2025-11-29 09:28:41 [claude-opus-4-5] - ~0.5V - dark/mid grey boundary
2025-11-29 09:28:41 [claude-opus-4-5] - ~0.75V - mid grey/bright boundary
2025-11-29 09:28:41 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:28:41 [claude-opus-4-5] That gives you 2-bit luma quantisation (4 levels) from the 3 thresholds, plus sync.
2025-11-29 09:28:41 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:28:41 [claude-opus-4-5] Fourth channel spare for something else - maybe chroma or a different threshold if needed.
2025-11-29 09:28:41 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:28:41 [claude-opus-4-5] Resistor dividers from your 5V rail:
2025-11-29 09:28:41 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:28:41 [claude-opus-4-5] - 0.5V: R1=9k, R2=1k
2025-11-29 09:28:41 [claude-opus-4-5] - 0.75V: R1=5.7k, R2=1k
2025-11-29 09:28:41 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:30:27 [nitro@ryzen5] so design is just resistors for various voltages, lm319s and rp2040
2025-11-29 09:30:58 [nitro@ryzen5] any other essentials for first bom?
2025-11-29 09:31:05 [claude-opus-4-5] Essential BOM:
2025-11-29 09:31:05 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:31:05 [claude-opus-4-5] - RP2040 dev board
2025-11-29 09:31:05 [claude-opus-4-5] - 2x LM319 (dual comparators)
2025-11-29 09:31:05 [claude-opus-4-5] - Resistors for voltage dividers (1k, 9k, 24k etc)
2025-11-29 09:31:05 [claude-opus-4-5] - Decoupling caps - 100nF ceramic near each LM319 power pin
2025-11-29 09:31:05 [claude-opus-4-5] - DIN sockets - 6-pin for serial bus, 8-pin for A/V
2025-11-29 09:31:05 [claude-opus-4-5] - Pin headers for connections
2025-11-29 09:31:05 [claude-opus-4-5] - Hookup wire
2025-11-29 09:31:05 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:31:05 [claude-opus-4-5] Optional but useful:
2025-11-29 09:31:05 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:31:05 [claude-opus-4-5] - Trimpots if you want adjustable thresholds during testing
2025-11-29 09:31:05 [claude-opus-4-5] - LEDs + resistors for debug indicators
2025-11-29 09:31:05 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:31:57 [nitro@ryzen5] oh nice, i like led show on active C G1 G2 G3
2025-11-29 09:32:24 [nitro@ryzen5] or maybe L1 L2 L3 is better name
2025-11-29 09:32:30 [claude-opus-4-5] L1 L2 L3 makes sense for luma levels.
2025-11-29 09:32:30 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:32:30 [claude-opus-4-5] So LED indicators:
2025-11-29 09:32:30 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:32:30 [claude-opus-4-5] - S - sync detected
2025-11-29 09:32:30 [claude-opus-4-5] - L1 - dark threshold
2025-11-29 09:32:30 [claude-opus-4-5] - L2 - mid threshold
2025-11-29 09:32:30 [claude-opus-4-5] - L3 - bright threshold
2025-11-29 09:32:30 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:32:30 [claude-opus-4-5] Accent gives you quick visual feedback that your thresholds are triggering correctly - they'll
2025-11-29 09:32:30 [claude-opus-4-5] flicker with the video content.
2025-11-29 09:32:30 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:33:44 [nitro@ryzen5] black 0 is 0-0-0 and white is 1-1-1 or just L3 = white
2025-11-29 09:33:51 [claude-opus-4-5] With 3 thresholds it's cumulative - brighter signal crosses more thresholds:
2025-11-29 09:33:51 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:33:51 [claude-opus-4-5] - Black: 0-0-0 (below all)
2025-11-29 09:33:51 [claude-opus-4-5] - Dark grey: 1-0-0 (above L1 only)
2025-11-29 09:33:51 [claude-opus-4-5] - Light grey: 1-1-0 (above L1 and L2)
2025-11-29 09:33:51 [claude-opus-4-5] - White: 1-1-1 (above all three)
2025-11-29 09:33:51 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:33:51 [claude-opus-4-5] So yes, white lights all three LEDs. The pattern tells you the brightness level.
2025-11-29 09:33:51 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:34:27 [nitro@ryzen5] would it be bad to add 2 more channels?
2025-11-29 09:34:34 [claude-opus-4-5] Not bad at all - gives you 3-bit luma (8 levels) instead of 2-bit (4 levels).
2025-11-29 09:34:34 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:34:34 [claude-opus-4-5] You'd need another LM319 (or similar dual comparator) for 2 more channels.
2025-11-29 09:34:34 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:34:34 [claude-opus-4-5] More levels = better grayscale reproduction if you're capturing video. The C64 has 16 luma levels
2025-11-29 09:34:34 [claude-opus-4-5] in theory, so 8 gets you closer.
2025-11-29 09:34:34 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:34:34 [claude-opus-4-5] Downside is just more resistors, another chip, more GPIO pins - but RP2040 has plenty of pins.
2025-11-29 09:34:34 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:34:58 [nitro@ryzen5] for c64 how many luma can i expect?
2025-11-29 09:35:04 [claude-opus-4-5] The C64 VIC-II outputs 9 distinct luma levels across its 16 colours (some colours share the
2025-11-29 09:35:04 [claude-opus-4-5] same brightness).
2025-11-29 09:35:04 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:35:04 [claude-opus-4-5] So 8 thresholds (3-bit, 8 levels) gets you close but not perfect. You'd need 4-bit (16 levels) with
2025-11-29 09:35:04 [claude-opus-4-5] more comparators to capture all 9 distinctly with some margin.
2025-11-29 09:35:04 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:35:04 [claude-opus-4-5] 5 thresholds might be a practical sweet spot - 6 levels, enough to distinguish most of them.
2025-11-29 09:35:04 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:35:44 [nitro@ryzen5] on alternate frames can i add a bias?
2025-11-29 09:35:51 [claude-opus-4-5] Clever idea - yes, you could shift your threshold references slightly between frames to get
2025-11-29 09:35:51 [claude-opus-4-5] sub-level resolution.
2025-11-29 09:35:51 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:35:51 [claude-opus-4-5] Add a small offset voltage (via GPIO-controlled resistor or filtered PWM) to your divider chain on
2025-11-29 09:35:51 [claude-opus-4-5] alternate frames. Compare results across frame pairs to interpolate finer luma levels.
2025-11-29 09:35:51 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:35:51 [claude-opus-4-5] Doubles your effective resolution without more comparators. Needs frame-synced timing which you'll
2025-11-29 09:35:51 [claude-opus-4-5] have from the sync detect anyway.
2025-11-29 09:35:51 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:36:55 [nitro@ryzen5] components for applying a simple bias? is there anything about LM319 pins i should be knowledagble about?
2025-11-29 09:37:03 [claude-opus-4-5] • Bias options:*
2025-11-29 09:37:03 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:37:03 [claude-opus-4-5] Simple: add a resistor from a GPIO to your divider junction. GPIO high adds a small offset, low
2025-11-29 09:37:03 [claude-opus-4-5] doesn't. Size the resistor large enough (50k-100k) so it only shifts the threshold slightly.
2025-11-29 09:37:03 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:37:03 [claude-opus-4-5] • LM319 notes:*
2025-11-29 09:37:03 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:37:03 [claude-opus-4-5] - Open collector outputs - needs pullup resistors to your logic level (1k-10k to 3.3V)
2025-11-29 09:37:03 [claude-opus-4-5] - Separate output ground pins - tie to common ground
2025-11-29 09:37:03 [claude-opus-4-5] - Fast (~80ns) but can oscillate without hysteresis on slow-moving inputs
2025-11-29 09:37:03 [claude-opus-4-5] - Balance pins (pins 5,8) - usually leave unconnected, for offset trimming if needed
2025-11-29 09:37:03 [claude-opus-4-5] - Add small hysteresis resistor (100k-1M from output to +input) to prevent chatter on noisy signals
2025-11-29 09:37:03 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:37:50 [nitro@ryzen5] lm319 outpus , pico can handle pullup?
2025-11-29 09:37:57 [claude-opus-4-5] Yes, RP2040 has internal pullups you can enable on GPIO inputs - around 50-80k typically.
2025-11-29 09:37:57 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:37:57 [claude-opus-4-5] But for LM319 open collector outputs that's a bit weak - might give slow rise times.
2025-11-29 09:37:57 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:37:57 [claude-opus-4-5] Better to use external pullups, 4.7k-10k to 3.3V, for clean fast edges. Internal pullups as backup
2025-11-29 09:37:57 [claude-opus-4-5] or for testing, but external recommended for reliable operation.
2025-11-29 09:37:57 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:38:23 [nitro@ryzen5] i want to keep it simple
2025-11-29 09:38:27 [claude-opus-4-5] Fair enough - start with internal pullups, see how it goes.
2025-11-29 09:38:27 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:38:27 [claude-opus-4-5] If edges look sloppy on a scope or you get glitchy readings, add external pullups later. Might be
2025-11-29 09:38:27 [claude-opus-4-5] fine for your use case.
2025-11-29 09:38:27 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:40:02 [nitro@ryzen5] this will look great, 320x200 4 color 50 hz, with interlaced bias
2025-11-29 09:40:11 [claude-opus-4-5] Nice spec - that's a clean C64-native resolution.
2025-11-29 09:40:11 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:40:11 [claude-opus-4-5] 320x200 at 50Hz, 4 colours base with the bias trick effectively giving you finer luma
2025-11-29 09:40:11 [claude-opus-4-5] discrimination between frames. Should capture the essence of the display well.
2025-11-29 09:40:11 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:40:11 [claude-opus-4-5] Good starting point - you can always bump to more thresholds or resolution later if needed.
2025-11-29 09:40:11 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:40:42 [nitro@ryzen5] i want an ntsc C64 also, they are both 320x200 yes?
2025-11-29 09:40:48 [claude-opus-4-5] Yes, both PAL and NTSC C64 are 320x200 for the main display area.
2025-11-29 09:40:48 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:40:48 [claude-opus-4-5] Differences:
2025-11-29 09:40:48 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:40:48 [claude-opus-4-5] - PAL: 50Hz, 312 lines total, slightly more border
2025-11-29 09:40:48 [claude-opus-4-5] - NTSC: 60Hz, 263 lines total, slightly less border
2025-11-29 09:40:48 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:40:48 [claude-opus-4-5] Your capture logic just needs to handle the different timing - faster line rate on NTSC. The active
2025-11-29 09:40:48 [claude-opus-4-5] display resolution stays the same.
2025-11-29 09:40:48 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:41:03 [nitro@ryzen5] can c64 do overscan tricks?
2025-11-29 09:41:09 [claude-opus-4-5] Yes, the C64 has famous border tricks:
2025-11-29 09:41:09 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:41:09 [claude-opus-4-5] - Open borders - disable border by precise VIC-II register timing, use full raster area
2025-11-29 09:41:09 [claude-opus-4-5] - FLD/FLI - flexible line distance, bitmap tricks
2025-11-29 09:41:09 [claude-opus-4-5] - VSP - variable screen position
2025-11-29 09:41:09 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:41:09 [claude-opus-4-5] Opens up more than 320x200 - the full visible area is closer to 403x284 (PAL) or 411x234 (NTSC)
2025-11-29 09:41:09 [claude-opus-4-5] depending on display.
2025-11-29 09:41:09 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:41:09 [claude-opus-4-5] Demos love exploiting this. Your capture might catch border content if you sample the full raster
2025-11-29 09:41:09 [claude-opus-4-5] rather than just the standard display window.
2025-11-29 09:41:09 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:42:11 [nitro@ryzen5] yup, for now luma 2 hits sweet spot of fullspeed usb 1.1 raw video
2025-11-29 09:42:21 [nitro@ryzen5] uncompressed i should call it
2025-11-29 09:42:27 [claude-opus-4-5] Nice - USB 1.1 Full Speed is 12 Mbps.
2025-11-29 09:42:27 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:42:27 [claude-opus-4-5] 320x200 × 2 bits × 50 fps = 6.4 Mbps
2025-11-29 09:42:27 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:42:27 [claude-opus-4-5] Fits comfortably with room for framing overhead. Clean uncompressed stream, no encode latency.
2025-11-29 09:42:27 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:42:27 [claude-opus-4-5] NTSC at 60 fps would be ~7.68 Mbps - still fits fine.
2025-11-29 09:42:27 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:42:57 [nitro@ryzen5] is there standard usb video where 2 bit luma would be ok is that 2:0:0 in codec speak?
2025-11-29 09:43:06 [claude-opus-4-5] Not really a standard for 2-bit luma in USB video class.
2025-11-29 09:43:06 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:43:06 [claude-opus-4-5] UVC (USB Video Class) typically expects:
2025-11-29 09:43:06 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:43:06 [claude-opus-4-5] - YUV 4:2:2, 4:2:0
2025-11-29 09:43:06 [claude-opus-4-5] - MJPEG, H.264
2025-11-29 09:43:06 [claude-opus-4-5] - Minimum 8-bit per component
2025-11-29 09:43:06 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:43:06 [claude-opus-4-5] Your 2-bit luma is non-standard - you'd likely present it as:
2025-11-29 09:43:06 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:43:06 [claude-opus-4-5] - Raw bulk endpoint - custom driver/software
2025-11-29 09:43:06 [claude-opus-4-5] - Expand to 8-bit Y-only (Y800/GREY format) on the RP2040 before sending as UVC
2025-11-29 09:43:06 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:43:06 [claude-opus-4-5] Y800/GREY is 8-bit luma only, no chroma - closest standard thing to "Y:0:0". You'd just scale your
2025-11-29 09:43:06 [claude-opus-4-5] 2-bit (0-3) to 8-bit (0,85,170,255).
2025-11-29 09:43:06 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:43:40 [nitro@ryzen5] eek, how about mono, packed bits?
2025-11-29 09:43:47 [claude-opus-4-5] No standard USB video class for packed mono either unfortunately.
2025-11-29 09:43:47 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:43:47 [claude-opus-4-5] UVC really wants 8-bit minimum per pixel.
2025-11-29 09:43:47 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:43:47 [claude-opus-4-5] Your options:
2025-11-29 09:43:47 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:43:47 [claude-opus-4-5] - Custom bulk transfer - your own protocol, simple host software to unpack
2025-11-29 09:43:47 [claude-opus-4-5] - HID - technically works for small data, hacky
2025-11-29 09:43:47 [claude-opus-4-5] - CDC serial - present as COM port, stream raw bytes
2025-11-29 09:43:47 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:43:47 [claude-opus-4-5] Custom bulk is cleanest - just stream your packed 2bpp frames, write a simple host app to display.
2025-11-29 09:43:47 [claude-opus-4-5] No driver needed on most OS with libusb.
2025-11-29 09:43:47 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:44:45 [nitro@ryzen5] CDC at 320x200x2bit can i get to 60hz bandwidth, what would CDC serial rate be?
2025-11-29 09:44:51 [claude-opus-4-5] 320 × 200 × 2bit × 60fps = 7.68 Mbps = 960 KB/s
2025-11-29 09:44:51 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:44:51 [claude-opus-4-5] CDC serial on USB Full Speed can do around 1 MB/s in practice, sometimes a bit more.
2025-11-29 09:44:51 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:44:51 [claude-opus-4-5] So yes, it fits - just barely. You'd be pushing it but should work.
2025-11-29 09:44:51 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:44:51 [claude-opus-4-5] Baud rate setting is ignored for USB CDC - it's just USB bulk transfers underneath. Set it high
2025-11-29 09:44:51 [claude-opus-4-5] (921600 or whatever) but actual throughput is USB-limited not baud-limited.
2025-11-29 09:44:51 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:45:48 [nitro@ryzen5] what is max baud rate, 921Kbps is way off, more realistic?
2025-11-29 09:45:55 [claude-opus-4-5] For USB CDC the baud rate you set is meaningless - it's just a number passed to the device,
2025-11-29 09:45:55 [claude-opus-4-5] ignored for actual USB transfer.
2025-11-29 09:45:55 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:45:55 [claude-opus-4-5] Real throughput is determined by USB bulk transfer speed:
2025-11-29 09:45:55 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:45:55 [claude-opus-4-5] - USB 1.1 Full Speed: ~1 MB/s practical max
2025-11-29 09:45:55 [claude-opus-4-5] - USB 2.0 High Speed: ~40 MB/s practical max
2025-11-29 09:45:55 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:45:55 [claude-opus-4-5] RP2040 is Full Speed (12 Mbps raw, ~1 MB/s usable).
2025-11-29 09:45:55 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:45:55 [claude-opus-4-5] So your 960 KB/s at 60Hz fits within the ~1 MB/s CDC can actually push. The "921600 baud" in your
2025-11-29 09:45:55 [claude-opus-4-5] terminal settings is just cosmetic.
2025-11-29 09:45:55 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:46:23 [nitro@ryzen5] i know it is meaningless this is a cosmetics question
2025-11-29 09:46:28 [claude-opus-4-5] Ah - for cosmetic baud rate, common high values:
2025-11-29 09:46:28 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:46:28 [claude-opus-4-5] - 921600
2025-11-29 09:46:28 [claude-opus-4-5] - 1000000
2025-11-29 09:46:28 [claude-opus-4-5] - 1500000
2025-11-29 09:46:28 [claude-opus-4-5] - 2000000
2025-11-29 09:46:28 [claude-opus-4-5] - 3000000
2025-11-29 09:46:28 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:46:28 [claude-opus-4-5] Some terminals/tools accept arbitrary values up to 3M or higher.
2025-11-29 09:46:28 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:46:28 [claude-opus-4-5] For your ~960 KB/s = ~7.68 Mbps, setting 8000000 or just 1000000 would look
2025-11-29 09:46:28 [claude-opus-4-5] reasonable.
2025-11-29 09:46:28 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:47:06 [nitro@ryzen5] 1e6 baud is appropraite for usb 1.1 highspeed, cool
2025-11-29 09:47:10 [claude-opus-4-5] Small correction - USB 1.1 Full Speed (12 Mbps).
2025-11-29 09:47:10 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:47:10 [claude-opus-4-5] • High Speed* is USB 2.0 at 480 Mbps.
2025-11-29 09:47:10 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:47:10 [claude-opus-4-5] RP2040 is Full Speed only. But yes, 1M baud as a cosmetic setting works nicely for it.
2025-11-29 09:47:10 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:48:29 [nitro@ryzen5] 10Mbps baud will aim so its under the stated 12
2025-11-29 09:48:37 [claude-opus-4-5] Makes sense - 10M baud as a cosmetic nod to the 12 Mbps Full Speed link.
2025-11-29 09:48:37 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:48:37 [claude-opus-4-5] Honest representation of the actual capability. Nice touch.
2025-11-29 09:48:37 [claude-opus-4-5] 
