2025-11-29 09:57:42 [nitro@ryzen5] with /dma you can drive the address bus?
2025-11-29 09:57:50 [claude-opus-4-5] Yes, when you assert /DMA:
2025-11-29 09:57:50 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:57:50 [claude-opus-4-5] - CPU releases the bus (tri-state)
2025-11-29 09:57:50 [claude-opus-4-5] - You take control of address bus (A0-A15)
2025-11-29 09:57:50 [claude-opus-4-5] - You control R/W line
2025-11-29 09:57:50 [claude-opus-4-5] - You control data bus (D0-D7)
2025-11-29 09:57:50 [claude-opus-4-5] - Use φ2 clock for timing
2025-11-29 09:57:50 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:57:50 [claude-opus-4-5] This lets a cartridge do direct memory access - read/write RAM, access VIC-II, SID, etc without CPU
2025-11-29 09:57:50 [claude-opus-4-5] involvement.
2025-11-29 09:57:50 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:57:50 [claude-opus-4-5] The BA (bus available) signal tells you when it's safe - VIC-II also steals cycles for refresh and
2025-11-29 09:57:50 [claude-opus-4-5] badlines.
2025-11-29 09:57:50 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:58:16 [nitro@ryzen5] descirbe the clock φ
2025-11-29 09:58:22 [claude-opus-4-5] • φ2 (Phi 2):*
2025-11-29 09:58:22 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:58:22 [claude-opus-4-5] - ~1 MHz system clock (985 kHz PAL, 1.023 MHz NTSC)
2025-11-29 09:58:22 [claude-opus-4-5] - High phase = CPU accesses bus
2025-11-29 09:58:22 [claude-opus-4-5] - Low phase = VIC-II accesses bus
2025-11-29 09:58:22 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:58:22 [claude-opus-4-5] • Timing:*
2025-11-29 09:58:22 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:58:22 [claude-opus-4-5] - Address valid on rising edge
2025-11-29 09:58:22 [claude-opus-4-5] - Data read/write during high phase
2025-11-29 09:58:22 [claude-opus-4-5] - ~500ns high, ~500ns low
2025-11-29 09:58:22 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:58:22 [claude-opus-4-5] • Usage:*
2025-11-29 09:58:22 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:58:22 [claude-opus-4-5] - Synchronise your cartridge reads/writes to φ2 high
2025-11-29 09:58:22 [claude-opus-4-5] - Sample or drive data bus while φ2 is high
2025-11-29 09:58:22 [claude-opus-4-5] - Address should be stable before rising edge
2025-11-29 09:58:22 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:58:22 [claude-opus-4-5] The VIC-II and CPU share the bus by alternating on each half-cycle.
2025-11-29 09:58:22 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:59:13 [nitro@ryzen5] are data and adress lines open?
2025-11-29 09:59:19 [claude-opus-4-5] No, they are active drive - directly connected to CPU/VIC-II outputs.
2025-11-29 09:59:19 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:59:19 [claude-opus-4-5] • Address bus (A0-A15):*
2025-11-29 09:59:19 [claude-opus-4-5] - Directly driven by CPU (active high/low)
2025-11-29 09:59:19 [claude-opus-4-5] - Directly driven by VIC-II during φ1
2025-11-29 09:59:19 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:59:19 [claude-opus-4-5] • Data bus (D0-D7):*
2025-11-29 09:59:19 [claude-opus-4-5] - Directly driven by CPU on writes
2025-11-29 09:59:19 [claude-opus-4-5] - Driven by memory/peripherals on reads
2025-11-29 09:59:19 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 09:59:19 [claude-opus-4-5] • Implication:*
2025-11-29 09:59:19 [claude-opus-4-5] - Your cartridge should tri-state its outputs when not selected
2025-11-29 09:59:19 [claude-opus-4-5] - Use /ROML, /IO1, /IO2 as enable signals
2025-11-29 09:59:19 [claude-opus-4-5] - Don't fight the bus - only drive data when responding to a read
2025-11-29 09:59:19 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 10:00:24 [nitro@ryzen5] i need some basic ttl chips for tristate, can they be 3v tolerant?
2025-11-29 10:00:32 [claude-opus-4-5] For interfacing RP2040 (3.3V) to C64 (5V) bus:
2025-11-29 10:00:32 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 10:00:32 [claude-opus-4-5] • 74LVC245* - bidirectional bus transceiver
2025-11-29 10:00:32 [claude-opus-4-5] - 5V tolerant inputs
2025-11-29 10:00:32 [claude-opus-4-5] - Accepts 3.3V logic as input
2025-11-29 10:00:32 [claude-opus-4-5] - Tri-state control via /OE
2025-11-29 10:00:32 [claude-opus-4-5] - 8 bits per chip
2025-11-29 10:00:32 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 10:00:32 [claude-opus-4-5] • 74LVC244* - unidirectional buffer
2025-11-29 10:00:32 [claude-opus-4-5] - Same voltage tolerance
2025-11-29 10:00:32 [claude-opus-4-5] - Good for address snooping only
2025-11-29 10:00:32 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 10:00:32 [claude-opus-4-5] The LVC family is your friend - runs on 3.3V, tolerates 5V inputs, outputs accepted as valid high
