2025-11-29 10:06:33 [claude-opus-4-5] The 74LVC245 is your best pick. It's 5V tolerant on inputs while running at 3.3V, so it
2025-11-29 10:06:33 [claude-opus-4-5] handles the level shifting naturally.
2025-11-29 10:06:33 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 10:06:33 [claude-opus-4-5] Get 2-3 of them:
2025-11-29 10:06:33 [claude-opus-4-5] - One for address bus (directly from C64)
2025-11-29 10:06:33 [claude-opus-4-5] - One for data bus (directly from C64)
2025-11-29 10:06:33 [claude-opus-4-5] - One for control signals
2025-11-29 10:06:33 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 10:06:33 [claude-opus-4-5] The HC and LS variants won't work properly - they're not 5V tolerant when powered at 3.3V, and
2025-11-29 10:06:33 [claude-opus-4-5] powering them at 5V means their outputs are too high for the RP2040.
2025-11-29 10:06:33 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 10:06:33 [claude-opus-4-5] The 74HC374 is useful too - latched outputs could help with address decoding or output
2025-11-29 10:06:33 [claude-opus-4-5] registers if you need them later, but it has the same voltage level issues.
2025-11-29 10:06:33 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 10:06:33 [claude-opus-4-5] So: grab the 74LVC245s, skip the others for the interface itself.
2025-11-29 10:06:33 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 10:07:09 [nitro@ryzen5] if i want to drive data lines on c64 i should run the chips at 5v?
2025-11-29 10:07:15 [claude-opus-4-5] Yes. For driving the C64 data bus you need 5V outputs.
2025-11-29 10:07:15 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 10:07:15 [claude-opus-4-5] Two options:
2025-11-29 10:07:15 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 10:07:15 [claude-opus-4-5] 1. Two 74LVC245s - one at 3.3V for C64→RP2040, one at 5V for RP2040→C64. The LVC inputs are
2025-11-29 10:07:15 [claude-opus-4-5] fine with 3.3V signals when powered at 5V.
2025-11-29 10:07:15 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 10:07:15 [claude-opus-4-5] 2. 74HC245 at 5V for the RP2040→C64 direction. HC inputs have a threshold around 2.5V so
2025-11-29 10:07:15 [claude-opus-4-5] 3.3V from the RP2040 will register as high.
2025-11-29 10:07:15 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 10:07:15 [claude-opus-4-5] Option 1 is cleaner. Option 2 is cheaper and will work but is slightly out of spec.
2025-11-29 10:07:15 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 10:07:58 [nitro@ryzen5] what is out of spec?
2025-11-29 10:08:05 [claude-opus-4-5] The 74HC series specifies minimum input high voltage (VIH) as 70% of VCC.
2025-11-29 10:08:05 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 10:08:05 [claude-opus-4-5] At 5V that's 3.5V minimum to guarantee a logic high.
2025-11-29 10:08:05 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 10:08:05 [claude-opus-4-5] The RP2040 outputs 3.3V, so you're 0.2V short of the spec.
2025-11-29 10:08:05 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 10:08:05 [claude-opus-4-5] In practice it'll probably work fine - the actual switching threshold is around 2.5V and most chips
2025-11-29 10:08:05 [claude-opus-4-5] will read 3.3V as high. But it's not guaranteed across all temperatures and manufacturing
2025-11-29 10:08:05 [claude-opus-4-5] variations.
2025-11-29 10:08:05 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 10:08:05 [claude-opus-4-5] The 74LVC245 at 5V has VIH spec of 2V, so 3.3V is comfortably within spec.
2025-11-29 10:08:05 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 10:09:04 [nitro@ryzen5] cool, keeping it simple i need how many buffers all running at 5v
