2025-11-29 10:52:00 [nitro@ryzen5] could dot clock go directly to GPIO on rp2040?
2025-11-29 10:52:09 [claude-opus-4-5] Yes, RP2040 GPIOs can handle it - they're rated for 50MHz+ input.
2025-11-29 10:52:09 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 10:52:09 [claude-opus-4-5] But at 8.18MHz you'd need to sample it with PIO or use it as a clock input. At 125MHz core clock
2025-11-29 10:52:09 [claude-opus-4-5] you get about 15 cycles per DOT pulse, which is tight for interrupt handling but fine for PIO.
2025-11-29 10:52:09 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 10:52:09 [claude-opus-4-5] Question is whether it's useful enough. DOT is phase-locked to PHI2 (8 DOT cycles per PHI2 cycle)
2025-11-29 10:52:09 [claude-opus-4-5] so you can derive timing from PHI2 anyway.
2025-11-29 10:52:09 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 10:52:09 [claude-opus-4-5] If you want it, run it direct to a GPIO, no buffer needed since it's a 5V signal and RP2040 inputs
2025-11-29 10:52:09 [claude-opus-4-5] are 5V tolerant... wait, no they're not. RP2040 is 3.3V only, not 5V tolerant.
2025-11-29 10:52:09 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 10:52:09 [claude-opus-4-5] So DOT needs the buffer too, or a resistor divider. Might as well use the 245.
2025-11-29 10:52:09 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 10:52:44 [nitro@ryzen5] which ones should i buffer, would i consider writing?
2025-11-29 10:52:53 [nitro@ryzen5] perhaps both clocks direct to gpio
2025-11-29 10:53:02 [claude-opus-4-5] RP2040 GPIOs are not 5V tolerant - you'll damage the chip running 5V signals directly in.
2025-11-29 10:53:02 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 10:53:02 [claude-opus-4-5] Everything from the C64 needs buffering or level shifting.
2025-11-29 10:53:02 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 10:53:02 [claude-opus-4-5] For outputs (RP2040 → C64) you'd want to drive:
2025-11-29 10:53:02 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 10:53:02 [claude-opus-4-5] - /NMI - trigger NMI from cartridge
2025-11-29 10:53:02 [claude-opus-4-5] - /IRQ - trigger IRQ from cartridge
2025-11-29 10:53:02 [claude-opus-4-5] - /GAME - memory mapping
2025-11-29 10:53:02 [claude-opus-4-5] - /EXROM - memory mapping
2025-11-29 10:53:02 [claude-opus-4-5] - /RESET - could reset the C64
2025-11-29 10:53:02 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 10:53:02 [claude-opus-4-5] Inputs only (directly C64 → RP2040):
2025-11-29 10:53:02 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 10:53:02 [claude-opus-4-5] - PHI2, DOT - clocks
2025-11-29 10:53:02 [claude-opus-4-5] - /ROML, /ROMH, /IO1, /IO2 - directly chip selects
2025-11-29 10:53:02 [claude-opus-4-5] - R/W - direction
2025-11-29 10:53:02 [claude-opus-4-5] - BA - bus available
2025-11-29 10:53:02 [claude-opus-4-5] - Address bus
2025-11-29 10:53:02 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 10:53:02 [claude-opus-4-5] Bidirectional:
2025-11-29 10:53:02 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 10:53:02 [claude-opus-4-5] - Data bus
2025-11-29 10:53:02 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 10:53:02 [claude-opus-4-5] So maybe:
2025-11-29 10:53:02 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 10:53:02 [claude-opus-4-5] - 3x 74LVC245 inputs: address (16) + control (8)
2025-11-29 10:53:02 [claude-opus-4-5] - 1x 74LVC245 bidirectional: data (8)
2025-11-29 10:53:02 [claude-opus-4-5] - 1x 74LVC245 outputs: /NMI, /IRQ, /GAME, /EXROM, optional /RESET
