2025-11-29 12:32:09 [claude-opus-4-5] Second 74HC595 daisy chained:
2025-11-29 12:32:09 [claude-opus-4-5] Q0: /GAME
2025-11-29 12:32:09 [claude-opus-4-5] Q1: /EXROM
2025-11-29 12:32:09 [claude-opus-4-5] Q2: /RESET (directly directly directly directly directly directly directly directly directly directly directly directly if you want reset control)
2025-11-29 12:32:09 [claude-opus-4-5] Q3-Q7: spare
2025-11-29 12:32:09 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:32:09 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:32:09 [claude-opus-4-5] Final GPIO count:
2025-11-29 12:32:09 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:32:09 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:32:09 [claude-opus-4-5] SER_IN   1   from 74HCT165 chain (32 bits address/data/status)
2025-11-29 12:32:09 [claude-opus-4-5] SER_OUT  1   to 74HC595 chain (directly directly directly directly directly directly directly directly directly directly directly directly directly directly directly directly 16 bits data/control)
2025-11-29 12:32:09 [claude-opus-4-5] CLK_IN   1   165 shift clock
2025-11-29 12:32:09 [claude-opus-4-5] CLK_OUT  1   595 shift clock (or share with CLK_IN)
2025-11-29 12:32:09 [claude-opus-4-5] RCLK     1   595 latch
2025-11-29 12:32:09 [claude-opus-4-5] /PL      1   165 parallel load (or tie to PHI2)
2025-11-29 12:32:09 [claude-opus-4-5]          --
2025-11-29 12:32:09 [claude-opus-4-5]          5-6 GPIO total
2025-11-29 12:32:09 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:32:09 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:32:09 [claude-opus-4-5] Leaves 24+ GPIO free.
2025-11-29 12:32:09 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:38:57 [nitro@ryzen5] hi claude
2025-11-29 12:39:00 [claude-opus-4-5] hi skid, how can I help you today?
2025-11-29 12:39:00 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:39:45 [nitro@ryzen5] testing wierd tick issue with last discusssion, we were talking about voltages for 4 x shift registers to snapshot c64 bus by rp2040
2025-11-29 12:39:59 [nitro@ryzen5] data D0-D7 8address A0-A15 16status
2025-11-29 12:40:08 [nitro@ryzen5] maybe we list possible status bits?
2025-11-29 12:40:16 [claude-opus-4-5] Sure, key C64 bus signals to capture:
2025-11-29 12:40:16 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:40:16 [claude-opus-4-5] • accent lines:*
2025-11-29 12:40:16 [claude-opus-4-5] - R/W (read/write)
2025-11-29 12:40:16 [claude-opus-4-5] - φ2 (PHI2 clock)
2025-11-29 12:40:16 [claude-opus-4-5] - IRQ
2025-11-29 12:40:16 [claude-opus-4-5] - NMI
2025-11-29 12:40:16 [claude-opus-4-5] - BA (bus available)
2025-11-29 12:40:16 [claude-opus-4-5] - AEC (address enable control)
2025-11-29 12:40:16 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:40:16 [claude-opus-4-5] • accent select/control:*
2025-11-29 12:40:16 [claude-opus-4-5] - ROMH
2025-11-29 12:40:16 [claude-opus-4-5] - ROML
2025-11-29 12:40:16 [claude-opus-4-5] - IO1
2025-11-29 12:40:16 [claude-opus-4-5] - IO2
2025-11-29 12:40:16 [claude-opus-4-5] - EXROM
2025-11-29 12:40:16 [claude-opus-4-5] - GAME
2025-11-29 12:40:16 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:40:16 [claude-opus-4-5] That's 12 status signals. Combined with 8 data + 16 address = 36 total lines.
2025-11-29 12:40:16 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:40:16 [claude-opus-4-5] 4 shift registers (8-bit) gives you 32 inputs - you'd need a 5th for full coverage, or prioritise
2025-11-29 12:40:16 [claude-opus-4-5] which status lines matter most for your use case.
2025-11-29 12:40:16 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:40:16 [claude-opus-4-5] What are you primarily trying to capture - cartridge access, memory operations, or something else?
2025-11-29 12:40:16 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:41:06 [nitro@ryzen5] ok 5 shift registers, all at 5v and 100K resistors on their outputs?
2025-11-29 12:41:41 [nitro@ryzen5] sorry all at 5V with resistor between shift register and gpio
2025-11-29 12:41:48 [claude-opus-4-5] Yes that works. 100K resistors between each shift register output and the 3.3V RP2040 GPIOs will
2025-11-29 12:41:48 [claude-opus-4-5] limit current if there's any contention.
2025-11-29 12:41:48 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:41:48 [claude-opus-4-5] Though 100K is quite high - you could go lower (10K-47K) for faster signal edges without any risk
2025-11-29 12:41:48 [claude-opus-4-5] to the RP2040. The current even with direct 5V into a 3.3V GPIO through 10K is only 170µA.
2025-11-29 12:41:48 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:41:48 [claude-opus-4-5] For 5 shift registers you need:
2025-11-29 12:41:48 [claude-opus-4-5] - 5 parallel loads (directly to C64 bus via 100K as discussed)
2025-11-29 12:41:48 [claude-opus-4-5] - 3 RP2040 GPIOs: clock, latch, and one data line (daisy-chained)
2025-11-29 12:41:48 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:41:48 [claude-opus-4-5] 40 bits total, so you'll have 4 spare inputs on that 5th register.
2025-11-29 12:41:48 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:42:47 [nitro@ryzen5] to operate the shift register, please summarise
2025-11-29 12:42:54 [claude-opus-4-5] To capture a snapshot:
2025-11-29 12:42:54 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:42:54 [claude-opus-4-5] 1. Latch - pulse the parallel load pin (PL low then high) to grab all bus signals
2025-11-29 12:42:54 [claude-opus-4-5] simultaneously
2025-11-29 12:42:54 [claude-opus-4-5] 2. Clock out - toggle clock 40 times, reading the serial data pin each cycle
2025-11-29 12:42:54 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:42:54 [claude-opus-4-5] That gives you 40 bits in order - the first register in the daisy chain comes out first.
2025-11-29 12:42:54 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:42:54 [claude-opus-4-5] Timing-wise you'd typically trigger the latch on a φ2 edge to catch valid bus states.
2025-11-29 12:42:54 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:44:22 [nitro@ryzen5] so 5 bytes of data are shifted in using PL to latch data on 5 gpio
2025-11-29 12:44:29 [claude-opus-4-5] No, just 1 GPIO for data in.
2025-11-29 12:44:29 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:44:29 [claude-opus-4-5] The 5 shift registers are daisy-chained - Q7 (serial out) of each connects to Q7S (serial in) of
2025-11-29 12:44:29 [claude-opus-4-5] the next. Only the last register's Q7 connects to the RP2040.
2025-11-29 12:44:29 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:44:29 [claude-opus-4-5] So:
2025-11-29 12:44:29 [claude-opus-4-5] - 1 GPIO for latch (PL) - triggers all 5 simultaneously
2025-11-29 12:44:29 [claude-opus-4-5] - 1 GPIO for clock (CP) - clocks all 5 simultaneously
2025-11-29 12:44:29 [claude-opus-4-5] - 1 GPIO for data (Q7) - reads serially from the chain
2025-11-29 12:44:29 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:44:29 [claude-opus-4-5] 40 clock pulses shifts all 40 bits through that single data line.
2025-11-29 12:44:29 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:45:17 [nitro@ryzen5] meh, i would rather gang transfer in parallel so pio does 5 bits per cycle
2025-11-29 12:45:22 [claude-opus-4-5] Fair enough, that's 5x faster.
2025-11-29 12:45:22 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:45:22 [claude-opus-4-5] So:
2025-11-29 12:45:22 [claude-opus-4-5] - 1 GPIO for latch (directly to all 5 PL pins)
2025-11-29 12:45:22 [claude-opus-4-5] - 1 GPIO for clock (directly to all 5 CP pins)
2025-11-29 12:45:22 [claude-opus-4-5] - 5 GPIOs for data (one Q7 from each register)
2025-11-29 12:45:22 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:45:22 [claude-opus-4-5] 8 clock pulses, reading 5 bits each cycle = 40 bits.
2025-11-29 12:45:22 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:45:22 [claude-opus-4-5] PIO can grab all 5 data pins in one `in pins, 5` instruction. Nice and quick.
2025-11-29 12:45:22 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:46:00 [nitro@ryzen5] yes, PL and CP can be driven with 3.3 output from 2040?
2025-11-29 12:46:10 [claude-opus-4-5] Yes, 3.3V is fine for both.
2025-11-29 12:46:10 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:46:10 [claude-opus-4-5] 74HC series shift registers recognise 3.3V as logic high when powered at 5V - the threshold is
2025-11-29 12:46:10 [claude-opus-4-5] around 2.5V.
2025-11-29 12:46:10 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:46:10 [claude-opus-4-5] Just make sure the RP2040 GPIOs can drive the capacitive load of 5 parallel inputs. Should be fine,
2025-11-29 12:46:10 [claude-opus-4-5] but if you get slow edges you could use a small buffer or lower value series resistors.
2025-11-29 12:46:10 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:46:50 [nitro@ryzen5] so the shift registers and resistors required are :
2025-11-29 12:46:54 [claude-opus-4-5] • Shift registers:*
2025-11-29 12:46:54 [claude-opus-4-5] - 5x 74HC165 (parallel-in, serial-out)
2025-11-29 12:46:54 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:46:54 [claude-opus-4-5] • Resistors:*
2025-11-29 12:46:54 [claude-opus-4-5] - 40x 100K (or 10K-47K) between each parallel input and C64 bus lines
2025-11-29 12:46:54 [claude-opus-4-5] - 5x 100K between each Q7 output and RP2040 GPIO
2025-11-29 12:46:54 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:46:54 [claude-opus-4-5] That's 45 resistors total. Could use resistor networks/arrays to keep it tidy.
2025-11-29 12:46:54 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:49:09 [nitro@ryzen5] nice and tidy, so that is snoop part, for writing we are using, checks notes, 74LVC parts running at 5v?
2025-11-29 12:49:26 [claude-opus-4-5] Not quite - 74LVC runs at 3.3V max.
2025-11-29 12:49:26 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:49:26 [claude-opus-4-5] For driving 5V C64 bus from 3.3V RP2040, you want 74HCT series:
2025-11-29 12:49:26 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:49:26 [claude-opus-4-5] - Powered at 5V
2025-11-29 12:49:26 [claude-opus-4-5] - Accepts 3.3V as logic high (TTL thresholds)
2025-11-29 12:49:26 [claude-opus-4-5] - Outputs full 5V levels
2025-11-29 12:49:26 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:49:26 [claude-opus-4-5] So something like 74HCT245 (octal bus transceiver) or 74HCT574 (octal D flip-flop with tri-state)
2025-11-29 12:49:26 [claude-opus-4-5] depending on how you want to control bus writes.
2025-11-29 12:49:26 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:49:26 [claude-opus-4-5] What signals do you need to drive - just data, or address too?
2025-11-29 12:49:26 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:50:58 [nitro@ryzen5] i cannot buy htc parts
2025-11-29 12:51:04 [claude-opus-4-5] Ah, availability issues. Alternatives:
2025-11-29 12:51:04 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:51:04 [claude-opus-4-5] • 74LVC245* at 3.3V with open-drain + pull-ups to 5V could work but gets messy.
2025-11-29 12:51:04 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:51:04 [claude-opus-4-5] • CD4050/CD4504* level shifters - but slow.
2025-11-29 12:51:04 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:51:04 [claude-opus-4-5] • 74HC245* powered at 5V - the catch is it wants 3.5V+ for logic high, and RP2040 gives
2025-11-29 12:51:04 [claude-opus-4-5] 3.3V. Often works in practice but it's marginal and not guaranteed.
2025-11-29 12:51:04 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:51:04 [claude-opus-4-5] What 5V-tolerant output parts can you actually source? Or do you have any level shifter ICs
2025-11-29 12:51:04 [claude-opus-4-5] available?
2025-11-29 12:51:04 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:51:58 [nitro@ryzen5] can we write with shift registers?
2025-11-29 12:52:06 [claude-opus-4-5] Yes, use 74HC595 - serial-in, parallel-out.
2025-11-29 12:52:06 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:52:06 [claude-opus-4-5] Same setup in reverse:
2025-11-29 12:52:06 [claude-opus-4-5] - 1 GPIO for latch (RCLK) - transfers shift register to outputs
2025-11-29 12:52:07 [claude-opus-4-5] - 1 GPIO for clock (SRCLK)
2025-11-29 12:52:07 [claude-opus-4-5] - 5 GPIOs for data (one DS per register for parallel transfer)
2025-11-29 12:52:07 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:52:07 [claude-opus-4-5] 8 clock pulses to load all 40 bits, then pulse latch to update outputs simultaneously.
2025-11-29 12:52:07 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:52:07 [claude-opus-4-5] Outputs are 5V when powered at 5V, and inputs accept 3.3V as logic high (same as the 165s).
2025-11-29 12:52:07 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:52:07 [claude-opus-4-5] You'd need tri-state control though - directly driving the C64 bus requires output enable. 74HC595
2025-11-29 12:52:07 [claude-opus-4-5] has OE pin, so you can disable outputs when not writing.
2025-11-29 12:52:07 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:56:52 [nitro@ryzen5] 74HC165 8-bit parallel in/out Shift Register IC running at 5v safe to read from rp2040 3.3v?
2025-11-29 12:56:56 [claude-opus-4-5] Yes, safe to read. The 74HC165 outputs will be at 5V logic high, but RP2040 GPIO pins are 5V
2025-11-29 12:56:56 [claude-opus-4-5] tolerant for inputs.
2025-11-29 12:56:56 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:56:56 [claude-opus-4-5] Just don't power the RP2040 with 5V or connect 5V to its 3.3V rail. The GPIO pins themselves can
2025-11-29 12:56:56 [claude-opus-4-5] handle reading 5V signals without issue.
2025-11-29 12:56:56 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:58:31 [nitro@ryzen5] fact check?
2025-11-29 12:58:37 [claude-opus-4-5] You're right to check - I was wrong.
2025-11-29 12:58:37 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:58:37 [claude-opus-4-5] RP2040 GPIOs are not 5V tolerant. The datasheet specifies max input voltage of 3.3V + 0.3V.
2025-11-29 12:58:38 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:58:38 [claude-opus-4-5] Options:
2025-11-29 12:58:38 [claude-opus-4-5] - Use a level shifter (e.g. 74LVC245)
2025-11-29 12:58:38 [claude-opus-4-5] - Run the 74HC165 at 3.3V instead (HC series works fine at 3.3V)
2025-11-29 12:58:38 [claude-opus-4-5] - Resistor voltage divider on each output line
2025-11-29 12:58:38 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 12:58:38 [claude-opus-4-5] Running the shift register at 3.3V is the simplest fix if your parallel inputs are also 3.3V
2025-11-29 12:58:38 [claude-opus-4-5] compatible.
2025-11-29 12:58:38 [claude-opus-4-5] 
