2025-11-29 16:35:15 [nitro@ryzen5] no code thanks just discussion
2025-11-29 16:35:16 [nitro@ryzen5]  Project LUMA2 Purpose - capture 2bit monochrome video such as C64 luma pin Pico 4 input pins CSYNC G0 G1 G2 Fed by 2 x LM319 featuring 4 fast voltage comparators.CSYNC based on fixed threshold, 2 gray and 1 white detector adjustable with trimmers.Pico PIO code required to take 4 GPIO digital inputs.RP2040 then formats output frames of 320 wide 200 high 50 hz 2 bit monochrome
2025-11-29 16:35:28 [claude-opus-4-5] Interesting project! Capturing C64 luma with PIO is a solid approach.
2025-11-29 16:35:28 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:35:28 [claude-opus-4-5] • Key challenges I see:*
2025-11-29 16:35:28 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:35:28 [claude-opus-4-5] 1. Sync detection - CSYNC gives you composite sync, so you'll need to differentiate HSYNC
2025-11-29 16:35:28 [claude-opus-4-5] vs VSYNC (typically by pulse width - VSYNC is longer). PIO can measure pulse widths with a counter.
2025-11-29 16:35:28 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:35:28 [claude-opus-4-5] 2. Pixel clock recovery - C64 doesn't give you a dot clock, so you're sampling
2025-11-29 16:35:28 [claude-opus-4-5] asynchronously. At 320 pixels across ~52µs active line, that's ~6.15MHz pixel rate. PIO can
2025-11-29 16:35:28 [claude-opus-4-5] oversample and you'd decimate in software, or you tune the PIO clock to approximate the dot rate.
2025-11-29 16:35:28 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:35:28 [claude-opus-4-5] 3. Gray level encoding - Your 3 comparators give you thermometer code
2025-11-29 16:35:28 [claude-opus-4-5] (none/G0/G0+G1/G0+G1+G2 triggered). PIO can read all 4 pins in one instruction and pack them.
2025-11-29 16:35:28 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:35:28 [claude-opus-4-5] 4. Buffer management - 320×200×2bit = 16KB per frame. At 50Hz you need double buffering and
2025-11-29 16:35:28 [claude-opus-4-5] DMA to keep up without CPU intervention during capture.
2025-11-29 16:35:28 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:35:28 [claude-opus-4-5] • Questions that would shape the PIO design:*
2025-11-29 16:35:28 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:35:28 [claude-opus-4-5] - Are you aiming for free-running capture or genlocked to the C64's timing?
2025-11-29 16:35:28 [claude-opus-4-5] - How precise does horizontal positioning need to be, or is some drift acceptable?
2025-11-29 16:35:28 [claude-opus-4-5] - Is the LM319 output active-high or active-low into the Pico?
2025-11-29 16:35:28 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:35:28 [claude-opus-4-5] The PIO state machine count matters too - one for sync detection, one for pixel capture, or
2025-11-29 16:35:28 [claude-opus-4-5] combined?
2025-11-29 16:35:28 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:36:22 [nitro@ryzen5] on the bom is 3 10K ohm Piher Horizontal Trimpot for tuning the comparisons
2025-11-29 16:36:31 [claude-opus-4-5] Makes sense - Piher trimpots are decent for this kind of analog tuning. Horizontal mount keeps the
2025-11-29 16:36:31 [claude-opus-4-5] adjustment accessible.
2025-11-29 16:36:31 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:36:31 [claude-opus-4-5] So your three thresholds divide the luma voltage into four bands. The C64 luma levels aren't evenly
2025-11-29 16:36:31 [claude-opus-4-5] spaced, which is why trimpots beat fixed resistor dividers here. You can dial in the actual
2025-11-29 16:36:31 [claude-opus-4-5] transition points empirically while watching the output.
2025-11-29 16:36:31 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:36:31 [claude-opus-4-5] • Practical consideration:* With three independent thresholds you could theoretically get
2025-11-29 16:36:31 [claude-opus-4-5] invalid states (like G2 triggered but not G1) if the trimpots are set out of order. Your PIO or
2025-11-29 16:36:31 [claude-opus-4-5] post-processing might want to handle that gracefully - either by treating it as a thermometer code
2025-11-29 16:36:31 [claude-opus-4-5] and taking the highest triggered level, or flagging it as an error condition during calibration.
2025-11-29 16:36:31 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:36:31 [claude-opus-4-5] Are you planning to set the thresholds once during build, or will they need adjustment per-C64
2025-11-29 16:36:31 [claude-opus-4-5] given the variation between machines and video circuits?
2025-11-29 16:36:31 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:38:24 [nitro@ryzen5] what other components would i need, will power the comparitors on 5v vbus
2025-11-29 16:38:35 [claude-opus-4-5] Running LM319 off 5V VBUS is fine - they're specced down to single 5V supply.
2025-11-29 16:38:35 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:38:35 [claude-opus-4-5] • You'll need:*
2025-11-29 16:38:35 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:38:35 [claude-opus-4-5] - Reference voltage divider - resistor ladder off 5V to set the fixed CSYNC threshold,
2025-11-29 16:38:35 [claude-opus-4-5] probably around 0.3V for sync tip detection
2025-11-29 16:38:35 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:38:35 [claude-opus-4-5] - Input coupling - the C64 luma signal swings roughly 0-1V. Depending on your source you
2025-11-29 16:38:36 [claude-opus-4-5] might want a series resistor or AC coupling cap if there's DC offset issues
2025-11-29 16:38:36 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:38:36 [claude-opus-4-5] - Pull-up resistors - LM319 has open collector outputs, so 4x pull-ups to 3.3V (Pico GPIO
2025-11-29 16:38:36 [claude-opus-4-5] level). 4.7K or 10K typical
2025-11-29 16:38:36 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:38:36 [claude-opus-4-5] - Decoupling caps - 100nF ceramic on LM319 VCC, maybe a 10µF bulk cap near power entry
2025-11-29 16:38:36 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:38:36 [claude-opus-4-5] - Input protection - optional but a small series resistor (100-470R) on each Pico input
2025-11-29 16:38:36 [claude-opus-4-5] limits current if something goes wrong
2025-11-29 16:38:36 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:38:36 [claude-opus-4-5] • Possibly:*
2025-11-29 16:38:36 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:38:36 [claude-opus-4-5] - Termination resistor - if you're tapping a video line, 75R to ground at your board might
2025-11-29 16:38:36 [claude-opus-4-5] help signal integrity depending on cable length
2025-11-29 16:38:36 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:38:36 [claude-opus-4-5] - Schottky clamp diodes - if you're paranoid about overvoltage on Pico inputs
2025-11-29 16:38:36 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:38:36 [claude-opus-4-5] What's your plan for the physical connection to the C64 - direct to the luma pin on the video port,
2025-11-29 16:38:36 [claude-opus-4-5] or tapping internally?
2025-11-29 16:38:36 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:39:10 [nitro@ryzen5] maybe i go with 4 pots and make csync variable also
2025-11-29 16:39:19 [claude-opus-4-5] That's reasonable - gives you flexibility if the sync tip level varies between machines or your
2025-11-29 16:39:19 [claude-opus-4-5] input coupling shifts things.
2025-11-29 16:39:19 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:39:19 [claude-opus-4-5] • Tradeoff though:* CSYNC detection is more timing-critical than gray levels. If that
2025-11-29 16:39:19 [claude-opus-4-5] threshold drifts or gets bumped, you lose sync entirely rather than just getting slightly wrong
2025-11-29 16:39:19 [claude-opus-4-5] gray mapping. A fixed threshold is one less thing to go wrong.
2025-11-29 16:39:19 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:39:19 [claude-opus-4-5] • Middle ground option:* Use a trimpot for CSYNC but a multi-turn one for finer adjustment
2025-11-29 16:39:19 [claude-opus-4-5] and better stability, keep the single-turn Pihers for the gray levels where exact setting is less
2025-11-29 16:39:19 [claude-opus-4-5] critical.
2025-11-29 16:39:19 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:39:19 [claude-opus-4-5] • Or:* Keep CSYNC fixed but add a trimpot as a master input level adjust before the
2025-11-29 16:39:19 [claude-opus-4-5] comparators - scales all thresholds together if you encounter a C64 with hotter or weaker luma
2025-11-29 16:39:19 [claude-opus-4-5] output. One pot, affects everything proportionally.
2025-11-29 16:39:19 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:39:19 [claude-opus-4-5] Depends whether you're building one unit tuned to your specific C64, or something that needs to
2025-11-29 16:39:19 [claude-opus-4-5] handle unknown machines.
2025-11-29 16:39:19 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:40:40 [nitro@ryzen5] vdd 3.3V + resistor for sync
2025-11-29 16:40:44 [nitro@ryzen5] ?
2025-11-29 16:40:51 [claude-opus-4-5] Yeah that works - simple resistor divider off 3.3V to set your sync threshold.
2025-11-29 16:40:51 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:40:51 [claude-opus-4-5] Something like 10K + 1K divider gives you ~0.3V at the comparator input, which should sit nicely
2025-11-29 16:40:51 [claude-opus-4-5] between sync tip and black level on C64 luma.
2025-11-29 16:40:51 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:40:51 [claude-opus-4-5] • Advantage:* More stable than a trimpot, 3.3V rail is regulated so threshold won't drift
2025-11-29 16:40:51 [claude-opus-4-5] with VBUS variation.
2025-11-29 16:40:51 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:40:51 [claude-opus-4-5] • Tweak if needed:* Swap one resistor value to shift the threshold. Or use a fixed resistor
2025-11-29 16:40:51 [claude-opus-4-5] plus a small trimpot in series if you want fine adjustment without full range swing.
2025-11-29 16:40:51 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:40:51 [claude-opus-4-5] Keeps your BOM simpler too - three trimpots for gray levels, fixed divider for sync. The gray
2025-11-29 16:40:51 [claude-opus-4-5] levels are where you actually need the tunability.
2025-11-29 16:40:51 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:41:13 [nitro@ryzen5] so 10k in series and 1k to ground?
2025-11-29 16:41:18 [claude-opus-4-5] Yes exactly - 3.3V to 10K, 10K to 1K, 1K to ground. Tap the middle point (junction of 10K and 1K)
2025-11-29 16:41:18 [claude-opus-4-5] for your ~0.3V reference to the comparator.
2025-11-29 16:41:18 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:41:18 [claude-opus-4-5] Quick sanity check: 3.3 × (1/(10+1)) ≈ 0.3V
2025-11-29 16:41:18 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:41:18 [claude-opus-4-5] That should catch sync tips while ignoring black level and blanking.
2025-11-29 16:41:18 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:42:15 [nitro@ryzen5] would it be better to use an 0.2 ref for blanking?
2025-11-29 16:42:24 [claude-opus-4-5] Hmm, depends what you want CSYNC to actually represent.
2025-11-29 16:42:24 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:42:24 [claude-opus-4-5] • 0.3V (sync tip):* Triggers only during sync pulses. Clean timing reference, what you'd
2025-11-29 16:42:24 [claude-opus-4-5] typically use for HSYNC/VSYNC detection.
2025-11-29 16:42:24 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:42:24 [claude-opus-4-5] • 0.2V (blanking):* Would trigger during both sync and blanking intervals. Gives you a
2025-11-29 16:42:24 [claude-opus-4-5] wider pulse that includes front porch, sync, back porch.
2025-11-29 16:42:24 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:42:24 [claude-opus-4-5] For PIO timing purposes you probably want the sharper sync pulse edges - easier to measure HSYNC vs
2025-11-29 16:42:24 [claude-opus-4-5] VSYNC by pulse width, cleaner trigger point for starting your pixel sampling.
2025-11-29 16:42:24 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:42:24 [claude-opus-4-5] If you go too low you risk noise triggering it during black picture content too.
2025-11-29 16:42:24 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:42:24 [claude-opus-4-5] • Actual question:* What's your C64's real luma levels at the pin? The nominal values vary
2025-11-29 16:42:24 [claude-opus-4-5] a bit. Might be worth scoping it first and setting threshold empirically rather than guessing at
2025-11-29 16:42:24 [claude-opus-4-5] exact voltage.
2025-11-29 16:42:24 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:42:24 [claude-opus-4-5] 0.3V is a safer starting point - clearly in the sync region on most machines.
2025-11-29 16:42:24 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:43:31 [nitro@ryzen5] ok 10K + 1K divider
2025-11-29 16:43:37 [claude-opus-4-5] Solid. One less trimpot to fiddle with, and sync detection will be rock stable.
2025-11-29 16:43:37 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:43:37 [claude-opus-4-5] So your final analog front end:
2025-11-29 16:43:37 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:43:37 [claude-opus-4-5] - 3x Piher trimpots for gray thresholds
2025-11-29 16:43:37 [claude-opus-4-5] - Fixed 10K/1K divider for sync reference
2025-11-29 16:43:37 [claude-opus-4-5] - 4x pull-ups on LM319 outputs to 3.3V
2025-11-29 16:43:37 [claude-opus-4-5] - Decoupling on the LM319
2025-11-29 16:43:37 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:43:37 [claude-opus-4-5] Clean and simple. The tricky bit will be the PIO timing once you get signals into the Pico.
2025-11-29 16:43:37 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:43:57 [nitro@ryzen5] the pullups can be provided by rp2040 no?
2025-11-29 16:44:04 [claude-opus-4-5] Yes, RP2040 has internal pull-ups you can enable on any GPIO. Saves 4 resistors.
2025-11-29 16:44:04 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:44:04 [claude-opus-4-5] • One caveat:* Internal pull-ups are weak, around 50-80K. LM319 open collectors will pull
2025-11-29 16:44:04 [claude-opus-4-5] them down fine, but rise time will be slower than with discrete 4.7K pull-ups.
2025-11-29 16:44:04 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:44:04 [claude-opus-4-5] At the speeds you're dealing with (6MHz-ish pixel rate, sync edges) it might matter. Slow rise time
2025-11-29 16:44:04 [claude-opus-4-5] could give you mushy edge detection in the PIO.
2025-11-29 16:44:04 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:44:04 [claude-opus-4-5] • Try it first* with internal pull-ups - if you get glitchy sync or noisy gray transitions,
2025-11-29 16:44:04 [claude-opus-4-5] add external 4.7K pull-ups. Easy fix later if needed.
2025-11-29 16:44:04 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:44:58 [nitro@ryzen5] on all 4 comparators yes?
2025-11-29 16:45:03 [claude-opus-4-5] Yes, all 4 LM319 outputs need pull-ups - CSYNC, G0, G1, G2. Open collector won't go high on its
2025-11-29 16:45:03 [claude-opus-4-5] own.
2025-11-29 16:45:03 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:45:03 [claude-opus-4-5] Start with internal pull-ups on all 4 GPIOs, see how it behaves. If edges are too slow you can add
2025-11-29 16:45:03 [claude-opus-4-5] external pull-ups to whichever channels are problematic - sync would be the first candidate since
2025-11-29 16:45:03 [claude-opus-4-5] timing is tightest there.
2025-11-29 16:45:03 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:45:18 [nitro@ryzen5] can you include 4.7 k pullups in bom then
2025-11-29 16:45:23 [claude-opus-4-5] Noted. BOM so far:
2025-11-29 16:45:23 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:45:23 [claude-opus-4-5] - 2x LM319 (4 comparators total)
2025-11-29 16:45:23 [claude-opus-4-5] - 3x 10K Piher horizontal trimpot
2025-11-29 16:45:23 [claude-opus-4-5] - 1x 10K resistor (sync divider)
2025-11-29 16:45:23 [claude-opus-4-5] - 1x 1K resistor (sync divider)
2025-11-29 16:45:23 [claude-opus-4-5] - 4x 4.7K resistor (pull-ups)
2025-11-29 16:45:23 [claude-opus-4-5] - 1x 100nF ceramic cap (decoupling)
2025-11-29 16:45:23 [claude-opus-4-5] - 1x 10µF cap (bulk)
2025-11-29 16:45:23 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:45:23 [claude-opus-4-5] Anything else you want captured - input protection, connectors, the Pico itself?
2025-11-29 16:45:23 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:45:42 [nitro@ryzen5] which cap is essential?
2025-11-29 16:45:47 [claude-opus-4-5] The 100nF ceramic on LM319 VCC - keeps the comparators stable, prevents oscillation and noise on
2025-11-29 16:45:47 [claude-opus-4-5] fast switching. Standard practice, don't skip it.
2025-11-29 16:45:47 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:45:47 [claude-opus-4-5] The 10µF bulk is nice-to-have if you're running longish wires from VBUS or sharing power with other
2025-11-29 16:45:47 [claude-opus-4-5] stuff. If your layout is compact and power path is short, you can probably drop it.
2025-11-29 16:45:47 [claude-opus-4-5] 
