2025-11-29 16:47:56 [nitro@ryzen5] as a stackable rp2040 design, i could stack 3 for component rgb no?
2025-11-29 16:48:29 [nitro@ryzen5] some kind of system to allocate gpio for each
2025-11-29 16:49:27 [nitro@ryzen5] possibly best to keep it simple and fine tune monochrome 2 bit 50 hz display with interlaces bias
2025-11-29 16:49:36 [claude-opus-4-5] Yeah stacking for RGB is tempting but you're right to keep it simple first.
2025-11-29 16:49:36 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:49:36 [claude-opus-4-5] • Problems with stacking:*
2025-11-29 16:49:36 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:49:36 [claude-opus-4-5] - Sync recovery needs to be shared or one board is master
2025-11-29 16:49:36 [claude-opus-4-5] - GPIO allocation gets messy - which Pico owns which pins
2025-11-29 16:49:36 [claude-opus-4-5] - Timing alignment between three capture streams
2025-11-29 16:49:36 [claude-opus-4-5] - Triple the edge cases to debug
2025-11-29 16:49:36 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:49:36 [claude-opus-4-5] • Get monochrome solid first:*
2025-11-29 16:49:36 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:49:36 [claude-opus-4-5] - Nail the sync detection
2025-11-29 16:49:36 [claude-opus-4-5] - Get clean 320x200 frames at 50Hz
2025-11-29 16:49:36 [claude-opus-4-5] - Sort out the interlace handling - C64 doesn't actually interlace but if you're seeing field bias
2025-11-29 16:49:36 [claude-opus-4-5] that's interesting
2025-11-29 16:49:36 [claude-opus-4-5] - Tune your gray thresholds
2025-11-29 16:49:36 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:49:36 [claude-opus-4-5] Once that's bulletproof, RGB is just three parallel capture streams with shared sync. The PIO code
2025-11-29 16:49:36 [claude-opus-4-5] and buffer management will be proven.
2025-11-29 16:49:36 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:49:36 [claude-opus-4-5] What's your output path for the captured frames - USB, SPI display, wireless?
2025-11-29 16:49:36 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:50:20 [nitro@ryzen5] no for odd frames add a bias to give 1 more bit resolution
2025-11-29 16:50:29 [claude-opus-4-5] Ah clever - temporal dithering to fake 3-bit from 2-bit capture.
2025-11-29 16:50:29 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:50:29 [claude-opus-4-5] Even frames: straight 2-bit thresholds (0,1,2,3)
2025-11-29 16:50:29 [claude-opus-4-5] Odd frames: shift thresholds slightly, captures the in-between levels
2025-11-29 16:50:29 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:50:29 [claude-opus-4-5] Combine them and you get 8 effective gray levels from 4 hardware comparators. Eye integrates over
2025-11-29 16:50:29 [claude-opus-4-5] 25Hz pairs.
2025-11-29 16:50:29 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:50:29 [claude-opus-4-5] • Implementation options:*
2025-11-29 16:50:29 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:50:29 [claude-opus-4-5] - Analog: Actually shift the reference voltages per frame (messy, need switching)
2025-11-29 16:50:29 [claude-opus-4-5] - Digital: Keep capture the same, apply bias in software when mapping to output (cleaner)
2025-11-29 16:50:29 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:50:29 [claude-opus-4-5] Or do you mean biasing the output display rather than the capture? Alternate between rounding up
2025-11-29 16:50:29 [claude-opus-4-5] and rounding down on edge cases to get dithered appearance?
2025-11-29 16:50:29 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:50:29 [claude-opus-4-5] Either way it's a smart way to squeeze more out of simple hardware. Keep the capture chain clean
2025-11-29 16:50:29 [claude-opus-4-5] and do the tricks in the digital domain.
2025-11-29 16:50:29 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:51:32 [nitro@ryzen5] could i bias the 3 bots with 3 gpio outputs?
2025-11-29 16:51:40 [claude-opus-4-5] Yes that could work - add a resistor from each GPIO to the corresponding trimpot wiper. GPIO high
2025-11-29 16:51:40 [claude-opus-4-5] shifts the threshold up slightly, GPIO low leaves it at the trimpot setting.
2025-11-29 16:51:40 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:51:40 [claude-opus-4-5] • Sizing matters:* You'd want a large resistor (maybe 47K-100K) so the GPIO only nudges the
2025-11-29 16:51:40 [claude-opus-4-5] voltage rather than overriding the trimpot. Small shift, just enough to catch the half-steps
2025-11-29 16:51:40 [claude-opus-4-5] between your main levels.
2025-11-29 16:51:40 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:51:40 [claude-opus-4-5] • Timing consideration:* You'd toggle the 3 GPIOs during VSYNC blanking, before each frame
2025-11-29 16:51:40 [claude-opus-4-5] capture starts. PIO or CPU can handle that easily.
2025-11-29 16:51:40 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:51:40 [claude-opus-4-5] • Neat side effect:* You could calibrate the bias amount in software by trying different
2025-11-29 16:51:40 [claude-opus-4-5] resistor values, or even PWM the GPIOs for finer control of the shift amount.
2025-11-29 16:51:40 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:51:40 [claude-opus-4-5] Simple addition to the circuit - 3 more resistors, 3 GPIOs. Worth experimenting with once basic
2025-11-29 16:51:40 [claude-opus-4-5] capture works.
2025-11-29 16:51:40 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:52:29 [nitro@ryzen5] could you make educated guess based on what is known about c64 palette and the luma pin
2025-11-29 16:52:39 [claude-opus-4-5] C64 has 16 colors but only 9 distinct luma levels on the luma pin. Roughly:
2025-11-29 16:52:39 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:52:39 [claude-opus-4-5] - Black: ~0V
2025-11-29 16:52:39 [claude-opus-4-5] - Dark grays: ~0.2-0.3V
2025-11-29 16:52:39 [claude-opus-4-5] - Mid grays: ~0.4-0.5V
2025-11-29 16:52:39 [claude-opus-4-5] - Light grays: ~0.6-0.7V
2025-11-29 16:52:39 [claude-opus-4-5] - White: ~0.8-1.0V
2025-11-29 16:52:39 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:52:39 [claude-opus-4-5] With 3 thresholds you're dividing into 4 bands. Optimal placement probably around 0.25V, 0.45V,
2025-11-29 16:52:39 [claude-opus-4-5] 0.65V to split the range evenly.
2025-11-29 16:52:39 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:52:39 [claude-opus-4-5] • For the bias shift:* You want to catch the levels that fall between your main thresholds.
2025-11-29 16:52:39 [claude-opus-4-5] The luma steps are roughly 0.1V apart in the mid range.
2025-11-29 16:52:39 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:52:39 [claude-opus-4-5] • So a bias of ~50mV* would shift your thresholds to interleave with the base positions.
2025-11-29 16:52:39 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:52:39 [claude-opus-4-5] If your trimpot wiper is around 0.45V and you add a 100K resistor to a 3.3V GPIO:
2025-11-29 16:52:39 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:52:39 [claude-opus-4-5] 3.3V through 100K into ~10K trimpot impedance gives roughly 0.3V shift - too much.
2025-11-29 16:52:39 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:52:39 [claude-opus-4-5] • Try 220K or 330K* from GPIO to wiper. That should give you 30-50mV shift, enough to catch
2025-11-29 16:52:39 [claude-opus-4-5] the half-steps without jumping a whole level.
2025-11-29 16:52:39 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:52:39 [claude-opus-4-5] Rough starting point - you'd tune by eye once it's running.
2025-11-29 16:52:39 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:54:19 [nitro@ryzen5] do i need to use 3 discrete gpio or will 1 be ok shared with 3 pots
2025-11-29 16:54:25 [claude-opus-4-5] One GPIO shared to all 3 pots through 3 resistors works fine. All thresholds shift together on odd
2025-11-29 16:54:25 [claude-opus-4-5] frames.
2025-11-29 16:54:25 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:54:25 [claude-opus-4-5] Simpler wiring, one control signal, same effect. The luma levels are roughly evenly spaced so
2025-11-29 16:54:25 [claude-opus-4-5] shifting all thresholds by the same amount makes sense.
2025-11-29 16:54:25 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:54:25 [claude-opus-4-5] Only reason for separate GPIOs would be if you wanted to bias each threshold by different amounts -
2025-11-29 16:54:25 [claude-opus-4-5] but that's overcomplicating it for marginal gain.
2025-11-29 16:54:25 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 16:54:25 [claude-opus-4-5] One GPIO, 3x 220K resistors to the 3 trimpot wipers. Toggle during VSYNC.
2025-11-29 16:54:25 [claude-opus-4-5] 
