2025-11-29 17:41:25 [nitro@ryzen5] do daisy chaining the pio will read 3 shift registers each count
2025-11-29 17:41:35 [claude-opus-4-5] Yes exactly - daisy chain means one PIO state machine clocks all 3 HC165s together, reads 24 bits
2025-11-29 17:41:35 [claude-opus-4-5] serially (8 clocks x 3 chips).
2025-11-29 17:41:35 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 17:41:35 [claude-opus-4-5] • Sequence:*
2025-11-29 17:41:35 [claude-opus-4-5] 1. Pulse LOAD low - all 3 latch their parallel inputs simultaneously
2025-11-29 17:41:35 [claude-opus-4-5] 2. Clock out 24 bits - first chip's Q7 feeds second chip's DS, etc
2025-11-29 17:41:35 [claude-opus-4-5] 3. PIO shifts in A0-A15 + D0-D7 in one burst
2025-11-29 17:41:35 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 17:41:35 [claude-opus-4-5] • Timing consideration:* At C64 bus speeds (~1MHz) you need to sample fast enough to catch
2025-11-29 17:41:36 [claude-opus-4-5] valid data. PIO can easily clock the shift registers at 10MHz+, so latency from LOAD to
2025-11-29 17:41:36 [claude-opus-4-5] data-in-hand is ~2.4µs worst case.
2025-11-29 17:41:36 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 17:41:36 [claude-opus-4-5] • GPIO count:*
2025-11-29 17:41:36 [claude-opus-4-5] - CLK: 1
2025-11-29 17:41:36 [claude-opus-4-5] - LOAD: 1
2025-11-29 17:41:36 [claude-opus-4-5] - DATA IN: 1
2025-11-29 17:41:36 [claude-opus-4-5] - Total: 3 GPIOs for 24 input bits
2025-11-29 17:41:36 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 17:41:36 [claude-opus-4-5] Clean and efficient. The 245s for output will need more GPIOs since they're parallel, unless you
2025-11-29 17:41:36 [claude-opus-4-5] add shift registers there too.
2025-11-29 17:41:36 [claude-opus-4-5] 
