2025-11-29 18:41:45 [claude-opus-4-5] For PCB fab with gold fingers (ENIG finish required for edge connector), rough estimates:
2025-11-29 18:41:45 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 18:41:45 [claude-opus-4-5] • JLCPCB/PCBWay typical pricing:*
2025-11-29 18:41:45 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 18:41:45 [claude-opus-4-5] - 16 pcs: ~40-60 total (2.50-4 each)
2025-11-29 18:41:45 [claude-opus-4-5] - 32 pcs: ~50-80 total (1.50-2.50 each)
2025-11-29 18:41:45 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 18:41:45 [claude-opus-4-5] • Factors that increase cost:*
2025-11-29 18:41:45 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 18:41:45 [claude-opus-4-5] - Gold fingers add ~$15-20 setup fee
2025-11-29 18:41:45 [claude-opus-4-5] - Beveled edge (recommended for insertion) adds a bit more
2025-11-29 18:41:45 [claude-opus-4-5] - Board size - if you're over 100x100mm it jumps
2025-11-29 18:41:45 [claude-opus-4-5] - ENIG vs HASL finish
2025-11-29 18:41:45 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 18:41:45 [claude-opus-4-5] • Rough per-unit BOM:*
2025-11-29 18:41:45 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 18:41:45 [claude-opus-4-5] - 3x 74HC165: $6.30
2025-11-29 18:41:45 [claude-opus-4-5] - 2x 74LVC245: $11.20
2025-11-29 18:41:45 [claude-opus-4-5] - PCB: ~$2-3
2025-11-29 18:41:45 [claude-opus-4-5] - Passives/caps: ~$1
2025-11-29 18:41:45 [claude-opus-4-5] - Edge connector is the PCB itself
2025-11-29 18:41:45 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 18:41:45 [claude-opus-4-5] • Total per unit: ~$20-22*
2025-11-29 18:41:45 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 18:41:45 [claude-opus-4-5] At 32 units you're looking at maybe $650-700 total build cost before assembly time.
2025-11-29 18:41:45 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 18:41:45 [claude-opus-4-5] Have you checked if the board fits under 100x100mm? That's the sweet spot for cheap fab.
2025-11-29 18:41:45 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 18:42:44 [nitro@ryzen5] what is standard width of a cart pcb for c64?
2025-11-29 18:42:53 [claude-opus-4-5] C64 cartridge PCB width is approximately 100mm (around 3.9 inches) to fit the cartridge
2025-11-29 18:42:53 [claude-opus-4-5] port slot.
2025-11-29 18:42:53 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 18:42:53 [claude-opus-4-5] The 44-pin edge connector has 22 pins per side at 0.156" (3.96mm) pitch, which works out to about
2025-11-29 18:42:53 [claude-opus-4-5] 86mm for the contact area, plus some margin on each side.
2025-11-29 18:42:53 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 18:42:53 [claude-opus-4-5] • Typical cart PCB dimensions:*
2025-11-29 18:42:53 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 18:42:53 [claude-opus-4-5] - Width: ~100mm
2025-11-29 18:42:53 [claude-opus-4-5] - Depth: ~80-100mm depending on enclosure
2025-11-29 18:42:53 [claude-opus-4-5] - Gold finger length: ~6mm
2025-11-29 18:42:53 [claude-opus-4-5] - Thickness: 1.6mm standard
2025-11-29 18:42:53 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 18:42:53 [claude-opus-4-5] You're right on the edge of the 100x100mm cheap PCB threshold. Might squeeze under if you keep the
2025-11-29 18:42:53 [claude-opus-4-5] depth minimal - just enough for your ICs and the Pico header.
2025-11-29 18:42:53 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 18:42:53 [claude-opus-4-5] If you're not using a cartridge shell, you could go narrower and just cover the contact area plus
2025-11-29 18:42:53 [claude-opus-4-5] components.
2025-11-29 18:42:53 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 18:43:14 [nitro@ryzen5] i was thinking 40mm?
2025-11-29 18:43:21 [claude-opus-4-5] 40mm would only cover about half the edge connector contacts - you'd miss a bunch of signals.
2025-11-29 18:43:21 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 18:43:21 [claude-opus-4-5] The 44 pins at 0.156" pitch spans ~86mm for the contacts alone. You need close to that width to hit
2025-11-29 18:43:21 [claude-opus-4-5] all the pins.
2025-11-29 18:43:21 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 18:43:21 [claude-opus-4-5] • Unless* you're only tapping a subset of signals? If you don't need all address lines or
2025-11-29 18:43:21 [claude-opus-4-5] certain control signals, you could make a narrower board that only contacts the pins you care
2025-11-29 18:43:21 [claude-opus-4-5] about.
2025-11-29 18:43:21 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 18:43:21 [claude-opus-4-5] • What signals do you actually need?*
2025-11-29 18:43:21 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 18:43:21 [claude-opus-4-5] - Full A0-A15: need most of the width
2025-11-29 18:43:21 [claude-opus-4-5] - D0-D7: center-ish
2025-11-29 18:43:21 [claude-opus-4-5] - Control signals: scattered across the connector
2025-11-29 18:43:21 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 18:43:21 [claude-opus-4-5] If you can live without the outer address lines you might get away with narrower, but 40mm is
2025-11-29 18:43:21 [claude-opus-4-5] probably too tight for a full bus tap.
2025-11-29 18:43:21 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 18:43:21 [claude-opus-4-5] What's driving the 40mm target - Pico footprint width?
2025-11-29 18:43:21 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 18:43:56 [nitro@ryzen5] A standard Commodore 64 (C64) cartridge is approximately 67-68 mm wide and 78-88 mm long.
2025-11-29 18:44:05 [claude-opus-4-5] Ah right, the cartridge shell is 67-68mm wide, but the PCB inside is narrower than the full port
2025-11-29 18:44:05 [claude-opus-4-5] width.
2025-11-29 18:44:05 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 18:44:05 [claude-opus-4-5] The edge connector contacts on a C64 cart PCB are around 58-60mm wide - the cartridge
2025-11-29 18:44:05 [claude-opus-4-5] doesn't use all 44 positions, some outer pins are unused or ground.
2025-11-29 18:44:05 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 18:44:05 [claude-opus-4-5] So your 40mm estimate was too narrow, but you don't need the full 86mm either.
2025-11-29 18:44:05 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 18:44:05 [claude-opus-4-5] • Practical dimensions:*
2025-11-29 18:44:05 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 18:44:05 [claude-opus-4-5] - PCB width: ~60mm for full signal coverage
2025-11-29 18:44:05 [claude-opus-4-5] - PCB depth: whatever fits your components + Pico header
2025-11-29 18:44:05 [claude-opus-4-5] - Gold fingers: centered, ~58mm contact area
2025-11-29 18:44:05 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 18:44:05 [claude-opus-4-5] At 60mm x 50mm you're well under 100x100mm - cheap PCB territory even with gold fingers.
2025-11-29 18:44:05 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 18:44:05 [claude-opus-4-5] Does 60mm width work for your layout with the 5 DIP chips and Pico connection?
2025-11-29 18:44:05 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 18:44:43 [nitro@ryzen5] actual dimension of the 44 pin edge connector part
2025-11-29 18:44:51 [claude-opus-4-5] 44 pins at 0.156" (3.96mm) pitch:
2025-11-29 18:44:51 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 18:44:51 [claude-opus-4-5] 22 pins per side, so 21 gaps = 21 x 3.96mm = 83.16mm contact span
2025-11-29 18:44:51 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 18:44:51 [claude-opus-4-5] Plus a small margin each side for the board edge, call it 85-88mm total PCB width at the
2025-11-29 18:44:51 [claude-opus-4-5] edge connector.
2025-11-29 18:44:51 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 18:44:51 [claude-opus-4-5] So you can't really go narrower than ~85mm if you need all 44 pins. The cartridge shell at 67mm
2025-11-29 18:44:51 [claude-opus-4-5] must have the PCB recessed or angled somehow, or I'm missing something about the C64 port geometry.
2025-11-29 18:44:51 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 18:44:51 [claude-opus-4-5] Worth measuring an actual cart PCB if you have one, or checking a KiCad footprint library for C64
2025-11-29 18:44:51 [claude-opus-4-5] cartridge edge connector - someone's surely done the exact dimensions.
2025-11-29 18:44:51 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 18:46:38 [nitro@ryzen5] no we are way off, need tostart with actual source Versa64Cart is 58mm so lets lock thatin for our width
2025-11-29 18:46:46 [claude-opus-4-5] Good call - 58mm it is, based on actual working design.
2025-11-29 18:46:46 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 18:46:46 [claude-opus-4-5] That means the pin pitch must be tighter than I calculated, or not all 44 positions are populated
2025-11-29 18:46:46 [claude-opus-4-5] on the PCB edge.
2025-11-29 18:46:46 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 18:46:46 [claude-opus-4-5] • Locked in:*
2025-11-29 18:46:46 [claude-opus-4-5] - PCB width: 58mm
2025-11-29 18:46:46 [claude-opus-4-5] - Gold fingers: sized to match Versa64Cart footprint
2025-11-29 18:46:46 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 18:46:46 [claude-opus-4-5] At 58mm x whatever depth you need, you're well under 100x100mm. Cheap fab even with ENIG and
2025-11-29 18:46:46 [claude-opus-4-5] beveling.
2025-11-29 18:46:46 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 18:46:46 [claude-opus-4-5] Depth depends on fitting:
2025-11-29 18:46:46 [claude-opus-4-5] - 3x 74HC165 (DIP16)
2025-11-29 18:46:46 [claude-opus-4-5] - 2x 74LVC245 (DIP20)
2025-11-29 18:46:46 [claude-opus-4-5] - Pico header/connection
2025-11-29 18:46:46 [claude-opus-4-5] - Passives
2025-11-29 18:46:46 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 18:46:46 [claude-opus-4-5] Probably 50-60mm depth? That gives you ~58x55mm board, nice and compact.
