2025-11-29 18:53:30 [nitro@ryzen5] i think pico should go across wise for orintation, which edge has most gpio?
2025-11-29 18:53:40 [claude-opus-4-5] The Pico has GPIO distributed on both long edges, but not evenly:
2025-11-29 18:53:40 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 18:53:40 [claude-opus-4-5] • Left edge (pin 1 side):* GP0-GP15 plus some ground/power
2025-11-29 18:53:40 [claude-opus-4-5] • Right edge (pin 40 side):* GP16-GP28 plus ADC, ground/power
2025-11-29 18:53:40 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 18:53:40 [claude-opus-4-5] Both edges have roughly similar GPIO count - about 15-16 usable GPIOs per side.
2025-11-29 18:53:40 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 18:53:40 [claude-opus-4-5] • For your layout* with edge connector at bottom and Pico across the top:
2025-11-29 18:53:40 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 18:53:40 [claude-opus-4-5] - One side of Pico faces your ICs (short traces)
2025-11-29 18:53:40 [claude-opus-4-5] - Other side faces away (could be expansion/debug)
2025-11-29 18:53:40 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 18:53:40 [claude-opus-4-5] Probably orient so the side with GP0-GP15 faces down toward your shift registers and 245s - keeps
2025-11-29 18:53:40 [claude-opus-4-5] the main data path short.
2025-11-29 18:53:40 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 18:53:40 [claude-opus-4-5] GP0-GP15 gives you enough for:
2025-11-29 18:53:40 [claude-opus-4-5] - 3 lines for HC165 (CLK, LOAD, DATA)
2025-11-29 18:53:40 [claude-opus-4-5] - 8 lines for 245 data bus
2025-11-29 18:53:40 [claude-opus-4-5] - 1 line for 245 direction
2025-11-29 18:53:40 [claude-opus-4-5] - Control signals
2025-11-29 18:53:40 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 18:53:40 [claude-opus-4-5] Fits nicely on one edge.
2025-11-29 18:53:40 [claude-opus-4-5] 
