2025-11-29 19:10:14 [nitro@ryzen5] pico castellated socket would be cool?
2025-11-29 19:10:23 [claude-opus-4-5] Yeah castellated mounting would be clean - Pico solders directly to the board, low profile, no
2025-11-29 19:10:23 [claude-opus-4-5] headers.
2025-11-29 19:10:23 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 19:10:23 [claude-opus-4-5] • Advantages:*
2025-11-29 19:10:23 [claude-opus-4-5] - Compact - no header height
2025-11-29 19:10:23 [claude-opus-4-5] - Solid connection - soldered not socketed
2025-11-29 19:10:23 [claude-opus-4-5] - Looks professional
2025-11-29 19:10:23 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 19:10:23 [claude-opus-4-5] • Disadvantages:*
2025-11-29 19:10:23 [claude-opus-4-5] - Pico is committed to that board - can't reuse it
2025-11-29 19:10:23 [claude-opus-4-5] - Harder to swap if Pico dies
2025-11-29 19:10:23 [claude-opus-4-5] - Need castellated footprint in KiCad (readily available)
2025-11-29 19:10:23 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 19:10:23 [claude-opus-4-5] • Alternative:* Castellated pads on your PCB but use low-profile female headers - gives you
2025-11-29 19:10:23 [claude-opus-4-5] the compact look but Pico is removable. Best of both if you're making multiple units or debugging.
2025-11-29 19:10:23 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 19:10:23 [claude-opus-4-5] At your quantities (16-32 boards), socketed might be smarter during development. Go castellated
2025-11-29 19:10:23 [claude-opus-4-5] direct-solder for a final production version.
