2025-11-29 20:40:47 [claude-opus-4-5] Ha! The classic "credit balance cliff" - at least it's a quick fix. Topped up and ready to go.
2025-11-29 20:40:47 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 20:40:47 [claude-opus-4-5] So, Project Toast - I like the name. Breadbin meets breadboard energy.
2025-11-29 20:40:47 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 20:40:47 [claude-opus-4-5] Looking at your BOM, a few discussion points:
2025-11-29 20:40:47 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 20:40:47 [claude-opus-4-5] • The 4x 74HC165 setup* - you've got 32 inputs there (A0-A15, D0-D7, 8 control). That's
2025-11-29 20:40:47 [claude-opus-4-5] tight packing. What's your clock speed target for shifting all that in? At C64 bus speeds you'll
2025-11-29 20:40:47 [claude-opus-4-5] need to be snappy.
2025-11-29 20:40:47 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 20:40:47 [claude-opus-4-5] • The 74LVC245 choice for output* - good call on LVC for the 3.3V to 5V direction, though
2025-11-29 20:40:47 [claude-opus-4-5] you'll want to verify the C64 bus can handle the drive characteristics. The original cartridge port
2025-11-29 20:40:47 [claude-opus-4-5] expects certain impedances.
2025-11-29 20:40:47 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 20:40:47 [claude-opus-4-5] • 10k series resistors on everything* - is that for protection during development, or
2025-11-29 20:40:47 [claude-opus-4-5] permanent? Might slow your edges more than you'd like depending on bus capacitance.
2025-11-29 20:40:47 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 20:40:47 [claude-opus-4-5] • Missing from BOM?* - no level shifting on the input side? The HC165s are 5V parts reading
2025-11-29 20:40:47 [claude-opus-4-5] 5V signals, but you're feeding a 3.3V Pico. How are you handling that interface? Voltage dividers?
2025-11-29 20:40:47 [claude-opus-4-5] Or running the HC165s at 3.3V and hoping the C64's 5V logic-high still registers?
2025-11-29 20:40:47 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 20:40:47 [claude-opus-4-5] What's the end goal - full cartridge emulation, memory expansion, something else?
2025-11-29 20:40:47 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 20:43:52 [nitro@ryzen5] i was expecting to run all chips with 5v supply, the pio should hopefully read the 4 shift register in parallel at start of phi2 clock and have enough cycles if needed to drive enable data bus with a bit of decoding
2025-11-29 20:44:04 [claude-opus-4-5] That makes sense. Running everything at 5V keeps the C64 side happy, and the RP2040 inputs are 5V
2025-11-29 20:44:04 [claude-opus-4-5] tolerant so you can read the HC165 outputs directly.
2025-11-29 20:44:04 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 20:44:04 [claude-opus-4-5] Parallel shifting is smart - four SPI-style reads simultaneously via PIO rather than
2025-11-29 20:44:04 [claude-opus-4-5] daisy-chaining. Keeps your latency down. You'd need four PIO state machines or a clever single SM
2025-11-29 20:44:04 [claude-opus-4-5] with four data pins, but you've got the resources.
2025-11-29 20:44:04 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 20:44:04 [claude-opus-4-5] The PHI2 timing is the interesting constraint. You've got roughly 500ns per half-cycle at 1MHz. If
2025-11-29 20:44:04 [claude-opus-4-5] you're triggering on PHI2 rising edge, shifting in 8 bits per register in parallel, that's 8 clock
2025-11-29 20:44:04 [claude-opus-4-5] pulses plus your decode logic. PIO at 125MHz gives you plenty of headroom there.
2025-11-29 20:44:04 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 20:44:04 [claude-opus-4-5] One thing worth thinking through - the address lines are valid before PHI2 rises, but the data bus
2025-11-29 20:44:04 [claude-opus-4-5] timing is tighter for writes. If the C64 is writing to your device, you need to tristate your 245s
2025-11-29 20:44:04 [claude-opus-4-5] quickly when you detect a write cycle. R/W line is part of your control inputs presumably?
2025-11-29 20:44:04 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 20:44:04 [claude-opus-4-5] The decode logic living in the PIO or in the ARM core? If it's ARM-side you're adding interrupt
2025-11-29 20:44:04 [claude-opus-4-5] latency into the mix.
2025-11-29 20:44:04 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 20:45:10 [nitro@ryzen5] in the pio i was thinking would be tidy
2025-11-29 20:45:19 [claude-opus-4-5] Agreed. Keeping decode in the PIO keeps your timing deterministic. No IRQ jitter to worry about.
2025-11-29 20:45:19 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 20:45:19 [claude-opus-4-5] You could have the PIO shift in all 32 bits, then do a quick compare against a base address range
2025-11-29 20:45:19 [claude-opus-4-5] right there in the state machine. Only poke the ARM when there's an actual hit. Keeps the core free
2025-11-29 20:45:19 [claude-opus-4-5] for heavier lifting and avoids thrashing on every bus cycle.
2025-11-29 20:45:19 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 20:45:19 [claude-opus-4-5] The PIO instruction set is limited for arithmetic but address decoding is usually just masking and
2025-11-29 20:45:19 [claude-opus-4-5] comparing upper bits anyway. A few JMP conditionals on specific pins or a lookup into the scratch
2025-11-29 20:45:19 [claude-opus-4-5] registers could handle DE00-DEFF or whatever range you're targeting.
2025-11-29 20:45:19 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 20:45:19 [claude-opus-4-5] Output side gets interesting - when you detect a valid read from your address space, you need to
2025-11-29 20:45:19 [claude-opus-4-5] clock the data out through the 245s and assert your active output. That sequencing all fits in one
2025-11-29 20:45:19 [claude-opus-4-5] state machine or are you splitting read and write handling?
2025-11-29 20:45:19 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 20:47:22 [nitro@ryzen5] i have the time i have shifted the address and controls in til the falling of phi2 to drive data and some control pins? if required, so yes was hoping it read and write all happen in same state machine
2025-11-29 20:47:32 [claude-opus-4-5] That's a solid approach. PHI2 high gives you the full window - shift in on the rising edge, decode
2025-11-29 20:47:32 [claude-opus-4-5] mid-cycle, drive outputs before falling edge if needed.
2025-11-29 20:47:32 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 20:47:32 [claude-opus-4-5] Single state machine keeping read and write together makes sense for coherency. You're looking at
2025-11-29 20:47:32 [claude-opus-4-5] something like:
2025-11-29 20:47:32 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 20:47:32 [claude-opus-4-5] - Wait for PHI2 rising
2025-11-29 20:47:32 [claude-opus-4-5] - Clock in address/control in parallel across four inputs
2025-11-29 20:47:32 [claude-opus-4-5] - Check R/W line and address match
2025-11-29 20:47:32 [claude-opus-4-5] - Branch to either read path (drive data onto bus via 245) or write path (latch data from bus) or
2025-11-29 20:47:32 [claude-opus-4-5] neither (not your address, stay quiet)
2025-11-29 20:47:32 [claude-opus-4-5] - Wait for PHI2 falling
2025-11-29 20:47:32 [claude-opus-4-5] - Tristate and loop
2025-11-29 20:47:32 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 20:47:32 [claude-opus-4-5] The 245 direction and output enable pins become part of your control outputs through the second
2025-11-29 20:47:32 [claude-opus-4-5] 245. Directly driven by PIO GPIO rather than through the shift register path I assume? You'd want
2025-11-29 20:47:32 [claude-opus-4-5] those responsive, not waiting on a shift out.
2025-11-29 20:47:32 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 20:47:32 [claude-opus-4-5] Main question is whether the shift-in time leaves enough cycles for decode plus shift-out on a
2025-11-29 20:47:32 [claude-opus-4-5] read. But at 125MHz PIO clock versus 500ns half-cycle, you've got around 60 PIO instructions to
2025-11-29 20:47:32 [claude-opus-4-5] play with. Should be comfortable.
2025-11-29 20:47:32 [claude-opus-4-5] 
