2025-11-29 21:33:06 [nitro@ryzen5] which GPIO are internal on rp2040?
2025-11-29 21:33:14 [claude-opus-4-5] On the Pico, GP23-GP25 are used internally:
2025-11-29 21:33:14 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 21:33:14 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 21:33:14 [claude-opus-4-5] GP23, SMPS power save (controls regulator)
2025-11-29 21:33:14 [claude-opus-4-5] GP24, VBUS sense (USB connected)
2025-11-29 21:33:14 [claude-opus-4-5] GP25, onboard LED
2025-11-29 21:33:14 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 21:33:14 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 21:33:14 [claude-opus-4-5] So your DOT clock on GP25 would conflict with the LED. Not a showstopper but you lose the blinky.
2025-11-29 21:33:14 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 21:33:14 [claude-opus-4-5] GP29 is ADC3/VSYS sense, used for voltage monitoring.
2025-11-29 21:33:14 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 21:33:14 [claude-opus-4-5] GP22 is fully free, no internal use.
2025-11-29 21:33:14 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 21:33:14 [claude-opus-4-5] So the truly flexible user GPIOs without conflicts:
2025-11-29 21:33:14 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 21:33:14 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 21:33:14 [claude-opus-4-5] GP2-GP22, free
2025-11-29 21:33:14 [claude-opus-4-5] GP26-GP28, free (also ADC capable)
2025-11-29 21:33:14 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 21:33:14 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 21:33:14 [claude-opus-4-5] Might want to move PHI2, R/W, DOT off GP23-25 onto the free range if you want clean separation from
