2025-11-29 21:36:55 [nitro@ryzen5] only data bus needs the /OE what do i do with control pull downs? is that what they are?
2025-11-29 21:37:04 [claude-opus-4-5] The control signals (/IRQ, /NMI, /RESET, /ROML, /ROMH, /EXROM) are active-low, directly mapped to
2025-11-29 21:37:04 [claude-opus-4-5] C64 signals. They need to be driven appropriately by the Pico.
2025-11-29 21:37:04 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 21:37:04 [claude-opus-4-5] The C64 typically expects these as active-low, active-high, active-low, active-low, active-low,
2025-11-29 21:37:04 [claude-opus-4-5] active-low respectively.
2025-11-29 21:37:04 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 21:37:04 [claude-opus-4-5] Typically cartridges use open-collector/open-drain outputs on these lines so multiple devices can
2025-11-29 21:37:04 [claude-opus-4-5] share them without bus contention. The C64 has internal pull-ups on most of these.
2025-11-29 21:37:04 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 21:37:04 [claude-opus-4-5] So rather than push-pull from the 245, you might want:
2025-11-29 21:37:04 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 21:37:04 [claude-opus-4-5] - Default state: high-Z (let C64 pull-ups hold them high)
2025-11-29 21:37:04 [claude-opus-4-5] - Active state: pull low when asserting
2025-11-29 21:37:04 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 21:37:04 [claude-opus-4-5] The 245 with /OE gives you that - tristate when inactive, drive low when active. So you do want /OE
2025-11-29 21:37:04 [claude-opus-4-5] control on transceiver 6 as well.
2025-11-29 21:37:04 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 21:37:04 [claude-opus-4-5] Alternatively, external pull-downs aren't what you want - you want the C64's pull-ups to do the
2025-11-29 21:37:04 [claude-opus-4-5] work, and only drive low when needed.
2025-11-29 21:37:04 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 21:37:04 [claude-opus-4-5] Keep /OE on both transceivers:
2025-11-29 21:37:04 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 21:37:04 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 21:37:04 [claude-opus-4-5] GP22, /OE data
2025-11-29 21:37:04 [claude-opus-4-5] GP28, /OE ctrl
2025-11-29 21:37:04 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 21:37:04 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 21:37:04 [claude-opus-4-5] That way control lines float until you need to assert them.
2025-11-29 21:37:04 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 21:38:34 [nitro@ryzen5] i just want to be able to pull 5v control lines low via gpio safely, is that ok?
2025-11-29 21:38:43 [claude-opus-4-5] Yes, the 74LVC245 handles that fine. With DIR tied to +5V, the A side (Pico) drives the B side
2025-11-29 21:38:43 [claude-opus-4-5] (C64).
2025-11-29 21:38:43 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 21:38:43 [claude-opus-4-5] Pico GPIO low → 245 B output low → pulls C64 line to ground
2025-11-29 21:38:43 [claude-opus-4-5] Pico GPIO high → 245 B output high → but C64 pull-ups already holding high, no conflict
2025-11-29 21:38:43 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 21:38:43 [claude-opus-4-5] The 245 can sink plenty of current to pull against the C64's pull-ups. And the 10K series resistors
2025-11-29 21:38:43 [claude-opus-4-5] on the Pico side protect the GPIOs.
2025-11-29 21:38:43 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 21:38:43 [claude-opus-4-5] So no /OE needed on transceiver 6 - just leave it enabled (pin 19 to GND) and drive the control
2025-11-29 21:38:43 [claude-opus-4-5] lines directly. Simpler.
2025-11-29 21:38:43 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 21:38:43 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 21:38:43 [claude-opus-4-5] 6.19, /OE, GND
2025-11-29 21:38:43 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 21:38:43 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 21:38:43 [claude-opus-4-5] That frees up GP28 for something else - third user pin or DOT clock.
2025-11-29 21:38:43 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 21:39:01 [nitro@ryzen5] GND it is
2025-11-29 21:39:55 [claude-opus-4-5] Looks good. One inconsistency - GP28 shows `/OE ctrl` in the GPIO allocation but transceiver 6 has
2025-11-29 21:39:55 [claude-opus-4-5] `/OE` tied to GND in the netlist.
2025-11-29 21:39:55 [claude-opus-4-5] 
