2025-11-29 22:02:56 [nitro@ryzen5] in only ever want to pull low or let float the /IRQ..DMA lines
2025-11-29 22:03:35 [claude-opus-4-5] That's the right approach for open-collector style signals. The C64's pull-ups hold them high, you
2025-11-29 22:03:35 [claude-opus-4-5] only pull low to assert.
2025-11-29 22:03:35 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 22:03:35 [claude-opus-4-5] But there's a problem - the 74LVC245 is push-pull, not open-drain. When you write a 1 to
2025-11-29 22:03:35 [claude-opus-4-5] GP11-15/26-27, the 245 actively drives high, not floats.
2025-11-29 22:03:35 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 22:03:35 [claude-opus-4-5] Options:
2025-11-29 22:03:35 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 22:03:35 [claude-opus-4-5] 1. Always write 0s, use /OE to float - but /OE is tied to GND so always enabled
2025-11-29 22:03:35 [claude-opus-4-5] 2. Add external diodes - cathode to B output, anode to cartridge line, only pulls low
2025-11-29 22:03:35 [claude-opus-4-5] 3. Use 74LVC07 instead - open-drain buffer, does exactly what you want
2025-11-29 22:03:35 [claude-opus-4-5] 4. Software discipline - configure GPIOs as inputs to tristate, outputs only when asserting
2025-11-29 22:03:35 [claude-opus-4-5] low
2025-11-29 22:03:35 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 22:03:35 [claude-opus-4-5] Option 4 works with current hardware - set GP11-15/26-27 as inputs normally (245 A-side sees
2025-11-29 22:03:35 [claude-opus-4-5] high-Z, B-side follows but weakly), switch to output-low only when asserting. The 10K series
2025-11-29 22:03:35 [claude-opus-4-5] resistors help here.
2025-11-29 22:03:35 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 22:03:35 [claude-opus-4-5] Actually wait - with DIR high, A drives B. If A-side is high-Z (GPIO as input), what does B do?
2025-11-29 22:03:35 [claude-opus-4-5] It'll float or hold last state depending on the 245. Not reliable.
2025-11-29 22:03:35 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 22:03:35 [claude-opus-4-5] Cleanest fix is replacing transceiver 6 with a 74LVC07 hex open-drain buffer. Matches your intent
2025-11-29 22:03:35 [claude-opus-4-5] exactly.
2025-11-29 22:03:35 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 22:04:57 [nitro@ryzen5] 74LVC07 not available at jaycar
2025-11-29 22:05:22 [claude-opus-4-5] Fair enough. Stick with the 245 and work around it.
2025-11-29 22:05:22 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 22:05:22 [claude-opus-4-5] • Software approach:*
2025-11-29 22:05:22 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 22:05:22 [claude-opus-4-5] - Default GPIO state: output high (245 drives B high, matches C64 pull-ups, no contention)
2025-11-29 22:05:22 [claude-opus-4-5] - Assert: output low (245 drives B low, pulls line down)
2025-11-29 22:05:22 [claude-opus-4-5] - Release: output high (back to matching pull-ups)
2025-11-29 22:05:22 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 22:05:22 [claude-opus-4-5] Since you're matching the pull-up voltage when high, no fighting. The 10K series resistors limit
2025-11-29 22:05:22 [claude-opus-4-5] current if there's any mismatch during transitions.
2025-11-29 22:05:22 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 22:05:22 [claude-opus-4-5] Only issue is if C64 actively drives those lines low while you're driving high - but for /IRQ,
2025-11-29 22:05:22 [claude-opus-4-5] /NMI, /ROML, /ROMH, /EXROM, /GAME that shouldn't happen. They're directly directly from C64 side,
2025-11-29 22:05:22 [claude-opus-4-5] active-low directly directly directly directly directly directly directly directly directly
