2025-11-29 22:08:36 [nitro@ryzen5] how can rp2040 safely pull low a 5v line?
2025-11-29 22:08:42 [claude-opus-4-5] Use an open-drain/open-collector configuration with the RP2040 GPIO.
2025-11-29 22:08:42 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 22:08:42 [claude-opus-4-5] Set the pin as output low to pull down, or set as input (high-Z) to release. The 5V side needs its
2025-11-29 22:08:42 [claude-opus-4-5] own pull-up resistor to 5V.
2025-11-29 22:08:42 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 22:08:42 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 22:08:42 [claude-opus-4-5] // Pull low
2025-11-29 22:08:42 [claude-opus-4-5] gpio_set_dir(PIN, GPIO_OUT);
2025-11-29 22:08:42 [claude-opus-4-5] gpio_put(PIN, 0);
2025-11-29 22:08:42 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 22:08:42 [claude-opus-4-5] // Release (let 5V pull-up take over)
2025-11-29 22:08:42 [claude-opus-4-5] gpio_set_dir(PIN, GPIO_IN);
2025-11-29 22:08:42 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 22:08:42 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 22:08:42 [claude-opus-4-5] The RP2040 pin only ever sees ground or floats - never the 5V rail directly. Works for I2C, reset
2025-11-29 22:08:42 [claude-opus-4-5] lines, interrupt signals, etc.
2025-11-29 22:08:42 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 22:08:59 [nitro@ryzen5] rp2040 is 3.3v
2025-11-29 22:09:04 [claude-opus-4-5] Yes, that's exactly why open-drain works here.
2025-11-29 22:09:04 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 22:09:04 [claude-opus-4-5] When the GPIO is output low, it pulls to ground (0V) - safe. When set to input, it floats and the
2025-11-29 22:09:04 [claude-opus-4-5] external 5V pull-up does the work, but no current flows into the pin.
2025-11-29 22:09:04 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 22:09:04 [claude-opus-4-5] The RP2040 pin never has 5V applied to it, only ground or high-impedance state.
2025-11-29 22:09:04 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-29 22:09:04 [claude-opus-4-5] If you need to read a 5V signal, that's different - you'd need a voltage divider or level
2025-11-29 22:09:04 [claude-opus-4-5] shifter.
2025-11-29 22:09:04 [claude-opus-4-5] 
