2025-11-30 06:23:25 [gemini-3-pro] Hello. I have reviewed the documentation for Project Toast. It looks like a capable interface
2025-11-30 06:23:25 [gemini-3-pro] design for the C64 cartridge port.
2025-11-30 06:23:25 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 06:23:25 [gemini-3-pro] Here are a few observations regarding the electrical design and consistency:
2025-11-30 06:23:25 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 06:23:25 [gemini-3-pro] 1.  74LVC245 Power Rail: The netlist shows the transceiver V_CC connected to +5V.
2025-11-30 06:23:25 [gemini-3-pro]     *   Risk: At 5V, the 74LVC245 typically requires a VIH (Input High) of \approx 3.5V
2025-11-30 06:23:25 [gemini-3-pro] (0.7 × VCC). The RP2040's 3.3V GPIO output may not reliably trigger a high state.
2025-11-30 06:23:25 [gemini-3-pro]     *   Suggestion: Power the 74LVC245 at 3.3V. It is 5V tolerant on the C64 side
2025-11-30 06:23:25 [gemini-3-pro] and will drive valid TTL logic levels (~3.3V) to the bus.
2025-11-30 06:23:25 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 06:23:25 [gemini-3-pro] 2.  RP2040 Input Protection: The 74HC165 shift registers are powered at +5V.
2025-11-30 06:23:25 [gemini-3-pro]     *   Risk: The `Q7` output lines (GP17–GP20) will drive 5V into the RP2040. The RP2040
2025-11-30 06:23:25 [gemini-3-pro] is not 5V tolerant. While the 10kΩ series resistors will limit the current into the internal
2025-11-30 06:23:25 [gemini-3-pro] clamping diodes, this relies on out-of-spec behavior.
2025-11-30 06:23:25 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 06:23:25 [gemini-3-pro] 3.  Documentation Discrepancy:
2025-11-30 06:23:25 [gemini-3-pro]     *   The ASCII block diagram lists 3× 74HC165.
2025-11-30 06:23:25 [gemini-3-pro]     *   The BOM and Netlist detail 4× 74HC165 (covering Address, Data, and Control lines).
2025-11-30 06:23:25 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 06:23:25 [gemini-3-pro] Let me know if you need any specific PIO code generation for the shift register timing.
2025-11-30 06:23:25 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] Hi. I have reviewed the netlist and BOM for Project Toast.
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] It is a split-bus architecture (Input via Shift Registers, Output via Transceiver). While the
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] concept is sound for a ROM emulator or bus sniffer, there is some "slop" in the netlist regarding
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] Pin Conflicts and Voltage Levels that will prevent it from working or potentially damage the
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] RP2040.
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] ⚠️ The "Slop" Report (Critical Issues)
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] • 1. Voltage Logic Levels (The 5V vs 3.3V Issue)*
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] • The Issue: Your netlist powers the 74HC165s at +5V (pins 1.16, 2.16,
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] etc). This means the `Q7` outputs (GP17, GP18, GP19, GP20) will drive 5V into the RP2040.
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] • The Risk: RP2040 GPIOs are not 5V tolerant.
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] • Mitigation: You mentioned "10kΩ series resistors" in the header. If these resistors are
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] placed between the HC165 Q7 pins and the RP2040, you are relying on the RP2040's internal clamping
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] diodes to sink the excess voltage. This is out of spec, but often works in "hacky" scenarios.
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] • Better Fix: Power the 74HC165s at 3.3V. The 74HC series at 3.3V has a V_IH (Input
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] High) of ~2.3V. The C64 (5V TTL/NMOS) outputs will easily trigger this. This makes the Q7 output
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] safe 3.3V logic for the Pico without relying on clamp diodes.
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] • 2. Pin Conflict: GP10 (/IRQ vs DOT)*
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] • Allocation List: Lists `GP10, /IRQ active low`.
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] • Direct Inputs: Lists `DOT, GP10`.
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] • Shift Reg 4: Lists `P0, /IRQ` (Input to shift reg).
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] • Conflict: You cannot drive the Open Drain `/IRQ` line low while simultaneously trying to
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] read the high-speed `DOT` clock (8MHz) on the same pin.
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] • Fix: Move `DOT` clock to a free pin or drop it (DOT clock is rarely needed for standard
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] cart emulation; PHI2 is usually sufficient).
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] • 3. Pin Conflict: GP22 (/OE vs R/W)*
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] • Allocation List: Lists `GP22, R/W edge`.
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] • 74LVC245 List: Lists `5.19, /OE, GP22`.
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] • Conflict: You are using GP22 to detect the R/W transition and to Enable the Data Bus
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] transceiver.
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] • Logic Failure: If R/W is High (Read), you might want /OE Low (Active) to drive data. If
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] you tie them together, the logic might be inverted or timing will be wrong. These need separate
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] pins.
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] • 4. 74LVC245 Direction*
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] • Netlist: `5.1, DIR, 3V3`.
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] • Effect: Direction is permanently locked A->B (RP2040 -> C64).
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] • Observation: This is correct for a cartridge emulator (RP2040 writes data to C64 during
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] Read cycles). Just ensure your code never enables `/OE` (GP22) when the C64 is writing (R/W
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] Low), or you will cause a bus fight and short circuit.
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] RP2040 PIO Implementation
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] To make this work, you need to read the 4 shift registers in parallel extremely fast to catch the
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] bus state within the PHI2 window. Here is a PIO program to read 32 bits (Address + Data + Control)
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] in just a few cycles.
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] • Wiring Assumption for Code:*
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] • `SIDE_SET` pin: GP15 (CLK)
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] • `SET` pin: GP16 (SH/LD)
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] • `IN` pins base: GP17 (The four Q7 outputs: GP17, 18, 19, 20 mapped to IN 0, 1, 2, 3)
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] // toast.pio
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] .program toast_read
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] .side_set 1
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] // C64 Cycle: ~1us.
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] // We need to latch, shift 8 times, and push to FIFO.
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] // 74HC165: Low on SH/LD loads data. High shifts.
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] // Data is valid on Q7 immediately after Load.
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] public entry_point:
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro]     set pins, 0     side 0  ; [1] Drive SH/LD Low (Load), CLK Low
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro]     set pins, 1     side 0  ; [2] Drive SH/LD High (Shift mode)
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro]     in pins, 4      side 1  ; [3] Sample bit 0 (MSB), Clock High
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro]     in pins, 4      side 0  ; [4] Sample bit 1, Clock Low
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro]     in pins, 4      side 1  ; [5] ...
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro]     in pins, 4      side 0
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro]     in pins, 4      side 1
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro]     in pins, 4      side 0
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro]     in pins, 4      side 1
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro]     in pins, 4      side 0  ; [10] Sample bit 7
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro]     push block      side 0  ; [11] Push 32 bits to RX FIFO
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] • C SDK Configuration:*
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] #include "hardware/pio.h"
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] #include "toast.pio.h"
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] // GPIO Mapping based on your netlist
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] #define GPIO_CLK    15
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] #define GPIO_SHLD   16
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] #define GPIO_Q7_BASE 17 // Expects 17, 18, 19, 20 to be consecutive
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] void toast_init(PIO pio, uint sm) {
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro]     uint offset = pio_add_program(pio, &toast_read_program);
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro]     pio_sm_config c = toast_read_program_get_default_config(offset);
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro]     // Map 4 input pins (Q7s) starting at GP17
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro]     sm_config_set_in_pins(&c, GPIO_Q7_BASE);
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro]     // Map SET pin (SH/LD) to GP16
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro]     sm_config_set_set_pins(&c, GPIO_SHLD, 1);
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro]     // Map SIDESET pin (CLK) to GP15
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro]     sm_config_set_sideset_pins(&c, GPIO_CLK);
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro]     // Configure pins
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro]     pio_gpio_init(pio, GPIO_CLK);
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro]     pio_gpio_init(pio, GPIO_SHLD);
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro]     for(int i=0; i<4; i++) pio_gpio_init(pio, GPIO_Q7_BASE + i);
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro]     // Directions
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro]     pio_sm_set_consecutive_pindirs(pio, sm, GPIO_CLK, 1, true);  // Output
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro]     pio_sm_set_consecutive_pindirs(pio, sm, GPIO_SHLD, 1, true); // Output
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro]     pio_sm_set_consecutive_pindirs(pio, sm, GPIO_Q7_BASE, 4, false); // Input
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro]     // Shifting: Shift Left (MSB first for 165), Autopush OFF (manual push)
2025-11-30 06:25:37 [gemini-3-pro]     sm_config_set_in_shift(&c, false, false, 32);
2025-11-30 06:25:38 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 06:25:38 [gemini-3-pro]     // Run full speed
2025-11-30 06:25:38 [gemini-3-pro]     sm_config_set_clkdiv(&c, 1.0f);
2025-11-30 06:25:38 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 06:25:38 [gemini-3-pro]     pio_sm_init(pio, sm, offset, &c);
2025-11-30 06:25:38 [gemini-3-pro]     pio_sm_set_enabled(pio, sm, true);
2025-11-30 06:25:38 [gemini-3-pro] }
2025-11-30 06:25:38 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 06:25:38 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 06:25:38 [gemini-3-pro] Revised BOM/Netlist Recommendation
2025-11-30 06:25:38 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 06:25:38 [gemini-3-pro] 1.  Resolve GP10: Use GP10 for `/IRQ` (Output). Do not connect DOT to GP10.
2025-11-30 06:25:38 [gemini-3-pro] 2.  Resolve GP22: Keep GP22 for `/OE` (Transceiver Enable). Move `R/W` monitoring to the
2025-11-30 06:25:38 [gemini-3-pro] shift register (Shift Reg 4.13 is already monitoring `/R/W`, so you might not need a dedicated edge
2025-11-30 06:25:38 [gemini-3-pro] pin if your PIO loop is tight enough).
2025-11-30 06:25:38 [gemini-3-pro] 3.  Power: Move 74HC165 VCC pins (1.16, 2.16, 3.16, 4.16) to 3V3.
2025-11-30 06:25:38 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 06:25:38 [gemini-3-pro] Good luck with the breadbin.
