2025-11-30 07:05:30 [nitro@ryzen5] hi gemini, latest version of project toast plan attached
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro] Thanks for sharing the latest spec. The parallel shift register readout (4 bits wide × 8 clocks) is
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro] a solid architectural choice for keeping up with the ~1MHz PHI2 bus, but there are some electrical
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro] risks in the "Slop" approach that might turn "Project Toast" into literal toast.
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro] Here is a review of the netlist and logic:
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro] 1. The "Toast" Risk: Open Drain Signals
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro] You correctly identified that Open Drain signals (IRQ, NMI, etc.) should toggle between Low and
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro] High-Z. However:
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro] • The Problem: C64 control lines are pulled up to 5V. When the RP2040 is in High-Z,
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro] the pin is exposed to 5V. The RP2040 is not 5V tolerant (Max V_GPIO is VDDIO + 0.5V).
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro] • The 10k Resistor Fix: While a 10k series resistor limits the current injection into the
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro] RP2040's protection diodes to safe levels (~120µA), it creates a voltage divider against the C64's
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro] internal pull-ups.
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro] • Result: If the C64 has a strong pull-up (e.g., 1kΩ), your 10k series resistor means you
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro] can never pull the line truly Low (Logic Low will be ~0.45V at the pin, but higher at the C64
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro] side).
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro] • Recommendation: Use N-channel MOSFETs (2N7000) or a transistor array (ULN2803) for the
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro] Open Drain outputs to safely switch 5V.
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro] 2. Shift Register Speed vs. 10k Protection
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro] • The Setup: 74HC165 powered at 5V, reading into RP2040 via 10k resistors.
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro] • The Issue: 10kΩ series resistance combined with pin/trace capacitance creates a Low-Pass
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro] Filter.
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro] • Impact: To beat the bus, you need to clock the shift registers fast (likely >20MHz PIO
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro] clock to fit 8 shifts + latch overhead into the valid data window). The RC time constant of the 10k
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro] resistors might round off your square waves, causing bit errors at high speeds.
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro] • Better Fix: Power the 74HC165s at 3.3V.
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro]     *   74HC inputs at 3.3V VCC trigger High at ~2.3V. C64 TTL Highs are >2.4V. This is compatible.
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro]     *   Output will be 3.3V (safe for RP2040).
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro]     *   Eliminates the 10k resistors on the data lines, allowing maximum PIO speed.
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro] 3. Pinout & Netlist Observations
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro] • GP29 (DOT Clock): You listed `GP29` for the DOT clock.
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro]        Note:* If you are using a Raspberry Pi Pico board, GP29 is used internally
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro] for VSYS monitoring and is not exposed as a standard GPIO pad. If you are using a raw RP2040 chip
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro] or a different breakout board, this is fine.
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro] • GP27 Conflict: The list shows `GP27, /DMA out`, but the summary mentions `GP26: ADC0`.
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro] Just ensure you aren't expecting to use ADC functionality on the digital logic pins.
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro] • 74LVC245: Wiring looks correct. 3.3V VCC on LVC is the correct way to interface 3.3V
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro] logic to a 5V bus (inputs are 5V tolerant, 3.3V output drives TTL inputs fine).
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro] 4. Optimized PIO Strategy
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro] With the 4x parallel data lines (GP17-20), your PIO program can read the entire bus in 8 cycles.
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro] • Mapping: Map `IN` pins to start at GP17 (count 4).
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro] • Side-set: Map `SIDESET` to GP15 (CLK).
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro] • Set: Map `SET` to GP16 (SH/LD).
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro] If you need the PIO assembly code for this parallel capture, let me know.
2025-11-30 07:06:19 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:12:06 [gemini-3-pro] This looks like a solid architecture for a "Bus Sniffing" cartridge, particularly the move to
2025-11-30 07:12:06 [gemini-3-pro] parallelize the shift registers to reduce read latency.
2025-11-30 07:12:06 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:12:06 [gemini-3-pro] Here is the "slop" detected on port 8081 regarding the BOM and GPIO constraints:
2025-11-30 07:12:06 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:12:06 [gemini-3-pro] 1. BOM vs. Netlist Mismatch
2025-11-30 07:12:06 [gemini-3-pro] • The Slop: The BOM header lists "3× 74HC165" but the logic requires 4
2025-11-30 07:12:06 [gemini-3-pro] (Address Low, Address High, Data, Control).
2025-11-30 07:12:06 [gemini-3-pro] • The Fix: The Netlist correctly defines 4 units. Ensure the order quantity matches the
2025-11-30 07:12:06 [gemini-3-pro] Netlist ($8.40 calculation implies 4 units, so just a typo in the count).
2025-11-30 07:12:06 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:12:06 [gemini-3-pro] 2. GP29 Availability (Critical)
2025-11-30 07:12:06 [gemini-3-pro] • The Slop: The Netlist assigns DOT clock to GP29.
2025-11-30 07:12:06 [gemini-3-pro] • The Context: The BOM specifies "Pico socket". On a standard Raspberry Pi Pico, GP29
2025-11-30 07:12:06 [gemini-3-pro] is not broken out to the headers (it is used internally for VSYS measurement/mode).
2025-11-30 07:12:06 [gemini-3-pro] • The Fix: You must move DOT clock to a different pin or solder directly to the RP2040 chip
2025-11-30 07:12:06 [gemini-3-pro] if using a custom board.
2025-11-30 07:12:06 [gemini-3-pro]        Suggestion:* If you don't need `UART` for debugging, move DOT to GP0 or GP1.
2025-11-30 07:12:06 [gemini-3-pro] Alternatively, reclaim `ADC0` (GP26) if analog isn't strictly necessary.
2025-11-30 07:12:06 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:12:06 [gemini-3-pro] 3. Shift Register Input Protection
2025-11-30 07:12:06 [gemini-3-pro] • The Slop: The Netlist powers the 74HC165s at 3.3V. The C64 bus is 5V.
2025-11-30 07:12:06 [gemini-3-pro] • The Physics: This relies on the internal clamping diodes of the 74HC165.
2025-11-30 07:12:06 [gemini-3-pro] • The Fix: The "10k series resistors" mentioned in the BOM are mandatory between
2025-11-30 07:12:06 [gemini-3-pro] the C64 bus and the 74HC165 inputs. Without them, the 5V bus will fry the 3.3V-powered shift
2025-11-30 07:12:06 [gemini-3-pro] registers or lift the 3.3V rail.
2025-11-30 07:12:06 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:12:06 [gemini-3-pro] 4. Open Drain Logic Levels
2025-11-30 07:12:06 [gemini-3-pro] • The Slop: Pulling C64 lines (IRQ/NMI) low via RP2040 GPIO.
2025-11-30 07:12:06 [gemini-3-pro] • The Detail: When the RP2040 releases the line (Input/High-Z), the C64 pull-up pulls it to
2025-11-30 07:12:06 [gemini-3-pro] 5V. The RP2040 is not 5V tolerant on inputs; its protection diodes will clamp the line to ~3.8V
2025-11-30 07:12:06 [gemini-3-pro] (3.3V + 0.5V).
2025-11-30 07:12:06 [gemini-3-pro] • The Fix: While 3.8V is a valid Logic High for the C64 (TTL), this constantly drains
2025-11-30 07:12:06 [gemini-3-pro] current through the RP2040 diodes. Ensure the 10k resistors are also applied to these OD
2025-11-30 07:12:06 [gemini-3-pro] output lines to limit that clamping current.
2025-11-30 07:12:06 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:12:06 [gemini-3-pro] Summary
2025-11-30 07:12:06 [gemini-3-pro] The logic is sound, but GP29 is the showstopper if you are using a standard Pico. Move DOT
2025-11-30 07:12:06 [gemini-3-pro] to GP26 (currently allocated to ADC0) to resolve this.
2025-11-30 07:12:06 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:14:24 [gemini-3-pro] Project Toast looks like a viable architecture for sniffing and injecting data onto the C64 bus,
2025-11-30 07:14:24 [gemini-3-pro] particularly by leveraging the RP2040's PIO for the parallel shift register readout.
2025-11-30 07:14:24 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:14:24 [gemini-3-pro] Here are a few technical observations regarding the netlist and electrical safety:
2025-11-30 07:14:24 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:14:24 [gemini-3-pro] • 1. RP2040 5V Tolerance (Critical)*
2025-11-30 07:14:24 [gemini-3-pro] The RP2040 GPIOs are not 5V tolerant.
2025-11-30 07:14:24 [gemini-3-pro] • Open Drain Outputs (GP10–14, GP22, GP27): When driving lines like `/IRQ` or `/EXROM`,
2025-11-30 07:14:24 [gemini-3-pro] setting the GPIO to High-Z allows the C64 pull-ups to pull the line to 5V. This exceeds the
2025-11-30 07:14:24 [gemini-3-pro] RP2040's V_MAX. While the 10kΩ series resistors mentioned in your summary will limit the current
2025-11-30 07:14:24 [gemini-3-pro] flowing into the RP2040's internal clamping diodes, this relies on those diodes for voltage
2025-11-30 07:14:24 [gemini-3-pro] clamping. A dedicated level shifter or discrete N-channel MOSFETs would be electrically safer.
2025-11-30 07:14:24 [gemini-3-pro] • 74HC165 Inputs: Since the shift registers are powered at 3V3 but receiving 5V logic from
2025-11-30 07:14:24 [gemini-3-pro] the C64, the 10kΩ series resistors are essential to protect the 74HC inputs (clamping to VCC).
2025-11-30 07:14:24 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:14:24 [gemini-3-pro] • 2. Bus Contention*
2025-11-30 07:14:24 [gemini-3-pro] • GP21 (/OE): Ensure the logic driving the 74LVC245 Output Enable is tightly synchronized
2025-11-30 07:14:24 [gemini-3-pro] with the C64's `R/W` line and `PHI2` clock. Driving D0–D7 while the VIC-II or CPU is also driving
2025-11-30 07:14:24 [gemini-3-pro] the bus will cause short circuits.
2025-11-30 07:14:24 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:14:24 [gemini-3-pro] • 3. PIO Optimization*
2025-11-30 07:14:24 [gemini-3-pro] • GP17–GP20: Wiring the `Q7` outputs of the four shift registers to contiguous GPIO pins is
2025-11-30 07:14:24 [gemini-3-pro] an excellent design choice. It allows a single PIO `IN` instruction to capture Address, Data, and
2025-11-30 07:14:24 [gemini-3-pro] Control lines simultaneously after the registers are clocked.
2025-11-30 07:14:24 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:14:24 [gemini-3-pro] • 4. Clock Inputs*
2025-11-30 07:14:24 [gemini-3-pro] • GP26 & GP28: The voltage divider (2.2K / 3.3K) correctly steps 5V down to ~3V, which is
2025-11-30 07:14:24 [gemini-3-pro] safe for the RP2040 inputs.
2025-11-30 07:14:24 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:14:24 [gemini-3-pro] If you require a PIO snippet to handle the shift register clocking and reading, please ask.
2025-11-30 07:14:24 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:17:55 [nitro@ryzen5] option is 2N7000 N-Channel FET ($0.90) for the 7 open drain outputs?
2025-11-30 07:18:32 [gemini-3-pro] Yes, using 2N7000 N-Channel FETs is a robust solution for the 7 open-drain outputs.
2025-11-30 07:18:32 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:18:32 [gemini-3-pro] Here is an assessment of that option versus the current netlist approach:
2025-11-30 07:18:32 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:18:32 [gemini-3-pro] • 2N7000 Implementation*
2025-11-30 07:18:32 [gemini-3-pro] • Voltage Safety: It fully isolates the RP2040 GPIO (3.3V) from the C64 bus voltage (5V).
2025-11-30 07:18:32 [gemini-3-pro] • Logic Level: It ensures a strong logic `LOW` (0V) on the C64 bus.
2025-11-30 07:18:32 [gemini-3-pro] • Logic Inversion: Note that this inverts your control logic.
2025-11-30 07:18:32 [gemini-3-pro]     *   GPIO HIGH (3.3V): FET Conducts → C64 Line pulled LOW.
2025-11-30 07:18:32 [gemini-3-pro]     *   GPIO LOW (0V): FET Off → C64 Line Floats (pulled up by C64).
2025-11-30 07:18:32 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:18:32 [gemini-3-pro] • Why this is better than Series Resistors*
2025-11-30 07:18:32 [gemini-3-pro] Your current BOM suggests 10kΩ series resistors for protection. While this protects the RP2040
2025-11-30 07:18:32 [gemini-3-pro] inputs, it creates a voltage divider when driving outputs.
2025-11-30 07:18:32 [gemini-3-pro] • The C64 internal pull-ups (often ~3kΩ to 10kΩ) combined with your 10kΩ series resistor will
2025-11-30 07:18:32 [gemini-3-pro] likely prevent the RP2040 from pulling the voltage below the TTL Logic Low threshold (0.8V).
2025-11-30 07:18:32 [gemini-3-pro] • The 2N7000 eliminates this divider issue.
2025-11-30 07:18:32 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:18:32 [gemini-3-pro] • Alternative Suggestion*
2025-11-30 07:18:32 [gemini-3-pro] If board space is a concern, consider using a 74LVC07A (Hex Buffer with Open-Drain Outputs)
2025-11-30 07:18:32 [gemini-3-pro] or 74LVC06A (Hex Inverter with Open-Drain Outputs).
2025-11-30 07:18:32 [gemini-3-pro] • Pros: Replaces 6 discrete FETs with one SOIC-14 chip; 5V tolerant inputs/outputs.
2025-11-30 07:18:32 [gemini-3-pro] • Cons: You need 7 lines, so you would need two chips (or 1 chip + 1 discrete FET).
2025-11-30 07:18:32 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro] Using 2N7000 FETs is a highly recommended upgrade over driving the Open Drain signals
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro] directly from the RP2040.
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro] Why the 2N7000 is better
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro] 1.  Voltage Safety: The RP2040 GPIOs are not 5V tolerant. The C64 control lines
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro] (IRQ, NMI, etc.) are pulled up to 5V on the C64 motherboard. Connecting these directly to the
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro] RP2040, even in Open Drain mode, risks damaging the Pico because the voltage exceeds the internal
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro] protection diode rating. The 2N7000 isolates the 3.3V RP2040 from the 5V C64 bus completely.
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro] 2.  Current Handling: It offloads the sinking current from the CPU to the FET.
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro] Implementation Notes
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro] • Logic Inversion: Using an N-Channel FET in this configuration inverts your software
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro] logic.
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro]     *   GPIO HIGH (3.3V) → FET ON → Drain pulls C64 line LOW.
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro]     *   GPIO LOW (0V) → FET OFF → Drain floats → C64 line pulled HIGH (by C64
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro] internal resistors).
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro] • Pinout: Ensure the TO-92 pinout is mapped correctly: Source to GND, Gate to GPIO, Drain
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro] to C64 Pin.
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro] Critical Observation on 74HC165 (Shift Registers)
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro] You are powering the 74HC165s at 3.3V (`VCC, 3V3` in netlist), but feeding them 5V
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro] signals from the C64.
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro] • The Risk: The 74HC series input diodes will clamp the 5V signals down to ~3.9V, dumping
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro] current into your 3.3V rail.
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro] • The Fix: The "10kΩ series resistors" mentioned in your summary are mandatory here
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro] to limit that current.
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro] • Timing Warning: 10kΩ is a high value for a data bus. Combined with input capacitance,
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro] this creates a low-pass filter that might "smear" the edges of the 1MHz signals, potentially
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro] causing data sampling errors.
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro]        Recommendation:* If you miss data, try lowering those series resistors to
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro] 1kΩ or 2.2kΩ. This is still enough to protect the diodes but improves signal
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro] rise/fall times.
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro] Updated Netlist wiring for 2N7000
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro] Here is how the netlist looks with the FETs added to the Open Drain lines.
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro] ## 8x 2N7000 FETs (Open Drain Drivers)
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro] # Logic: GPIO HIGH = Signal LOW (Active)
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro] 6.1,  /IRQ,   Gate: GP10, Source: GND, Drain: C64_IRQ
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro] 6.2,  /NMI,   Gate: GP11, Source: GND, Drain: C64_NMI
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro] 6.3,  /ROML,  Gate: GP12, Source: GND, Drain: C64_ROML
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro] 6.4,  /ROMH,  Gate: GP13, Source: GND, Drain: C64_ROMH
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro] 6.5,  /EXROM, Gate: GP14, Source: GND, Drain: C64_EXROM
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro] 6.6,  /GAME,  Gate: GP22, Source: GND, Drain: C64_GAME
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro] 6.7,  /DMA,   Gate: GP27, Source: GND, Drain: C64_DMA
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro] 6.8,  Spare,  Reserved
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:29:27 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:32:28 [gemini-3-pro] Yes, using 2N7000 N-Channel FETs is the superior option for the Open Drain control signals
2025-11-30 07:32:28 [gemini-3-pro] in this context.
2025-11-30 07:32:28 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:32:28 [gemini-3-pro] Here is the technical reasoning and a verification of the updated netlist.
2025-11-30 07:32:28 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:32:28 [gemini-3-pro] Why the 2N7000 is better than Direct GPIO
2025-11-30 07:32:28 [gemini-3-pro] 1.  5V Tolerance: The C64 bus is 5V. The RP2040 is strictly 3.3V. While internal pull-ups
2025-11-30 07:32:28 [gemini-3-pro] on the C64 are weak, connecting a 3.3V pin (even in Open Drain/Input mode) to a 5V rail relies on
2025-11-30 07:32:28 [gemini-3-pro] the RP2040's internal protection diodes, which is risky ("slop"). The 2N7000 isolates the RP2040
2025-11-30 07:32:28 [gemini-3-pro] completely from the 5V rail.
2025-11-30 07:32:28 [gemini-3-pro] 2.  Safety: If your software crashes and leaves a pin in the wrong state, the FET protects
2025-11-30 07:32:28 [gemini-3-pro] the MCU from high voltage.
2025-11-30 07:32:28 [gemini-3-pro] 3.  Drive Strength: The 2N7000 Vgs(th) (Gate Threshold Voltage) is typically 2.1V (max 3V).
2025-11-30 07:32:28 [gemini-3-pro] The RP2040's 3.3V output is sufficient to switch the FET fully "ON" for the low currents required
2025-11-30 07:32:28 [gemini-3-pro] to pull down C64 signal lines (typically <5mA).
2025-11-30 07:32:28 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:32:28 [gemini-3-pro] Netlist Review Notes
2025-11-30 07:32:28 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:32:28 [gemini-3-pro] 1.  Logic Inversion:
2025-11-30 07:32:28 [gemini-3-pro]     With the 2N7000, your logic is now inverted compared to a direct connection.
2025-11-30 07:32:28 [gemini-3-pro]     *   GPIO HIGH (3.3V) → FET ON → Drain connects to GND → C64 Signal LOW
2025-11-30 07:32:28 [gemini-3-pro] (Active).
2025-11-30 07:32:28 [gemini-3-pro]     *   GPIO LOW (0V) → FET OFF → Drain Floating → C64 Signal HIGH
2025-11-30 07:32:28 [gemini-3-pro] (Inactive/Pull-up).
2025-11-30 07:32:28 [gemini-3-pro]        Make sure your firmware initialization sets these GPIOs LOW immediately on boot to
2025-11-30 07:32:28 [gemini-3-pro] prevent accidental bus asserts.*
2025-11-30 07:32:28 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:32:28 [gemini-3-pro] 2.  Shift Registers (74HC165):
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro]     *   You are running VCC at 3.3V.
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro]     *   Input signals are 5V via 10kΩ resistors.
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro]     *   The 10kΩ resistors are crucial here. Without them, 5V into a 74HC chip powered at 3.3V
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro] would trigger internal clamping diodes. The resistors limit the current to safe levels.
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro]     *   Optimization: If you haven't bought chips yet, 74LVC165 or 74AHC165
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro] are 5V tolerant on inputs even when VCC is 3.3V, allowing you to potentially drop the input
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro] resistors (though keeping them for protection is never a bad idea).
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro] C SDK Example for 2N7000 Driver
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro] Since the logic is inverted, a helper function is recommended to avoid confusion.
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro] #include "pico/stdlib.h"
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro] // Define Control Pins
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro] #define PIN_IRQ     10
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro] #define PIN_NMI     11
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro] #define PIN_ROML    12
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro] #define PIN_ROMH    13
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro] #define PIN_EXROM   14
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro] #define PIN_GAME    22
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro] #define PIN_DMA     27
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro] void bus_init_control_lines() {
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro] 	// Initialize all as outputs, set LOW (FET OFF / Signal Inactive)
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro] 	const uint pins[] = {PIN_IRQ, PIN_NMI, PIN_ROML, PIN_ROMH, PIN_EXROM, PIN_GAME, PIN_DMA};
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro] 	for (int i = 0; i < 7; i++) {
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro] 		gpio_init(pins[i]);
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro] 		gpio_set_dir(pins[i], GPIO_OUT);
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro] 		gpio_put(pins[i], 0); // 0 = FET Off = Bus High (Inactive)
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro] 	}
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro] }
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro] // Helper to assert C64 signals (Active Low)
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro] // active = true pulls the line LOW (asserted)
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro] // active = false lets the line float HIGH (inactive)
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro] inline void bus_set_signal(uint pin, bool active) {
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro] 	// With 2N7000: GPIO HIGH turns FET ON, pulling bus LOW
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro] 	gpio_put(pin, active ? 1 : 0);
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro] }
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro] // Example usage
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro] void assert_interrupt() {
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro] 	bus_set_signal(PIN_IRQ, true);
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro] 	sleep_us(10);
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro] 	bus_set_signal(PIN_IRQ, false);
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro] }
2025-11-30 07:32:29 [gemini-3-pro] 
