2025-11-30 08:09:10 [nitro@ryzen5] how fast can pio shift the register?
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] 1. The Commodore 1540
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] • Yes, the 1540 existed.*
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] • Target Machine: It was designed specifically for the VIC-20.
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] • Speed: It runs slightly faster than the well-known 1541.
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] • C64 Compatibility: It is generally incompatible with a stock C64. The C64's
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] VIC-II video chip steals cycles from the CPU to draw the screen ("bad lines"), causing the C64 to
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] miss bits from the 1540 because the drive sends data too fast.
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] • The Fix: The 1541 was released with modified ROMs to slow the data transfer down to
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] accommodate the C64's video interrupts. You can use a 1540 on a C64 only if you disable the C64's
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] video output (blank the screen) via software.
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] 2. RP2040 PIO Speed capabilities
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] The RP2040 Programmable I/O (PIO) is significantly faster than the 74HC165 logic or the IEC bus
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] requires.
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] • Theoretical PIO Speed:*
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] • Clock: Runs at system clock (default 125MHz).
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] • Throughput: Can shift 1 bit per clock cycle (125Mbps).
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] • Loop: A typical "bit-bang" loop to read a shift register (Set Clock High, Read Data, Set
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] Clock Low) takes ~2-4 cycles.
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] • Result: The PIO can easily attempt to drive the shift clock at 30MHz to 60MHz.
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] • Hardware Bottleneck (74HC165):*
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] The limiting factor is the 74HC165 chip itself, not the RP2040.
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] • At 2.0V: Max freq is ~6MHz.
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] • At 4.5V: Max freq is ~30MHz.
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] • IEC Bus Speed: The standard C64 serial bus runs at roughly 1–2 kB/s (standard) or
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] up to 10–20 kB/s (fast loaders).
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] • Conclusion:* The PIO will spend 99.9% of its time waiting. It is overkill in the best
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] possible way.
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] Project Fifteen Feedback
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] • 5V Logic Warning:*
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] The IEC bus operates at 5V. The RP2040 GPIO is 3.3V and is not 5V tolerant.
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] 1.  Reading (74HC165):
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro]     *   If you power the 74HC165 with 5V: The `Q7` output sent to the RP2040 will be 5V.
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] This will damage the RP2040. You need a voltage divider or level shifter on the data line.
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro]     *   If you power the 74HC165 with 3.3V: The 5V signals from the IEC bus entering the
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] shift register inputs might exceed the datasheet absolute maximum (VCC + 0.5V). However, standard
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] 74HC inputs usually have clamping diodes. With your 10kΩ series resistors (from Project Toast
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] logic), this is likely safe, as the current will be limited.
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] 2.  Writing (2N7000):
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro]     *   This is the correct design. The Gate is driven by 3.3V (RP2040), the Source is GND, and the
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] Drain connects to the 5V IEC line. The FET handles the voltage difference perfectly.
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] • GPIO Usage Note:*
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] The IEC bus has 5 signal lines: `ATN`, `CLK`, `DATA`, `RESET`, `SRQ` (SRQ is unconnected on C64,
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] used on C128).
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] Since the RP2040 has ~26 GPIOs and you aren't doing the 44-pin cartridge logic in this specific
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] project, you could technically wire the 5 IEC lines directly to GPIOs (with voltage dividers for
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] protection) and skip the Shift Register entirely to reduce BOM count and latency, unless you are
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] daisy-chaining this with Project Toast.
2025-11-30 08:09:50 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 08:11:50 [nitro@ryzen5] i prefer voltage dividers then, lets drop the shift register
2025-11-30 08:12:22 [gemini-3-pro] Yes, the Commodore 1540 was the disk drive released for the VIC-20. It is physically almost
2025-11-30 08:12:22 [gemini-3-pro] identical to the 1541, but the ROM code runs the serial bus slightly faster. The C64's VIC-II video
2025-11-30 08:12:22 [gemini-3-pro] chip steals cycles from the CPU, making the C64 too slow to keep up with a 1540 (unless the screen
2025-11-30 08:12:22 [gemini-3-pro] is blanked), which led to the slowed-down 1541.
2025-11-30 08:12:22 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 08:12:22 [gemini-3-pro] Regarding the RP2040 PIO: It runs at system clock speed (default 125MHz). It can sample or
2025-11-30 08:12:22 [gemini-3-pro] toggle pins every cycle (8ns). It is vastly faster than the IEC bus (which operates in the kHz
2025-11-30 08:12:22 [gemini-3-pro] range).
2025-11-30 08:12:22 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 08:12:22 [gemini-3-pro] Dropping the shift register is the right choice here. The IEC bus only has 3 main signal lines
2025-11-30 08:12:22 [gemini-3-pro] (plus Reset and SRQ), so you have plenty of GPIOs on the Pico to connect them directly.
2025-11-30 08:12:22 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 08:12:22 [gemini-3-pro] Here is the updated project structure for a direct-connect IEC emulator.
2025-11-30 08:12:22 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 08:12:22 [gemini-3-pro] Project Fifteen
2025-11-30 08:12:22 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 08:12:22 [gemini-3-pro] An RP2040 emulator for the Commodore Serial Bus (IEC), acting as a device (like a 1541) or
2025-11-30 08:12:22 [gemini-3-pro] controller.
2025-11-30 08:12:22 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 08:12:22 [gemini-3-pro] [IEC Bus 5V] ↔ [Voltage Dividers / FETs] ↔ [RP2040]
2025-11-30 08:12:22 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 08:12:22 [gemini-3-pro] Architecture Change
2025-11-30 08:12:22 [gemini-3-pro] • Removed: 74HC165 Shift Register (Not needed, IEC is slow enough for direct GPIO, and pin
2025-11-30 08:12:22 [gemini-3-pro] count is low).
2025-11-30 08:12:22 [gemini-3-pro] • Added: Voltage Dividers for Input (Reading 5V IEC lines safely).
2025-11-30 08:12:22 [gemini-3-pro] • Kept: 2N7000 FETs for Output (Driving Open Collector IEC lines).
2025-11-30 08:12:22 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 08:12:22 [gemini-3-pro] BOM
2025-11-30 08:12:22 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 08:12:22 [gemini-3-pro] • 5x 2N7000 N-Channel FET (Output drivers for ATN, CLK, DATA, SRQ, RESET)
2025-11-30 08:12:22 [gemini-3-pro] • 5x 2.2kΩ Resistors (Input Divider Series)
2025-11-30 08:12:22 [gemini-3-pro] • 5x 3.3kΩ Resistors (Input Divider to GND)
2025-11-30 08:12:22 [gemini-3-pro] • 2x DIN 45322 6-pin connectors (IEC ports)
2025-11-30 08:12:22 [gemini-3-pro] • 1x RP2040 (Pico)
2025-11-30 08:12:22 [gemini-3-pro] • 2x 20-pin female header
2025-11-30 08:12:22 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 08:12:22 [gemini-3-pro] GPIO Interface Logic
2025-11-30 08:12:22 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 08:12:22 [gemini-3-pro] The IEC bus is Open Collector with pull-ups on the host/drive side.
2025-11-30 08:12:22 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 08:12:22 [gemini-3-pro] 1.  Reading (Input):
2025-11-30 08:12:22 [gemini-3-pro]     *   IEC Line (5V) → 2.2kΩ → GPIO Input → 3.3kΩ → GND.
2025-11-30 08:12:22 [gemini-3-pro]     *   Logic High at IEC (5V) ≈ 3.0V at GPIO (Safe logic 1).
2025-11-30 08:12:22 [gemini-3-pro] 2.  Writing (Output):
2025-11-30 08:12:22 [gemini-3-pro]     *   GPIO Output → Gate of 2N7000.
2025-11-30 08:12:22 [gemini-3-pro]     *   Source → GND.
2025-11-30 08:12:22 [gemini-3-pro]     *   Drain → IEC Line.
2025-11-30 08:12:22 [gemini-3-pro]     *   Logic: High on GPIO pulls IEC Line LOW.
2025-11-30 08:12:22 [gemini-3-pro] 
