2025-11-30 08:27:39 [nitro@ryzen5] tx0 and rx0 are gp0 and gp1 on rp2040?
2025-11-30 08:28:02 [gemini-3-pro] Yes, the Commodore 1540 was the disk drive designed for the VIC-20. It is electrically compatible
2025-11-30 08:28:02 [gemini-3-pro] with the C64 but runs slightly faster than the 1541.
2025-11-30 08:28:02 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 08:28:02 [gemini-3-pro] Regarding your technical questions:
2025-11-30 08:28:02 [gemini-3-pro] 1.  PIO Speed: The RP2040 PIO can run at the system clock speed (125MHz+). It is
2025-11-30 08:28:02 [gemini-3-pro] significantly faster than the IEC bus (Standard ~2.4kB/s, Fast ~10kB/s) and can easily handle
2025-11-30 08:28:02 [gemini-3-pro] bit-banging or serialization without shift registers.
2025-11-30 08:28:02 [gemini-3-pro] 2.  UART Pins: Yes, on the RP2040 (Pico), GP0 is UART0 TX and GP1 is UART0
2025-11-30 08:28:02 [gemini-3-pro] RX. To keep these available for USB/Serial debugging, I have mapped the IEC bus starting at GP2
2025-11-30 08:28:02 [gemini-3-pro] below.
2025-11-30 08:28:02 [gemini-3-pro] 
