2025-11-30 08:53:03 [nitro@ryzen5] c64 user port is 24 pin, is there a standard edge connector I can add to the bom for a new design
2025-11-30 08:53:10 [claude-opus-4-5] Yep, 2x12 (24 pin) 0.156" pitch card edge connector. Common part numbers:
2025-11-30 08:53:10 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 08:53:10 [claude-opus-4-5] - Sullins EBC12DRXN or similar
2025-11-30 08:53:10 [claude-opus-4-5] - EDAC 395-024-520-202
2025-11-30 08:53:10 [claude-opus-4-5] - Amphenol/FCI equivalent
2025-11-30 08:53:10 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 08:53:10 [claude-opus-4-5] Search for "24 pin 0.156 inch card edge connector" or "3.96mm pitch edge connector" - same thing.
2025-11-30 08:53:10 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 08:53:10 [claude-opus-4-5] Make sure you get the right profile (active pins on both sides) since the C64 user port uses both
2025-11-30 08:53:10 [claude-opus-4-5] rows.
2025-11-30 08:53:10 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 08:58:03 [claude-opus-4-5] These are solid designs. A few observations:
2025-11-30 08:58:03 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 08:58:03 [claude-opus-4-5] • node.md* - For the user port edge connector, the EDAC 395-024-520-202 is correct. Note
2025-11-30 08:58:03 [claude-opus-4-5] it's 0.156" (3.96mm) pitch. You'll want to confirm the PCB edge thickness spec (typically 1.6mm
2025-11-30 08:58:03 [claude-opus-4-5] works).
2025-11-30 08:58:03 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 08:58:03 [claude-opus-4-5] • toast.md* - The 74LVC245 running at 3V3 driving into 5V C64 data bus might need
2025-11-30 08:58:03 [claude-opus-4-5] consideration. The C64 expects VOH around 2.4V minimum which 3V3 logic meets, but you're reading
2025-11-30 08:58:03 [claude-opus-4-5] back via the 165s - watch for bus contention if the C64 is also driving the data lines. The /OE
2025-11-30 08:58:03 [claude-opus-4-5] gating on GP21 handles this if timed to PHI2 correctly.
2025-11-30 08:58:03 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 08:58:03 [claude-opus-4-5] • fifteen.md* - Clean IEC design. Missing /RESET output driver? You have input on GP6 but
2025-11-30 08:58:03 [claude-opus-4-5] no FET to assert it. Might want a 5th 2N7000 if you need to reset peripherals.
2025-11-30 08:58:03 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 08:58:03 [claude-opus-4-5] • luma2.md* - The 220K bias resistors from GPIO to trimpot wipers - is that for calibration
2025-11-30 08:58:03 [claude-opus-4-5] via software? Clever if so. The LM319 is fast enough for the ~8MHz dot clock bandwidth you'd need
2025-11-30 08:58:03 [claude-opus-4-5] for 320px capture.
2025-11-30 08:58:03 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 08:58:03 [claude-opus-4-5] Any specific area you want to dig into?
2025-11-30 08:58:03 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:00:01 [nitro@ryzen5] for node, i expect 8 data pins to use similar design to toast?
2025-11-30 09:00:11 [claude-opus-4-5] Yes, same approach works. For CIA2 Port B (PB0-PB7):
2025-11-30 09:00:11 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:00:11 [claude-opus-4-5] • Reading from C64:* 10K series resistors to GPIO (static signals, not clock speed)
2025-11-30 09:00:11 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:00:11 [claude-opus-4-5] • Writing to C64:* 74LVC245 with /OE gating, or 8x 2N7000 open drain if you only need to
2025-11-30 09:00:11 [claude-opus-4-5] pull low
2025-11-30 09:00:11 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:00:11 [claude-opus-4-5] The user port is bidirectional per-pin via CIA2 DDRB register, so you need to handle both
2025-11-30 09:00:11 [claude-opus-4-5] directions. Simplest is:
2025-11-30 09:00:11 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:00:11 [claude-opus-4-5] - 8x 2N7000 for output (open drain, directly on bus)
2025-11-30 09:00:11 [claude-opus-4-5] - 8x 10K series for input (to separate GPIO pins)
2025-11-30 09:00:11 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:00:11 [claude-opus-4-5] Or mirror toast's 245 approach if you want to drive high as well as low.
2025-11-30 09:00:11 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:00:11 [claude-opus-4-5] PA2, FLAG2, PC2, SP1/2, CNT1/2 are additional signals worth mapping out if you need serial or
2025-11-30 09:00:11 [claude-opus-4-5] handshaking.
2025-11-30 09:00:11 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:00:11 [claude-opus-4-5] Want me to draft a GPIO allocation for node?
2025-11-30 09:00:11 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:01:24 [nitro@ryzen5] so 8+8 daata gpio starting from 2?
2025-11-30 09:01:35 [nitro@ryzen5] then what?
2025-11-30 09:01:45 [claude-opus-4-5] Starting from GP2:
2025-11-30 09:01:45 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:01:45 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:01:45 [claude-opus-4-5] GP0   UART TX
2025-11-30 09:01:45 [claude-opus-4-5] GP1   UART RX
2025-11-30 09:01:45 [claude-opus-4-5] GP2   PB0 in (10K series)
2025-11-30 09:01:45 [claude-opus-4-5] GP3   PB1 in
2025-11-30 09:01:45 [claude-opus-4-5] GP4   PB2 in
2025-11-30 09:01:45 [claude-opus-4-5] GP5   PB3 in
2025-11-30 09:01:45 [claude-opus-4-5] GP6   PB4 in
2025-11-30 09:01:45 [claude-opus-4-5] GP7   PB5 in
2025-11-30 09:01:45 [claude-opus-4-5] GP8   PB6 in
2025-11-30 09:01:45 [claude-opus-4-5] GP9   PB7 in
2025-11-30 09:01:45 [claude-opus-4-5] GP10  PB0 out (2N7000 gate)
2025-11-30 09:01:45 [claude-opus-4-5] GP11  PB1 out
2025-11-30 09:01:45 [claude-opus-4-5] GP12  PB2 out
2025-11-30 09:01:45 [claude-opus-4-5] GP13  PB3 out
2025-11-30 09:01:45 [claude-opus-4-5] GP14  PB4 out
2025-11-30 09:01:45 [claude-opus-4-5] GP15  PB5 out
2025-11-30 09:01:45 [claude-opus-4-5] GP16  PB6 out
2025-11-30 09:01:45 [claude-opus-4-5] GP17  PB7 out
2025-11-30 09:01:45 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:01:45 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:01:45 [claude-opus-4-5] Then for the remaining signals:
2025-11-30 09:01:45 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:01:45 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:01:45 [claude-opus-4-5] GP18  PA2 in
2025-11-30 09:01:45 [claude-opus-4-5] GP19  PA2 out
2025-11-30 09:01:45 [claude-opus-4-5] GP20  /FLAG2 in
2025-11-30 09:01:45 [claude-opus-4-5] GP21  /PC2 in
2025-11-30 09:01:45 [claude-opus-4-5] GP22  SP1
2025-11-30 09:01:45 [claude-opus-4-5] GP26  CNT1
2025-11-30 09:01:45 [claude-opus-4-5] GP27  SP2
2025-11-30 09:01:45 [claude-opus-4-5] GP28  CNT2
2025-11-30 09:01:45 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:01:45 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:01:45 [claude-opus-4-5] Skip GP23-25 (internal use).
2025-11-30 09:01:45 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:01:45 [claude-opus-4-5] /RESET on the user port could go to GP22 if you don't need SP1, or use an ADC pin.
2025-11-30 09:01:45 [claude-opus-4-5] 
