2025-11-30 09:28:06 [claude-opus-4-5] Good instinct. Bidir on same GPIO with 5V bus is risky:
2025-11-30 09:28:06 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:28:06 [claude-opus-4-5] • Problem:* 10K series resistor protects input, but when you switch to output mode the GPIO
2025-11-30 09:28:06 [claude-opus-4-5] drives directly into the 5V pulled-up line. Even at 3.3V output high, you get current flowing back
2025-11-30 09:28:06 [claude-opus-4-5] through the 10K from the 5V side.
2025-11-30 09:28:06 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:28:06 [claude-opus-4-5] • Your other designs avoid this by:*
2025-11-30 09:28:06 [claude-opus-4-5] - Separate input GPIO (10K series protection)
2025-11-30 09:28:06 [claude-opus-4-5] - Separate output GPIO (2N7000 open drain)
2025-11-30 09:28:06 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:28:06 [claude-opus-4-5] Clean, safe, no mode switching glitches.
2025-11-30 09:28:06 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:28:06 [claude-opus-4-5] • Bidir on single GPIO would need:*
2025-11-30 09:28:06 [claude-opus-4-5] - Always open drain output (never drive high)
2025-11-30 09:28:06 [claude-opus-4-5] - Read back through same pin
2025-11-30 09:28:06 [claude-opus-4-5] - Still risky with 5V bus
2025-11-30 09:28:06 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:28:06 [claude-opus-4-5] Stick with your dual-GPIO approach. Costs more pins but bulletproof.
2025-11-30 09:28:06 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:28:06 [claude-opus-4-5] So for SP1/SP2 full bidir you need 4 GPIOs total:
2025-11-30 09:28:06 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:28:06 [claude-opus-4-5] GP22,SP1 in,Input,10K series,5
2025-11-30 09:28:06 [claude-opus-4-5] GP26,SP1 out,Output,2N7000,5
2025-11-30 09:28:06 [claude-opus-4-5] GP27,SP2 in,Input,10K series,7
2025-11-30 09:28:06 [claude-opus-4-5] GP28,SP2 out,Output,2N7000,7
2025-11-30 09:28:06 [claude-opus-4-5] 
