2025-11-30 09:38:13 [claude-opus-4-5] CIA serial shift register max clock is tied to system clock.
2025-11-30 09:38:13 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:38:13 [claude-opus-4-5] CNT can be driven externally up to ~1MHz reliably, maybe 2MHz pushed.
2025-11-30 09:38:13 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:38:13 [claude-opus-4-5] At PHI2 = 985kHz, internal CIA serial tops out around 985kbaud per channel.
2025-11-30 09:38:13 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:38:13 [claude-opus-4-5] • 8Mbit?* No chance with CIA hardware. The 6526 wasn't designed for that.
2025-11-30 09:38:13 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:38:13 [claude-opus-4-5] Fast loaders like JiffyDOS use parallel PB0-7 for speed, not SP1/SP2.
2025-11-30 09:38:13 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:38:13 [claude-opus-4-5] If you need 8Mbit, you'd bypass CIA entirely and bitbang with PIO on the Pico side - but C64 can't
2025-11-30 09:38:13 [claude-opus-4-5] keep up anyway.
2025-11-30 09:38:13 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:38:13 [claude-opus-4-5] For practical CIA serial work, plan for ~1Mbit max per SP line.
2025-11-30 09:38:13 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:38:13 [claude-opus-4-5] Does that change your design priorities?
2025-11-30 09:38:13 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:38:50 [nitro@ryzen5] cool at 1mbit no problem reading them with shift register capture
2025-11-30 09:38:59 [claude-opus-4-5] Right. 1MHz signal, 74HC165 can handle 25MHz+ clock rates. Plenty of margin.
2025-11-30 09:38:59 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:38:59 [claude-opus-4-5] Shift register captures SP1/SP2 state at sample time, Pico clocks out at whatever rate it wants.
2025-11-30 09:38:59 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:38:59 [claude-opus-4-5] So:
2025-11-30 09:38:59 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:38:59 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:38:59 [claude-opus-4-5] 165 #1: PB0-7
2025-11-30 09:38:59 [claude-opus-4-5] 165 #2: SP1, SP2, PA2, FLAG2, PC2, CNT1, CNT2, spare
2025-11-30 09:38:59 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:38:59 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:38:59 [claude-opus-4-5] CNT1/CNT2 as sampled state (not edge-triggered) works for seeing clock levels. If you need precise
2025-11-30 09:38:59 [claude-opus-4-5] edge timing, PIO direct input is better.
2025-11-30 09:38:59 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:38:59 [claude-opus-4-5] For most serial decode work, sampling fast enough catches everything.
2025-11-30 09:38:59 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:38:59 [claude-opus-4-5] 3 GPIOs for 16 inputs. That opens up:
2025-11-30 09:38:59 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:38:59 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:38:59 [claude-opus-4-5] GP2-9    PB0-7 out (8x 2N7000)
2025-11-30 09:38:59 [claude-opus-4-5] GP10     PA2 out (2N7000)
2025-11-30 09:38:59 [claude-opus-4-5] GP11     SP1 out (2N7000)
2025-11-30 09:38:59 [claude-opus-4-5] GP12     SP2 out (2N7000)
2025-11-30 09:38:59 [claude-opus-4-5] GP15     shift CLK
2025-11-30 09:38:59 [claude-opus-4-5] GP16     shift SH/LD
2025-11-30 09:38:59 [claude-opus-4-5] GP17     shift Q7
2025-11-30 09:38:59 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:38:59 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:38:59 [claude-opus-4-5] 11 outputs, 16 inputs, lots of spare GPIOs.
2025-11-30 09:38:59 [claude-opus-4-5] 
