2025-11-30 09:46:54 [nitro@ryzen5] should CNT1 and CNT2be drivable?
2025-11-30 09:47:05 [claude-opus-4-5] Good question. CNT1/CNT2 are clock lines for CIA serial shift registers.
2025-11-30 09:47:05 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:47:05 [claude-opus-4-5] • C64 as slave (Pico provides clock):*
2025-11-30 09:47:05 [claude-opus-4-5] Pico drives CNT, C64 shifts data on SP. Useful for fast transfers where Pico is master.
2025-11-30 09:47:05 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:47:05 [claude-opus-4-5] • C64 as master (C64 provides clock):*
2025-11-30 09:47:05 [claude-opus-4-5] C64 drives CNT, Pico reads. Standard CIA serial mode.
2025-11-30 09:47:05 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:47:05 [claude-opus-4-5] • Bidirectional:*
2025-11-30 09:47:05 [claude-opus-4-5] Either side can clock depending on mode.
2025-11-30 09:47:05 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:47:05 [claude-opus-4-5] For maximum flexibility, yes - make CNT1/CNT2 drivable:
2025-11-30 09:47:05 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:47:05 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:47:05 [claude-opus-4-5] GP17,CNT1 out,Output,2N7000,4
2025-11-30 09:47:05 [claude-opus-4-5] GP18,CNT2 out,Output,2N7000,6
2025-11-30 09:47:05 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:47:05 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 09:47:05 [claude-opus-4-5] CNT input still via shift register.
2025-11-30 09:47:05 [claude-opus-4-5] 
