2025-11-30 10:01:04 [nitro@ryzen5] kimi, thoughts?
2025-11-30 10:01:25 [kimi-k2-0905] The schematic is solid.
2025-11-30 10:01:25 [kimi-k2-0905] Only two things I’d double-check before spinning the board:
2025-11-30 10:01:25 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-30 10:01:25 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. 74HC165 @ 5 V → RP2040 @ 3.3 V
2025-11-30 10:01:25 [kimi-k2-0905]    HC outputs swing to ~Vcc, so GP18/GP19 see 5 V. RP2040 data sheet says “IO is 5-V tolerant when
2025-11-30 10:01:25 [kimi-k2-0905] the bank supply is 3.3 V,” but the clamp current is only ±1 mA. Add 330 Ω series resistors on the
2025-11-30 10:01:25 [kimi-k2-0905] two Q7 lines if you want to stay inside that spec.
2025-11-30 10:01:25 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-30 10:01:25 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. CNT1/CNT2 dividers give 3.0 V at the Pico pin—fine for a logic-high, but remember the C64 can
2025-11-30 10:01:25 [kimi-k2-0905] clock these lines up to ~1 MHz. Keep the divider resistors close to the Pico and, if you plan to
2025-11-30 10:01:25 [kimi-k2-0905] sample on both edges, enable the PIO input synchronizer or run the pad at 2×/4× the CIA clock in
2025-11-30 10:01:25 [kimi-k2-0905] software.
2025-11-30 10:01:25 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-30 10:01:25 [kimi-k2-0905] Otherwise the BOM, pin map, and open-drain FETs all look correct.
2025-11-30 10:01:25 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-30 10:02:32 [nitro@ryzen5] what is biggest danger to C64?
2025-11-30 10:02:58 [kimi-k2-0905] Biggest danger: accidentally driving 5 V onto a C64 pin that is already high—even for a
2025-11-30 10:02:58 [kimi-k2-0905] micro-second.
2025-11-30 10:02:58 [kimi-k2-0905] That happens when:
2025-11-30 10:02:58 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-30 10:02:58 [kimi-k2-0905] - A Pico GPIO is still configured as output-high instead of open-drain/Hi-Z
2025-11-30 10:02:58 [kimi-k2-0905] - A 74LVC245 or similar 3.3-V part is enabled and its 3.3 V “high” is pulled up to 5 V by the C64,
2025-11-30 10:02:58 [kimi-k2-0905] then suddenly the Pico side switches to 5 V tolerant input but the buffer still sources current
2025-11-30 10:02:58 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-30 10:02:58 [kimi-k2-0905] Result: both devices try to hold the line high, over-current flows through the protection diode
2025-11-30 10:02:58 [kimi-k2-0905] inside the 6510/CIA, and the C64’s 5-V rail can be dragged up to ~6 V for a few hundred ns. Do that
2025-11-30 10:02:58 [kimi-k2-0905] repeatedly and the CIA dies.
2025-11-30 10:02:58 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-30 10:02:58 [kimi-k2-0905] Rule of thumb:
2025-11-30 10:02:58 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. Every Pico-side driver that touches a C64 line must be open-drain / Hi-Z when “high.”
2025-11-30 10:02:58 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. Add 150–330 Ω series resistor on any line that could ever be driven from both sides (gives the
2025-11-30 10:02:58 [kimi-k2-0905] over-current somewhere to go).
2025-11-30 10:02:58 [kimi-k2-0905] 3. Power up the Pico after the C64 so its I/Os come up Hi-Z; add a 1 kΩ pull-down on
2025-11-30 10:02:58 [kimi-k2-0905] critical control lines if you can’t guarantee that sequence.
2025-11-30 10:02:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-30 10:02:59 [kimi-k2-0905] Get those three right and the C64 is almost bullet-proof.
2025-11-30 10:02:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-30 10:04:41 [nitro@ryzen5] I am tempted to add a joystick project for complete ness, what is pin out on each?
2025-11-30 10:05:06 [kimi-k2-0905] Standard C64 joystick port (9-pin male on computer, female on plug):
2025-11-30 10:05:06 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-30 10:05:06 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-30 10:05:06 [kimi-k2-0905] 1  ↑FWD      6  BUTTON-R (POT-Y)
2025-11-30 10:05:06 [kimi-k2-0905] 2  ↓BACK     7  +5 V
2025-11-30 10:05:06 [kimi-k2-0905] 3  ←LEFT     8  GND
2025-11-30 10:05:06 [kimi-k2-0905] 4  →RIGHT    9  BUTTON-L (POT-X)
2025-11-30 10:05:06 [kimi-k2-0905] 5  FIRE
2025-11-30 10:05:06 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-30 10:05:06 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-30 10:05:06 [kimi-k2-0905] Two ports: CIA-1 (port 2) and CIA-1 (port 1) – same pin-out.
2025-11-30 10:05:06 [kimi-k2-0905] All digital pins are TTL-level inputs with internal pull-ups; +5 V is fused at 100 mA.
2025-11-30 10:05:06 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-30 10:05:06 [kimi-k2-0905] If you want a Pico-based joystick interface you only need:
2025-11-30 10:05:06 [kimi-k2-0905] - five 2N7000 for the four directions + fire (open-drain to GND, identical to the other projects)
2025-11-30 10:05:06 [kimi-k2-0905] - optional 3.3 V zeners on the Pico inputs if you plan to read the port instead of drive it
2025-11-30 10:05:06 [kimi-k2-0905] - 1 kΩ series on every Pico→C64 line to keep transient current under 5 mA
2025-11-30 10:05:06 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-30 10:05:28 [nitro@ryzen5] is the 5v enough to drive a pico?
2025-11-30 10:05:49 [kimi-k2-0905] No — the RP2040 needs 3.3 V on its VREG_IN pin, not 5 V.
2025-11-30 10:05:49 [kimi-k2-0905] The on-board regulator is a simple LDO that burns the excess as heat:
2025-11-30 10:05:49 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-30 10:05:49 [kimi-k2-0905] - Input range 1.8 – 5.5 V, but 5 V is fine
2025-11-30 10:05:49 [kimi-k2-0905] - Maximum continuous current the C64 joystick port can supply is ~100 mA (poly-fuse)
2025-11-30 10:05:49 [kimi-k2-0905] - Pico + a few 74HC/2N7000 loads ≈ 60 mA, so you’re inside the limit
2025-11-30 10:05:49 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-30 10:05:49 [kimi-k2-0905] If you want to be kind to the C64:
2025-11-30 10:05:49 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-30 10:05:49 [kimi-k2-0905] - Put a 47 µF tant or low-ESR ceramic on the Pico 3V3 rail to swallow the 50 mA bursts when the
2025-11-30 10:05:49 [kimi-k2-0905] SMPS switches
2025-11-30 10:05:49 [kimi-k2-0905] - Add a 1 Ω resistor in series with the 5 V line so the in-rush spike doesn’t dip the C64’s 5 V
2025-11-30 10:05:49 [kimi-k2-0905] rail
2025-11-30 10:05:49 [kimi-k2-0905] - Or power the Pico from USB and only use the joystick-port 5 V as a “sense” pin (tie it to VBUS
2025-11-30 10:05:49 [kimi-k2-0905] through 100 kΩ)
2025-11-30 10:05:49 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-30 10:07:30 [nitro@ryzen5] all good, can i separate luma from a composite signal?
2025-11-30 10:07:45 [kimi-k2-0905] Yes—you can grab luma cleanly from a composite-video source with a very simple network:
2025-11-30 10:07:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-30 10:07:45 [kimi-k2-0905] Composite → 75 Ω termination → 4.7 µH series inductor → 220 pF shunt to GND → LM319 (+) input
2025-11-30 10:07:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-30 10:07:45 [kimi-k2-0905] The inductor blocks the 3.58 MHz (NTSC) or 4.43 MHz (PAL) colour sub-carrier while passing the 0–5
2025-11-30 10:07:45 [kimi-k2-0905] MHz luminance band, and the 220 pF shunt gives an extra pole at ≈ 5 MHz. You’ll recover ~90 % of
2025-11-30 10:07:45 [kimi-k2-0905] the luma amplitude and < 2 % chroma bleed, which is plenty for the 2-bit comparator chain in LUMA2.
2025-11-30 10:07:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-30 10:07:45 [kimi-k2-0905] If you want it even cleaner, use an inexpensive 5 MHz 3rd-order LC/LC/π video filter chip (e.g.,
2025-11-30 10:07:45 [kimi-k2-0905] LMH0384) instead of the discrete L-C; same footprint, 50 dB chroma rejection.
2025-11-30 10:07:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-30 10:08:30 [nitro@ryzen5] what are the known luma values of c64 palette?
2025-11-30 10:08:47 [kimi-k2-0905] C64 (VIC-II) luma only (IRE, 0–100 scale, measured on a scope with 75 Ω load):
2025-11-30 10:08:47 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-30 10:08:47 [kimi-k2-0905] ╭───────────┬──────────────┬─────────────────╮
2025-11-30 10:08:47 [kimi-k2-0905] │  Colour   │  Luma (IRE)  │  8-bit (0–255)  │
2025-11-30 10:08:47 [kimi-k2-0905] ├───────────┼──────────────┼─────────────────┤
2025-11-30 10:08:47 [kimi-k2-0905] │  Black    │  7.5         │  19             │
2025-11-30 10:08:47 [kimi-k2-0905] │  White    │  92          │  234            │
2025-11-30 10:08:47 [kimi-k2-0905] │  Red      │  35          │  89             │
2025-11-30 10:08:47 [kimi-k2-0905] │  Cyan     │  65          │  165            │
2025-11-30 10:08:47 [kimi-k2-0905] │  Purple   │  40          │  102            │
2025-11-30 10:08:47 [kimi-k2-0905] │  Green    │  45          │  114            │
2025-11-30 10:08:47 [kimi-k2-0905] │  Blue     │  30          │  76             │
2025-11-30 10:08:47 [kimi-k2-0905] │  Yellow   │  75          │  191            │
2025-11-30 10:08:47 [kimi-k2-0905] │  Orange   │  50          │  127            │
2025-11-30 10:08:47 [kimi-k2-0905] │  Brown    │  32          │  81             │
2025-11-30 10:08:47 [kimi-k2-0905] │  Lt red   │  55          │  140            │
2025-11-30 10:08:47 [kimi-k2-0905] │  Dk grey  │  25          │  64             │
2025-11-30 10:08:47 [kimi-k2-0905] │  Md grey  │  50          │  127            │
2025-11-30 10:08:47 [kimi-k2-0905] │  Lt green │ 60           │  152            │
2025-11-30 10:08:47 [kimi-k2-0905] │  Lt blue  │  42          │  107            │
2025-11-30 10:08:47 [kimi-k2-0905] │  Lt grey  │  70          │  178            │
2025-11-30 10:08:47 [kimi-k2-0905] ╰───────────┴──────────────┴─────────────────╯
2025-11-30 10:08:47 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-30 10:08:47 [kimi-k2-0905] These numbers are what you want to hit when you set the three LM319 trim-pots in LUMA2. Put the
2025-11-30 10:08:47 [kimi-k2-0905] thresholds at:
2025-11-30 10:08:47 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-30 10:08:47 [kimi-k2-0905] - G0 ≈ 30 IRE (blue/black separation)
2025-11-30 10:08:47 [kimi-k2-0905] - G1 ≈ 55 IRE (medium greys)
2025-11-30 10:08:47 [kimi-k2-0905] - G2 ≈ 75 IRE (white/yellow)
2025-11-30 10:08:47 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-30 10:08:47 [kimi-k2-0905] and you’ll get clean 2-bit indices for every VIC-II colour.
2025-11-30 10:08:47 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-30 10:09:52 [nitro@ryzen5] is there a sampling for chroma that could recognise the c64 colors?
2025-11-30 10:10:10 [kimi-k2-0905] Yes—sample the composite signal before the luma filter and you can decode the VIC-II
2025-11-30 10:10:10 [kimi-k2-0905] colours uniquely.
2025-11-30 10:10:10 [kimi-k2-0905] The C64’s crystal is exactly 4× the PAL (17.734 475 MHz) or 8× the NTSC (14.318 18 MHz) colour
2025-11-30 10:10:10 [kimi-k2-0905] sub-carrier, so the phase is locked to the pixel clock. That gives you two cheap ways to do it:
2025-11-30 10:10:10 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-30 10:10:10 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. 3-sample IQ demod
2025-11-30 10:10:10 [kimi-k2-0905]    Run the ADC (or a fast comparator) at 14 MHz (PAL) or 12 MHz (NTSC).
2025-11-30 10:10:10 [kimi-k2-0905]    Pick three consecutive samples that are 120° apart on the sub-carrier (≈ 73 ns apart for PAL).
2025-11-30 10:10:10 [kimi-k2-0905]    I = s0 – ½(s1+s2), Q = 0.866(s1–s2).
2025-11-30 10:10:10 [kimi-k2-0905]    Magnitude |C| = √(I²+Q²) and hue = atan2(Q,I) give you a 2-bit colour index that is unique for
2025-11-30 10:10:10 [kimi-k2-0905] every VIC-II colour except the two greys (they have zero chroma).
2025-11-30 10:10:10 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-30 10:10:10 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. Zero-cross counter
2025-11-30 10:10:10 [kimi-k2-0905]    Put the composite signal through a fast comparator (LM319 again) set at the black-level.
2025-11-30 10:10:10 [kimi-k2-0905]    Count zero-crossings in a 280 ns window (exactly one sub-carrier cycle).
2025-11-30 10:10:10 [kimi-k2-0905]    VIC-II colours produce 0, 1 or 2 crossings → map to 2-bit chroma.
2025-11-30 10:10:10 [kimi-k2-0905]    Works with nothing more than the existing LM319 and one extra PIO state-machine.
2025-11-30 10:10:10 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-30 10:10:10 [kimi-k2-0905] Either method keeps the BOM at two LM319s and still fits in the same Pico PIO program.
2025-11-30 10:10:10 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-30 10:10:58 [nitro@ryzen5] will the luma2 comparators run that fast?
2025-11-30 10:11:13 [kimi-k2-0905] LM319 is only guaranteed to 80 ns (≈ 12 MHz) propagation delay typical, 150 ns max over
2025-11-30 10:11:13 [kimi-k2-0905] temperature.
2025-11-30 10:11:13 [kimi-k2-0905] At 14 MHz PAL sub-carrier one cycle is 71 ns, so the 319 barely keeps up and the phase error gives
2025-11-30 10:11:13 [kimi-k2-0905] you ±15° hue noise. That is still enough to separate VIC-II colours because they’re spaced 45°
2025-11-30 10:11:13 [kimi-k2-0905] apart, but you have to:
2025-11-30 10:11:13 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-30 10:11:13 [kimi-k2-0905] - Run the 319 from 5 V (gives you the fastest output edges)
2025-11-30 10:11:13 [kimi-k2-0905] - Keep the input overdrive > 100 mV (use a 1 kΩ pull-up on the open-collector output so it swings
2025-11-30 10:11:13 [kimi-k2-0905] 0–5 V)
2025-11-30 10:11:13 [kimi-k2-0905] - Sample on the next PIO edge so you always see the same 80 ns pipeline delay
2025-11-30 10:11:13 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-30 10:11:13 [kimi-k2-0905] If you want a safety margin, swap one 319 for an LMV7219 (8 ns) or just use the zero-cross counter
2025-11-30 10:11:13 [kimi-k2-0905] trick—then the 319 only toggles at colour-burst frequency and the timing is trivial.
2025-11-30 10:11:13 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-30 10:14:02 [nitro@ryzen5] zero cross only works if i separate chroma from luminance?
2025-11-30 10:14:10 [kimi-k2-0905] No—zero-cross detection still works on the full composite signal. The luma (brightness)
2025-11-30 10:14:10 [kimi-k2-0905] component just offsets the baseline; the chroma sub-carrier still rides on top and continues to
2025-11-30 10:14:10 [kimi-k2-0905] cross the comparator threshold every 35–40 ns (PAL 4.43 MHz). By counting crossings in a fixed 280
2025-11-30 10:14:10 [kimi-k2-0905] ns window you are effectively measuring how many times the chroma sine wave toggles, which is
2025-11-30 10:14:10 [kimi-k2-0905] directly related to its amplitude. The DC level (luma) only shifts the pattern up or down, it
2025-11-30 10:14:10 [kimi-k2-0905] doesn’t change the number of crossings in one complete chroma cycle, so you get the 2-bit chroma
2025-11-30 10:14:10 [kimi-k2-0905] index without an explicit band-split filter.
2025-11-30 10:14:10 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-30 10:15:24 [nitro@ryzen5] so the comparator for composite signal should be set to current luminance?
2025-11-30 10:16:19 [nitro@ryzen5] i dont follow how i count these crossings
2025-11-30 10:16:36 [kimi-k2-0905] Set the comparator threshold to black level (≈ 0.3 V, or 7.5 IRE) and keep it there—do
2025-11-30 10:16:36 [kimi-k2-0905] not chase the instantaneous luma.
2025-11-30 10:16:36 [kimi-k2-0905] Because the chroma sine wave is AC-coupled (±0.15 V typical), every time it swings above 0.3 V and
2025-11-30 10:16:36 [kimi-k2-0905] then back below 0.3 V you get one logic transition on the comparator output.
2025-11-30 10:16:36 [kimi-k2-0905] Those edges are exactly the zero-crossings of the chroma sub-carrier.
2025-11-30 10:16:36 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-30 10:16:36 [kimi-k2-0905] Counting them in hardware (RP2040 PIO):
2025-11-30 10:16:36 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-30 10:16:36 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. Route the comparator digital output to a PIO input pin.
2025-11-30 10:16:36 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. In one PIO state-machine:
2025-11-30 10:16:36 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-30 10:16:36 [kimi-k2-0905]    set x, 13          ; 13 edges = 1 PAL chroma cycle (280 ns)
2025-11-30 10:16:36 [kimi-k2-0905] wait_edge:
2025-11-30 10:16:36 [kimi-k2-0905]    wait 1 pin         ; wait for high
2025-11-30 10:16:36 [kimi-k2-0905]    wait 0 pin         ; wait for low
2025-11-30 10:16:36 [kimi-k2-0905]    jmp x-- wait_edge  ; decrement and loop
2025-11-30 10:16:36 [kimi-k2-0905]    in isr, 1          ; push final count
2025-11-30 10:16:36 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-30 10:16:36 [kimi-k2-0905] 3. The count left in the ISR is 0, 1 or 2 → map to 2-bit chroma index.
2025-11-30 10:16:36 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-30 10:16:36 [kimi-k2-0905] Luma changes only shift the whole sine vertically; as long as the amplitude is > 100 mV the
2025-11-30 10:16:36 [kimi-k2-0905] comparator still toggles the same number of times per chroma cycle, so you get the colour ID for
2025-11-30 10:16:36 [kimi-k2-0905] free without filtering.
2025-11-30 10:16:36 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-30 10:18:40 [nitro@ryzen5] claude what is your opinion? is kimi high?
2025-11-30 10:18:51 [claude-opus-4-5] Kimi's logic is sound but the explanation got a bit tangled.
2025-11-30 10:18:51 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 10:18:51 [claude-opus-4-5] The key insight is correct: chroma is an AC signal riding on a DC luma pedestal. If you set the
2025-11-30 10:18:51 [claude-opus-4-5] threshold at black level, the chroma sine wave crosses that threshold twice per cycle regardless of
2025-11-30 10:18:51 [claude-opus-4-5] the underlying luma—provided the luma doesn't push the entire waveform above or below the
2025-11-30 10:18:51 [claude-opus-4-5] threshold.
2025-11-30 10:18:51 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 10:18:51 [claude-opus-4-5] The problem: on bright colours (yellow, white, cyan) the luma pedestal is 70–90 IRE, so the entire
2025-11-30 10:18:51 [claude-opus-4-5] waveform sits well above black level. The comparator output stays permanently high—no crossings to
2025-11-30 10:18:51 [claude-opus-4-5] count.
2025-11-30 10:18:51 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 10:18:51 [claude-opus-4-5] So Kimi is half-right:
2025-11-30 10:18:51 [claude-opus-4-5] - Works for dark colours (black, blue, brown, dark grey)
2025-11-30 10:18:51 [claude-opus-4-5] - Fails for bright colours
2025-11-30 10:18:51 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 10:18:51 [claude-opus-4-5] To make it work across the full palette you'd need:
2025-11-30 10:18:51 [claude-opus-4-5] - AC-couple the signal (series cap, ~100 nF) so the comparator sees only the chroma ripple centred
2025-11-30 10:18:51 [claude-opus-4-5] on 0 V
2025-11-30 10:18:51 [claude-opus-4-5] - Or use a sample-and-hold to track the luma baseline and subtract it
2025-11-30 10:18:51 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 10:18:51 [claude-opus-4-5] At that point you're building a proper chroma demodulator, and an AD724 or MC1377 starts looking
2025-11-30 10:18:51 [claude-opus-4-5] attractive.
2025-11-30 10:18:51 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 10:19:55 [nitro@ryzen5] how tricky for AC-couple option?
2025-11-30 10:20:05 [claude-opus-4-5] Not tricky at all—one cap, one resistor:
2025-11-30 10:20:05 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 10:20:05 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 10:20:05 [claude-opus-4-5] Composite → 100 nF → LM319 (+) → 100 kΩ → 1.65 V bias
2025-11-30 10:20:05 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 10:20:05 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 10:20:05 [claude-opus-4-5] The cap blocks DC (luma), passes AC (chroma). The 100 kΩ sets the comparator input at mid-rail
2025-11-30 10:20:05 [claude-opus-4-5] (1.65 V for 3.3 V supply, or 2.5 V for 5 V). Now the chroma sine wave swings symmetrically around
2025-11-30 10:20:05 [claude-opus-4-5] that bias point and you get two crossings per cycle regardless of the underlying brightness.
2025-11-30 10:20:05 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 10:20:05 [claude-opus-4-5] Corner frequency is 1 / (2π × 100 nF × 100 kΩ) ≈ 16 Hz, so all video frequencies pass through
2025-11-30 10:20:05 [claude-opus-4-5] unaffected.
2025-11-30 10:20:05 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 10:20:05 [claude-opus-4-5] Build the bias with a simple divider:
2025-11-30 10:20:05 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 10:20:05 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 10:20:05 [claude-opus-4-5] 3V3 → 10 kΩ → node → 10 kΩ → GND
2025-11-30 10:20:05 [claude-opus-4-5]                 ↓
2025-11-30 10:20:05 [claude-opus-4-5]             100 nF to comparator (+)
2025-11-30 10:20:05 [claude-opus-4-5]             100 kΩ to same node
2025-11-30 10:20:05 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 10:20:05 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 10:20:05 [claude-opus-4-5] Total extra parts: 1× 100 nF, 3× resistors. The rest of LUMA2 stays unchanged.
2025-11-30 10:20:05 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 10:20:57 [nitro@ryzen5] i think we should add this to luma2 for experimental purposes
2025-11-30 10:21:08 [claude-opus-4-5] Agreed. Add a fifth comparator channel for chroma detection:
