2025-11-30 17:17:42 [gemini-3-pro] This is a comprehensive suite of retro-computing interfaces for the RP2040.
2025-11-30 17:17:42 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 17:17:42 [gemini-3-pro] • Project Crumpet (Sega Megadrive) and cbm node* (C64 User Port) appear to be the
2025-11-30 17:17:42 [gemini-3-pro] newest additions compared to previous iterations of this list.
2025-11-30 17:17:42 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 17:17:42 [gemini-3-pro] Here are a few technical observations regarding the new and updated designs:
2025-11-30 17:17:42 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 17:17:42 [gemini-3-pro] Project Crumpet (Megadrive)
2025-11-30 17:17:42 [gemini-3-pro] • Timing Constraints: The Megadrive 68k bus runs at ~7.67 MHz. Using shift registers
2025-11-30 17:17:42 [gemini-3-pro] (74HC165/595) to read the Address Bus and write the Data Bus introduces significant serialization
2025-11-30 17:17:42 [gemini-3-pro] latency.
2025-11-30 17:17:42 [gemini-3-pro]     *   This design is excellent for a Cartridge Dumper or Bus Logic Analyzer.
2025-11-30 17:17:42 [gemini-3-pro]     *   It is likely too slow for Real-time ROM Emulation. The time required to shift in 24
2025-11-30 17:17:42 [gemini-3-pro] address bits + control lines, process, and shift out 16 data bits will exceed the bus read cycle
2025-11-30 17:17:42 [gemini-3-pro] window.
2025-11-30 17:17:42 [gemini-3-pro] • GPIO Allocation: `GP12` is listed as `shift out, QH D0-d7`. Assuming this drives the
2025-11-30 17:17:42 [gemini-3-pro] `SER` (Serial Data Input) of the 74HC595 chain, the netlist looks consistent for a serial setup.
2025-11-30 17:17:42 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 17:17:42 [gemini-3-pro] Project Toast (C64 Cartridge)
2025-11-30 17:17:42 [gemini-3-pro] • Latency: Similar to Crumpet, while the C64 bus is slower (1 MHz), the valid data window
2025-11-30 17:17:42 [gemini-3-pro] during PHI2 high is roughly 450-500ns. Serializing address reads via shift registers consumes a
2025-11-30 17:17:42 [gemini-3-pro] large portion of this budget, leaving very little time for the RP2040 to fetch data and stabilize
2025-11-30 17:17:42 [gemini-3-pro] the transceivers.
2025-11-30 17:17:42 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 17:17:42 [gemini-3-pro] Project cbm node (User Port)
2025-11-30 17:17:42 [gemini-3-pro] • Viability: This is a very robust design. The User Port (CIA2) generally handles
2025-11-30 17:17:42 [gemini-3-pro] handshake-driven or slower asynchronous communications, making the shift-register approach
2025-11-30 17:17:42 [gemini-3-pro] perfectly viable here compared to the system bus projects.
2025-11-30 17:17:42 [gemini-3-pro] • BOM/Netlist: The mixture of 165s for inputs and 245s for parallel output (PB0-PB7) is
2025-11-30 17:17:42 [gemini-3-pro] well-optimized for the bidirectional nature of the User Port.
2025-11-30 17:17:42 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 17:17:42 [gemini-3-pro] Project LUMA2 (Chroma Mod)
2025-11-30 17:17:42 [gemini-3-pro] • Zero-Cross: Adding the AC coupling and comparator for zero-crossing detection (`GP6`) to
2025-11-30 17:17:42 [gemini-3-pro] capture Chroma phase information is a significant functional upgrade over the pure Luma capture.
2025-11-30 17:17:42 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 17:17:42 [gemini-3-pro] Project Fifteen (IEC)
2025-11-30 17:17:42 [gemini-3-pro] • Logic: The open-drain FET logic combined with voltage dividers for inputs is the
2025-11-30 17:17:42 [gemini-3-pro] standard, reliable method for 3.3V logic interfacing with the 5V IEC bus.
2025-11-30 17:17:42 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 17:17:42 [gemini-3-pro] The netlists are well-structured for PCB generation. If the intent for Toast and
2025-11-30 17:17:42 [gemini-3-pro] Crumpet is real-time emulation, you may need to switch from shift registers to direct GPIO
2025-11-30 17:17:42 [gemini-3-pro] parallel access (requiring pin multiplexing or larger MCUs) to meet bus timing.
2025-11-30 17:17:42 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 17:18:27 [nitro@ryzen5] shifting is done in parallel so only 8 shifts to snapshot cart pins
2025-11-30 17:18:56 [gemini-3-pro] Thank you for sharing the specifications for Project Crumpet. I have added the Sega
2025-11-30 17:18:56 [gemini-3-pro] Megadrive interface to the context alongside Project Fifteen, LUMA2, Toast, and the CBM node.
2025-11-30 17:18:56 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 17:18:56 [gemini-3-pro] Here are a few observations regarding the new content, specifically concerning the "8 shifts"
2025-11-30 17:18:56 [gemini-3-pro] constraint and the BOM:
2025-11-30 17:18:56 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 17:18:56 [gemini-3-pro] 1. Parallel Output Logic (D0–D15)
2025-11-30 17:18:56 [gemini-3-pro] To achieve the 16-bit write (D0–D15) in only 8 clock cycles, the two 74HC595 shift
2025-11-30 17:18:56 [gemini-3-pro] registers must be fed in parallel, not daisy-chained.
2025-11-30 17:18:56 [gemini-3-pro] • Current GPIO: `GP12` is assigned to `shift out, QH D0-d7`.
2025-11-30 17:18:56 [gemini-3-pro] • Missing GPIO: You will likely need a specific GPIO assigned to the second 74HC595
2025-11-30 17:18:56 [gemini-3-pro] (D8–D15) serial input (DS pin).
2025-11-30 17:18:56 [gemini-3-pro] • Logic: The PIO would output 2 bits per clock (one to GP12, one to the new pin) to fill
2025-11-30 17:18:56 [gemini-3-pro] both registers simultaneously in 8 cycles.
2025-11-30 17:18:56 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 17:18:56 [gemini-3-pro] 2. Bus Contention & Output Enable
2025-11-30 17:18:56 [gemini-3-pro] Unlike Project Toast (which uses 74LVC245 transceivers), Project Crumpet uses
2025-11-30 17:18:56 [gemini-3-pro] 74HC595s for output.
2025-11-30 17:18:56 [gemini-3-pro] • Risk: 74HC595 outputs are always active unless the `/OE` (Output Enable) pin is high. If
2025-11-30 17:18:56 [gemini-3-pro] the Megadrive CPU and the '595s drive the data bus simultaneously, it causes a short.
2025-11-30 17:18:56 [gemini-3-pro] • Recommendation: Ensure the `/OE` pins of the 74HC595s are controlled by the RP2040 (or
2025-11-30 17:18:56 [gemini-3-pro] logic tied to the Megadrive's `R/W` line) so the shift registers are High-Z when the RP2040 is
2025-11-30 17:18:56 [gemini-3-pro] reading via the 74HC165s.
2025-11-30 17:18:56 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 17:18:56 [gemini-3-pro] 3. Input Capacity Check
2025-11-30 17:18:56 [gemini-3-pro] You have 6× 74HC165 registers (48 bits total capacity).
2025-11-30 17:18:56 [gemini-3-pro] • Address: 23 bits (A1–A23).
2025-11-30 17:18:56 [gemini-3-pro] • Data: 16 bits (D0–D15).
2025-11-30 17:18:56 [gemini-3-pro] • Control: ~9 bits available.
2025-11-30 17:18:56 [gemini-3-pro] • Total: 48 bits.
2025-11-30 17:18:56 [gemini-3-pro] This fits perfectly into the 6 parallel input streams (GP4–GP9) over 8 clocks.
2025-11-30 17:18:56 [gemini-3-pro] 
