2025-11-30 18:19:12 [gemini-3-pro] To facilitate writing from the RP2040 to the 68K data bus (D0-D15) using two 74LVC245 transceivers,
2025-11-30 18:19:12 [gemini-3-pro] please consider the following hardware and logic configuration:
2025-11-30 18:19:12 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 18:19:12 [gemini-3-pro] • Power Supply: Power the 74LVC245 transceivers at 3.3V. The LVC family is 5V
2025-11-30 18:19:12 [gemini-3-pro] tolerant on inputs, and the 3.3V output high level is sufficient to trigger standard 68000 TTL
2025-11-30 18:19:12 [gemini-3-pro] inputs (V_IH \ge 2.0V).
2025-11-30 18:19:12 [gemini-3-pro] • Port Mapping: Connect the RP2040 GPIOs to the A ports and the 68K data lines to
2025-11-30 18:19:12 [gemini-3-pro] the B ports.
2025-11-30 18:19:12 [gemini-3-pro] • Direction Control (DIR): Drive the DIR pin High to route data from A (RP2040) to
2025-11-30 18:19:12 [gemini-3-pro] B (68K).
2025-11-30 18:19:12 [gemini-3-pro] • Output Enable (\overlineOE): Drive the \overlineOE pin Low to enable the outputs.
2025-11-30 18:19:12 [gemini-3-pro] Ensure this pin is pulled High (Hi-Z state) whenever the RP2040 is not actively performing a write
2025-11-30 18:19:12 [gemini-3-pro] cycle to prevent bus contention.
2025-11-30 18:19:12 [gemini-3-pro] • Control Signals: You must synchronize the transceiver activation with the 68K bus control
2025-11-30 18:19:12 [gemini-3-pro] signals, specifically asserting R/\overlineW low (Write) and managing address strobes (\overlineAS,
2025-11-30 18:19:12 [gemini-3-pro] \overlineUDS, \overlineLDS).
2025-11-30 18:19:12 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 18:22:38 [nitro@ryzen5]  descriptionGangs of shift registers transport data between millenium.The RP2040 PIO empties shift registers in parallel so only 8 cycles required.Once address decoded and confirmation write cycle is required data is place on megadrive bus with some buffers?
2025-11-30 18:22:55 [gemini-3-pro] Based on your specifications, here is a description of the hardware interface and data flow:
2025-11-30 18:22:55 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 18:22:55 [gemini-3-pro] • System Architecture*
2025-11-30 18:22:55 [gemini-3-pro] The design interfaces an RP2040 microcontroller with a Motorola 68000-based system (Mega Drive)
2025-11-30 18:22:55 [gemini-3-pro] using shift registers for data transport and transceivers for bus isolation.
2025-11-30 18:22:55 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 18:22:55 [gemini-3-pro] • Operational Flow*
2025-11-30 18:22:55 [gemini-3-pro] 1.  Data Ingestion: The RP2040 PIO state machines read the "gangs" of shift registers in
2025-11-30 18:22:55 [gemini-3-pro] parallel. This parallelization allows the microcontroller to empty the registers and reconstruct
2025-11-30 18:22:55 [gemini-3-pro] the data word in just 8 clock cycles.
2025-11-30 18:22:55 [gemini-3-pro] 2.  Bus Interface: Two 74LVC245 transceivers manage the 16-bit data bus (D0-D15). These
2025-11-30 18:22:55 [gemini-3-pro] devices serve as bidirectional buffers and voltage level translators between the RP2040 and the 5V
2025-11-30 18:22:55 [gemini-3-pro] system.
2025-11-30 18:22:55 [gemini-3-pro] 3.  Bus Access:
2025-11-30 18:22:55 [gemini-3-pro]     *   External logic or the RP2040 performs address decoding to detect a valid bus cycle.
2025-11-30 18:22:55 [gemini-3-pro]     *   Once a write cycle is confirmed, the RP2040 asserts the Output Enable (OE) and Direction
2025-11-30 18:22:55 [gemini-3-pro] (DIR) pins on the 74LVC245s.
2025-11-30 18:22:55 [gemini-3-pro]     *   The buffered data is then actively driven onto the Mega Drive bus for the 68000 to access.
2025-11-30 18:22:55 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 18:23:43 [nitro@ryzen5] can i use a buffer chip instead of transceiver and 8 data gpio to write to low and high chips
2025-11-30 18:24:08 [gemini-3-pro] To drive a 16-bit bus using only 8 data GPIO pins, a standard buffer or transceiver (like the
2025-11-30 18:24:08 [gemini-3-pro] 74LVC245) is insufficient. You cannot present the full 16-bit word to the 68K simultaneously if the
2025-11-30 18:24:08 [gemini-3-pro] pins are shared, as the low and high bytes would mirror each other or require precise timing that
2025-11-30 18:24:08 [gemini-3-pro] the bus cycle may not support.
2025-11-30 18:24:08 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 18:24:08 [gemini-3-pro] You require Octal Latches (such as the 74LVC573 or 74LVC374) instead of
2025-11-30 18:24:08 [gemini-3-pro] standard transceivers.
2025-11-30 18:24:08 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 18:24:08 [gemini-3-pro] Here is how this configuration functions:
2025-11-30 18:24:08 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 18:24:08 [gemini-3-pro] 1.  Latching: You connect the 8 RP2040 data pins to the inputs of both chips. You write the
2025-11-30 18:24:08 [gemini-3-pro] Low Byte and trigger the latch on the first chip, holding that data. You then write the High Byte
2025-11-30 18:24:08 [gemini-3-pro] to the second chip.
2025-11-30 18:24:08 [gemini-3-pro] 2.  Presentation: Once both latches are loaded, the full 16-bit word is ready at the
2025-11-30 18:24:08 [gemini-3-pro] outputs.
2025-11-30 18:24:08 [gemini-3-pro] 3.  Bus Isolation: You must control the Output Enable (/OE) pins to ensure the latches only
2025-11-30 18:24:08 [gemini-3-pro] drive the Mega Drive bus when the address decode logic confirms a write cycle. This prevents bus
2025-11-30 18:24:08 [gemini-3-pro] contention with the 68K.
2025-11-30 18:24:08 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 18:24:08 [gemini-3-pro] • Summary: Use 2x 74LVC573* latches. This allows 8 data pins + 2 latch control pins
2025-11-30 18:24:08 [gemini-3-pro] to drive the full 16-bit bus.
2025-11-30 18:24:08 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 18:25:19 [nitro@ryzen5] 74LS374 Ocatl Tri D Flip Flop IC are available at local jaycar NZ$3.00
2025-11-30 18:25:42 [gemini-3-pro] Yes, you can use the 74LS374 to drive the 16-bit bus using only 8 GPIO data lines from the RP2040.
2025-11-30 18:25:42 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 18:25:42 [gemini-3-pro] Since the 74LS374 is an Octal D-Type Flip-Flop (Latch), it has memory, whereas the 74LVC245
2025-11-30 18:25:42 [gemini-3-pro] Transceiver does not. This latching capability is required if you intend to reuse the same 8 GPIO
2025-11-30 18:25:42 [gemini-3-pro] pins to populate a 16-bit bus.
2025-11-30 18:25:42 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 18:25:42 [gemini-3-pro] • Implementation Strategy:*
2025-11-30 18:25:42 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 18:25:42 [gemini-3-pro] 1.  Multiplexing: You can write the low byte (D0-D7) to the GPIOs and toggle the clock pin
2025-11-30 18:25:42 [gemini-3-pro] on the first 74LS374 to latch the data. Then, write the high byte (D8-D15) to the same GPIOs and
2025-11-30 18:25:42 [gemini-3-pro] toggle the clock on the second 74LS374.
2025-11-30 18:25:42 [gemini-3-pro] 2.  Bus Driving: Once both chips hold the correct data, you can assert the Output Enable
2025-11-30 18:25:42 [gemini-3-pro] (OE) pins on both chips simultaneously to drive the full D0-D15 width onto the Megadrive bus during
2025-11-30 18:25:42 [gemini-3-pro] the write cycle.
2025-11-30 18:25:42 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 18:25:42 [gemini-3-pro] • Hardware Considerations:*
2025-11-30 18:25:42 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 18:25:42 [gemini-3-pro] • Voltage Levels: The 74LS series runs on 5V. The RP2040 outputs 3.3V logic, which is
2025-11-30 18:25:42 [gemini-3-pro] typically high enough to register as a logic '1' on 74LS inputs (V_IH is usually 2.0V). However,
2025-11-30 18:25:42 [gemini-3-pro] ensure you do not read from the 74LS374 back into the RP2040, as the 5V output would damage the
2025-11-30 18:25:42 [gemini-3-pro] 3.3V GPIOs.
2025-11-30 18:25:42 [gemini-3-pro] • Timing: Ensure your PIO timing accounts for the setup and hold times required to latch
2025-11-30 18:25:42 [gemini-3-pro] the data into the flip-flops before the 68K bus cycle expects valid data.
2025-11-30 18:25:42 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 18:27:13 [gemini-3-pro] Yes, you can use 2x 74LS374 (Octal D-type Flip-Flops) instead of the 74LVC245/595
2025-11-30 18:27:13 [gemini-3-pro] combination. This is a valid approach.
2025-11-30 18:27:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 18:27:13 [gemini-3-pro] • Why this works:*
2025-11-30 18:27:13 [gemini-3-pro] 1.  Voltage: The 74LS series runs on 5V (required for Megadrive) but accepts 3.3V logic
2025-11-30 18:27:13 [gemini-3-pro] highs from the RP2040 (Input High V_IH is ~2.0V).
2025-11-30 18:27:13 [gemini-3-pro] 2.  Logic: You will multiplex the 16-bit write. You put the low byte on the GPIO, latch it
2025-11-30 18:27:13 [gemini-3-pro] into Chip 1, put the high byte on the GPIO, latch it into Chip 2, then enable the outputs onto the
2025-11-30 18:27:13 [gemini-3-pro] bus.
2025-11-30 18:27:13 [gemini-3-pro] 3.  Pin Count: To free up the 8 GPIOs needed for the data bus, the input shift registers
2025-11-30 18:27:13 [gemini-3-pro] (74HC165) must be daisy-chained (Serial Q7 to DS) rather than reading them in parallel
2025-11-30 18:27:13 [gemini-3-pro] groups.
2025-11-30 18:27:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
