2025-11-30 18:51:13 [gemini-3-pro] I have received and indexed the specifications for Project Fifteen, Project LUMA2,
2025-11-30 18:51:13 [gemini-3-pro] Project Toast, Cbm Node, and the newly added Project Crumpet.
2025-11-30 18:51:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 18:51:13 [gemini-3-pro] Here are a few observations regarding the new and updated content:
2025-11-30 18:51:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 18:51:13 [gemini-3-pro] 1.  Project Crumpet (Megadrive): This is a significant jump in complexity from the C64
2025-11-30 18:51:13 [gemini-3-pro] projects. The architecture relies on a clever optimization: using the RP2040 PIO to clock six
2025-11-30 18:51:13 [gemini-3-pro] 74HC165 registers simultaneously. By reading 6 GPIO pins in parallel for 8 cycles, you capture the
2025-11-30 18:51:13 [gemini-3-pro] entire 48-bit state (Address, Data, Control) very quickly. This is essential to meet the timing
2025-11-30 18:51:13 [gemini-3-pro] constraints of the 68000 bus (~7.67MHz).
2025-11-30 18:51:13 [gemini-3-pro] 2.  LUMA2 Chroma Mod: The addition of the AC-coupled comparator on GP6 changes the nature
2025-11-30 18:51:13 [gemini-3-pro] of the capture. You are now measuring phase/frequency (zero-crossings) within a specific window to
2025-11-30 18:51:13 [gemini-3-pro] derive color, rather than just voltage levels.
2025-11-30 18:51:13 [gemini-3-pro] 3.  Project Toast: The logic mirrors Crumpet but for the C64. While the 1MHz bus is slower,
2025-11-30 18:51:13 [gemini-3-pro] the timing window for `PHI2` is still tight when accounting for the shift register latency.
2025-11-30 18:51:13 [gemini-3-pro] 4.  Format: The `module.exports` JS structure provided for Crumpet is much more robust for
2025-11-30 18:51:13 [gemini-3-pro] automated config generation than the raw text netlists.
