2025-11-30 19:03:34 [gemini-3-pro] Yes, the Sega Megadrive cartridge port has two analog pins used for mixing cartridge audio into the
2025-11-30 19:03:34 [gemini-3-pro] console's sound output.
2025-11-30 19:03:34 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 19:03:34 [gemini-3-pro] Missing Pins in Project Crumpet
2025-11-30 19:03:34 [gemini-3-pro] • Pin B1 (Audio Left / Mono): Analog Audio Input to Console.
2025-11-30 19:03:34 [gemini-3-pro] • Pin B3 (Audio Right): Analog Audio Input to Console.
2025-11-30 19:03:34 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 19:03:34 [gemini-3-pro] Analog Audio & GPIO
2025-11-30 19:03:34 [gemini-3-pro] • Is the audio analog? Yes. The console expects an analog signal on pins B1 and B3. Since
2025-11-30 19:03:34 [gemini-3-pro] the RP2040 is digital, you typically generate audio via PWM (Pulse Width Modulation) on a digital
2025-11-30 19:03:34 [gemini-3-pro] pin and pass it through an RC Low-pass filter (Resistor + Capacitor) to create the analog voltage
2025-11-30 19:03:34 [gemini-3-pro] for the console.
2025-11-30 19:03:34 [gemini-3-pro] • What GPIO are Analog In? The RP2040 has 3 Analog-to-Digital Converter (ADC) pins exposed
2025-11-30 19:03:34 [gemini-3-pro] on a standard Pico: GP26, GP27, and GP28.
2025-11-30 19:03:34 [gemini-3-pro]     *   In your current netlist, GP28 is used for `MD_CLK`.
2025-11-30 19:03:34 [gemini-3-pro]     *   This leaves GP26 and GP27 (currently spare) available as Analog Inputs.
2025-11-30 19:03:34 [gemini-3-pro] 
