2025-11-30 19:40:28 [claude-opus-4-5] Acknowledged. I see five RP2040 interface projects for retro hardware:
2025-11-30 19:40:28 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 19:40:28 [claude-opus-4-5] - Fifteen - IEC serial bus interface (C64/1541)
2025-11-30 19:40:28 [claude-opus-4-5] - Luma2 - 2-bit monochrome video capture with optional chroma
2025-11-30 19:40:28 [claude-opus-4-5] - Toast - C64 cartridge port interface (44-pin)
2025-11-30 19:40:28 [claude-opus-4-5] - Node - C64 user port interface (CIA2)
2025-11-30 19:40:28 [claude-opus-4-5] - Crumpet - Sega Megadrive cartridge interface (64-pin)
2025-11-30 19:40:28 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 19:40:28 [claude-opus-4-5] Common patterns across projects: shift registers for input expansion, 74LVC245 for output, 2N7000
2025-11-30 19:40:28 [claude-opus-4-5] FETs for open-drain signals, voltage dividers for clock inputs.
2025-11-30 19:40:28 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 19:42:36 [nitro@ryzen5] i think crumpet is done, c64 is mono for audio ?
2025-11-30 19:42:44 [claude-opus-4-5] Yes, C64 has mono audio output via the SID chip (MOS 6581/8580).
2025-11-30 19:42:44 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 19:42:44 [claude-opus-4-5] Toast and Node don't include audio - they're data interfaces. If you want audio capture/injection,
2025-11-30 19:42:44 [claude-opus-4-5] that would be a separate consideration.
2025-11-30 19:42:44 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 19:42:44 [claude-opus-4-5] Crumpet includes stereo PWM audio (GP26/GP27) because the Megadrive has stereo output from the
2025-11-30 19:42:44 [claude-opus-4-5] YM2612 FM chip.
2025-11-30 19:42:44 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 19:43:14 [nitro@ryzen5] optional audio in on the luma2 circuit may be feasible
2025-11-30 19:43:26 [claude-opus-4-5] Good idea. The LM319 has 4 comparators - Luma2 base uses 4 (sync + 3 grey levels), chroma mod adds
2025-11-30 19:43:26 [claude-opus-4-5] 1 more.
2025-11-30 19:43:26 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 19:43:26 [claude-opus-4-5] For audio input you'd want ADC not comparator though. GP26-28 are ADC capable on RP2040.
2025-11-30 19:43:26 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 19:43:26 [claude-opus-4-5] Simple addition:
2025-11-30 19:43:26 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 19:43:26 [claude-opus-4-5] Audio input option
2025-11-30 19:43:26 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 19:43:26 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 19:43:26 [claude-opus-4-5] RCA audio in → 100nF → bias node → GP27 (ADC1)
2025-11-30 19:43:26 [claude-opus-4-5] Bias: 3V3 → 10kΩ → node → 10kΩ → GND
2025-11-30 19:43:26 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 19:43:26 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 19:43:26 [claude-opus-4-5] Reuses same bias divider topology as chroma mod. AC coupled, centred at ~1.65V for ADC range.
2025-11-30 19:43:26 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 19:43:26 [claude-opus-4-5] Could share the second LM319's unused comparator for sync detection if needed, but straight ADC
2025-11-30 19:43:26 [claude-opus-4-5] sampling is cleaner for audio.
2025-11-30 19:43:26 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 19:45:08 [nitro@ryzen5] oh nice, perhaps a male connector so it canplug into c64 av port
2025-11-30 19:45:21 [claude-opus-4-5] Nice. C64 AV port is 8-pin DIN (262° horseshoe pattern).
2025-11-30 19:45:21 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-11-30 19:45:21 [claude-opus-4-5] Male DIN-8 262° would let Luma2 plug directly into the C64, picking up:
