2025-12-01 11:13:05 [nitro@ryzen5] * 4 x DB9 to 10-pin header, joy,in* 4 x DE9 to 10-pin header, joy,out* 2 x PSX 6-pin header, DualShock
2025-12-01 11:13:12 [claude-opus-4-5] The original PlayStation controller connector doesn't have a catchy standard name like DB9/DE9.
2025-12-01 11:13:12 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-12-01 11:13:12 [claude-opus-4-5] It's usually just called "PlayStation controller port" or "PSX connector". Technically it's a
2025-12-01 11:13:12 [claude-opus-4-5] proprietary 9-pin connector (7 signal + 2 mechanical/shield pins).
2025-12-01 11:13:12 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-12-01 11:13:12 [claude-opus-4-5] Some catalogues list it as "PS1/PS2 controller socket" or "PlayStation mini-DIN style" though it's
2025-12-01 11:13:12 [claude-opus-4-5] not actually mini-DIN.
2025-12-01 11:13:12 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-12-01 11:13:12 [claude-opus-4-5] For your BOM you could just call it:
2025-12-01 11:13:12 [claude-opus-4-5] - "PSX controller port"
2025-12-01 11:13:12 [claude-opus-4-5] - "PlayStation 9-pin socket"
2025-12-01 11:13:12 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-12-01 11:13:12 [claude-opus-4-5] Or if sourcing: search "PlayStation controller PCB connector" or "PS2 controller socket
2025-12-01 11:13:12 [claude-opus-4-5] replacement"
2025-12-01 11:13:12 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-12-01 11:13:12 [claude-opus-4-5] The 6-pin header you mention - that's your internal breakout to the DualShock signals (data,
2025-12-01 11:13:12 [claude-opus-4-5] command, clock, attention, power, ground)?
2025-12-01 11:13:12 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-12-01 11:13:35 [nitro@ryzen5] * 2 x PSX Sony9 to 10-pin header, DualShock
2025-12-01 11:13:52 [claude-opus-4-5] Sony9 works - clear and descriptive.
2025-12-01 11:13:52 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-12-01 11:13:52 [claude-opus-4-5] Keeps it consistent with your DE9 naming convention too. Anyone reading the BOM will get it.
2025-12-01 11:13:52 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-12-01 11:14:15 [nitro@ryzen5] 5V?
2025-12-01 11:14:20 [claude-opus-4-5] DualShock runs at 3.3V for logic, but the motor rumble needs more current than 3.3V rails typically
2025-12-01 11:14:20 [claude-opus-4-5] supply.
2025-12-01 11:14:20 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-12-01 11:14:20 [claude-opus-4-5] Some setups feed 5V to the controller and let its internal regulator handle it, others use 3.3V
2025-12-01 11:14:20 [claude-opus-4-5] throughout (weaker rumble or no rumble).
2025-12-01 11:14:20 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-12-01 11:14:20 [claude-opus-4-5] What's your board providing? If you're interfacing with 5V logic elsewhere you'll want level
2025-12-01 11:14:20 [claude-opus-4-5] shifting on the data lines regardless.
2025-12-01 11:14:20 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-12-01 11:14:41 [nitro@ryzen5] oh nice, direct connect to gpio
2025-12-01 11:14:46 [claude-opus-4-5] Nice - 3.3V logic means straight to GPIO, no level shifting needed.
2025-12-01 11:14:46 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-12-01 11:14:46 [claude-opus-4-5] Just watch the current draw if you're powering rumble motors - they can spike a bit. Might want a
2025-12-01 11:14:46 [claude-opus-4-5] separate supply or at least decent decoupling if you're running them.
2025-12-01 11:14:46 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-12-01 11:14:46 [claude-opus-4-5] What's the host - Pi, ESP32, STM32?
2025-12-01 11:14:46 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-12-01 11:14:53 [nitro@ryzen5] no motors
2025-12-01 11:14:56 [claude-opus-4-5] Even cleaner then - just the SPI-like signals (data, command, clock, attention) plus power and
2025-12-01 11:14:57 [claude-opus-4-5] ground.
2025-12-01 11:14:57 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-12-01 11:14:57 [claude-opus-4-5] Straight to GPIO, minimal passives. Should be straightforward.
2025-12-01 11:14:57 [claude-opus-4-5] 
