2025-12-01 13:12:50 [claude-opus-4-5] • Summary:*
2025-12-01 13:12:50 [claude-opus-4-5] - 3 digital inputs (buttons)
2025-12-01 13:12:50 [claude-opus-4-5] - 2 analog inputs (paddles, 0-150K)
2025-12-01 13:12:50 [claude-opus-4-5] - 2 digital outputs (annunciators)
2025-12-01 13:12:50 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-12-01 13:12:50 [claude-opus-4-5] No D-pad. Your current U5 mapping assumes digital joystick which won't work for real Apple II
2025-12-01 13:12:50 [claude-opus-4-5] input.
2025-12-01 13:12:50 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-12-01 13:12:50 [claude-opus-4-5] Options:
2025-12-01 13:12:50 [claude-opus-4-5] - Read paddles via ADC (GP26/GP27)
2025-12-01 13:12:50 [claude-opus-4-5] - Or treat as digital-only adapter for modern controllers
2025-12-01 13:12:50 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-12-01 13:14:56 [nitro@ryzen5] what were annunciators used for?
2025-12-01 13:15:08 [claude-opus-4-5] Annunciators were general purpose output bits. Software toggled them for:
2025-12-01 13:15:08 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-12-01 13:15:08 [claude-opus-4-5] - Robotics - control relays, motors
2025-12-01 13:15:08 [claude-opus-4-5] - Home automation - lights, doors
2025-12-01 13:15:08 [claude-opus-4-5] - Music - trigger external synths
2025-12-01 13:15:08 [claude-opus-4-5] - Slot machines - physical coin mechanisms
2025-12-01 13:15:08 [claude-opus-4-5] - Printers - custom parallel interfaces
2025-12-01 13:15:08 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-12-01 13:15:08 [claude-opus-4-5] Rarely used in games. Mostly hobbyist/industrial.
2025-12-01 13:15:08 [claude-opus-4-5] 
2025-12-01 13:15:08 [claude-opus-4-5] For Joy project, safe to ignore unless you want to drive external hardware from Apple II software.
2025-12-01 13:15:08 [claude-opus-4-5] 
