2025-12-01 13:45:57 [nitro@ryzen5] big tidy
2025-12-01 13:46:40 [gemini-3-pro] Hi, I have reviewed the Project Joy section of your WIP document (specifically the "big
2025-12-01 13:46:40 [gemini-3-pro] tidy" revision at the bottom).
2025-12-01 13:46:40 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 13:46:40 [gemini-3-pro] Here are the specific technical points for review:
2025-12-01 13:46:40 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 13:46:40 [gemini-3-pro] 1. Shift Register Daisy Chaining (Wiring)
2025-12-01 13:46:41 [gemini-3-pro] In the "Shift In" section, you are chaining the registers using the inverted output:
2025-12-01 13:46:41 [gemini-3-pro] 	U1.7, /Q7, U2 DS`
2025-12-01 13:46:41 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 13:46:41 [gemini-3-pro] • Issue: Connecting `/Q7` (Pin 7) to `DS` (Pin 10) will invert the data bits for every
2025-12-01 13:46:41 [gemini-3-pro] subsequent register in the chain. U2 will be inverted, U3 normal, U4 inverted, etc.
2025-12-01 13:46:41 [gemini-3-pro] • Fix: Connect Q7 (Pin 9) to DS (Pin 10) for standard serial chaining.
2025-12-01 13:46:41 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 13:46:41 [gemini-3-pro] 2. ADC Voltage Protection
2025-12-01 13:46:41 [gemini-3-pro] You are using a CD4051 to multiplex joystick potentiometers (Apple II / PC) to the RP2040 ADC
2025-12-01 13:46:41 [gemini-3-pro] (`GP26`, `GP27`).
2025-12-01 13:46:41 [gemini-3-pro] • Issue: Vintage joystick potentiometers (especially Apple II) operate on 5V. The
2025-12-01 13:46:41 [gemini-3-pro] RP2040 ADC is 3.3V tolerant. Feeding 5V directly from the MUX to the ADC will damage the
2025-12-01 13:46:41 [gemini-3-pro] RP2040.
2025-12-01 13:46:41 [gemini-3-pro] • Fix: Ensure the CD4051 is powered by 3.3V (clipping the signal range) or, if the MUX must
2025-12-01 13:46:41 [gemini-3-pro] run at 5V, place a voltage divider (e.g., 2.2kΩ / 3.3kΩ) between the MUX Output and the RP2040 ADC
2025-12-01 13:46:41 [gemini-3-pro] pin.
2025-12-01 13:46:41 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 13:46:41 [gemini-3-pro] 3. Apple II Inputs
2025-12-01 13:46:41 [gemini-3-pro] You have Apple II inputs mapped to U5 (Shift Register):
2025-12-01 13:46:41 [gemini-3-pro] 	U5.11, P0, Apple2 Button0` ... `U5.12, P1, Apple2 Button1`
2025-12-01 13:46:41 [gemini-3-pro] • Observation: Standard Apple II paddles/joysticks are analog (150kΩ potentiometers). They
2025-12-01 13:46:41 [gemini-3-pro] cannot be read by a digital shift register (74HC165).
2025-12-01 13:46:41 [gemini-3-pro] • Assumption: I assume U5 is intended for the Buttons (digital) and the MUX is for
2025-12-01 13:46:41 [gemini-3-pro] the Paddles (analog).
2025-12-01 13:46:41 [gemini-3-pro] • Note: If you intend to read the directionals (Up/Down) via U5, this only works for
2025-12-01 13:46:41 [gemini-3-pro] non-standard "digital" Apple II controllers, not the standard analog sticks.
2025-12-01 13:46:41 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 13:46:41 [gemini-3-pro] 4. Latch Bit-Splitting (74LS374)
2025-12-01 13:46:41 [gemini-3-pro] Your output latch mapping splits joysticks across chips to save pins:
2025-12-01 13:46:41 [gemini-3-pro] • Joy 2 is split between U9 (Q5-Q7) and U10 (Q0-Q1).
2025-12-01 13:46:41 [gemini-3-pro] • Joy 4 is split between U10 (Q7) and U11 (Q0-Q3).
2025-12-01 13:46:41 [gemini-3-pro] • Review: This is electrically fine, but your software driver will need to perform
2025-12-01 13:46:41 [gemini-3-pro] bit-masking and shifting to reconstruct the bytes before writing to the latches.
2025-12-01 13:46:41 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 13:46:41 [gemini-3-pro] 5. Shift Register Input Grounding
2025-12-01 13:46:41 [gemini-3-pro] 	U1.10, DS, GND`
2025-12-01 13:46:41 [gemini-3-pro] • Review: Correct. The first device in the chain must have its Serial Input (DS) grounded
2025-12-01 13:46:41 [gemini-3-pro] (or tied low) so that zeroes are shifted in behind the data.
