2025-12-01 15:10:26 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-01 15:10:26 [deepseek-chat] The design efficiently handles 35 digital inputs via shift registers and 20 digital outputs via
2025-12-01 15:10:26 [deepseek-chat] latches, with 10 analog channels multiplexed to 2 ADC inputs.
2025-12-01 15:10:26 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-01 15:11:47 [nitro@ryzen5] resistors needed on all digital inputs?
2025-12-01 15:11:57 [deepseek-chat] Yes, resistors needed on all digital inputs:
2025-12-01 15:11:57 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-01 15:11:57 [deepseek-chat] • Digital Input Protection:*
2025-12-01 15:11:57 [deepseek-chat] - 74HC165 inputs (35 total): 10kΩ series resistors on all parallel inputs from 5V joystick
2025-12-01 15:11:57 [deepseek-chat] ports
2025-12-01 15:11:57 [deepseek-chat] - GPIO shift data input (GP4): 10kΩ series resistor on Q7 chain output
2025-12-01 15:11:57 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-01 15:11:57 [deepseek-chat] • Why:*
2025-12-01 15:11:57 [deepseek-chat] - Prevents overvoltage damage to 3.3V GPIO from 5V joystick signals
2025-12-01 15:11:57 [deepseek-chat] - Limits current during voltage transitions
2025-12-01 15:11:57 [deepseek-chat] - Essential for reliable operation with mixed 5V/3.3V logic
2025-12-01 15:11:57 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-01 15:11:57 [deepseek-chat] • Note:* The 74HC165s are powered by 3.3V, so their inputs need protection from the 5V
2025-12-01 15:11:57 [deepseek-chat] joystick signals. The 80x 10kΩ resistors in BOM covers this plus pull-ups.
2025-12-01 15:11:57 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-01 15:13:57 [nitro@ryzen5] shift registers are 3.3v in joy?
2025-12-01 15:14:07 [deepseek-chat] Yes, in Project Joy the shift registers are 3.3V:
2025-12-01 15:14:07 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-01 15:14:07 [deepseek-chat] • 74HC165 Power:*
2025-12-01 15:14:07 [deepseek-chat] - VCC connected to 3.3V rail
2025-12-01 15:14:07 [deepseek-chat] - Inputs from 5V joystick ports need 10kΩ series resistors for protection
2025-12-01 15:14:07 [deepseek-chat] - 74HC165 can tolerate 5V inputs when powered at 3.3V (typical VIH ~2.1V)
2025-12-01 15:14:07 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-01 15:14:07 [deepseek-chat] • 74LS374 Latches:*
2025-12-01 15:14:07 [deepseek-chat] - Powered by 5V (standard for driving joystick outputs)
2025-12-01 15:14:07 [deepseek-chat] - Inputs from 3.3V RP2040 GPIO need level shifting or direct connection (74LS VIH ~2.0V)
2025-12-01 15:14:07 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-01 15:14:07 [deepseek-chat] • Design Note:* The mixed voltage approach requires careful input protection but avoids
2025-12-01 15:14:07 [deepseek-chat] needing level shifters for the 74LS374 outputs driving 5V joystick ports.
2025-12-01 15:14:07 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-01 15:15:13 [nitro@ryzen5] are the latches 5v in other designs?
2025-12-01 15:15:23 [deepseek-chat] Yes, latches are 5V in other designs:
2025-12-01 15:15:23 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-01 15:15:23 [deepseek-chat] • Crumpet (Megadrive):*
2025-12-01 15:15:23 [deepseek-chat] - 74LS374 latches powered by 5V
2025-12-01 15:15:23 [deepseek-chat] - Driving 5V Megadrive data bus
2025-12-01 15:15:23 [deepseek-chat] - RP2040 GPIO → 74LS374 inputs (3.3V to 5V logic)
2025-12-01 15:15:23 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-01 15:15:23 [deepseek-chat] • Toast (C64):*
2025-12-01 15:15:23 [deepseek-chat] - 74LVC245 transceiver powered by 3.3V
2025-12-01 15:15:23 [deepseek-chat] - But driving 5V C64 data bus (74LVC245 is 5V tolerant)
2025-12-01 15:15:23 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-01 15:15:23 [deepseek-chat] • Node (C64 User Port):*
2025-12-01 15:15:23 [deepseek-chat] - 74LVC245 transceiver powered by 3.3V
2025-12-01 15:15:23 [deepseek-chat] - Driving 5V PB0-PB7 bus
2025-12-01 15:15:23 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-01 15:15:23 [deepseek-chat] • Consistency:* Only Project Joy uses 5V-powered 74LS374 latches. Other designs prefer
2025-12-01 15:15:23 [deepseek-chat] 3.3V-powered 74LVC245 for 5V-tolerant bus driving.
2025-12-01 15:15:23 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-01 15:16:47 [nitro@ryzen5] so c64 sega and atari are the same, active low for button pressing?
2025-12-01 15:16:56 [deepseek-chat] • Mixed standards:*
2025-12-01 15:16:56 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-01 15:16:56 [deepseek-chat] • Atari/C64 (DE9):*
2025-12-01 15:16:56 [deepseek-chat] - Active LOW - buttons short to ground when pressed
2025-12-01 15:16:56 [deepseek-chat] - Pull-ups required on all inputs (5V typically)
2025-12-01 15:16:56 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-01 15:16:56 [deepseek-chat] • Sega (DE9):*
2025-12-01 15:16:56 [deepseek-chat] - Active HIGH - buttons output +5V when pressed
2025-12-01 15:16:56 [deepseek-chat] - Different pinout and multiplexing scheme
2025-12-01 15:16:56 [deepseek-chat] - Requires select line (pin 7) to read extra buttons
2025-12-01 15:16:56 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-01 15:16:56 [deepseek-chat] • Key difference:*
2025-12-01 15:16:56 [deepseek-chat] - Atari/C64: Pressed = 0V (LOW)
2025-12-01 15:16:56 [deepseek-chat] - Sega: Pressed = +5V (HIGH)
2025-12-01 15:16:56 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-01 15:16:56 [deepseek-chat] Project Joy handles both by using shift registers that capture the actual voltage state, but needs
2025-12-01 15:16:56 [deepseek-chat] to accommodate the different active levels in software.
2025-12-01 15:16:56 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-01 15:18:40 [nitro@ryzen5] sorry all db9 joypads are active low
2025-12-01 15:18:50 [deepseek-chat] You're absolutely right - my mistake. All classic DB9 joysticks are active low:
2025-12-01 15:18:50 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-01 15:18:50 [deepseek-chat] • Atari/C64/Sega (DB9):*
2025-12-01 15:18:50 [deepseek-chat] - All buttons and directions short to ground when pressed
2025-12-01 15:18:50 [deepseek-chat] - Pull-ups required on all inputs
2025-12-01 15:18:50 [deepseek-chat] - Standard pinout across platforms
2025-12-01 15:18:50 [deepseek-chat] 
