2025-12-01 16:54:28 [gpt-5-nano] Good question. From the Joy GPIO map, a couple of signals are missing or ambiguous:
2025-12-01 16:54:28 [gpt-5-nano] 
2025-12-01 16:54:28 [gpt-5-nano] - GP9: currently unassigned. A good candidate for a debug, test, or spare signal (e.g., JOY_DEBUG,
2025-12-01 16:54:28 [gpt-5-nano] or an extra control line if you later repurpose a port).
2025-12-01 16:54:28 [gpt-5-nano] 
2025-12-01 16:54:28 [gpt-5-nano] - GP22: currently unassigned. Could be used for a spare/open-drain enable, an optional LED, or a
2025-12-01 16:54:28 [gpt-5-nano] status/sense line.
2025-12-01 16:54:28 [gpt-5-nano] 
2025-12-01 16:54:28 [gpt-5-nano] - GP28: there’s an inconsistency in the docs:
2025-12-01 16:54:28 [gpt-5-nano]   - One part lists GP28 as WS2812B (LED strip).
2025-12-01 16:54:28 [gpt-5-nano]   - Another part lists GP28 as an external clock input (CLK) with a 2.2k/3.3k divider.
2025-12-01 16:54:28 [gpt-5-nano]   Decide one role. If you truly need both, you’ll need to rework pin usage or bring in an extra MCU
2025-12-01 16:54:28 [gpt-5-nano] pin or external mux.
2025-12-01 16:54:28 [gpt-5-nano] 
2025-12-01 16:54:28 [gpt-5-nano] Other notes:
2025-12-01 16:54:28 [gpt-5-nano] - The 24 FET gates (Q1–Q24) are not MCU pins. They’re outputs of the three 74LS374 latches (U6–U8).
2025-12-01 16:54:28 [gpt-5-nano] The MCU only drives D0–D7 (GP10–GP17) and the latch controls (GP18–GP21) plus the common shift
2025-12-01 16:54:28 [gpt-5-nano] clock (GP2) and shift/load (GP3). No direct MCU pins for Q1–Q24, unless you route them back through
2025-12-01 16:54:28 [gpt-5-nano] additional hardware.
2025-12-01 16:54:28 [gpt-5-nano] - If you want to expose any of the Q1–Q24 signals, you’d need to add extra gating or taps from the
2025-12-01 16:54:28 [gpt-5-nano] LS374 outputs.
2025-12-01 16:54:28 [gpt-5-nano] 
