2025-12-01 17:42:39 [nitro@ryzen5] summary of pio steps to read and write joy
2025-12-01 17:43:06 [gemini-3-pro] Based on the pinout and component specifications, here is the summary of PIO steps required to
2025-12-01 17:43:06 [gemini-3-pro] handle the inputs and outputs.
2025-12-01 17:43:06 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 17:43:06 [gemini-3-pro] Input PIO Strategy (74HC165 Shift Chain)
2025-12-01 17:43:06 [gemini-3-pro] • Goal:* Read 40 bits of data (5 chips × 8 bits) from `SHIFTDATAIN` (GP4).
2025-12-01 17:43:06 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 17:43:06 [gemini-3-pro] 1.  Pin Configuration: Map `SHIFTCLK` (GP2) as a side-set pin, `SHIFTSHLD`
2025-12-01 17:43:06 [gemini-3-pro] (GP3) as a set pin, and `SHIFTDATA_IN` (GP4) as the input source.
2025-12-01 17:43:06 [gemini-3-pro] 2.  Latch Step: Drive `SHIFTSHLD` low for one cycle, then high. This captures the
2025-12-01 17:43:06 [gemini-3-pro] parallel state of the buttons into the shift registers.
2025-12-01 17:43:06 [gemini-3-pro] 3.  Shift Loop: Enter a loop to read the data stream.
2025-12-01 17:43:06 [gemini-3-pro]     *   Read the state of `SHIFTDATAIN` using the `in` instruction.
2025-12-01 17:43:06 [gemini-3-pro]     *   Toggle `SHIFT_CLK` high then low using side-set to shift the next bit into position.
2025-12-01 17:43:06 [gemini-3-pro] 4.  Push: Enable auto-push on the State Machine (e.g., every 8 or 32 bits) to send the
2025-12-01 17:43:06 [gemini-3-pro] collected button states to the main processor via the RX FIFO.
2025-12-01 17:43:06 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 17:43:06 [gemini-3-pro] Output PIO Strategy (74LS374 Latch Array)
2025-12-01 17:43:06 [gemini-3-pro] • Goal:* Drive 24 bits of data to 3 latches using a shared 8-bit bus (GP10-GP17) and
2025-12-01 17:43:06 [gemini-3-pro] individual clocks (GP18-GP20).
2025-12-01 17:43:06 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 17:43:06 [gemini-3-pro] 1.  Pin Configuration: Map GP10-GP17 as the `out` pin group (Data Bus). Map GP18 (CP0),
2025-12-01 17:43:06 [gemini-3-pro] GP19 (CP1), and GP20 (CP2) as `set` or `side-set` pins.
2025-12-01 17:43:06 [gemini-3-pro] 2.  Data Formatting: The main CPU packs the 24 bits of FET state data into a single 32-bit
2025-12-01 17:43:06 [gemini-3-pro] word and pushes it to the TX FIFO.
2025-12-01 17:43:06 [gemini-3-pro] 3.  Latch 1 (Joy 1+2):
2025-12-01 17:43:06 [gemini-3-pro]     *   Pull 8 bits from the FIFO to the Output pins (GP10-GP17).
2025-12-01 17:43:06 [gemini-3-pro]     *   Pulse `LATCH_CP0` (GP18) high then low to lock the data into U6.
2025-12-01 17:43:06 [gemini-3-pro] 4.  Latch 2 (Joy 2+3+4):
2025-12-01 17:43:06 [gemini-3-pro]     *   Pull the next 8 bits from the FIFO to the Output pins.
2025-12-01 17:43:06 [gemini-3-pro]     *   Pulse `LATCH_CP1` (GP19) high then low to lock the data into U7.
2025-12-01 17:43:06 [gemini-3-pro] 5.  Latch 3 (Joy 4 + Apple):
2025-12-01 17:43:06 [gemini-3-pro]     *   Pull the final 8 bits from the FIFO to the Output pins.
2025-12-01 17:43:06 [gemini-3-pro]     *   Pulse `LATCH_CP2` (GP20) high then low to lock the data into U8.
2025-12-01 17:43:06 [gemini-3-pro] 6.  Repeat: The PIO program wraps back to the start, waiting for the next data update from
2025-12-01 17:43:06 [gemini-3-pro] the CPU.
2025-12-01 17:43:06 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] This is a fascinating collection of hardware definitions. You have established a distinct "Vintage
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] Kid" architecture: using the RP2040's high-speed PIO to trade pin-count for shift-register
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] cycles, allowing a low-pin-count MCU to interface with wide retro buses.
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] Here are my comments on the specific designs, focusing on novelty, technical feasibility, and
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] potential "gotchas."
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] 1. Project Crumpet (Megadrive Cartridge)
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] • Status:* Highly Experimental / Aggressive Timing.
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] • The "Parallel Shift" Strategy: Reading 6x 74HC165s in parallel is the only way this
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] works. The Megadrive has a ~130ns access cycle.
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro]        The Math:* You need to read address lines, decide on data, and assert data. If your
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] PIO runs at ~133MHz (7.5ns/cycle), and you shift 8 times, that is approx 60ns + overhead. This
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] leaves very little room for logic lookup (ROM emulation), but is sufficient for simple banking or
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] streaming.
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] • Voltage Handling: You are using 10kΩ series resistors to rely on the RP2040's internal
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] clamping diodes for 5V->3.3V logic. While common in hobbyist "hacks," be aware that this injects
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] current into the 3.3V rail. With 48 resistors (Address/Data) all high, you might raise the rail
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] voltage if the load is light.
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] • Novelty: Most MD flash carts use CPLDs/FPGAs. Doing this with purely shift registers and
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] an MCU is a unique challenge.
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] 2. Project Joy (Universal Input Hub)
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] • Status:* Solid Design / High Utility.
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] • Remote Driving: Using 74LS374 latches to drive 2N7000 FET gates is an excellent way to
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] simulate button presses safely. It isolates the RP2040 from the target console's voltage and
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] polling logic.
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] • Apple II Support: Including the Analog Mux (CD4051) for Paddles makes this significantly
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] more versatile than standard "digital only" adapters.
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] • Feedback: Ensure the "Sega Select" (Pin 7) logic on the output side handles the fast
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] switching required by the Genesis controller polling.
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] 3. Project LUMA2 (Video Capture)
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] • Status:* Creative / Niche.
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] • Comparator Approach: Using LM319s with trim pots is a very "analog" solution to a digital
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] problem. It avoids the need for a high-speed ADC by treating video levels as digital thresholds.
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] • Chroma Mod: This is the hardest part. Counting edges in a ~280ns window (C64 pixel clock)
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] via PIO to determine phase/color is theoretically possible but requires extremely precise
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] synchronization with the `CSYNC` signal.
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] 4. Project Toast (C64 Expansion)
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] • Status:* Feasible.
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] • Timing: The C64 bus (1MHz) is much more forgiving than the Megadrive. The shift register
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] approach here is very safe.
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] • Open Drain Control: Using FETs for `/IRQ`, `/NMI`, `/GAME`, and `/EXROM` is the correct
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] approach to play nice with the C64's open-collector lines.
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] Software Helper Suggestion
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] Given your heavy reliance on mapping serial bitstreams from Shift Registers to logical names, a
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] Javascript helper to generate bitmasks for your PIO code would be valuable.
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] Here is a generator function based on your `Project Joy` definition:
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] /**
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro]  * Generates bitmasks and shift offsets for PIO state machines
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro]  * based on the physical wiring of the 74HC165 chain.
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro]  */
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] function generateShiftMap(shiftChain) {
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] 	const map = {};
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] 	let globalBitIndex = 0;
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] 	// Iterate through the chained chips (U1 -> U5)
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] 	shiftChain.forEach((chip) => {
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] 		chip.inputs.forEach((inputName, pinIndex) => {
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] 			if (!inputName) return; // Skip NC
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] 			// Calculate mask based on position in the stream
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] 			// Assuming MSB first or LSB first depending on PIO 'in' config
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] 			// This example assumes bit 0 is the first bit read
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] 			map[`${chip.device}_${inputName}`] = {
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] 				chip: chip.id,
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] 				bitIndex: globalBitIndex,
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] 				mask: `(1 << ${globalBitIndex})`,
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] 				hexMask: `0x${(1n << BigInt(globalBitIndex)).toString(16)}`
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] 			};
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] 			globalBitIndex++;
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] 		});
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] 	});
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] 	return {
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] 		totalBits: globalBitIndex,
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] 		mapping: map
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] 	};
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] }
2025-12-01 17:54:22 [gemini-3-pro] 
