2025-12-01 18:09:35 [gemini-3-pro] Thank you for the detailed update. The specifications for the Vintage Kid suite are
2025-12-01 18:09:35 [gemini-3-pro] comprehensive.
2025-12-01 18:09:35 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 18:09:35 [gemini-3-pro] Here are observations regarding the new BOM details and technical consistency across the designs:
2025-12-01 18:09:35 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 18:09:35 [gemini-3-pro] 1. Component Selection & Logic Levels
2025-12-01 18:09:36 [gemini-3-pro] • 74HC165 (Shift Inputs): You are powering these at 3.3V.
2025-12-01 18:09:36 [gemini-3-pro]     *   Crumpet/Toast: You specified 10kΩ series resistors. This is the correct
2025-12-01 18:09:36 [gemini-3-pro] value to limit current through the clamping diodes when interfacing 5V targets.
2025-12-01 18:09:36 [gemini-3-pro]     *   Node: You specified 1kΩ series resistors. Recommendation: Increase
2025-12-01 18:09:36 [gemini-3-pro] "Node" resistors to 10kΩ to match Crumpet/Toast. 1kΩ allows ~1.7mA through the clamp diodes
2025-12-01 18:09:36 [gemini-3-pro] (5V - 3.3V / 1000), which is safe but higher than necessary. 10kΩ reduces this to ~0.17mA.
2025-12-01 18:09:36 [gemini-3-pro] • 74LS374 (Latches): You have selected LS (Low-power Schottky) TTL.
2025-12-01 18:09:36 [gemini-3-pro]     *   These run on 5V. The RP2040 (3.3V) can reliably drive LS TTL inputs (V_IH = 2.0V).
2025-12-01 18:09:36 [gemini-3-pro]     *   Note: LS parts consume significantly more static power than CMOS (HC/HCT). Ensure
2025-12-01 18:09:36 [gemini-3-pro] your power budget (USB or external) can handle the quiescent current of multiple LS chips, though
2025-12-01 18:09:36 [gemini-3-pro] for mains-powered retro consoles this is rarely an issue.
2025-12-01 18:09:36 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 18:09:36 [gemini-3-pro] 2. Project Crumpet (Megadrive)
2025-12-01 18:09:36 [gemini-3-pro] • Timing Constraint: The architecture uses shift registers to read the Address Bus
2025-12-01 18:09:36 [gemini-3-pro] (A1-A23).
2025-12-01 18:09:36 [gemini-3-pro]     *   Observation: Serializing a 23-bit address read via PIO takes significant clock
2025-12-01 18:09:36 [gemini-3-pro] cycles compared to the Megadrive's access window (~150ns-250ns).
2025-12-01 18:09:36 [gemini-3-pro]     *   Implication: This design is excellent for a Cartridge Dumper/Writer or
2025-12-01 18:09:36 [gemini-3-pro] save-game manager. However, it may be too slow to act as a real-time Flash Cart (ROM emulator) for
2025-12-01 18:09:36 [gemini-3-pro] playing games, as the RP2040 cannot shift address in, fetch data, and latch data out fast enough
2025-12-01 18:09:36 [gemini-3-pro] for the 68000 bus cycle.
2025-12-01 18:09:36 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 18:09:36 [gemini-3-pro] 3. Project Joy
2025-12-01 18:09:36 [gemini-3-pro] • Sega Mux: The logic correctly handles the specific multiplexing required for Sega
2025-12-01 18:09:36 [gemini-3-pro] 3-button and 6-button controllers using the `SEGA_SEL` line.
2025-12-01 18:09:36 [gemini-3-pro] • Protection: The use of 10kΩ series resistors on the 74HC165 inputs is crucial here, as
2025-12-01 18:09:36 [gemini-3-pro] vintage controllers often short lines directly to 5V.
2025-12-01 18:09:36 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 18:09:36 [gemini-3-pro] 4. Project LUMA2
2025-12-01 18:09:36 [gemini-3-pro] • Comparators: The switch to LM319 is noted. This is a high-speed comparator (80ns
2025-12-01 18:09:36 [gemini-3-pro] response), which is suitable for the pixel clock of the C64.
2025-12-01 18:09:36 [gemini-3-pro] • Grey Levels: With 3 comparators for luma (plus sync), you can detect 4 states (Sync,
2025-12-01 18:09:36 [gemini-3-pro] Dark, Mid, Light). To get full 2-bit luma (Black, Dark Grey, Light Grey, White), the threshold
2025-12-01 18:09:36 [gemini-3-pro] tuning via the Piher trimpots will be critical.
2025-12-01 18:09:36 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 18:09:36 [gemini-3-pro] 5. BOM & Sourcing
2025-12-01 18:09:36 [gemini-3-pro] • The Jaycar parts listed are standard through-hole components, making assembly accessible.
2025-12-01 18:09:36 [gemini-3-pro] • 2N7000: Excellent choice for the "Open Drain" simulation on the C64/Amiga control lines
2025-12-01 18:09:36 [gemini-3-pro] (/IRQ, /NMI, /DTACK). This prevents bus contention voltage spikes.
2025-12-01 18:09:36 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 18:09:36 [gemini-3-pro] Summary of Changes
2025-12-01 18:09:36 [gemini-3-pro] The primary new content is the specific Jaycar BOM integration and the finalized
2025-12-01 18:09:36 [gemini-3-pro] GPIO/Netlist for Project Node. The logic remains consistent with previous iterations, with
2025-12-01 18:09:36 [gemini-3-pro] the specific refinement of using 74LVC245 for high-speed output buffering and 74HC165/Resistors for
2025-12-01 18:09:36 [gemini-3-pro] input protection.
2025-12-01 18:09:36 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 18:10:13 [nitro@ryzen5] just looking at first draft of parts, mostly i can source from jaycar locally which i love
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro] It is excellent that you can source these locally from Jaycar. For prototyping, the ability to walk
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro] into a store and pick up parts same-day beats waiting for overseas shipping, even if the per-unit
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro] cost is slightly higher.
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro] Here is a technical sanity check on your selected parts against the RP2040 logic levels and your
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro] specific project requirements:
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro] 1. 74LS374 (Octal Flip-Flop)
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro] • Application: Output latches (Crumpet, Joy).
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro] • Compatibility: Good.
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro]     *   You will power these with 5V (VBUS).
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro]     *   The RP2040 (3.3V) can successfully drive the inputs of the "LS" series (Low-power
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro] Schottky). LS logic considers anything over 2.0V as a HIGH, so the 3.3V output from the Pico is
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro] well within range.
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro]     *   Note: LS chips consume more power than CMOS (HC/HCT) chips, but for a mains-powered
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro] console or USB-powered hub, this is negligible.
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro] 2. 74HC165 (Shift Register)
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro] • Application: Parallel Inputs (Crumpet, Joy, Node, Toast).
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro] • Compatibility: Good (with your design).
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro]     *   You will power these with 3.3V (3V3).
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro]     *   Crucial: Your design includes 10kΩ series resistors on the inputs. This is
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro] essential because you are feeding 5V signals (from the consoles) into a chip running at 3.3V. The
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro] resistors limit the current into the internal clamping diodes of the 74HC165, protecting the chip
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro] and the RP2040. Without the resistors, the magic smoke would escape.
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro] 3. 74LVC245 (Transceiver)
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro] • Application: Bidirectional Data Bus / Buffers (Crumpet, Node, Toast).
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro] • Compatibility: Perfect.
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro]     *   The "LVC" family is the gold standard for 3.3V <-> 5V interfacing.
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro]     *   They are 5V tolerant on inputs even when powered at 3.3V.
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro]     *   They are fast enough for the Megadrive and C64 bus speeds.
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro] 4. 2N7000 (N-Channel FET)
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro] • Application: Open Drain logic (driving lines LOW).
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro] • Compatibility: Good.
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro]     *   The Gate Threshold Voltage (V_GS(th)) of a 2N7000 is typically between 0.8V and 3.0V.
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro]     *   The RP2040 drives 3.3V, which is enough to fully switch these FETs "ON" to pull the signal
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro] lines to Ground.
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro]     *   This is a very robust way to handle "Active Low" signals on vintage buses without risking
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro] voltage contention.
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro] 5. 10K Piher Trimpot
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro] • Application: Luma2 Video Thresholds.
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro] • Compatibility: Good.
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro]     *   Piher is a reputable brand; these horizontal mounts are physically stable, which is helpful
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro] for video tuning where vibration might otherwise cause flickering.
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro] Summary
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro] Your "Jaycar Draft" is technically sound. You have navigated the voltage mismatch between the
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro] modern RP2040 (3.3V) and vintage hardware (5V) correctly by mixing logic families (LS for 5V
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro] output, LVC for bus buffering, HC at 3.3V for inputs).
2025-12-01 18:10:45 [gemini-3-pro] 
