2025-12-01 18:55:18 [nitro@ryzen5] yes to stereo audio, i worry that chroma sampling will need some more bits / comparators
2025-12-01 18:56:20 [nitro@ryzen5] or is edge counting as good as it gets?
2025-12-01 18:56:55 [gemini-3-pro] Yes, Luma2 has plenty of spare GPIO. The RP2040 exposes 26 GPIO pins (plus inputs on the
2025-12-01 18:56:55 [gemini-3-pro] ADC pins).
2025-12-01 18:56:55 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 18:56:55 [gemini-3-pro] Your current Luma2 allocation uses roughly 10 pins, leaving 16 pins free.
2025-12-01 18:56:55 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 18:56:55 [gemini-3-pro] Regarding your technical questions:
2025-12-01 18:56:55 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 18:56:55 [gemini-3-pro] 1. Stereo Audio
2025-12-01 18:56:55 [gemini-3-pro] • Yes.* You can simply enable `GP27` as `ADC1` for the second channel.
2025-12-01 18:56:55 [gemini-3-pro] • Note: The RP2040 ADC inputs are 0V to 3.3V. Audio signals are AC (swinging positive and
2025-12-01 18:56:55 [gemini-3-pro] negative). You will need a simple DC Bias circuit (two resistors and a capacitor) to lift the audio
2025-12-01 18:56:55 [gemini-3-pro] signal to ~1.65V so the Pico can sample the full wave without clipping the negative voltages.
2025-12-01 18:56:55 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 18:56:55 [gemini-3-pro] 2. Chroma: Edge Counting vs More Bits
2025-12-01 18:56:55 [gemini-3-pro] For the Commodore 64 specifically, Edge Counting (1-bit Chroma) is "good enough" to get 90%
2025-12-01 18:56:55 [gemini-3-pro] of the way there, but it has limits.
2025-12-01 18:56:55 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 18:56:55 [gemini-3-pro] • The Physics:*
2025-12-01 18:56:55 [gemini-3-pro] C64 Color (PAL/NTSC) is defined by two things:
2025-12-01 18:56:55 [gemini-3-pro] 1.  Phase (Hue): Where the wave starts relative to the "Burst".
2025-12-01 18:56:55 [gemini-3-pro] 2.  Amplitude (Saturation): How "tall" the wave is.
2025-12-01 18:56:55 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 18:56:55 [gemini-3-pro] • Edge Counting (1 Comparator):*
2025-12-01 18:56:55 [gemini-3-pro] This turns the sine wave into a square wave.
2025-12-01 18:56:55 [gemini-3-pro] • Pros: You capture the Phase perfectly. The PIO counts clocks between edges to
2025-12-01 18:56:55 [gemini-3-pro] determine if it's Red, Purple, Green, etc.
2025-12-01 18:56:55 [gemini-3-pro] • Cons: You lose Amplitude. You cannot tell the difference between a "strong" red
2025-12-01 18:56:55 [gemini-3-pro] and a "weak" red, or—more importantly—between "Grey" (No color) and "Color".
2025-12-01 18:56:55 [gemini-3-pro] • Workaround: The PIO can detect "No Signal" (flat line) vs "Signal" (toggling). Since the
2025-12-01 18:56:55 [gemini-3-pro] C64 palette is limited (16 colors), and the Greys/White/Black are distinct Luma levels, you can
2025-12-01 18:56:55 [gemini-3-pro] usually infer the correct color just by combining Luma Level + Phase.
2025-12-01 18:56:55 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 18:56:55 [gemini-3-pro] • The "More Bits" Solution (2 Comparators):*
2025-12-01 18:56:55 [gemini-3-pro] If you want to be precise, you add a second comparator to the Chroma line with a higher voltage
2025-12-01 18:56:55 [gemini-3-pro] threshold.
2025-12-01 18:56:55 [gemini-3-pro] • Comparator A (Zero Cross): Detects Phase.
2025-12-01 18:56:55 [gemini-3-pro] • Comparator B (Threshold): Detects Saturation (only triggers on strong color signals).
2025-12-01 18:56:55 [gemini-3-pro] • Verdict: For a C64, this is likely overkill. The 5-level Luma slicer you have designed is
2025-12-01 18:56:55 [gemini-3-pro] the most important part. If you get Luma right, 1-bit Chroma edge counting is sufficient.
2025-12-01 18:56:55 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 18:56:55 [gemini-3-pro] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
