2025-12-01 18:59:21 [gemini-3-pro] Yes, the RP2040 has enough GPIO for Luma2 even with the Waveshare 1.14" LCD and
2025-12-01 18:59:21 [gemini-3-pro] Stereo Audio added, but it requires a "Tetris-style" pin re-allocation because the LCD HAT
2025-12-01 18:59:21 [gemini-3-pro] hard-wires specific pins.
2025-12-01 18:59:21 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 18:59:21 [gemini-3-pro] Chroma Sampling Logic
2025-12-01 18:59:21 [gemini-3-pro] Regarding your worry about Chroma bits:
2025-12-01 18:59:21 [gemini-3-pro] 1.  Phase (Hue): The C64 generates color by changing the phase of the subcarrier
2025-12-01 18:59:21 [gemini-3-pro] relative to the color burst. A single high-speed comparator (1-bit) is actually optimal here. The
2025-12-01 18:59:21 [gemini-3-pro] PIO can time the distance between zero-crossings (edges) of the burst and the signal. More bits
2025-12-01 18:59:21 [gemini-3-pro] won't help much with phase detection; edge precision is king.
2025-12-01 18:59:21 [gemini-3-pro] 2.  Amplitude (Saturation): While C64 colors do have different saturations, the Phase is
2025-12-01 18:59:21 [gemini-3-pro] the primary identifier. Adding bits here would require a flash ADC (resistor ladder), consuming 3-4
2025-12-01 18:59:21 [gemini-3-pro] more pins.
2025-12-01 18:59:21 [gemini-3-pro] 3.  Verdict: Stick to Edge Counting with one comparator. It is "as good as it gets"
2025-12-01 18:59:21 [gemini-3-pro] for a digital GPIO approach without an external video decoder chip.
2025-12-01 18:59:21 [gemini-3-pro] 
