2025-12-01 20:16:28 [nitro@ryzen5] is lcd ips? whats ips?
2025-12-01 20:17:13 [gemini-3-pro] Yes, Luma2 has plenty of spare GPIO.
2025-12-01 20:17:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 20:17:13 [gemini-3-pro] The RP2040 exposes 26 GPIO pins. Your current Luma2 spec uses roughly 10 pins.
2025-12-01 20:17:13 [gemini-3-pro] You have enough room for Stereo Audio (ADC), the Chroma comparator, and an SPI screen.
2025-12-01 20:17:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 20:17:13 [gemini-3-pro] Here is the breakdown of your questions regarding Chroma, the LCD, and the pinout strategy.
2025-12-01 20:17:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 20:17:13 [gemini-3-pro] 1. Chroma Sampling: Edge Counting vs. More Comparators
2025-12-01 20:17:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 20:17:13 [gemini-3-pro] For the Commodore 64 (and most 8-bit composite signals), Edge Counting (Phase Detection) is the
2025-12-01 20:17:13 [gemini-3-pro] correct approach, not more voltage comparators.
2025-12-01 20:17:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 20:17:13 [gemini-3-pro] • How C64 Color Works: The color is determined by the Phase (timing delay) of the
2025-12-01 20:17:13 [gemini-3-pro] AC sine wave relative to the "Color Burst" sync pulse.
2025-12-01 20:17:13 [gemini-3-pro] • Edge Counting: A single comparator turns the Chroma sine wave into a square wave. The
2025-12-01 20:17:13 [gemini-3-pro] RP2040 PIO is fast enough (running at >125MHz) to measure the exact time between the Sync pulse and
2025-12-01 20:17:13 [gemini-3-pro] the Chroma rising edge.
2025-12-01 20:17:13 [gemini-3-pro] • More Comparators? Adding more comparators would measure Saturation (how vivid the
2025-12-01 20:17:13 [gemini-3-pro] color is vs grey). While technically "better," a single comparator is standard for retro upscalers
2025-12-01 20:17:13 [gemini-3-pro] because the C64 has a fixed palette; if you detect the phase, you know which of the 16 colors it
2025-12-01 20:17:13 [gemini-3-pro] is.
2025-12-01 20:17:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 20:17:13 [gemini-3-pro] • Verdict:* Stick to the single comparator for Chroma. It is simpler and relies on the
2025-12-01 20:17:13 [gemini-3-pro] PIO's timing precision, which is the RP2040's strength.
2025-12-01 20:17:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 20:17:13 [gemini-3-pro] 2. What is IPS?
2025-12-01 20:17:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 20:17:13 [gemini-3-pro] • IPS (In-Plane Switching)* is a type of LCD panel technology.
2025-12-01 20:17:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 20:17:13 [gemini-3-pro] • Old screens (TN): If you look at them from an angle, the colors invert or turn black.
2025-12-01 20:17:13 [gemini-3-pro] • IPS screens: You can look at the screen from almost any angle (up, down, side), and the
2025-12-01 20:17:13 [gemini-3-pro] colors stay accurate and vibrant.
2025-12-01 20:17:13 [gemini-3-pro] • Relevance: The Waveshare 1.14" is an IPS display. It looks excellent and is perfect for a
2025-12-01 20:17:13 [gemini-3-pro] handheld or small monitor project.
2025-12-01 20:17:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 20:17:13 [gemini-3-pro] 3. Waveshare 1.14" LCD Integration
2025-12-01 20:17:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 20:17:13 [gemini-3-pro] The Waveshare 1.14" Pico Module is a "Hat" that plugs directly onto the Pico headers. It uses a
2025-12-01 20:17:13 [gemini-3-pro] specific set of pins for the SPI interface and 4 buttons.
2025-12-01 20:17:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 20:17:13 [gemini-3-pro] • Resolution Warning: The 1.14" LCD is 240x135* pixels.
2025-12-01 20:17:13 [gemini-3-pro] The C64 is 320x200.
2025-12-01 20:17:13 [gemini-3-pro] • You will need to downscale (skip pixels) to fit the image on this screen, or show a cropped
2025-12-01 20:17:13 [gemini-3-pro] window.
2025-12-01 20:17:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 20:17:13 [gemini-3-pro] Revised Luma2 + LCD Pinout
2025-12-01 20:17:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-01 20:17:13 [gemini-3-pro] To make Luma2 work with the Waveshare LCD, we must avoid the pins the LCD uses (GP8-GP13 for
2025-12-01 20:17:13 [gemini-3-pro] screen, GP15/GP20 for buttons).
