2025-12-02 07:13:43 [nitro@ryzen5] how many pins is gba cart?
2025-12-02 07:13:54 [deepseek-chat] The Nintendo Game Boy Advance cartridge connector has 32 pins (16 pins per side).
2025-12-02 07:13:54 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-02 07:13:54 [deepseek-chat] GBA Cartridge Pinout Summary:
2025-12-02 07:13:54 [deepseek-chat] - 32 total pins (2.54mm/0.1" pitch)
2025-12-02 07:13:54 [deepseek-chat] - 16 pins per side (edge connector)
2025-12-02 07:13:54 [deepseek-chat] - 3.3V logic levels (compatible with RP2040 directly)
2025-12-02 07:13:54 [deepseek-chat] - 5V tolerant I/O on some pins (for legacy GB/GBC games)
2025-12-02 07:13:54 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-02 07:13:54 [deepseek-chat] Key Signal Groups:
2025-12-02 07:13:54 [deepseek-chat] 1. Address Bus: A0-A15 (16-bit, 64KB banked addressing)
2025-12-02 07:13:54 [deepseek-chat] 2. Data Bus: D0-D7 (8-bit)
2025-12-02 07:13:54 [deepseek-chat] 3. Control Signals: /RD, /WR, /CS, /RESET
2025-12-02 07:13:54 [deepseek-chat] 4. Bank Switching: A16-A23 (via GPIO or latch)
2025-12-02 07:13:54 [deepseek-chat] 5. Save Memory: /CS2, various control lines for SRAM/Flash/EEPROM
2025-12-02 07:13:54 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-02 07:13:54 [deepseek-chat] For Your Waffle Design:
2025-12-02 07:13:54 [deepseek-chat] - Direct 3.3V compatibility simplifies design (no level shifters needed)
2025-12-02 07:13:54 [deepseek-chat] - Need to handle wait state timing (GBA has strict access timing)
2025-12-02 07:13:54 [deepseek-chat] - Bank switching required for >64KB ROMs
2025-12-02 07:13:54 [deepseek-chat] - Save memory support adds complexity
2025-12-02 07:13:54 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-02 07:13:54 [deepseek-chat] The 32-pin count makes it one of your simpler physical interfaces (compared to 64-pin Megadrive or
2025-12-02 07:13:54 [deepseek-chat] 44-pin C64).
2025-12-02 07:13:54 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-02 07:16:53 [nitro@ryzen5]  Project WaffleA GBA RP2040 hookup using 32 pin 3V3 GBA cartridge port[USB] � RP2040 � 3x74HC165 and 1x74LVC245 � cartridge port � [GBA]
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] Good start! Here's my review of your Waffle concept:
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] Design Assessment
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] Strengths
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] - 3.3V Native: No level shifting needed - cleanest electrical design
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] - Reasonable IC Count: 3x 74HC165 + 1x 74LVC245 is manageable
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] - Direct Compatibility: RP2040 GPIO can interface directly with GBA bus
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] Key Considerations
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] 1. GBA Bus Timing
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] The GBA has strict timing requirements:
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] - /CS (Chip Select): Must assert within specific window
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] - Wait States: ROM can request additional wait cycles via WAIT signal
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] - Access Time: ~120ns for normal ROM access
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] 2. Bank Switching
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] GBA uses 16-bit address bus (A0-A15) but supports up to 32MB ROM via bank switching:
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] - Typically uses A16-A23 via GPIO or external latch
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] - Your shift register approach may be too slow for bank switching during active gameplay
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] 3. Save Memory Support
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] GBA carts support various save types:
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] - SRAM (battery backed)
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] - Flash (64KB-128KB)
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] - EEPROM (512B-8KB)
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] Each requires different control signals and timing
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] Suggested Architecture
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] [USB] ─ RP2040 ─ 74LVC245 ─ [GBA Data Bus D0-D7]
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat]                     │
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat]                 74HC165 ─ [GBA Address/Control]
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat]                     │
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat]                 74HC165 ─ [GBA Status/SAV]
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] • Alternative Approach*: Consider using RP2040 PIO for:
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] - Direct bus interfacing (faster than shift registers)
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] - Wait state generation
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] - Bank switching control
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] Pin Allocation Suggestion
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] GP0-7: Data Bus (via 74LVC245)
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] GP8-15: Address/Control capture (via 74HC165 chain)
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] GP16: /CS control
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] GP17: /RD control
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] GP18: /WR control
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] GP19: Bank select latch
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] GP20-22: Save memory control
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] GP26-28: Optional direct bank address lines
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] Critical Questions
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] 1. Primary Use Case: ROM dumping? Flash cart? Development?
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] 2. Speed Requirements: Need real-time game access or just loading?
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] 3. Save Support: Essential for your users?
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] The shift register approach works for reading, but may need augmentation for writing/bank
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] switching. Have you considered a hybrid approach with some direct GPIO connections for
2025-12-02 07:17:13 [deepseek-chat] timing-critical signals?
