2025-12-02 12:38:43 [nitro@ryzen5] how does kicad colorise its step files, or does it use the older file format
2025-12-02 12:39:03 [gemini-3-pro] KiCad uses the STEP AP214 protocol for colorizing models. It does not rely on an external
2025-12-02 12:39:03 [gemini-3-pro] palette like CGA, nor does it default to the older VRML (.wrl) format for color unless specifically
2025-12-02 12:39:03 [gemini-3-pro] configured to do so.
2025-12-02 12:39:03 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-02 12:39:03 [gemini-3-pro] You should avoid a CGA palette; it is too limited for PCB rendering (which requires specific shades
2025-12-02 12:39:03 [gemini-3-pro] for resins, metals, and silkscreens). Instead, you should extract the RGB values directly from the
2025-12-02 12:39:03 [gemini-3-pro] STEP entities.
2025-12-02 12:39:03 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-02 12:39:03 [gemini-3-pro] To extract materials from a STEP file, you need to follow a chain of entity references. The
2025-12-02 12:39:03 [gemini-3-pro] geometry (like `MANIFOLDSOLIDBREP`) is linked to color definitions via `STYLED_ITEM`.
2025-12-02 12:39:03 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-02 12:39:03 [gemini-3-pro] Here is the typical entity chain you need to parse to find the color of a shape:
2025-12-02 12:39:03 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-02 12:39:03 [gemini-3-pro] 1.  `STYLED_ITEM`: Links a geometric representation (the item) to a style.
2025-12-02 12:39:03 [gemini-3-pro] 2.  `PRESENTATIONSTYLEASSIGNMENT`: Referenced by the styled item.
2025-12-02 12:39:03 [gemini-3-pro] 3.  `SURFACESTYLEUSAGE`: Defines how the style is applied to the surface.
2025-12-02 12:39:03 [gemini-3-pro] 4.  `SURFACESIDESTYLE` & `SURFACESTYLEFILL_AREA`: Defines the
2025-12-02 12:39:03 [gemini-3-pro] visual properties.
2025-12-02 12:39:03 [gemini-3-pro] 5.  `COLOUR_RGB`: Contains the actual Red, Green, and Blue values (usually 0.0 to 1.0).
2025-12-02 12:39:03 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-02 12:39:03 [gemini-3-pro] I can provide a named function to traverse these relationships and extract colors if you would
2025-12-02 12:39:03 [gemini-3-pro] like.
2025-12-02 12:39:03 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-02 12:39:35 [nitro@ryzen5] can we start by logging all styled_items
2025-12-02 12:39:53 [gemini-3-pro] KiCad exports STEP files using the AP214 protocol, which supports color and layers natively. It
2025-12-02 12:39:53 [gemini-3-pro] does not rely on legacy formats or fixed palettes like CGA; instead, it defines specific RGB values
2025-12-02 12:39:53 [gemini-3-pro] linked to geometric faces.
2025-12-02 12:39:53 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-02 12:39:53 [gemini-3-pro] The hierarchy for color in STEP usually looks like this:
2025-12-02 12:39:53 [gemini-3-pro] 1.  STYLEDITEM: Links a geometric item (like a MANIFOLDSOLID_BREP) to a style.
2025-12-02 12:39:53 [gemini-3-pro] 2.  PRESENTATIONSTYLEASSIGNMENT: Linked by the Styled Item.
2025-12-02 12:39:53 [gemini-3-pro] 3.  SURFACESTYLEUSAGE: Defines how the surface looks.
2025-12-02 12:39:53 [gemini-3-pro] 4.  COLOUR_RGB: The actual color definition.
2025-12-02 12:39:53 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-02 12:39:53 [gemini-3-pro] Here is the named function to log `STYLED_ITEM` entities and their parameters to help you trace the
2025-12-02 12:39:53 [gemini-3-pro] color references.
2025-12-02 12:39:53 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-02 12:39:53 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-02 12:39:53 [gemini-3-pro] function logStyledItems(entities: any[]) {
2025-12-02 12:39:53 [gemini-3-pro] 	const styledItems = entities.filter(e => e.type === "STYLED_ITEM");
2025-12-02 12:39:53 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-02 12:39:53 [gemini-3-pro] 	if (styledItems.length === 0) return;
2025-12-02 12:39:53 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-02 12:39:53 [gemini-3-pro] 	console.log(`\n--- Styled Items (${styledItems.length}) ---`);
2025-12-02 12:39:53 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-02 12:39:53 [gemini-3-pro] 	styledItems.forEach(item => {
2025-12-02 12:39:53 [gemini-3-pro] 		// Params usually format: (name, list_of_presentation_style_assignment_refs, item_ref)
2025-12-02 12:39:53 [gemini-3-pro] 		console.log(`#${item.id} STYLED_ITEM: ${item.params}`);
2025-12-02 12:39:53 [gemini-3-pro] 	});
2025-12-02 12:39:53 [gemini-3-pro] }
2025-12-02 12:39:53 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-02 12:39:53 [gemini-3-pro] // Add this call inside your main loop:
2025-12-02 12:39:53 [gemini-3-pro] // const stepJSON = await parseStepFile(entry.path);
2025-12-02 12:39:53 [gemini-3-pro] // logStyledItems(stepJSON.entities);
2025-12-02 12:39:53 [gemini-3-pro] 
