2025-12-02 12:42:13 [gemini-3-pro] Regarding the CGA Palette
2025-12-02 12:42:13 [gemini-3-pro] You should not use the CGA palette. STEP files (specifically AP214, which KiCad uses)
2025-12-02 12:42:13 [gemini-3-pro] contain explicit high-fidelity RGB definitions. KiCad exports the specific hex values defined in
2025-12-02 12:42:13 [gemini-3-pro] the footprint properties (e.g., the beige of a plastic connector or the gold of a pin). Using a
2025-12-02 12:42:13 [gemini-3-pro] fixed palette would lose that visual data.
2025-12-02 12:42:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-02 12:42:13 [gemini-3-pro] How KiCad Colorizes STEP Files
2025-12-02 12:42:13 [gemini-3-pro] KiCad uses the ISO 10303-21 (AP214) protocol for styling. It does not rely on the older
2025-12-02 12:42:13 [gemini-3-pro] VRML sidecar files when you are processing the raw STEP export.
2025-12-02 12:42:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-02 12:42:13 [gemini-3-pro] The coloring mechanism is a relational chain:
2025-12-02 12:42:13 [gemini-3-pro] 1.  `COLOUR_RGB`: Defines the actual Red, Green, Blue values (usually floats between 0.0
2025-12-02 12:42:13 [gemini-3-pro] and 1.0).
2025-12-02 12:42:13 [gemini-3-pro] 2.  `FILLAREASTYLECOLOUR` or `SURFACESIDE_STYLE`: Wraps the color.
2025-12-02 12:42:13 [gemini-3-pro] 3.  `PRESENTATIONSTYLEASSIGNMENT`: Groups the styles.
2025-12-02 12:42:13 [gemini-3-pro] 4.  `STYLEDITEM`: The bridge entity you found in your logs. It assigns the
2025-12-02 12:42:13 [gemini-3-pro] `PRESENTATIONSTYLE` to a specific Geometric Representation Item (the shape).
2025-12-02 12:42:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-02 12:42:13 [gemini-3-pro] Analyzing Your Log Snippet
2025-12-02 12:42:13 [gemini-3-pro] Your log entries confirm the structure:
2025-12-02 12:42:13 [gemini-3-pro] `#15169 STYLED_ITEM: 'color',(#15170),#8224`
2025-12-02 12:42:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-02 12:42:13 [gemini-3-pro] • `#15169`: The ID of this assignment.
2025-12-02 12:42:13 [gemini-3-pro] • `'color'`: A generic label.
2025-12-02 12:42:13 [gemini-3-pro] • `(#15170)`: The Style ID. You must look up this entity to find the color chain
2025-12-02 12:42:13 [gemini-3-pro] eventually leading to `COLOUR_RGB`.
2025-12-02 12:42:13 [gemini-3-pro] • `#8224`: The Target Geometry ID. This is the `MANIFOLDSOLIDBREP` or
2025-12-02 12:42:13 [gemini-3-pro] `SHELL` that needs to be painted.
2025-12-02 12:42:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-02 12:42:13 [gemini-3-pro] Strategy for the Next Step
2025-12-02 12:42:13 [gemini-3-pro] To implement this, you shouldn't just log them; you need to map them.
2025-12-02 12:42:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-02 12:42:13 [gemini-3-pro] 1.  Parse Phase: When parsing the file, store entities in a Map for O(1) lookup.
2025-12-02 12:42:13 [gemini-3-pro] 2.  Color Resolution: Iterate over all `STYLED_ITEM`s.
2025-12-02 12:42:13 [gemini-3-pro]     *   Recursively resolve the Style ID (e.g., `#15170`) until you find the RGB values.
2025-12-02 12:42:13 [gemini-3-pro]     *   Store the RGB values associated with the Target ID (e.g., `#8224`).
2025-12-02 12:42:13 [gemini-3-pro] 3.  Mesh Generation: When you tesselate (convert shapes to triangles), check if the shape
2025-12-02 12:42:13 [gemini-3-pro] ID has an assigned color.
2025-12-02 12:42:13 [gemini-3-pro]        Note for glTF:* A single glTF `primitive` can only have one material. You will
2025-12-02 12:42:13 [gemini-3-pro] likely need to group your triangles by color/material when generating the mesh arrays.
2025-12-02 12:42:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
