2025-12-02 15:21:24 [kimi-k2-0905] While you wait you can do a full “dry run” on a breadboard with nothing more than:
2025-12-02 15:21:24 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-02 15:21:24 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. One LM319 (or any 5 V comparator you have—LM393, LM358, even a 74HC14 with its input biased near
2025-12-02 15:21:24 [kimi-k2-0905] threshold).
2025-12-02 15:21:24 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. A 75 Ω resistor across the video source and a 10 kΩ + 1 kΩ divider to set sync threshold.
2025-12-02 15:21:24 [kimi-k2-0905] 3. A Pico, a single GPIO for CSYNC, and the PIO program that simply:
2025-12-02 15:21:24 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-02 15:21:24 [kimi-k2-0905]    - arms on CSYNC leading edge
2025-12-02 15:21:24 [kimi-k2-0905]    - samples that same pin (or another if you add a grey comparator) every 70 ns (14× sysclk) for
2025-12-02 15:21:24 [kimi-k2-0905] 320 loops
2025-12-02 15:21:24 [kimi-k2-0905]    - pushes the 320-bit line to USB as a 40-byte packet
2025-12-02 15:21:24 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-02 15:21:24 [kimi-k2-0905] 4. A tiny Python script that listens to the USB serial stream and plots the 0/1 trace in real time
2025-12-02 15:21:24 [kimi-k2-0905] (matplotlib imshow, one line per frame).
2025-12-02 15:21:24 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-02 15:21:24 [kimi-k2-0905] With that you can verify sync separation, check that the sample clock lines up with the 15.6 kHz
2025-12-02 15:21:24 [kimi-k2-0905] h-sync, and tweak threshold voltage before the real comparators arrive. Nothing else on the BOM is
2025-12-02 15:21:24 [kimi-k2-0905] in the signal path, so when the panels and LM319s land you only swap the breadboard comparator for
2025-12-02 15:21:24 [kimi-k2-0905] the final five-channel circuit and redirect the same PIO code to the extra grey GPIOs.
2025-12-02 15:21:24 [kimi-k2-0905] 
