2025-12-03 11:52:32 [nitro@ryzen5] is blender file format stable public?
2025-12-03 11:52:38 [kimi-k2-0905] The .blend format is not a frozen public spec—every new Blender release can (and often
2025-12-03 11:52:38 [kimi-k2-0905] does) change it. If you need stability, export to an interchange format (OBJ, glTF, STL, etc.) and
2025-12-03 11:52:38 [kimi-k2-0905] version those files instead.
2025-12-03 11:52:38 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-03 11:53:28 [nitro@ryzen5] can you outline basic steps to parse, no code thanks
2025-12-03 11:53:43 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. Grab the open-source “blendfile_reader” doc (official, C).
2025-12-03 11:53:43 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. Read 12-byte header → identify pointer-size, endian, version.
2025-12-03 11:53:43 [kimi-k2-0905] 3. From header, jump to first “file block”.
2025-12-03 11:53:43 [kimi-k2-0905] 4. Walk the linked list of blocks (each has code, size, old memory address, SDNA index count).
2025-12-03 11:53:43 [kimi-k2-0905] 5. Collect the “DNA1” block; it is a self-contained type table (structures, names, sizes,
2025-12-03 11:53:43 [kimi-k2-0905] alignments).
2025-12-03 11:53:43 [kimi-k2-0905] 6. Build two lookup arrays from DNA: one for type → size/alignment, one for field → offset.
2025-12-03 11:53:43 [kimi-k2-0905] 7. For every non-DNA block, use its code to pick the correct SDNA type entry, then stride through
2025-12-03 11:53:43 [kimi-k2-0905] the raw bytes with the offset table to pull any field you care about.
2025-12-03 11:53:43 [kimi-k2-0905] 8. Ignore old memory addresses; they’re just relocation hints.
2025-12-03 11:53:43 [kimi-k2-0905] 9. Stop when next-block pointer is zero—end of file.
2025-12-03 11:53:43 [kimi-k2-0905] 
